<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Specifically, these proposals are the result of the historical inability of each of the three areas to develop adequate
state implementation plans (SIPs) under the Clean Air Act of 1977. Because of these failures, EPA was required by law
to produce federal plans. The courts have interpreted that this federal responsibility was continued by the U.S.
Congress even as it substantially amended the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although EPA is issuing this NPRM today because we are legally required to do so, EPA intends to use this opportunity
to assist renewed state and local efforts to achieve clean, healthy air for the citizens of California. Therefore,
the agency has tried to keep one central principle in mind as we fashioned our approach: EPA wants to do this in conjunction
with the ongoing efforts of the local communities, not simply impose federal plans upon them. EPA believes that if
these combined efforts are to be successful, the FIP process must both develop appropriate emissions reduction strategies,
and support the local-state-federal-public cooperation necessary to attain clean air. These proposals therefore
contain not only the specific control strategies necessary to demonstrate attainment in each of the three areas,
but also plans to coordinate, to the extent feasible, an expanded public participation process with that of each local
air board. This coordination is especially important because each local air board is currently developing its own
SIP under the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990. These SIPs must be adopted and submitted to EPA by November 15, 1994.
If they can be approved by EPA, then these local plans can replace proposed federal controls. In light of these ongoing
efforts, EPA strongly believes that a coordinated, inclusive process is the best way to address the regulatory, social
and economic changes which will be required to achieve clean air. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Looked at collectively, the plans proposed here will directly affect more than 15 million people_almost half of California's
population_and virtually all businesses in the South Coast, Sacramento, and Ventura areas. In each FIP area the proposed
stationary controls will require emission reductions from small commercial sources such as bakeries and print shops
to large industrial sources such as petroleum refineries and large manufacturing operations. Most commercial and
industrial activities will be affected. Proposed area source controls may result in cost increases and reformulation
of products as diverse as hair spray, deodorant, pesticides and house paint. Mobile source emission reduction requirements
for autos, trucks, planes, trains, boats, ships, and off-road equipment may result in higher costs to some and significant
changes in the mode of transportation for others. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00031 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
These reductions are made even more difficult_and costly_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because they must come on top of years of significant efforts and reductions. For example, the South Coast area has
managed a reduction in peak ozone levels of nearly 75% since its worst days of the mid-1950's. Yet it remains the center
of the worst air pollution in the country. Sacramento and Ventura, though not facing the same extreme levels of air
pollution as the South Coast, have also made significant strides in reducing ozone levels; but they, too, remain close
to the bottom of any urban area ranking of air quality levels. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Taken together, the cost associated with the proposed pollution control strategies necessary to reach attainment
in these three areas ranges from $4 billion to $6 billion per year over the next 16 years. While these are not new, additional
costs (they were included in the analysis done prior to the passage of the CAAA of 1990), clearly part of developing
these plans is coming to terms with the fact_regardless of which governmental body assumes responsibility for the
effort_that most of the emission reductions that were easy or inexpensive were achieved in these areas some time ago.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The obvious questions at this point are, ``What are the benefits of clean air for these areas? Is it really worth the
cost?'' Ultimately, the second question will be answered by the citizens, and future citizens, who live in these communities.
In order to make this judgement, citizens will need to know the answer to the first question. While we have a firm understanding
of the many health, economic, and aesthetic benefits of clean air, quantifying these benefits has proved difficult.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
What we do know is that the reactivity of ozone causes health problems because it damages lung tissue and sensitizes
the lungs to other irritants. Scientific evidence indicates that ambient levels of ozone not only affect people with
impaired respiratory systems, such as asthmatics, but healthy adults and children as well. Regular exposure to ozone
for 6&hyph;7 hours at concentrations 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
below
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 the levels in the South Coast, Ventura or Sacramento has been found to significantly reduce lung function in normal,
healthy people during periods of moderate exercise. Animal studies have demonstrated that repeated exposure to
ozone for months or years can produce permanent structural damage in the lungs and accelerate the rate of lung function
loss and aging of the lungs. It is also well accepted that ozone can cause significant crop yield losses, extensive
damage to forests, and deterioration of buildings as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
We also know that exposure to elevated carbon monoxide levels is associated with impairment of visual perception,
work capacity, manual dexterity, and learning ability, and with illness and death for those who already suffer from
cardiovascular disease, particularly angina or peripheral vascular disease. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
What we don't know enough about yet is how to assign a direct monetary value to protection from these air pollutants.
Benefits such as reduced health care costs, increased employee attendance and productivity, increased crop yields,
reduced materials damage, and a healthier ecosystem clearly have value. The quality of life values associated with
more than 15 million people finally being able to breathe air free of unhealthful levels of smog and carbon monoxide
must be added to the total as well. On balance, these values_when compared against the costs_were strong enough to
compel the U. S. Congress to mandate that all Americans be provided with this public health protection. That is what
the Clean Air Act is all about. Ultimately, these federal implementation plans, and the state and local efforts which
will coincide with them, are about finally bringing that protection to the citizens of California. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. EPA's Goals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00032 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As the above discussion suggests, we seek to meet several 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
goals as we engage in the development and promulgation of FIPs for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these California areas. These goals compel EPA to go beyond normal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
boundaries of rule development and implementation. EPA views both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the SIP and FIP processes as an opportunity to make real progress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
towards clean air on a variety of fronts. To do that, these FIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must include more than rules which reduce emissions; they must also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
involve a concerted effort to make the process of bringing clean 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air to California work better. This effort will require even 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
better cooperation and coordination among local, state and federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air pollution control agencies, better coordination with the other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
governmental agencies which must contribute to the solutions, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more public involvement and education. Success demands that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
everyone work together on creative and innovative solutions to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difficult air pollution problems these areas still face. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Provide for the Emissions Reductions Needed To Meet EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Obligations
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
First and foremost, EPA's goal is to meet its obligations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the Clean Air Act and under court orders, to promulgate plans 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Sacramento, Ventura, and the South Coast which provide for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment of the health-based ozone and carbon monoxide standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Due to historical failures of these three nonattainment areas to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop adequate plans themselves, the courts have determined that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Clean Air Act, as amended in 1990, requires EPA to provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plans now. Although EPA disagreed with the retention of the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligations in the wake of the 1990 amendments, EPA takes its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligations, and their ultimate objective of clean air very 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seriously.
<FOOTCITE>3</FOOTCITE>
 In EPA's view, any efforts to fulfill other goals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stated below must complement this ultimate objective of clean air. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
3
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA has previously published advance notices of proposed rulemakings (ANPRs) and/or notices of proposed rulemaking
(NPRs) for FIPs in the three areas. For Sacramento, ANPRs were published on April 5, 1990 (55 FR 12669) and May 27, 1992
(57 FR 22194). For the South Coast, EPA published an ANPR on December 7, 1988 (53 FR 49494) and an NPR on September 5, 1990
(55 FR 36458). EPA published an NPR for Ventura on January 17, 1991 (56 FR 1754). While the approach of these FIPs is quite
different, the reader should consult these notices for an account of the litigation, settlement agreements, modifications
to the agreements, regulatory history, and EPA's then-current interpretations of its obligations. For an account
of EPA's views on timing and circumstances of these FIP obligations, see especially the executive summary of the South
Coast NPR. See also Coalition for Clean Air, 971 F.2d 219 (9th Cir. 1992).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Develop the FIPs in a Manner Which Encourages and Assists the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Implementation of Air Quality Measures by State and Local Agencies
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
It is most appropriate for state and local agencies to play 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the lead roles in addressing their own air pollution problems. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fact, that is how Congress, through the Clean Air Act, intended it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to work. Traditionally, this approach has placed EPA in the roles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of technical advisor, grant officer, and, as a last resort when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local or state responsibilities are not met, as a backstop. But 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the air quality problems faced by these California areas are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tremendous, and despite their efforts the state and local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
governments have been as yet unable to provide a plan for attaining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the health standards. It has therefore become necessary, both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
legally and practically, for EPA to play the backstop role at this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As EPA pursues this role, we believe that we must do so in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manner which both encourages and supports the efforts of the state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and local governments to meet their continuing air quality related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibilities, especially regarding the development of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 15 SIP submittal. In short, this means moving beyond the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mere fulfillment of our legal responsibilities toward the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishment of a full working partnership among all parties. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Obviously, the first real test of this new partnership begins 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
now. As has been previously discussed, these FIPs are being 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed at the same time as state and local agencies are preparing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to meet many of the Act's most important requirements, including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their own plans to address the very same obligation as these FIPs: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment of the ozone NAAQS.
<FOOTCITE>4</FOOTCITE>
 EPA is concerned that issuance of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comprehensive regulatory FIPs within the FIP time frame has the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potential to disrupt the SIP planning process. State and local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies will be in the process of designing air quality measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for legislative or board consideration at the same time the federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
government is proposing to promulgate measures to cover the very 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
same emission sources. At the very least, these parallel planning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
processes are likely to create confusion for the public and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulated community. EPA's goal is to develop the FIPs in a manner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that supports effective air quality planning by the responsible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state and local agencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
4
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
For carbon monoxide, the South Coast is revising its CO SIP which was submitted to EPA in 1992. The South Coast is currently
scheduled to submit this revised SIP to EPA in November 1994.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Minimize Federal Intrusion Into State and Local Affairs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA will meet its obligations to prepare federal plans for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
clean air in these three areas of California, and EPA must, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will if necessary, implement the measures it promulgates. However, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA firmly believes that clean air is more likely to be achieved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
faster, and in greater harmony with local economic and community 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
goals, if its own role is minimized by effective state and local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00033 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA's current challenge to minimize federal intrusion is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through the proposal and promulgation of these federal plans_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plans which EPA hopes to see replaced by state and local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. Consequently, EPA will withdraw or rescind any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal measure it proposes or promulgates as soon as it can 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve acceptable SIP measures submitted by the state which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide for commensurate emissions reductions. In fact, EPA hopes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these FIPs will become obsolete as a regulatory matter. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
remaining value of these FIPs would then be the partnerships and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solutions which both minimize federal intrusion and enable the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state and local agencies to achieve the clean air goals as the law 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
envisions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To put this another way, while EPA's goal is to minimize 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal intrusion into state and local decision making and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implementation authority, only prompt action by California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
officials_within the timetables of the CAAA and the court ordered 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines_can significantly diminish the FIPs' impact on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state. EPA intends to assist the state and local agencies in their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efforts to adopt and submit ozone SIPs with the requisite emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions and ozone attainment demonstrations. As pointed out 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
earlier in this discussion, successful state and local action will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allow EPA to withdraw the regulatory pieces of the federal plan or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduce the scope and stringency of its imposed or impending 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Minimize Adverse Socio-Economic Impacts
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA has attempted to fashion these proposed FIPs to meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment obligations while trying hard to avoid unnecessarily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
severe social and economic impacts, both through the choice of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particular regulations and by the timing of their implementation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Despite these mitigatory approaches, federal regulation sufficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to achieve the NAAQS in these heavily polluted areas has the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potential for significant social and economic disruption. This is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the inevitable result of the extraordinary emission reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required, the advanced level of control that already exists, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the constraints on EPA's authority and practical ability to enact 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and implement many of the most appropriate control approaches for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these areas.
<FOOTCITE>5</FOOTCITE>
 Current control strategies in these areas, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
especially in the South Coast, already employ state of the art 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technology and regulatory design for many pollution sources. To 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieve attainment in these areas, in effect, we must do even 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
better. To mitigate the severe impact of further near-term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reduction requirements, EPA has attempted to identify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control options which provide emission sources with flexibility and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentives for technological development, product reformulation and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pollution prevention. We will look to the public comments on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposals to further identify such strategies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
5
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposals in this rulemaking are designed to achieve the substantial emission reductions required in these three
areas and are not necessarily appropriate for areas with lesser problems.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
We want to emphasize here again that state and local agencies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are in a far better position to tailor plans for the areas that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimize adverse impacts of the attainment obligations. Through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proximity they have a better understanding of their emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources and their local communities. They can also achieve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant mobile source reductions from transportation and land 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use measures that are beyond EPA's resources and authority to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement directly. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For example, there is a growing body of evidence supporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the use of market-based programs, sometimes incorporating fees, to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
encourage emissions reductions and transitions to cleaner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technologies and processes. Such policies can be effectively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designed and implemented at the local level where a specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
understanding of local conditions and economies can be integrated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into a flexible program and, regarding fee-based programs, where 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revenues can be redistributed within the community. A well 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designed program can effectively minimize the disruptive impacts of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major emissions reductions. Conversely, federal measures for local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implementation must be general enough to provide for enforcement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from a distance, and have severe legal restraints relative to local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revenue issues. The federal Miscellaneous Receipts Act, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example, requires revenues from any such federal fee-based programs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be deposited in the U.S. Treasury, thereby preventing their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
redistribution within the local community. In short then, locally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designed and implemented plans become the best means of minimizing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
socio-economic impacts. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5. Ensure Fairness with Respect to Impacts on the Population as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Whole and on Individual Air Pollution Sources
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The extent of the air quality problems in these three areas, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as well as principles of equity, demand that emission reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must come from every source of pollution_from cars to refineries 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to hand-held spray paint. Fairness is an elusive goal_what seems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fair to one may not seem fair to another_but there are a few 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facts to bear in mind as we formulate a plan for clean air: (1) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Some emission sources have been required to reduce more than others 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the past; (2) Some emissions can be reduced more cheaply than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
others; (3) Some individuals and sources of emissions can more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
easily afford to comply with air pollution regulations than others. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Myriad sources comprise the pieces of the air quality problem, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and each of these sources must be part of the solution. What part 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the solution each will contribute, and how to fashion those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contributions into a plan is where the challenge lies. EPA has and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will continue to consider the issue of fairness as it develops 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these FIP strategies. We encourage the regulated community and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
public to make sure that the clean air strategies which ultimately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
get implemented are fair by: (1) Constructively participating in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP public involvement processes; and (2) supporting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development of appropriate SIP measures to displace as much of each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the FIPs as possible. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
6. Ensure That the Federal Government Does its Part to Contribute 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the Solution
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00034 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The solution to these air quality problems will require the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
participation of all sectors that can contribute. There are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
several ways that these FIP proposals embody the federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
government's contribution to the solution. First, EPA is meeting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its legal obligations to provide plans for attainment in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. EPA has proposed plans which, building upon existing state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission control strategies, will meet the ultimate goal of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment. EPA hopes that this federal effort will, by actually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
providing a complete plan, leverage state and local efforts to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
complete their 1994 ozone attainment SIPs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Second, some of the federal rules proposed today would achieve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions from emission sources over which the federal government 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has sole jurisdiction. Emissions from sources such as new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
locomotives, ship engines, aircraft engines, and some farm and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construction equipment have not been previously included in state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and local efforts to develop comprehensive and complete attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plans. While EPA believes that these sources are appropriate for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation, there remain unresolved legal and policy issues 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regarding the extent to which, if at all, EPA can or should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to regulate them indefinitely. In other words, should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SIPs, such as those due in November 1994, be allowed to take credit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for federally-mandated reductions from these types of sources? EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is requesting comment on this issue. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Third, the FIPs proposed today include reductions from federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities such as military bases. EPA intends to continue working 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with other federal agencies to reduce emissions from their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations in these FIP areas as well as other nonattainment areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, as we have stated earlier, EPA views these FIPs as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
opportunity to contribute much more than a comprehensive set of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. The ultimate goal of clean air requires public 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support and education, development of new clean technologies, use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of environmentally sound modes of transportation, and a fundamental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shift to include pollution prevention into our everyday decision 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
making both at home and at work. Along with these proposed FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules, EPA as an agency is pursuing a set of initiatives which will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contribute to these pieces of the clean air solution. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efforts include implementation of the President's Climate Change 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Action Plan; an increasing emphasis by the agency on public 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
awareness of environmental issues and solutions and implementation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the National Environmental Education Act; participation in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development and funding of a government-wide Environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Technology Initiative; and a direct, agency-wide focus on pollution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevention in every rulemaking we undertake. Over time, these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efforts will provide significant emissions reductions not only for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California, but across America as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Summary of the FIP Contents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under the Clean Air Act, nonattainment areas are classified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
according to the severity of their pollution problem. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment deadlines that are then assigned reflect the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
classification. The current classifications for the three FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ozone nonattainment areas are set forth below. Also depicted are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the attainment deadlines, which are as expeditious as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practicable, but not later than the following dates: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<TABLE>

<!-- PJG tableformat 3,L2,tp0,i1,s25,r25,8 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=1 -->
&blank;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Area     
<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Classification     
<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Attainment date
<!-- PJG 007 0152 intable -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=1 f=1 -->
South Coast   
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Extreme    
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
2010 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura        
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Severe      
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
2005 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento      
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Serious      
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
1999
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</TABLE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For a number of reasons, EPA believes that the control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies available to the Agency to attain the ozone standard in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Sacramento area by 1999 could result in unacceptable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
socioeconomic hardship. For example, nitrogen oxides (NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions within the Sacramento area are overwhelmingly from mobile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources. And, while significant reductions of mobile source NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions will result from the scheduled phasing in of cleaner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines and fuels, this changeover to cleaner engines and fuels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will take some time (e.g. manufacturing changes, replacement costs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
infrastructure changes). This is especially true since owners 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
legitimately want to continue to receive the benefits of a full 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
useful life from their present vehicles and engines. Therefore, in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to get the planned emissions reductions earlier, i.e., before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999, EPA must either restrict the use of current, dirtier vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and engines, or somehow force turnover in the current vehicle and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine fleet. That means considering options like no-drive days, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or dramatically increasing the costs associated with operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles and engines currently in use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Moreover, in order to attain by 1999, stationary sources in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Sacramento area would be required to reduce their volatile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organic compounds (VOC) emissions by 20% by 1999, which because of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the magnitude of reductions could lead to potential production 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cutbacks or shutdowns. As a result of these negative socioeconomic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impacts, EPA is proposing, in addition to the 1999 attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
option, the option of reclassifying the Sacramento area to severe. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's reclassification proposal would move the attainment deadline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to 2005, and would allow the Sacramento area, for purposes of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment, to benefit from substantial emissions reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
without the use of more disruptive emissions reduction options. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Even with the additional requirements associated with a severe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designation (e.g. the Employee Commute Option program, and more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stringent New Source Review requirements) the Agency believes that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this 2005 deadline can result in attainment without the onerous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consequences associated with Sacramento's current classification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
