<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00035 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The South Coast is classified as a serious CO nonattainment area and is required to attain the CO NAAQS as expeditiously
as practicable, but not later than 2000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given these attainment deadlines, the principal technical objectives of the proposed FIPs are to: (1) Determine
the pollutant emission reductions needed to attain the NAAQS in each area; (2) select the additional control strategies
best suited to achieve these reductions; and (3) develop specific measures for each strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Required Emission Reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The first task depends on the collection and analysis of critical data relating emissions to ambient concentrations.
To the greatest extent possible, EPA's proposed FIPs use data compiled by the California Air Resources Board (CARB)
and the responsible local agencies (e.g. the local air pollution control districts and councils of governments)
regarding current and future sources and levels of emissions in each area. In order to remain consistent with state
and local planning efforts, EPA has made adjustments to these data only where absolutely necessary to correct errors,
improve the precision and currency of the data, or analyze more effectively the impact of control measures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Of necessity, the proposed FIPs rely on air quality measurements and meteorological input from the extensive CARB
and district monitoring networks. Finally, in order to relate emissions levels to ambient pollutant concentrations,
EPA has chosen to rely on modeling analyses developed cooperatively with the involved state and local agencies and
used in their ongoing SIP development activities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These technical foundations of the FIPs are discussed in section III.H., and further details may be found in the technical
support documents accompanying this proposal. The data and analyses will be revised to reflect the final FIP strategies
and to conform, to the extent possible, with new information and assumptions used by the state and local agencies in
the 1994 ozone SIPs for each area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The results of these complex technical assessments confirm the need for massive further reductions in emissions
of the ozone precursor pollutants in each area.
<FOOTCITE>6</FOOTCITE>
 In the most challenging case, current levels of VOC must be reduced by 90 percent and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions must be reduced by 70 percent to reach attainment of the ozone NAAQS in the South Coast by the target date of
2010. In Ventura, approximately a 40 percent reduction of both VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 appears to be necessary for ozone attainment by 2005. The reduction requirements for the Sacramento area are slightly
lower, but still amount to roughly 40 percent for VOC and 30 percent for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 by 1999 or 2005. Finally, approximately a 45 percent reduction in CO emissions is needed in order to attain the CO standard
in the South Coast Air Basin by 2000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
6
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Ground-level ozone, more commonly known as smog, is formed when nitrogen oxides (NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
) and volatile organic compounds (VOCs) react with high temperatures. Strategies for reducing smog generally require
reductions in both VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions. Ozone causes health problems by damaging lung tissue and sensitizing the lungs to other irritants.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Selection of Control Strategies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA's selection of control strategies for the FIP areas began with a review of the existing emissions inventories
to determine which pollution source categories were present in each area and the extent to which each category contributed
ozone precursor (VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) pollutant emissions. All mobile source categories are present in each FIP area with the exception of ocean-going
vessels in Sacramento, but the relative importance of individual mobile source categories varies significantly
among the three areas. While the South Coast area includes most industrial and commercial source categories, the
emissions inventory for the Sacramento area includes very few of these sources. The importance of agriculture in
Ventura County is reflected in the unusual proportion of that area's VOC inventory assigned to pesticides use. More
details on the inventory profile for each area may be found in sections II.A and III.H. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For stationary sources, EPA next reviewed the extent to which each category was currently controlled under either
federal, state, or local regulations. In those few cases where the existing level of control failed to meet existing
national requirements for application of reasonably available control technology (RACT), EPA prepared remedial
regulations for specific industrial sources identified in section III.C.2., below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA then reviewed the potential for further reductions from each stationary source category in each area, based on
upgrading the level of control to match that required by the best existing rules, by further enhancements to existing
rules to reflect available technology, or by imposing requirements that could be met through technology transfer
from similar sources. From this exercise, EPA selected and prepared most of the industrial and commercial source
control measures discussed in section III.C.3. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
That brings us to the first decision point where the awkwardness of having the federal government undertaking air
quality planning for a particular state or locality becomes obvious. How will the remaining emission reductions
be apportioned among mobile sources, factories, and consumer products and services? The choice is best made with
both a consideration of the costs and benefits associated with particular control measures and a knowledge of the
local impacts that will occur when a specific choice is made. In addition, each locality has a different mix of industries
in different states of economic health and with differing capabilities to absorb the costs of emission control. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00036 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Lacking detailed, specific information about every emissions source in each FIP area, the choice EPA has made in this
proposal is to seek further reductions in all categories of pollution sources generally in the same proportion as
their remaining contribution to the problem. Given EPA's distant starting point in this area of regulation (i.e.
EPA is not the local air board) this approach is as fair as EPA can be. This means that the FIPs' reductions are generally
apportioned in an ``equal,'' across-the-board fashion even though individual pollution sources or source categories
may vary substantially in their starting points (the extent to which they have already reduced their emissions) and
in their ability to achieve further emission reductions. Local air boards have a greater capacity for developing
the kind of detailed, specific information about local emissions sources which can improve and replace these policies.
EPA encourages the local boards to do so wherever possible. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As a result, significant further reductions must be achieved by industrial sources, such as petroleum refineries,
which may have already invested hundreds of millions of dollars in control equipment. EPA is proposing that these
sources reduce their VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions still further, not only through conventional, technology-based regulations (for example, the fugitive
emissions regulation presented in section III.C.3.j.), but also through market-based ``cap'' rules, which allow
sources the flexibility to select their reduction approach so long as overall emissions are reduced annually (see
section III.C.5.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Area source emissions must also be dramatically reduced through regulations that apply either to individuals or,
in some cases, the national manufacturers and distributors of materials whose application, storage, or use contribute
evaporative VOC emissions. The category of area sources proposed for new (or further) regulation in the FIPs includes
such diverse substances or activities as clean-up solvents, architectural coatings, waste burning, landfills,
pesticides, consumer products, livestock waste, and residential water heaters (see sections III.C.3. and III.C.4.).

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Certain of the area sources present special challenges both to the regulated community and to the regulator. For example,
today's NPRM includes a proposal that manufacturers of pesticides not only continue to comply with existing state
and federal rules that address toxics concerns but also reformulate their products sold in California to diminish
VOC emissions (section III.C.4.d.). Regulation of other area sources, including most categories of consumer products,
involves complex considerations of the extent to which additional reductions can be achieved without negatively
affecting the products' quality or its effectiveness.
<FOOTCITE>7</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
7
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
In the case of consumer products, CARB has already undertaken these analyses and adopted regulations. These FIPs
do not include any new requirements for these consumer product categories, but would make the existing state requirements
federally enforceable.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00037 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA began its selection process for mobile source control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies with those measures that were required in all states by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990, and those measures being implemented by the State of California and local agencies.
EPA considered the impact of these measures as well as the expected benefit of national mobile source rules in process.
For example, the California Low Emissions Vehicle program (LEV) relies on advanced emission control technologies,
clean gasoline, and an on-board diagnostics system which achieve substantial reductions beyond those which will
result from federal cars. As described above, EPA then developed mobile source control strategies such that generally
equal, proportional reductions would be achieved in all stationary, area and mobile source categories. EPA developed
strategies which, if implemented, would result in cleaner vehicles and engines via further improvements in technology
or mobile source users selecting alternatives with lower polluting characteristics. EPA also developed strategies
which rely on declining emission limits for other remaining mobile source categories, both to provide sufficient
reductions for attainment and to assure that  each category of emission source was contributing to the solution.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In 1990, passenger cars, other highway vehicles, nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines and equipment, and the transportation facilities used in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interstate commerce produced from 53 to 63 percent of the total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inventory of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and from 70 to 94 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent of the total inventory of oxides of nitrogen (NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP areas covered by this proposal. In order to demonstrate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment of the ozone standard in these areas, EPA is proposing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations which assure significant reductions in mobile source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions_beyond those already expected from the continued 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
turnover to newer, cleaner vehicles and engines. The mobile source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
category covers a very broad range of sizes of equipment, types of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fuels, uses and operating modes, and extent of current pollution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control. Therefore, there is no one measure or even one uniform 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
type of measure which is proposed across the category to achieve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the reductions needed for the attainment plans (see section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.D.1.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The ozone attainment responsibility also falls on mobile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources that may operate on a national (or even an international) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scale. Many of these mobile sources have not previously been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controlled and must now undertake for the first time challenging 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pollution abatement assignments. For example, EPA is proposing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that locomotives and large ships work to reduce NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from their respective activities in the FIP areas (see 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sections III.D.4.e.(3). and (4).). Airline operators, though to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some extent previously regulated for air quality purposes, will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also be asked to make further emissions reductions from aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and other airport activities (see section III.D.4.e.(2).). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, high quality inspection and maintenance (I/M) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs for vehicles remain the single most cost effective and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
important measure for achieving mobile source emissions reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the FIPs, EPA is proposing a program which follows closely the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
model for meeting the performance standards for all I/M programs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nationwide, and which EPA could effectively implement. EPA and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State of California are continuing to hold discussions on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different enhanced program design which would meet the national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced I/M performance standard if implemented by the state. If 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an agreement is reached, appropriate legislation and regulations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are adopted, and an approvable SIP revision is submitted, EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
withdraw or rescind the FIP I/M program (section III.D.2.c). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One reason that vehicle emissions continue to be such a large 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portion of total VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions_despite increasingly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stringent emission standards for vehicles_is because of high 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
growth in vehicle miles traveled (VMT). This VMT increase reflects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the simple facts that more cars are on the road today than ever 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before, and more people are driving more miles than ever before. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to encourage a reduction in VMT, and in the number of cars 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the road, EPA is proposing two programs designed to decrease 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
growth in VMT by targeting single occupant vehicle use. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed measures will require increases in average occupancy in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personal vehicles in Sacramento and encourage shifts to carpooling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and public modes of transportation in all the FIP areas (sections 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.F.2 and III.D.2.g). Various other strategies could be used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieve decreases in VMT. As discussed below and elsewhere in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
today's NPRM, EPA resources, and statutory authority have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constrained our ability to propose these possibly more effective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In designing these FIPs, EPA has attempted, wherever possible, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to structure the proposed FIP rules so that responsible state and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local agencies can adopt the rules as part of their SIPs or accept 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA delegation to implement the federal rules (section III.J.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For example, the proposed New Source Review (NSR) regulations for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Sacramento FIP area tracks the existing rule format and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
terminology shared by the 5 Sacramento area air pollution control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
districts, rather than following the design of EPA's existing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal NSR regulations (section III.F.4.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While the emission reduction targets in Sacramento and Ventura 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are formidable, the overwhelming reductions needed for ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment in the South Coast appear to require that each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual pollution source within the FIP area eventually abate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its emissions almost completely. For practically every 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controllable source category, this ultimate degree of control is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beyond a level now foreseeable with existing technology and control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
techniques. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Congress recognized that this unique South Coast attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
challenge required sustained commitment to seek and apply new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technological solutions. EPA's proposed FIP employs the special 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provision in the 1990 Clean Air Act Amendments, which allows for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commitments (rather than fully adopted regulations) as part of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast ozone attainment demonstration. The South Coast FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes these commitments and outlines some of the federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
activities currently underway to support the development and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercialization of new control technologies or pollution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevention techniques (see section III.B.5.d.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00038 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The state has already submitted a SIP for the South Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrating attainment of the CO NAAQS by the year 2000. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstration relies primarily on existing state programs for clean 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fuels and vehicles, supplemented by an enhanced motor vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection and maintenance (I/M) program. Since the state has not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
yet developed and submitted legislation and regulations to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement an enhanced I/M program, EPA is proposing to complete the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state's attainment demonstration with a single federal measure: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the contractor-run I/M program discussed in section III.D.2.c. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed above, EPA believes that an I/M program is a necessary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part of the ozone FIPs as well.
<FOOTCITE>8</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
8
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
See earlier discussion on I/M.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Specific Control Measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
a. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Stationary/area source measures.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 With few exceptions, industrial and commercial sources in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP areas are already subject to air pollution controls that match 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or exceed the stringency of rules applied elsewhere in the country. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the Sacramento FIP area, however, EPA was able to identify four 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major industrial facilities (Michigan-California Lumber Company; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Formica Corporation; SierraPine Limited; and Reynold's Metals) for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which the applicable local regulations do not reflect the minimum 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level of control required nationally. The state and local air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
districts remain under an obligation to adopt and submit ``RACT'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations for these sources. Since these agencies (specifically, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Placer County and El Dorado County Air Pollution Control Districts) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have not yet acted, EPA is today proposing detailed provisions that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will contribute to attainment in the Sacramento area by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplementing the applicable regulations to match available control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technology. See section III.C.2, and proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(p)&hyph;(s). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA selected a second set of proposed FIP controls by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comparing existing air pollution regulations in each FIP area with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the best existing state or local regulation, on a category-by-category basis. Where a more effective control measure
that could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide significant reductions already existed (either as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopted or draft rule), EPA has adapted and proposed the rule for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal administration in the applicable FIP areas. Since existing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast controls are generally the most stringent in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
country, many of these proposed rules were based on existing SCAQMD 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations and are proposed for the Sacramento and Ventura areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In some cases, the proposed FIP rules newly regulate sources in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area (e.g., commercial bakeries and municipal landfills in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento FIP area). In other cases, the proposed rules 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly strengthen existing regulatory provisions (e.g., 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tighter coatings and solvents limits in Sacramento and Ventura). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The costs, regulatory strategy, and technical basis of these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures are discussed individually in section III.C.3. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations themselves appear in proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(a)&hyph;(o) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and (t)&hyph;(v). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing to enforce on a statewide basis a group of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
four area source rules. EPA proposes that they be administered on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a statewide basis primarily because ensuring compliance only within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP areas would be relatively ineffective. EPA's rationale for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statewide control appears in section III.C.4.a. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The first of these controls simply proposes CARB's existing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer product regulations, which regulate products such as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
detergents, floor finishes and deodorants, and are enforced against 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers, distributors, and retailers. While the CARB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations have been adopted and enforced by the state for several 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years, California has not yet submitted them to EPA for inclusion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the SIP. The proposed FIP measure would make the measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federally enforceable, and is discussed in sections II.B.1. and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.4.c. The proposed regulation is 40 CFR 52.2957. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The second proposed statewide control is based on a draft CARB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer product rule for the aerosol spray paint category, as well 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as rules developed by SCAQMD and the Bay Area Air Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Management District. Proposed rule 40 CFR 52.2958 would limit the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC content of spray paints that could be sold or used within the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The third proposed statewide control deals with one of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
largest nonmobile sources of VOC: architectural and industrial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance (AIM) coatings, including most primers, paints, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lacquers, stains, and specialty coatings used for residential, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial, and industrial purposes. The proposed rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progressively tightens restrictions on VOC content in three phases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of reduction through the year 2003 in order to allow for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development of additional VOC reduction strategies. The FIP rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may be amended if appropriate, in the event that EPA issues a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national AIM coatings rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00039 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The fourth rule proposed for statewide application is designed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to reduce VOC emissions from agricultural and structural pesticides 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use. Many other pesticides are subject to CARB's consumer product 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule and EPA's proposed 40 CFR 52.2957. The proposed agricultural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and structural pesticides rule, 40 CFR 52.2960, is necessarily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
complex and is discussed extensively in section III.C.4.d. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule initially requires producers to submit VOC analyses of their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pesticides. Following EPA review of the data, EPA will implement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a six-step procedure for setting a VOC limit to achieve target VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to achieve further necessary reductions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stationary and area sources, EPA proposes declining emission ``cap'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules in each of the FIP areas. The cap rules apply to a large 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portion of the stationary sources of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. For these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources, the rules require annual reductions in emissions from 2001 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through 2005, but allow each source the flexibility to determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
how reductions are to be made. If a 1999 attainment option is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
selected for Sacramento, the Sacramento VOC cap rule would require 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that all reductions be achieved in the attainment year (i.e., by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
January 1, 1999). EPA strongly encourages affected sources to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identify pollution prevention approaches as the ideal response to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this market-based initiative. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed cap rules require sources to submit compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plans in the year 2000, showing how the mandated reductions will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieved, using 1990 actual emissions as the baseline but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subtracting emissions required to be reduced through any SIP or FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules in the period after 1990. EPA is proposing VOC cap rules in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each area, with annual reductions of between 4 and 9 percent, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending upon public comment and EPA's final determination of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate and necessary reduction rate. A NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 cap rule with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual reductions of between 6 and 9 percent is proposed only in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura, since further NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reductions are not needed in Sacramento, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the South Coast has recently adopted a cap program (RECLAIM) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieving comparable reductions to the proposed FIP NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 cap rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is inviting comment on a number of important issues 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
associated with these innovative rules. The Agency particularly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solicits comment on whether a trading component should be added to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase compliance options, and whether it is preferable to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish a manufacturers' ``bubble'' rule for industrial and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial solvents and coatings, rather than regulating users (see 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.5. and 40 CFR 52.2952&hyph;52.2955). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
b. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Mobile source measures_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) Programs for vehicles. Because the magnitude of needed emissions reductions is so 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
great, the FIPs seek to build on reductions already being achieved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the California Low Emission Vehicle (LEV) program. In order to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieve these reductions, EPA is asking for comment on the need for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and benefits of adopting an enhanced in-use compliance program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which would provide extra in-use emissions reductions beyond the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantial reductions already provided by the California LEV 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00040 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Specifically, EPA is proposing to strengthen the recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program for cars and light and medium duty trucks. Vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer's recall responsibilities will be determined by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing the emissions performance of typical vehicles instead of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only vehicles which have been properly maintained. EPA is also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing to increase manufacturer's liability for repair of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle emissions control equipment. Both of these proposals are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intended to provide incentives to manufacturers to increase the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
durability of the emissions control systems in the vehicles they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
produce (III.D.2.d., Appendix I.A., and 40 CFR 52.2962). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is also seeking comment on a program which would require 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that on-board diagnostic systems of new vehicles be capable of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
communicating, to on-road sensors, the operational status of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission control system. This approach would be intended to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particularly target high emitting vehicles, and would remain in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect for the life of the vehicle (III.D.2.d.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For the South Coast, EPA is also proposing a requirement that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain commercial vehicle fleets make the majority of their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchases from inherently low emitting vehicles (ILEVs),
<FOOTCITE>9</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including, for example, natural gas and electric vehicles, 40 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
52.2962. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
9
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Beyond very low exhaust emissions, these vehicles must have little or no evaporative emissions even when emissions
control systems malfunction. In practice this means using fuel sources which have little or no evaporative emissions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For light-duty vehicles, EPA proposes to prohibit California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
residents from importing or registering vehicles purchased outside 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of California that are not certified to meet California's stringent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle standards. This prohibition would begin in 1999, and is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in section III.D.2.h. The proposed regulation is 40 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
52.2964. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given the level of emissions reductions necessary to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate attainment in each of the three FIP areas, EPA believes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it is absolutely essential to propose an enhanced inspection and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance (I/M) program for vehicles which meets national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance standards. As previously discussed, the structure of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP proposal will follow EPA's model program. If the state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enacts and submits approvable legislation and regulations for its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
own enhanced program which meets national performance standards, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will withdraw or rescind the FIP I/M program. This issue is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in more detail in sections III.D.1.b.(7).(a). and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.D.2.c. The proposed regulation is 40 CFR 52.2963. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For heavy duty trucks, the FIP proposals include tighter 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hydrocarbon (HC) and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 exhaust standards for model years 1999 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beyond, strict evaporative HC standards to minimize shifting from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diesel to gasoline beginning in 1999 as well, and a declining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 level for fleet operators beginning in 2000. Fees for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fleet emission averages above the declining NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 targets are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed to help maintain turnover to the newer, cleaner engines.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
At the option of the fleet owner, interstate trucks may comply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with limitations on the number of stops allowed within FIP areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning in 1998 rather then complying with the declining NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
levels. The heavy duty truck proposals are discussed in section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.D.3. and the proposed regulations are in 40 CFR 52.2966. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, for the 1999 attainment date option in Sacramento, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing to include an accelerated retirement strategy for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-highway heavy duty engines. This accelerated retirement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy is discussed in section III.B.3.. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Programs for nonroad vehicles and engines. EPA is proposing to regulate a number of categories of nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles and equipment: Nonroad heavy duty engines, small nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment, motorcycles and recreation vehicles, and marine engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
See discussion in section III.D.4.(a)-(d). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For nonroad heavy duty engines (at or above 50 horsepower, 37 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
kW), a phased approach would begin with a national program targeted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for final rulemaking in May 1994. In the next phase, EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing additional standards for the FIP areas and increased 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers liability for repair of emissions control systems to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
help ensure continued low emission performance. An emissions- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based, fleet-average fee system with a declining emission levels is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed for the FIP areas to maintain turnover to the cleaner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines. For the 1999 attainment date option in Sacramento, EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing a growth cap for the nonroad heavy equipment category. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This cap is discussed in section III.B.3. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For small nonroad equipment (spark ignited engines at or below 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
25 horsepower, 19kW), EPA is proposing to employ a two-phased 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy of first setting national standards for implementation by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1995, and then employing negotiated rulemaking to achieve still 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
further reductions after 2000 or 2001. This strategy reflects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussions already underway at the national level. The standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will propose in the first phase will be similar to the State of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California's regulation for utility and lawn and garden equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines manufactured in 1995 and beyond. In both phases, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect of this action will be to extend regulations to engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which California is currently pre-empted from regulating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(III.D.4.b.2.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The FIP proposals include more stringent exhaust emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards for motorcycles and recreational vehicles such as mopeds, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dirt bikes, all terrain vehicles (ATVs), and go-karts sold in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California after January 1996. The level of stringency may require 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substitution of 4-stroke nonroad engines for some current 2-stroke 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applications (III.D.4.c.(1).). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, EPA is proposing to apply national emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards, scheduled to be proposed in 1994 and finalized by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 1995, to spark-ignited marine propulsion engines such as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outboard engines, personal watercraft (``jet-skis,'' etc.), and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sterndrive and inboard engines. These national rules will apply to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new spark ignition marine propulsion engines produced after August 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1, 1998 and are proposed to be combined with a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
registration/permitting and fee system for marine engine use in FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area waters. Marine engines meeting the new national standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be exempt from the fees which are proposed to begin in 2004 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(III.D.4.b.(3). and III.D.4.c.(2).). For the 1999 attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
option in Sacramento, EPA is proposing a fee system or boating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restriction to reduce emissions from recreational boating by one-third. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00041 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
(3) Program for National Transportation Sources and Federal Activities
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The litigation which resulted in today's FIP proposals arose in part from the South Coast's concern that emission
regulation of ``federal sources'' such as those associated with interstate transportation or military bases was
not keeping pace with controls in other sectors. In order to achieve appropriate emissions reductions in these areas,
the FIP proposals include controls for locomotives, airports and aircraft, large marine vessels, and military installations.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For locomotives, EPA is relying on a national regulation that will apply to NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions from newly manufactured locomotive engines in two phases, first in 2000, then in 2005. Tighter standards
for remanufactured locomotive engines are also being proposed and may be implemented either statewide or nationwide.
Because the necessary reductions in NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions are so significant in the South Coast, EPA is proposing a supplemental program for railroads in the South
Coast FIP. This program would require that railroads reduce their average emission rate of their fleet by 2010 to a
level consistent with the reductions proposed for stationary sources. See discussion in section III.D.4.e.(3)
and proposed regulation 40 CFR 52.2971.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in section III.D.4.e.(2), EPA is proposing that commercial aviation operations be subject to an environmental
performance target in the FIPs, including mobile emissions sources under the direct control of the airline (aircraft,
aircraft auxiliary power units, ground service equipment, captive vehicle fleets, and any other airline-operated
mobile source). Declining emissions rate targets for these emissions, consistent with the caps proposed for stationary
sources, would be set beginning with the ozone season of 2001. This level of allowable emissions would translate into
an industry-wide environmental performance factor expressed as an allowable pounds of pollutant per passenger
equivalent unit. Airlines which exceed their allowable performance factor would pay a fee based on the amount of excess
emissions. In order to provide additional flexibility in meeting these targets, EPA is also proposing intra-airline
averaging for airlines with operations at multiple airports within the same FIP area. The Agency is also seeking comment
on the establishment of an inter- airline credit and trading program. The proposed regulation is 40 CFR 52.2970.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For general aviation in the FIP areas, EPA is proposing two fee systems in the alternative. One proposal would simply
charge a fee for each takeoff sufficient to discourage use without making general aviation prohibitively expensive.
The second approach would incorporate an exemption into the fee program for engines that are certified to ``clean''
emissions levels. EPA also requests comment on differentiation of fees based on airport proprietor's programs for
other emissions reductions such as refueling vapor recovery and other alternative control programs. See discussion
in section III.D.4.e.(2).(d). and proposed regulation at 40 CFR 52.2970.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section III.D.4.e.(2).(c). and (5) describes EPA's proposed military installation bubble for each FIP area encompassing
all mobile emission sources under the control of the Department of Defense (DOD), with the exception of military aircraft
and vessels. This includes auxiliary power units, ground service equipment, captive vehicle fleets, privately
owned vehicles, and any other mobile source operated within the boundaries of the installation. A declining emissions
cap would apply similar to the cap imposed on stationary sources within each FIP area. Each installation would be required
to determine a baseline inventory from which the reductions will be achieved. The proposed regulation, 40 CFR 52.2972,
allows trading among active bases and credit for base closures that generate permanent emission reductions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Marine vessels are the largest uncontrolled source of emissions of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and sulfur oxides in California. In order to encourage emissions reductions from these vessels in a manner consistent
with existing international standards, EPA is proposing a flexible control strategy for the reduction of emissions
from ships in port. Specifically, a user fee would be based on the type emission control that a particular ship used.
Ship operators would be allowed (and encouraged) to reduce the fees which would be applied by using low emission engines,
using electrical onshore power service instead of the ship's engines while in port, and for travelling outside of
the Channel Islands. Compliance with all three operational changes would eliminate the fees altogether. Ships that
use South Coast ports more frequently would be faced with higher annual fees, and therefore would have a greater incentive
to make operational changes and install more efficient emission control devices to reduce or avoid fees.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In Ventura there are no large ports, but marine vessel emissions are still large contributors to the area's NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 inventory, because of the emissions that are blown onshore from passing ships by the prevailing winds. As mentioned
above, the South Coast FIP includes a discount in the proposed fee system to encourage ships to move further out to sea
when passing the Ventura coastline. See discussion of marine vessels and ports in section III.D.4.e.(4). and proposed
regulation 40 CFR 52.2973.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
(4) Programs to Reduce Vehicle Miles Traveled (VMT)
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As described earlier, vehicle emissions continue to be a large portion of future total VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions inventory, despite increasingly stringent emission standards, because of projections of continued
high growth in vehicle miles traveled (VMT). Due to current growth projections, EPA is proposing programs designed
to decrease growth in VMT by targeting single occupant vehicle use. The proposed regulations require increases in
average occupancy in personal vehicles in Sacramento (for the 1999 attainment option only) and encourage shifts
to carpooling and public modes of transportation in all the FIP areas. Various other strategies could be used to achieve
decreases in VMT, but most depend on local planning and implementation to be successful. Unfortunately, EPA resources
and statutory authority have constrained our ability to propose these possibly more effective strategies. These
constraints are discussed generally in section III.A.2. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00042 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Finally, for purposes of the 1999 attainment date option for Sacramento, EPA is proposing a restriction on the use
of on-road vehicles such that each vehicle in the Sacramento nonattainment area would be prohibited from being driven
on one day out of five weekdays. This is, of course, one of the least desirable of mobile source options available to
EPA, but, as is described in detail in III.B.3.d.(3), it appears necessary in order to meet the 1999 attainment deadline.
It is also one of the main reasons why EPA is recommending the 2005 attainment option.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
D. Role of State and Local Agencies
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. SIP Responsibilities
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA has every intention of using the FIP process to propose and promulgate real solutions to the ozone problems in Sacramento
and Ventura, as well as both the ozone and carbon monoxide problems in the South Coast. EPA will implement these solutions
as it becomes necessary. However, under the CAAA of 1990, nothing in the FIPs proposed today or to be finalized in February,
1995 releases the state and local governments from their independent legal responsibilities to meet all requirements
of the Clean Air Act. Furthermore, failure to meet any of these requirements will subject applicable areas of the state
to the mandatory sanctions required by section 179 of the Act.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One of the most important responsibilities that any nonattainment area has under the Clean Air Act is to adopt and submit
to EPA in November 1994, comprehensive plans to attain the ozone standard by its applicable attainment date. Regardless
of the FIPs, the state must still implement plans sufficient to demonstrate attainment. Nothing in the FIP can relieve
them of that responsibility under the law. As discussed earlier, EPA is requesting comment on whether there should
be an exception to this requirement. Specifically, comment is requested on whether, as a legal and policy matter,
states should be allowed to take credit for emission reductions associated with FIP controls applied to sources over
which EPA has sole jurisdiction.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. State and Local Opportunities
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA hopes that state and local agencies will take full advantage of the opportunities provided by the FIP development
process. EPA believes that the emission reduction strategies proposed today, together with the public involvement
process described below, can be used to leverage support for state and local emission reduction strategies. EPA has
purposefully chosen to maximize the number of strategies in this proposal that could be adopted wholesale by state
and local agencies. In other cases, the necessarily blunt EPA strategies should provide support for better tailored
state and local regulation of the same emission sources. Finally, state and local agencies have the ability to adopt
innovative emissions growth management, transportation control and land use strategies that EPA cannot propose.
These types of strategies adopted at the local level could displace the more onerous strategies in the FIPs. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00043 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
E. Public Involvement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Public Participation in Development of the FIPs
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
One of the more difficult aspects of developing these FIPs has been to build in meaningful public participation. Sufficient
time is at best a luxury in meeting the court-ordered deadlines to promulgate the final FIPs. Conversely, sufficient
time is an absolute necessity for full public involvement. An inevitable conflict arises when trying to meet both
of these needs.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If community-wide support for the changes necessary to reach attainment were easy to achieve, there would be no need
for these FIPs or even the attainment deadlines in the first place. Unfortunately, any level of real public participation
in matters this controversial can easily degenerate into parochial disagreements with no real benefit to anyone
involved. Any participation process which facilitates consensus solutions must be designed to minimize these disagreements.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, given the limited resources available to EPA for actually developing the strategies in these FIPs, it is
a measure of the Agency's commitment to public involvement that we have and will continue to direct them toward increasing
public involvement in this effort.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Irrespective of these difficulties, EPA has and will continue to engage in every possible effort to make the final
plans a product of local involvement and consensus. We will do so because we believe strongly that we can best fulfill
the goal of the Clean Air Act_that is, clean and healthy air for all Americans_and meet our court-ordered obligations
by preparing these plans with the state and local communities, not in spite of them. We are highly cognizant of the fact
that each area affected by our rules has its own unique qualities and concerns. We also believe that the only way in which
EPA can fully understand those concerns_and take them into account_is through direct participation by the affected
interests and communities.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ultimately we believe that the success of 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
any 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
air quality plan_federal, state or local_will be dependent upon the level of understanding and support which exists
in the community regarding the need for and benefits of the plan. Without that understanding and support, we are unlikely
to meet the difficult challenges which lie ahead.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Public Meetings Prior to Proposal
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As a result of these beliefs, we have initiated an effort to develop these plans in a manner which encourages local input.
First, our plans, and their assumptions, were built with significant input from state and local air agencies, other
local and state officials, and representatives of various affected interests. EPA has tried to use the best and most
current data that these entities could provide within our FIP proposal timeframe.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Second, our FIP development effort has included eight informal, pre-proposal public meetings (two each in Ventura
and Sacramento, and four in the South Coast). The purposes of these meetings were to: (1) Begin the process of education
and interaction among the various parties_local, state, federal, and the public; (2) solicit initial thoughts and
concerns from the affected communities as we developed the proposals; and (3) solicit suggestions about how best
to continue the interactive process through the development of the final plans due in February 1995. The results of
these meetings are found throughout these FIP proposals, both in the emission reductions strategies and in the post-proposal
process.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, our FIP development process has included numerous staff-to-staff discussions among the local and state
air boards and EPA. The results of these discussions, as well, are found throughout the proposals.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Process for Post-Proposal Public Involvement
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In order to organize and facilitate meaningful community involvement during the period between proposal and the
final plans, EPA plans to enhance the normal notice and comment period. Our primary effort in this area will consist
of working to coordinate the FIP processes with the ongoing local processes so as to minimize public confusion and
coordinate planning resources. This approach is based on comments and requests received in pre-proposal meetings,
and will rely_to the greatest extent feasible_on the processes already in place at the local level. Following proposal
of the FIPs, EPA will continue to meet with responsible state and local air agencies to coordinate the SIP and FIP public
processes, as well as with community groups, environmental and business interests.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A set of conflicts exist though, which may make it necessary for the FIP processes to also follow its own separate track.
For example, the local air boards are currently developing plans which not only meet the requirements of the Clean
Air Act Amendments of 1990, but also must meet the requirements of the California Clean Air Act. No such California
specific requirement exists for the FIPs.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Further, EPA believes that it is necessary for the FIPs to apply many of the controls to all three areas or in some cases,
state-wide. Reliance on the various local SIP processes to engage the public on these particular FIP measures would
make it difficult for interested parties to participate fully in all aspects of the dialogue, and could lead the public
to believe that EPA has the ability to tailor these measures to the conditions of the local areas.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, EPA must meet the mandates of the federal courts and the terms of settlement agreements which require it to
promulgate final FIPs by February 1995. This deadline cannot be changed by EPA and means that coordination with ongoing
local processes must be constrained to fit within EPA's timeframe.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For these reasons, EPA believes that we should take additional steps outside of the local processes in order to provide
an opportunity for productive public input on the proposed FIPs in a timeframe which is consistent with the final promulgation
deadline of February 1995. These steps will include:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Following proposal of the FIPs, EPA will convene a public workshop in each FIP area in order to describe in some detail
our proposals, to help us identify appropriate issues for in-depth examination, and to encourage local participation
and support for issue-specific focus meetings.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Depending on the level of support that exists, issue-specific focus meetings may be held in each FIP area, and possibly
a central forum for selected statewide issues, to help develop comments on the FIP issues of greatest concern. The
goals of each issue-specific group will be to develop a consensus position, if possible, and to submit consensus comments,
if possible, on its set of FIP issues. EPA will also encourage each group to direct its comments to the state and local
air agencies which will be developing ozone plans due in November 1994.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) EPA will not ``select'' specific attendees or chairpersons for these focus meetings, but will encourage participation
that is sufficiently representative and inclusive so as to make any consensus real and meaningful. To the extent possible,
EPA will provide contractor and staff support for the meetings. EPA will encourage the local air districts and the
Air Resources Board to provide staff support as well.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) All group meetings sponsored by EPA will be open to the public. The number of meetings for each issue area will vary
by issue.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Following the workshops and group meetings, formal public hearings will be held in each FIP area, probably in early
July 1994. Notice will be given of the formal public hearings in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 thirty (30) days prior to such hearings.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) The public comment period will be held open from the publication of the FIP proposals until 30 days after the public
hearings.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) It is EPA's intention to give great weight to any comments on which local consensus has been reached through this
public involvement process. EPA, of course, remains ultimately responsible for the content of the final rule which
must meet federal legal requirements and address national policy concerns. Although EPA encourages all interested
parties to participate in focus meetings and workshops, any member of the public who has chosen not to do so has the right
to submit written comments and/or testify at the public hearings. EPA will consider all comments received during
the public comment period.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA realizes that the time constraints involved pose a very real obstacle to achieving consensus on issues as complex
and controversial as those raised in developing clean air strategies. The requirement to finalize these FIPs by February
1995 means that EPA, and the public, will need to make very efficient use of this period until the public comment period
closes in mid-summer. EPA is realistic about the level of consensus that can be reached in such a short period of time.
We have decided to go forward with an expanded public involvement process because we believe that the potential benefits
are great_and at a minimum, all parties will benefit from a greater understanding of the air quality problems, the
concerns of affected parties, and the range of solutions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00044 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In summary, we see the development of the final federal implementation plans due in February of 1995 as a 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
real 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
opportunity for local communities, in conjunction with federal and state authorities, to build air quality plans
that can be successfully implemented. Optimally, this process will also provide state and local authorities with
workable ideas and strategies that can be incorporated into the 1994 ozone plans, thus facilitating recision of all
or significant parts of the FIPs. Through the enhanced notice and comment process each participant_state, local,
federal, and every interested member of the public_will have the opportunity to take responsibility where it should
be taken, and to work cooperatively where it can be done.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Solicitation of Comment
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Written comments on the proposals contained in today's NPRM will be accepted throughout the public process described
above. EPA expects to hold public hearings in early July, 1994 and expects that the deadline for written comments will
be approximately 30 days after the public hearings. EPA will give notice in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 of the public hearings and public comment deadline as soon as possible, but no later than 30 days prior to the hearing
dates.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA encourages the public to participate both through formal hearing and written comments as well as in the informal
workshop and focus meeting forums described above. Workshops will be held in each FIP area in mid to late March 1994.
Throughout today's NPRM, EPA has encouraged the public to comment on specific issues and alternatives. We expect
that these issues will be appropriate for further examination in informal workshop and focus meeting forums.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
II. Background and Proposed SIP Actions
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Description of the Affected Areas
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Sacramento Area
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Sacramento area includes all of Sacramento and Yolo Counties, the northeast portion of Solano County, the southern
portion of Sutter County, and all of El Dorado and Placer Counties except for the Lake Tahoe area.
<FOOTCITE>10</FOOTCITE>
 The population of this area is approximately 1.6 million, and is projected to increase to 1.9 million by 2000 and 2.2
million by 2005.
<FOOTCITE>11</FOOTCITE>
 The annual personal income of the area exceeds $30 billion. The economy is dominated by agricultural, commercial,
and service sectors, with almost no heavy industry.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
10
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
At the time EPA's FIP obligation arose, the Sacramento nonattainment area did not include any portions of El Dorado
and Sutter Counties. These portions were added in revised designations promulgated in conformance with the 1990
Amendments. See 56 FR 58694, 56726 (November 6, 1991).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
11
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Population projections for 2000 and 2005 are based on the latest SACOG data and do not yet include estimates from the
northeast portion of Solano County.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Mobile sources, particularly motor vehicles, are the dominant source of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 in the Sacramento area. By 1999, mobile sources will account for approximately 50 percent of VOC emissions and over
90 percent of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions. Industrial, commercial, and residential solvents and coatings are also significant sources of VOC,
contributing almost 30 percent of VOC emissions in 1999.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Violations of the ozone NAAQS occur in the Sacramento area from as early as April through mid-November, although most
violations in recent years have been confined to the warmer months of June through October. The Sacramento ozone design
value at the time of classification was 0.16 ppm (based on 1987&hyph;1989 data), substantially above the NAAQS for
ozone, which is 0.12 ppm, measured as a one-hour average. Over the period 1990&hyph;1992, the design value was still
0.16 ppm. For this 1990&hyph;1992 measurement period, only four areas had a higher design value: South Coast (.30
ppm); the Southeast Desert area, immediately to the east of the South Coast (.23 ppm); Houston/Galveston (.21 ppm);
and San Diego (.17 ppm). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Based on this design value, the Sacramento area was classified as ``Serious'' under section 181(a) of the Clean Air
Act, with an attainment deadline of no later than November 15, 1999. See 56 FR 56728. However, according to EPA's most
recent national air quality and emissions trends data (through calendar year 1992), the area also has the eighth largest
number of average expected exceedances of the ozone standard in the country (9.0 days).
<FOOTCITE>12</FOOTCITE>
 Outside of southern and central California, only the Houston and Philadelphia areas have a greater number of expected
exceedances. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
12
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The average estimated number of exceedances is the number of days the 0.12 ppm standard was exceeded on average at the
site recording the highest second maximum 1-hour concentration, after adjustment for incomplete or missing data
days.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This unusual frequency of exceedances_coupled with the absence of easy-to-control pollution sources, the immense
emissions reductions needed, the rapid population growth predicted for this area, and the possibility that some
of the ozone violations in Sacramento may be due primarily to transport of pollution from the south and southwest_suggest
the appropriateness of a bump-up to a ``Severe'' classification, in accordance with the provisions of section 181(b)(3).
See discussion below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Urban airshed modeling suggests that a 40 percent reduction in current levels of VOC coupled with a 30 percent reduction
in NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions would be needed for attainment. As displayed in a table included in the Sacramento FIP attainment demonstration
(see section III.H.3.a.), other combinations of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reductions also could yield attainment, but in EPA's judgment the 40:30 option appears to have a greater potential
to minimize dislocation and inequities among source categories.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Ventura Area
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Ventura area includes all of Ventura County, which lies on the California coast between Santa Barbara and Los Angeles
Counties. The 1990 population was 660,000 and is projected to increase to 840,000 by 2005. The County ranks third in
California in petroleum production and 17th in the Country in agricultural income. Twenty-five percent of the work
force commute to jobs in Los Angeles.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura's emissions inventory is less dominated by the mobile source component than is Sacramento's. For the year
2005, mobile sources account for approximately 40 percent of VOC emissions and 70 percent of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions. The ozone season extends from April through November, but very few violations have been recorded before
June. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00045 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Ventura is classified as a ``Severe'' ozone area, with a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 15, 2005 attainment deadline.
<FOOTCITE>13</FOOTCITE>
 For the period 1990&hyph;1992, the design value was .15 ppm. While this design value is the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
same as Sacramento's for the 1990&hyph;1992 period, Ventura had more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than twice the number of expected exceedance days (17.6). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
13
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Based on a request and supporting information from the State, EPA's designation under the 1990 CAA Amendments divided
the Los Angeles Consolidated Metropolitan Statistical Area into four distinct areas with respect to ozone designations
and classifications. This action made the entire Ventura County a separate nonattainment area with a ``Severe''
classification, although the 1988&hyph;1990 design value calculated for the area would have supported a ``Serious''
classification. See 56 FR 56698, 56700, 56731 (November 6, 1991). Prior to this designation, the Ventura ozone nonattainment
area included only that portion of the County south of the southern boundary of the Los Padres National Forest.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Transport of ozone and ozone precursors from Los Angeles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Santa Barbara, and outer continental shelf activity can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant. However, emissions from Ventura itself can result in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ozone standard exceedances independent of transport from other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. In addition, mountains bordering the Ventura urban areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contribute to a meteorology that favors high ozone concentrations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particularly within the southern portions of the County. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Based on recent urban airshed modeling analyses, EPA has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determined that attainment requires 40 percent reductions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current levels of both NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and VOC. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. South Coast Area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The South Coast area (South Coast Air Basin) includes all of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Orange County and the more populated portions of Los Angeles, San 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Bernardino, and Riverside Counties.
<FOOTCITE>14</FOOTCITE>
 Ventura County lies to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
northwest, the Southeast Desert ``Severe'' ozone nonattainment area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(portions of San Bernardino, Riverside, Kern, and Los Angeles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Counties) is to the north and east, and the San Diego ``Severe'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ozone area is to the south. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
14
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
For a description of the boundaries of the South Coast Air Basin, see 40 C.F.R. 81.305 and 56 FR 56726&hyph;56728 (November
6, 1991).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The current population of approximately 13 million is expected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to grow to more than 18 million by 2010. The South Coast is the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
largest industrial area in the United States, with a gross domestic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
product of $300 billion. Practically every commercial and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industrial category is represented in the area's economy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the year 2010, mobile sources will account for 46 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of VOC emissions, 81 percent of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions, and 97 percent of CO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions in the South Coast. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The South Coast area has by far the worst ozone levels in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Country and is the only area classified as ``Extreme.'' For the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period 1990&hyph;1992, the design value was .30 ppm and the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of actual exceedance days was 134.3. Ozone levels are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particularly high during the months of July through October, but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
violations also occur under warm and stagnant conditions in April, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
May, June, and November. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The South Coast is one of only three ``Serious'' CO areas in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Country. Its design value is 16.4 ppm for the period 1991&hyph;1992. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The average yearly number of exceedances for those years was 38. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In recent years, CO violations have occurred only in the months of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November, December, January, and February, and at certain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
monitoring sites in Los Angeles and Orange Counties. No CO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
violations have been recorded in the Riverside and San Bernardino 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portions of the South Coast area since the 1970's. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These uniquely high levels of pollution are the result of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
massive emissions generated within the area, combined with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
especially adverse meteorology and topography. A number of factors_sunshine, high temperatures, ocean breezes
carrying pollutants 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into the inland valleys, mountains to the north and east trapping 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the pollutants within the basin, and prolonged thermal inversion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
layers_conspire to create ideal conditions for the generation of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
high ozone levels. Extreme wintertime inversions retard the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dispersion of CO emissions from the 9 million motor vehicles within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the area. This leads to a buildup of CO concentrations over time, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particularly within west-central Los Angeles County. Attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requires approximately a 45 percent reduction in CO emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the baseyear. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. SIP Status 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Introduction; Relationship of the FIP to the SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
At the same time EPA is proposing federal plans for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas, the responsible State and local agencies have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developing and adopting their own plans and rules to meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheduled Clean Air Act requirements. In relation to the FIPs, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
most important of these new SIP requirements and submittal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines are:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) CO plan for the South Coast demonstrating attainment and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addressing the provisions of section 187 of the Act_due 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 15, 1992; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Ozone 15 percent rate-of-progress plans for each area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting the requirements of section 182(b)(1)_due November 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
15, 1993; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and (3) ozone plans for each area demonstrating attainment and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
satisfaction of the relevant portions of section 182_due 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 15, 1994.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA has issued preliminary interpretations of the amended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act's provisions applicable to these SIP obligations. See, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example, the ``General Preamble for the Implementation of Title I of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990,'' [see generally 57 FR 13498 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(April 16, 1992) and 57 FR 18070 (April 28, 1992)], and ``Guidance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for Growth Factors, Projections, and Control Strategies for the 15 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Percent Rate-of-Progress Plans,'' (EPA&hyph;452/R&hyph;93&hyph;002, March 1993). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ultimately, EPA expects that SIP measures meeting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of the 1990 amendments and more carefully tailored to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP areas will supplant the potentially severe measures EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compelled to propose here. EPA understands that this scenario 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presents facilities with the prospect of changing compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements as the FIP is replaced by SIP measures. In the long 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
term, however, it will better serve the area to have in place 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures which are designed by State, regional, and local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorities, who are best situated to minimize any disruption of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the local economy and communities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the discussion below, the more important completed and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pending SIP submittals for the State and each area are briefly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described. To the extent that the submittals satisfy, or help to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
satisfy EPA's obligations under the existing court orders, EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve the SIPs and refrain from promulgating FIP provisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addressing those aspects of the air quality problem. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00046 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A submittal from the State and local districts of an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approvable
<FOOTCITE>15</FOOTCITE>
 SIP demonstrating attainment by the applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadline will enable EPA to rescind the FIP in its entirety. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that this rescission is appropriate even if the SIP for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the area has other deficiencies (such as deficient RACT or NSR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules) which trigger separate FIP actions.
<FOOTCITE>16</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
15
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Under section 110(k)(4) of the Act, EPA can conditionally approve a state plan which lacks adopted enforceable measures
as long as the state has been made a commitment to adopt the enforceable measures within one year of the conditional
approval.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
16
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The Act allows states to replace FIP requirements with SIP measures, subject, of course, to EPA's approval of the SIP
measures under sections 110(a), (k), and (l) of the Act. The Act provides that EPA is relieved of having to promulgate
a FIP at all if the state submits and EPA approves a replacement SIP before the FIP must be promulgated. Section 110(c)(1)
of the Act provides: ``The Administrator shall promulgate a Federal implementation plan at any time within 2 years
after the Administrator [makes certain findings or disapproves a SIP] unless the State corrects the deficiency,
and the Administrator approves the plan or plan revision, before the Administrator promulgates such Federal implementation
plan.'' Section 302(y) of the Act defines a FIP as ``a plan (or portion thereof) promulgated by the Administrator to
fill all or a portion of a gap or otherwise correct all or a portion of an inadequacy in a State implementation plan.&blank;.&blank;.&blank;.''
If a state fills a gap or cures an inadequacy in its SIP subsequent to EPA promulgating a FIP, the FIP may be rescinded
or revised. The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit recognized this principle in 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=3 -->
Coalition for Clean Air v. EPA
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 (reported as 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=3 -->
Coalition for Clean Air v. Southern California Edison
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
), 971 F.2d 219.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA wishes to emphasize that all existing and future SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligations remain the responsibility of State and local agencies, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
despite provisions in the FIP that may fulfill, in whole or in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part, these obligations. Thus, the State and the applicable areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the State are subject to the sanctions provisions of section 179 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for any failure to submit a required plan or plan element, or upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA disapproval of a required plan or plan element.
<FOOTCITE>17</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
17
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Under section 179 of the Act, two principal sanctions are authorized: a highway funding restriction and an increased
offset requirement for major new or modified sources. The highway funding sanction is enforced through an EPA prohibition
on approval by the U.S. Secretary of Transportation of projects or grants in the area except where the Secretary has
determined that the purpose of the project or grant is to improve a demonstrated safety problem. Section 179(b)(1)(B)
also allows the Secretary to exempt certain projects and grants that are intended to minimize air pollution problems.
The offset sanction requires that major new or modified sources in the area obtain at least 2 to 1 offsets before construction.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 179(a) requires EPA to impose one of these sanctions within 18 months and the remaining sanction within 24
months if: (1) The state has failed to submit a required plan or element; (2) the required submission is deemed incomplete;
(3) EPA disapproves the required submission; or (4) an approved SIP provision is not being implemented. Section 110(m)
allows EPA to impose these sanctions at any time after EPA has made one of these findings.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
While EPA's FIP promulgation process unfolds, the responsible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State and local agencies in Sacramento, Ventura, and the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast are developing their own plans under the schedule provided by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 1990 Amendments. Depending upon the time EPA is able to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve SIP replacement measures submitted by these agencies, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may upon approval of such measures either refrain from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgating, or withdraw the corresponding portions of the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sections 110(c) and 302(y). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As the relationship between the FIPs and the developing SIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evolves, EPA will confront a number of issues. One question that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has been raised is whether the State may claim credit toward 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting progress or attainment requirements for the emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions from FIP measures that apply to a limited category of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources over which State or local control is legally preempted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The issue is whether the State, in its plan, may seek not to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replace these FIP measures, but rather continue to rely upon them, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at the same time obtaining EPA's full approval for the state plan. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although EPA has not previously addressed this specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
question, a straightforward reading of the Clean Air Act's text and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
structure suggests that a state plan to be approvable must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate attainment of the relevant standard, without reliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on measures that EPA has promulgated solely pursuant to its FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority. (Under this theory, a state could still rely on any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures that EPA has promulgated under its independent authority, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such as Title II, as opposed to its authority under section 110(c) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to assume California's waiver role under, for example, section 209. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
See discussion of EPA's FIP authority, section III.A.2.a. To 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decide otherwise would require the conclusion that Congress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intended FIP measures to remain in place, perhaps permanently, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
without sanctions being imposed on the state_a view arguably at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
odds with the Act's historic emphasis on the primacy of state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility and the role of FIPs as temporary substitutes for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state failures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On the other hand, it may be argued that, for a limited number 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of sources over which the federal government, when promulgating a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP, demonstrably has sole legal authority, and over which the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state is barred from exerting control, removing the FIP measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and hence requiring the state to compensate for the lack of control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of these sources may force it to impose unreasonably and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inequitably harsher controls on other sources. Thus, the argument 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
goes, EPA should consider whether there is any basis in law, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
justification in policy, for carving out a limited exception to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
traditional view. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA does not resolve this complex issue here, but intends to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to analyze and explore it. In this proposal we solicit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment on the legal and policy implications raised by this aspect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the relationship between the FIP and the SIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On November 15, 1993, the State submitted Sacramento ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate-of-progress plans for the Sacramento Metropolitan Area, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addressing the Act's 15 percent rate-of-progress plan requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Sacramento area's 15 percent plans involved a coordinated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effort by the Sacramento Area Council of Governments (SACOG) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the five local air pollution control agencies. Each of the air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies developed a plan for their portion of the area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The reductions in Sacramento's 15 percent plans rely on an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced I/M program, measures that the State and local governments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have adopted or have committed to adopt, measures that the Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
government will have to develop and implement for the Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area, and substitution of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 for VOC reductions. However, an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approvable enhanced I/M program has not been authorized by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State legislature, EPA has decided preliminarily that it will not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accept commitments for measures for purposes of the Act's 15 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent rate-of-progress provision, and EPA cannot credit NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in place of the necessary VOC reductions. Therefore, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA cannot take action at this time to approve this submittal with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
respect to the Clean Air Act progress demonstration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00047 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Sacramento Metropolitan Air Quality Management District 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(SMAQMD), the Yolo-Solano Air Pollution Control District (YSAPCD), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Placer County Air Pollution Control District (PCAPCD), the El 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Dorado County Air Pollution Control District (EDCAPCD), the Feather 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
River Air Quality Management District (FRAQMD), SACOG and CARB plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to submit a comprehensive revised ozone attainment SIP on or before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 15, 1994. The local agencies hope to release a draft of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this SIP in the Spring of 1994 and to modify it after EPA finalizes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP in February 1995. The air quality modeling in the SIP and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP are based on similar data and should be consistent. There may, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
however, be significant differences between the regulations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included in the two plans to attain the ozone standard. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Ventura 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On November 15, 1993, the State submitted a Ventura ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate-of-progress plan addressing the Act's requirement that a plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be submitted by that date demonstrating creditable VOC reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of at least 15 percent from 1990 to 1996. The plan describes how 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this reduction can be accomplished with an enhanced I/M program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plus measures that the State committed to adopt and implement by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1996 in Ventura's 1991 Air Quality Management Plan. Many of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promised local measures have already been adopted, but an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approvable enhanced I/M program has not been authorized by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State legislature. As in the case of Sacramento, EPA cannot now 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve this Ventura plan submittal with respect to the progress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Ventura County Air Pollution Control District (VCAPCD), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Southern California Association of Governments (SCAG), and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB plan to submit a comprehensive revised ozone attainment SIP on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or before November 15, 1994. VCAPCD hopes to release a draft of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this SIP by the Spring of 1994 and to modify it after EPA finalizes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP in February 1995. The air quality modeling in the SIP and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP are based on similar data and should be consistent. As in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
case of Sacramento, however, the regulations included in the two 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plans may be different. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. South Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On December 31, 1992, the State submitted the CO plan for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast. This plan includes a demonstration of attainment by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the year 2000 relying on emission reductions from previously 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopted regulations as well as from an enhanced I/M program meeting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the requirements of sections 182(c)(3) and 187(a)(6). Because the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State legislature has not yet enacted legislation authorizing such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an enhanced I/M program, EPA proposes to disapprove the CO plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with respect to the requirements for an attainment demonstration, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonable further progress, reasonably available control measures, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and enhanced I/M. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On January 15, 1993, the Agency issued a finding of failure to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submit two other mandatory elements of the CO plan: (1) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Transportation control measures (TCMs) to offset growth, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required by section 182(d)(1)(A); and (2) adopted contingency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures to be implemented if vehicle miles traveled (VMT) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forecasts are exceeded, in accordance with section 187(a)(3). In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section II.C.1., below, EPA is proposing to disapprove the CO SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with respect to these six elements: attainment demonstration, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonable further progress, reasonably available control measures, provision for an enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M program, TCMs to offset growth, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contingency measures to be implemented if VMT forecasts are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeded. EPA is also proposing a partial approval of other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portions of the plan that represent an improvement over what is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently in the SIP, do not conflict with proposed FIP provisions, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and meet some of the applicable requirements of the Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On November 15, 1993, the State submitted a 15 percent rate-of-progress plan for the South Coast. The 15 percent plan
relies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on reductions from currently adopted rules, but also depends upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a relatively small contribution from 10 SCAQMD measures scheduled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for full adoption in 1994 or 1995 and an improved I/M program. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the case of Sacramento and Ventura, EPA cannot at this time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
propose to approve this South Coast submittal with respect to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
15 percent rate-of-progress demonstration. However, in section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
II.C.2., below, for entirely different purposes EPA is proposing to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditionally approve the SCAQMD commitments to adopt rules during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1994 and 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On October 15, 1993, the SCAQMD adopted rules implementing the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Regional Clean Air Incentives Market (RECLAIM) program for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sulfur oxides (SO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
). This innovative program establishes a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
declining cap ``bubble'' requirement on most facilities with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions greater than 4 tons per year of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 or SO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. The program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes emissions allocation and emissions trading components. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA intends to propose to approve (or conditionally approve) the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RECLAIM regulations in separate rulemaking, following SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submittal. For the purposes of the FIP attainment demonstration, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes to assign credit to the adopted RECLAIM program, which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
went into effect on January 1, 1994. If EPA does not finally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve the NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 RECLAIM rules, EPA would not be able to grant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
credits for associated reductions, and would need to amend the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accordingly. For more details on the RECLAIM rules, see section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.5. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The SCAQMD, SCAG, and CARB are currently preparing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comprehensive revision to existing air quality plans, addressing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all pollutants and the requirements of both the federal and State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Clean Air Acts. SIP submittal of this comprehensive plan (or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portions of the plan) is scheduled to occur on or before November 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
15, 1994. The new plan will include revised 1990 baseyear 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions inventories, and projected inventories for interim years 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through the year 2020. With respect to motor vehicle emissions, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these inventories may be significantly different from those used in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed ozone and CO FIPs, because the 1994 plan is expected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to rely on new trip modeling input data and revised motor vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions factors. For ozone, the plan may analyze additional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
episodes, including a weekend episode. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5. State of California 
