<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
III. California FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Basis for the FIP Actions in Applicable Law and EPA Policies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. FIP Obligation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
a.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
 Introduction.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In 1988, in the wake of lawsuits and ensuing court orders, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disapproved the 1982 South Coast ozone and CO plans and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento and Ventura ozone plans on the ground that, among other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
things, they failed to demonstrate attainment of the NAAQS by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
December 1987 statutory attainment date, as required by Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
172(a) of the Act. See 53 FR 1780 (January 22, 1988). In March 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1989, EPA entered into a settlement agreement with the South Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plaintiffs which obligated EPA to promulgate federal plans for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast in final form by February 1991. EPA issued a proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP in September 1990. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For Sacramento, EPA also negotiated settlement agreements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
containing schedules by which EPA would propose and promulgate a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal plan. Pursuant to these schedules, EPA published Advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Notices of Proposed Rulemakings (ANPRMs) on April 5, 1990 (55 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
12669) and May 27, 1992 (57 FR 22194). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For Ventura, EPA similarly entered into settlements setting a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
schedule, and published an NPRM on January 17, 1991. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
But as time progressed, it became increasingly clear that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast, Sacramento, and Ventura_all areas with difficult 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air pollution problems_could not possibly meet the ambitious 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of the 1977 CAA Amendments without severe economic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dislocation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Passage of 1990 amendments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On November 15, 1990, Congress enacted the Clean Air Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Amendments of 1990. Pub. L. No. 101&hyph;549, 104 Stat. 2422&hyph;2423. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Among other things, Congress completely revised the Part D 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonattainment provisions of the Act. In so doing Congress repealed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the provisions of Section 172 that had required SIPs for the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast, Ventura, and Sacramento to demonstrate attainment by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
December 31, 1987. Those provisions had formed the basis for EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disapprovals of the California SIP submissions for those areas, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thus the 1990 Amendments removed the statutory justification for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those disapprovals. Congress replaced the provisions of section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
172 with a detailed scheme of new requirements and extended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines specifically applicable to ozone and CO nonattainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SIPs. Sections 171&hyph;193, 42 U.S.C. 7501&hyph;7515. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In particular, the 1990 Amendments established a graduated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
series of requirements for different areas, depending on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
severity of the air quality problem in each. Congress established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
five classifications of ozone nonattainment areas based on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level of each area's ozone problem_ranging from ``Marginal'' to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``Extreme''_and two classifications of CO nonattainment areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Amendments also set new, extended deadlines for the attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the primary NAAQS for each classification. Sections 181(a)(1) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 186(a)(1). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Consistent with the new Amendments, EPA issued a regulation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
classifying Sacramento as a ``Serious'' area for ozone nonattainment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the statute, ``Serious'' areas must attain the ozone standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as expeditiously as practicable but no later than November 15, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999. 40 C.F.R. 81.305 (1992); CAA section 181(a). Ventura is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
classified as a ``Severe'' area for ozone, and must attain as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expeditiously as practicable, but no later than November 15, 2005. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The South Coast is classified as the only ``Extreme'' area for ozone, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and must demonstrate attainment as expeditiously as practicable but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
no later than November 15, 2010. For carbon monoxide, the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast is classified as a ``Serious'' area, and must demonstrate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment as expeditiously as practicable but no later than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
December 31, 2000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

