<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In the 1990 Amendments Congress also imposed specific and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increasingly rigorous requirements for Part D SIPs, depending on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the area's nonattainment classification, with new deadlines, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ranging from immediately to six years from enactment, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submitting various components of the plans to EPA. Sections 181, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
182, 186, and 187. In addition, the 1990 Amendments amended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 110(c)(l), the principal provision governing EPA's FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA concluded that these revisions, directing States to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop SIPs meeting new requirements and deadlines, and to submit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SIPs to EPA on specific schedules, demonstrated that EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation to promulgate FIPs would arise only upon future 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disapprovals of those newly submitted SIPs. EPA appealed to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
courts to determine whether the Amendments had relieved the Agency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of its pre-existing FIP obligations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In Coalition for Clean Air, 971 F.2d 219 (9th Cir. 1992), a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
divided panel of the Ninth Circuit held that although Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
110(c)(l) had been revised by the 1990 Amendments, the language of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 110(c)(l)(B), which requires EPA to promulgate a FIP within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
two years after it ``disapproves'' a SIP, applies to pre-Amendment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disapprovals. Thus, the court ruled that new section 110(c)(l)(B) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operates retroactively to retain EPA's preexisting FIP obligation, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
even though the statutory provisions forming the basis for that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation have been repealed. In reaching this conclusion, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
court declined to indicate whether the requirements of the old law 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or the 1990 Amendments would govern the contents of this FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Court acknowledged that ``the proper contents of FIPs for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast are not before us,'' and that ``we need not decide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether EPA would be required to meet any additional requirement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
imposed by the 1990 Amendments in promulgating FIPs for the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast,'' 971 F.2d at 225. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA sought, and was denied, a writ of certiorari (113 S.Ct. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1361 (1993)). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Applicable law. Thus, although the Ninth Circuit found that the passage of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Amendments did not release EPA from a FIP obligation arising from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a pre-Amendment disapproval, it did not decide whether any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement of the 1990 Amendments applies to this continuing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation. In the absence of any guidance from the Court, this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
task falls to EPA. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The threshold question, then, is what requirements govern the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
content of a FIP which will take effect after enactment of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Clean Air Act Amendments, but which is based on a plan failure and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disapproval arising from pre-Amendment Clean Air Act provisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which have since been repealed. The greatly altered legal regime 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
established by the 1990 Amendments creates a discontinuity between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the plan contents required under the new and old acts. Thus, EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
faced with a choice as to how to design the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) New law applies. It is well settled that ``[c]ourts must apply new laws to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pending cases, unless such application would result in manifest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
injustice or be contrary to statutory direction or legislative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
history.'' 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
United States 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Ford, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
737 F.2d 1508 (9th Cir. 1984). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
See Bradley 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
School Bd. of Richmond, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
416 U.S. 696, 711 (1974); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
DeGurules 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
INS, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
833 F.2d 861, 863 (9th Cir. 1987); 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Cartage Co. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
United States, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
802 F.2d 353, 357 (9th Cir. 1986). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In accordance with the principle embodied in these cases, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that the 1990 Amendments should be applicable to the FIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
arising from EPA's pre-Amendment disapprovals. But an analysis of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the new Act's provisions reveals that the new Act does not require 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this FIP to include each and every pollution control program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required of States under the Amendments. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The 1990 Amendments make explicit a principle that was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implicit in the preceding Act_that a FIP corrects or fills a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
void in a deficient state plan. The amended Act defines a FIP as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a plan to fill a gap or ``correct all or a portion of an 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
inadequacy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
in a State implementation plan.'' 42 U.S.C. 7602(y) (Supp. II. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1990) (emphasis added). The California SIPs at issue here were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rejected by EPA in 1988 because they were ``inadequa[te]'' to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate attainment under the pre-Amendment deadlines, and thus 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's obligation under the pre-1990 Act was to promulgate a FIP in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to correct that ``inadequacy.'' Thus EPA's FIP obligation is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to fulfill the requirement to demonstrate attainment. Stated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
differently, what the Ninth Circuit's ruling preserved was EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing FIP obligation to correct the state plan's failure to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide for attainment. That is the inadequacy that EPA must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address in this FIP, not any failure that might have arisen under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any of the numerous requirements of the new law. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Of course, if the State fails to meet any of the new Act's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements, that failure would trigger a new FIP obligation in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the future, but that would be an entirely different obligation, not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the one on which the district court orders under the old Act were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
premised. And even as to such obligations, the Administrator is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not required to promulgate a federal plan until two years after she 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disapproves a deficient state plan or finds that the state has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failed to submit a required plan element. Section 110(c)(l). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to new requirements imposed by the 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Amendments, there has either been no state failure, or the 24-month 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
clock for EPA to promulgate a FIP has not yet run. When a state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure giving rise to a new FIP obligation occurs, EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address that obligation in a separate FIP NPRM. For example, FIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addressing state failures to satisfy the VOC RACT catch-up 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement of the new Amendments may become due late in 1994 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in 1995. In the absence of approvable state measures correcting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for these deficiencies, EPA will issue separate FIPs to rectify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
them. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given EPA's position as to the effect of the new Amendments, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one might argue that the logical extension is that since the state 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has not yet failed to adopt an attainment demonstration under new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 182(c)(2)(A), no attainment demonstration is due. But 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
since the Ninth Circuit conclusively decided that the obligation to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgate an attainment demonstration survived, EPA must prepare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one under the new Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
