<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00056 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For policy as well as legal reasons, this resolution makes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sense, and it avoids extreme results. On the one hand, it would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unhelpful for EPA to design the FIP to meet the old law 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements that have since been repealed. And for EPA to impose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in 1995 a regulatory regime that became extinct in 1990 would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surely not serve Congressional intent or constitute sound 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulatory policy. On the other hand, it would equally disserve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Congressional intent to compel these FIPs, which are not based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state failures to meet any requirements of the new Act, to meet all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those requirements and to cure failures that were not part of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation that the Court orders preserved. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Coalition 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
case, the Ninth Circuit Court itself seemed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to imply that EPA's FIP need not address new requirements when it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
noted that the state could ``propose these new measures as revisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the FIP under the timetables provided in the 1990 Amendments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
just as the state would be required to do if a FIP had been in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect when those Amendments were adopted''. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Coalition for Clean 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Air, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
971 F.2d at 226. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Indeed, if the FIP were to try to meet all the requirements of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the new Act, it would have to anticipate and cure new failures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before they had arisen under the new Act, and before the State has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
had an opportunity to develop and submit plans to comply with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new Act. This would violate the express congressional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination that ``air pollution prevention'' and ``air pollution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control at its source is the primary responsibility of States and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local governments.'' Section 101(a)(3). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, EPA has concluded that the FIPs must cure the original 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure to demonstrate attainment. But in these FIPs the Agency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
need not assume additional obligations to meet new requirements as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to which no state delinquency has yet been established under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new Act, or as to which EPA's FIP obligation has not yet matured. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
b. The FIPs Must Demonstrate Attainment_(1) What Attainment Deadline Governs? 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Background. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Ninth Circuit last addressed the issue of what attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadline applied to a pre-Amendment FIP obligation in 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Delaney 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
898 F.2d 687 (9th Cir. 1990). But at that time the attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadline in the then extant Clean Air Act_December 31, 1987_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
had passed, and Congress had not yet extended it. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In a March 1990 opinion, prior to the passage of the Clean Air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act Amendments, the Ninth Circuit vacated EPA's approval of two 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Arizona carbon monoxide SIPs, and ordered EPA to promulgate FIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent with that court's opinion. Interpreting EPA's statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement to develop a FIP in light of the elapsed statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment date of December 31, 1987, the Court concluded that ``the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national ambient air quality standards must be attained as soon as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possible with every available control measure *&blank;*&blank;*.'' 898 F.2d at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
691. The Court relied upon EPA guidance, subsequently revoked, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the part of its formulation requiring ``all possible measures'' or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``every available measure.'' 55 FR 36505 (September 5, 1990). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Court, in formulating this attainment test, made plain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that, while it understood that a Congressional re-determination of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the attainment deadlines was in the works, the Court was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constrained until Congress acted. Thus, at the time the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Delaney
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Court and EPA last struggled to define an appropriate FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment deadline, they confronted not only a lapsed attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadline, but Congressional silence as to an appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replacement. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Quoting from its opinion in 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Abramowitz 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
832 F.2d 1071, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1079 (9th Cir. 1987), the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Delaney
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Court stated: ``We are informed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by counsel for both sides of their expectation that Congress will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extend the deadline once again in the near 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
future, but we must apply the law as it now stands, not as it may become. *&blank;*&blank;* Until the Clean Air Act is
further considered *&blank;*&blank;* the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agency must `give effect to the unambiguously expressed intent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of Congress.' We, and the EPA, are bound by the statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheme until Congress alters that scheme.'' 898 F.2d at 691. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Current law. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Now, however, Congress has indeed ``alter[ed] that scheme'', the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new law applies, and EPA, in formulating its FIPs, has the benefit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of Congressional determination of appropriate attainment dates. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With the passage of the Clean Air Act Amendments, Congress has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made specific attainment deadline determinations with respect to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each type of area. As with other aspects of the new law, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that the attainment dates of the new law apply. Indeed, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
even if it were not clear that the new attainment dates apply, it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is now impossible to design FIPs to meet the 1987 attainment date 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that governed the original state plans, and there is a clear 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expression of Congressional intent as to substitute attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As noted above, under the new Act, the ozone attainment date 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must be ``as expeditiously as practicable but not later than'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
November 15, 1999 for Sacramento; November 15, 2005 for Ventura; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and November 15, 2010 for the South Coast. Section 181(a). The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
carbon monoxide attainment date for the South Coast must be ``as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expeditiously as practicable but not later than'' December 31, 2000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) These FIPs need not address new requirements other than the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement to demonstrate attainment. As shown above, EPA's obligation to design a FIP arose from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state failures to demonstrate attainment under the old Clean Air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act. The Amendments imposed for the first time additional, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different requirements that were not in effect when EPA disapproved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the state plans giving rise to its FIP obligation, and which took 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effect after EPA's FIP obligation matured. Thus California was 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
given no opportunity to meet these requirements in its previous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SIPs, and any failure to meet these requirements would be a new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delinquency that was not the subject of EPA's original FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation. EPA therefore has concluded that the FIPs need not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address these requirements unless and until California has failed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to satisfy them and the time for EPA to promulgate a replacement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP under the new Act has elapsed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00057 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For example, the Amendments contain new and highly specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
targets for Reasonable Further Progress. Section 182(b)(l) in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Amendments requires plans under the Act to provide for VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions reductions of at least 15 percent by 1996, and section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
182(c)(2)(B) requires an additional 9 percent emissions reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
every three years after 1996. Each of these sections contains 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
detailed instructions for calculating the creditability of those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions. Sections 182(b)(1) and 182(c)(2). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While EPA does not believe that these progress requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are applicable to its FIP obligation, EPA has concluded that it is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wise policy to achieve some degree of progress during the time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preceding the attainment dates. As a result, the FIPs do establish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incremental reduction targets for the purpose of ensuring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment by the applicable attainment date. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
At this time, the State of California has become delinquent in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting a few requirements in the new Act, thereby giving rise to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new EPA FIPs obligations under the new Act's deadlines. FIPs to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address these deficiencies, which primarily involve submissions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RACT catch-ups and emission statements, are not yet due. EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address these FIPs in separate rulemakings as they become due. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
c. FIPs may use new provisions providing additional flexibility. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since EPA has concluded that the new law applies to its FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation, EPA may avail itself of new provisions of the law that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
furnish additional flexibility in designing implementation plans. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Ninth Circuit in its opinion recognized that EPA could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
take advantage of increased flexibility in the new Act. For example, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Court noted that EPA now has authority under new section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
182(e)(5) to approve some parts of the South Coast ozone SIP that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it had disapproved in 1988. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Coalition for Clean Air,
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 971 F. 2d at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
226. Similarly, as explained below, EPA believes it may invoke 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 182(e)(5) in designing its FIP to obtain greater 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flexibility to anticipate new control techniques or the improvement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of existing techniques. To the extent that EPA invokes such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discretionary provisions of the new Act, it will satisfy any new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prerequisites prescribed in those sections. For example, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
explained below, in order for EPA's FIP to obtain the benefit of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the latitude granted by section 182(e)(5) to rely on measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
anticipating improvements in technology, the FIP must address 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progress requirements of that section which otherwise would have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been inapplicable. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in more detail below, EPA in its FIPs is also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applying the new law's classifications for each ozone nonattainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area, and, for Sacramento, the provision for ``bumping'' up an area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to a new classification. As with section 182(e)(5), where EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relies on a new provision for added flexibility, it intends to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fulfill any conditions imposed on the exercise of that authority. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA recognizes that its FIPs must weave a complicated path 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
between the old and new laws, and that its FIP obligation is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessarily a hybrid of old and new. This complexity is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unavoidable, however, because of the anomalous nature of the task 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA faces: EPA must cure a failure under an old Act, since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repealed, but administer the cure under new law. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00058 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
d. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Maintenance demonstration for the south coast. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA's FIP obligations for the South Coast, Sacramento, and Ventura arose from disapprovals of their SIPs solely on
the ground of their failure to demonstrate attainment by 1987 or any fixed near-term date. See 53 FR 1280&hyph;1281
(January 22, 1988) (South Coast); 53 FR 39087&hyph;39088 (October 5, 1988) (Ventura); 53 FR 48535-48536 (Dec. 1,
1988) (Sacramento). The settlement agreements for Ventura and Sacramento describe EPA's FIP obligation solely
in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
terms of promulgating a plan for attainment. The South Coast settlement agreement refers to a plan for attainment
and maintenance. Thus, with respect to the South Coast, EPA must determine what requirements apply to maintenance.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As noted above, EPA is applying the new law to its FIP obligation. The 1990 Amendments changed the law with respect to
maintenance demonstrations. Under the pre-Amendment Clean Air Act, sections 172(a)(1) and 110(a)(2)(b) required
plans to include measures to ``insure attainment and
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
 maintenance
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
''. (emphasis added) But the 1990 Amendments revised both the general planning and ozone and CO specific planning
provisions to delete the requirement for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a maintenance demonstration to be submitted simultaneously with an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment demonstration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Amendments revised section 110(a)(2) to delete any reference to a maintenance demonstration. When we look to
Part D for requirements applicable to areas classified ``Serious'' and above for ozone, and ``Moderate'' (with a
design value greater than 12.7ppm) and above for CO, we find that section 172(c)(1), the closest analog to old section
172(a)(1), also drops any mention of planning for maintenance. Sections 182(c)(2)(A) and 187(a)(7) of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the new Amendments, which set forth the specific requirements for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ozone and CO nonattainment areas, respectively, to provide for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment, similarly makes no mention of any maintenance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement.
<FOOTCITE>21</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
21
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 110(a)(1) of both the pre-amended and amended Acts requires areas to provide for maintenance (as well as implementation
and enforcement) after EPA issues a new or revised NAAQS. But this is not applicable here, since that obligation is
past for ozone and CO, and the failure that gave rise to the FIP obligation here was grounded in maintenance provisions
of section 172(a) that were repealed in 1990.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Rather, the requirement for nonattainment areas to provide for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance in the amended Act is now found in new section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
107(d)(3)(E)(iv) and section 175A. These sections provide that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance plans must be submitted when an area requests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
redesignation to attainment. The combination of the amendments to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 172 and the addition of these new maintenance plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provisions indicates that the requirement for a maintenance plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
arises only much later, when it must accompany a request for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
redesignation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This reflects a Congressional determination to focus on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment, and to compel maintenance plans only when an area has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progressed to the point when it has already attained and is ready 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to demonstrate attainment. This legal regime and policy has thus 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
superseded any obligation arising out of old section 172(a). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's existing regulation, promulgated under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aforementioned pre-Amendment maintenance provisions, requires SIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to provide for maintenance for 20 years, or for 10 years upon the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
making of an appropriate finding. Section 193 of the 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Amendments, the ``General Savings Clause'', provides in pertinent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part that each regulation shall remain in effect except to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extent ``otherwise provided under this Act, or inconsistent with any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provision in effect. *&blank;*&blank;*'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As demonstrated above, the changes to section 172 and the new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of section 175A and related provisions deleted from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Act any requirement to provide for maintenance prior to a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
request for redesignation. The new Act thus establishes a whole 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new regime for demonstrating attainment and maintenance, the timing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and requirements of which are inconsistent with the old law. Under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Amendments, attainment plans no longer must provide ``up front'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a plan for maintenance. And when the Act does require submission of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance plans (at the time of a redesignation request), such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plan need demonstrate maintenance for only ten years instead of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
twenty. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although EPA has not yet accomplished the formal rescission of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its pre-1990 regulations on maintenance, we consider the rules 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nullified by operation of Section 193, and therefore no longer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable. EPA will propose in separate rulemaking to rescind 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these national regulations, which appear at 40 CFR part 51 subpart 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
D_Maintenance of National Standards. EPA may or may not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgate a replacement for the regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Other Issues Relating to EPA's Authority in Issuing a FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
a. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Comprehensive FIP authorities under the clean air act.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 When forced by a state planning delinquency to promulgate a FIP, EPA has wide-ranging authority under section 110(c)
to fill the gaps left by the state failure. EPA's authority to prescribe FIP measures is of three types. First, EPA may
promulgate any measure which it is expressly permitted to issue under any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
circumstances pursuant to pre-existing independent statutory authority_for example, explicit provisions of
Title II. That is, EPA may promulgate any measure which it has authority to issue in a non-FIP context, without reliance
on section 110(c). Second, EPA may invoke Section 110(c)'s general FIP authority, and act to cure a planning inadequacy
in any way not clearly prohibited by statute. Third, under section 110(c), the Courts have held that EPA may exercise
all authority that the State may exercise under the Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The second type of authority, EPA's general authority under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 110(c), is essentially remedial, and EPA has broad power 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under that section to cure a defective state plan. Thus, in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgating a FIP, EPA may exercise its own, independent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulatory authority under the Act in any way not clearly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prohibited by an explicit provision of the Act. When EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgates a FIP, courts have not required explicit authority for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific measures: ``We are inclined to construe Congress' broad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grant of power to the EPA as including all enforcement devices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonably necessary to the achievement and maintenance of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
goals established by the legislation.'' 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
South Terminal Corp. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
504 F.2d 646, 669. (1st Cir. 1974). See also 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
City of Santa 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rosa 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
534 F.2d 150, 153&hyph;154 (9th Cir. 1976) (upholding the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Administrator's authority to promulgate a FIP imposing gas-rationing in Los Angeles on a massive scale). ``The authority
to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulate pollution carries with it the power to do so in a manner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonably calculated to reach that end.'' 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Id.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 at 155. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, when a State's failure to discharge the primary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility to protect its air quality compels EPA to assume 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this task, the powers of the defaulting State accrue to EPA. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Ninth Circuit recently held, when EPA acts in place of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State pursuant to a FIP under section 110(c), EPA ```stands in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shoes of the defaulting State, and all of the rights and duties 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that would otherwise fall to the State accrue instead to EPA.''' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Central Arizona Water Conservation District 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
990 F.2d 1531, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at 1541 9th Cir. 1993). The First Circuit, in an early FIP case, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agreed: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
The Administrator must promulgate promptly regulations setting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forth `an implementation plan for a State should the State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
itself fail to propose a satisfactory one.' The statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scheme would be unworkable were it read as giving to EPA when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgating an implementation plan for a State, less than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those necessary measures allowed by Congress to a State to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accomplish federal clean air goals. We do not adopt any such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
crippling interpretation. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=3 -->
South Terminal Corp. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, supra 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
at 668. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
b. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA authority to charge fees. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
State Implementation Plans for nonattainment areas are clearly authorized to include emission fees as economic
incentives to reduce pollutants. Sections 110(a)(2)(A) (relating to SIPs generally) and 172(c)(6) broadly authorize
SIPs to meet the Act's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements through any ``control measures, means, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
techniques *&blank;*&blank;* necessary or appropriate'', including ``economic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentives such as fees *&blank;*&blank;*'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA interprets the FIP provisions to authorize fees as well, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by virtue of both the case law that grants FIPs much the same scope 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as SIPs, and by the provisions of section 302(y), which defines a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP to include_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=21 g=1 f=1 -->
Enforceable emission limitations or other control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures, means or techniques (including economic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentives, such as marketable permits or auctions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions allowances). 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 04 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA interprets this phrase, including the parenthetical reference 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to economic incentives, as providing authority to impose a broad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
range of economic incentives, including fees, even if not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specifically listed in the provision. Thus, EPA takes the position 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that a FIP includes authority to impose fees concerning ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generating emissions or other activities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
c. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Restrictions on EPA's authority regarding fees.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 As a legal matter, EPA's authority under section 110(c) and 302(y) to impose fees is limited only to the extent that
the fees must be enforceable, and must provide an incentive that may result in a reduction in ozone generating emissions
or other activities. As a practical matter, because only quantifiable emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions may be credited in the attainment demonstration, EPA may limit the types or amounts of fees imposed to those
that assure quantifiable emissions reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
