<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Mobile source regulations.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The mobile source control measures which are discussed in section III.D. of this NPRM range in scope from national
application to restricted application in one or more of the three 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP areas depending on the category. Technology improvements are generally required in phases, with new national
emission standards applicable in the early years, and California, or FIP area requirements scheduled for implementation
later. In some cases, the mobile source measures are applied only locally because they are adopted to fill a lack in
a particular area's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
plan, such as enhanced I/M or the employee commute options program. Local strategies are also proposed to deal with
the emissions from transportation and military facilities that contain more than one type of mobile emission source.
Following is a brief classification of the measures according to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
breadth of their application: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Nationwide. New emission standards have recently been proposed or are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shortly scheduled for proposal for several mobile source emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories. Both on-highway and off-road heavy duty engines will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meet tighter NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standards, as will locomotives. Lawn and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
garden, recreational marine, and other small nonroad equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are the subject of new exhaust and evaporative hydrocarbon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Statewide. In California, it is also possible to adopt standards more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stringent than those applied at the national level, if it is necessary to do so for attainment purposes. The market
for vehicles and equipment in California is large enough to encourage manufacturers to supply a full range of product
in the state, even though it may be different than similar product in the rest of the country. Today's NPRM proposes
special standards for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
medium duty vehicles, heavy duty highway engines, and onroad and off-road motorcycles sold and used in California
and gives notice that such standards may become necessary for lawn and garden equipment and other small engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) FIP areas. In the nonroad heavy duty engine category, the FIP proposal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contains special NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and evaporative HC requirements for engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in the FIP areas. The standards represent a significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
challenge for heavy duty engine manufacturers and are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered to be necessary for attainment in other areas of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
United States. Engines used in highway applications are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulated in the FIP at the statewide level, but the approach to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the special NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and evaporative HC standards for the nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines will involve an engine registration and sticker 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforcement system. For that reason, the standards are applied 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to engines actually used in each individual FIP area, and not to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all engines sold in California. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In several categories, the FIP proposes emission bubbles to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allow facilities or entities with many mobile sources as much 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flexibility as possible to find reductions across different 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile emission sources. These bubbles are proposed for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial airlines, marine vessels, railroads (South Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only), and military installations in the FIP areas. In some 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases, fees are proposed to encourage the purchase and use of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cleanest engines, to encourage activity reductions, or to promote 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the use of lower emitting operational procedures. These fees 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
