<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate or whether the advantages of attainment by an earlier 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
date justify the imposition of the kinds of measures which would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be necessary. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The FIP rules proposed for implementation in the Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area for the 1999 and 2005 attainment options are discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sections III.B.2 and III.B.3. Mobile source measures which yield 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reductions for both the 1999 and 2005 options include an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced I/M program, credit for national standards for nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles and engines, offroad recreational vehicle standards, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycle standards, and a parking cash out requirement. Other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile source measures needed for attainment, discussed above and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below, depend on the attainment year, 1999 or 2005. For 1999, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the additional measures include accelerating turnover for heavy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
duty truck engines, capping emissions for nonroad fleets, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restricting driving for highway vehicles and a strategy for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recreational boats. For 2005, the additional measures includes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies for motor vehicles, locomotives, recreational boats, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
airports, military installations, and an employee commute options 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ECO) program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For stationary sources in Sacramento, both the 1999 and 2005 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposals include the source specific reasonably available 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control technology (RACT) regulations discussed in III.C.2, all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the regulations for specific source categories discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.3 and the statewide regulations discussed in III.C.4. Many 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources will also be subject to the cap regulations discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.5. which will require approximately a 20&hyph;45 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction in VOCs by 1999 (for the 1999 attainment option) or a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gradual 4&hyph;9 percent per year reduction in VOCs from 2001 through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2005 (for the 2005 attainment option). 
