<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A bump-up is also important for stationary sources which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be required to meet EPA's Cap Regulations (section III.C.5). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under the 1999 attainment date, these stationary sources would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to submit compliance plans by 1998 which will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate an additional 20&hyph;45 percent reduction in VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions by 1999. With the additional time allowed by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``Severe'' area classification, these sources would instead be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to a declining cap requiring gradual 4&hyph;9 percent per year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions reductions, which will not be required to begin until 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2001. The additional years for compliance would allow a company 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to investigate control options and take greater advantage of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technological developments, and thus avoid production cutbacks or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shutdowns to meet emissions reductions levels. EPA believes it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is reasonable to anticipate that substantially improved emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction alternatives and control technologies will develop for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stationary and area sources during the additional time that a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
higher classification for Sacramento would provide. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) 2005 attainment option. The FIP measures common to both the 1999 and 2005 proposals for both stationary
<FOOTCITE>27</FOOTCITE>
 and mobile sources will have reduced the Sacramento VOC inventory from 236 tons per day in 1990 to 157 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tons per day in 1999, and the NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
x
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 inventory from 185 to 149 tons 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per day. Like the 1999 proposal discussed above, these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions come from the stationary source measures, continued 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefits of the current motor vehicle control programs, and a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantial improvement in in-use emissions of light duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles due to an enhanced I/M program. However, by 2005 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional reductions are realized from the new emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards affecting medium duty vehicles, heavy duty vehicles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad diesels and locomotives due to engine turnover, and from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the enhanced in-use compliance program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
27
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The stationary measures include all of the measures discussed in III.C that are identified as applying to Sacramento
and those that apply statewide, and the cap regulation which requires a gradual 4 percent per year VOC reduction from
2001 through 2005.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
With the benefits from the recreational boat measure and ECO 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program discussed above and in section III.D, total emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions by 2005 will be large enough to provide for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
It is important to note that a bump-up of the Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area to ``Severe'' also leads to the following additional Clean Air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act requirements associated with a ``Severe'' area classification, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which include: (1) A more stringent major source definition (25 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tons per year, down from 50 tons per year); (2) a more stringent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offset requirement for new major sources (1.3:1 instead of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.2:1); (3) TCMs to offset VMT growth; (4) and an employer-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
trip reduction rule. The responsibility for meeting these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements is discussed below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If EPA grants the request of a state to reclassify a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonattainment area to a higher classification under section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
181(b)(3), that area must meet all deadline and control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements applicable to the higher classification, and is not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eligible for any adjustment of those deadlines. H.R. Rep. No. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
101&hyph;490, Part 1, 101st Cong., 2d Sess., 233 (1990). If 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassification is promulgated by EPA unilaterally in the FIP, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the area likewise becomes responsible for meeting all such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deadlines and requirements for SIP purposes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The substance of these additional requirements is set forth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in section 182(d) of the Act.
<FOOTCITE>28</FOOTCITE>
 Because the statutory deadlines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for compliance with or submission to EPA of these requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have already passed, if EPA finalizes in February 1995 its bump-up
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 option for the Sacramento area, the State will be responsible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for submitting the actual regulations to comply with the ``Severe'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area requirements at that time. If the State fails to do so, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento area will be subject to a finding of failure to submit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these requirements upon final promulgation of the FIP in 1995. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
28
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
For a more complete description of the requirements and EPA preliminary policies with respect to them, readers should
consult the ``General Preamble for Implementation of Title I of the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990'' (57 FR 13521).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed previously in this NPRM, EPA's current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obligation is to design a FIP for the Sacramento area that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attains the ozone NAAQS by 1999 or 2005. EPA is not legally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to meet all SIP submittal requirements that appeared for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the first time in the 1990 Amendments and for which there are no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state failures which EPA must cure. Nevertheless, as a policy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
matter, and because it has invoked the provisions of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Amendments in initiating a bump-up, EPA is proposing in today's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NPRM the ``Severe'' area provisions (section III.F.) in regulatory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
form. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The measures proposed in III.F. were developed to help the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State and local agencies meet the additional ``Severe'' area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements if the Sacramento area is bumped up. For example, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed new source review rule discussed in section III.F.4. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is based on the actual language of existing rules in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento area in order to provide a rule that is easy for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local APCDs to adopt and implement. The employee commute options 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ECO) rule discussed in section III.F.2. is also adoptable by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local APCDs; alternatively, it may provide an incentive for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local APCDs to develop, adopt and submit ECO programs that are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently being considered. Thus, if bump-up is promulgated, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that the measures proposed in III.F. will help the State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and local agencies meet their mandate in a timely fashion and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
avoid sanctions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If EPA ultimately chooses its preferred option of bumping up 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento, EPA plans, if necessary, to implement the ``Severe'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area measures proposed in section III.F. on their respective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effective dates following final promulgation of the FIP in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
February 1995. Until a submittal of replacement measures is made 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by the State and approved by EPA, the ``Severe'' area requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed in section III.F. will remain in effect. If EPA finds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that there will be a significant time period before the submittal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is made and approved, EPA will try to delegate portions or all of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the federal program to the State and local agencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
f. 
