<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
EPA requests comment on whether the bump-up is an appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solution or whether the advantages of attainment by an earlier 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
date justify the imposition of the measures necessary for 1999 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
g. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Attainment demonstrations. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed in Section III.H.5.a, EPA believes that the set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of measures proposed in this NPRM, in conjunction with adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State and local measures, will bring the Sacramento area into 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment with the ozone standard by 1999 or 2005. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projection is based on extensive computer simulations of ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formation performed using the Urban Airshed Model. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Ventura Ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
a. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Reduction requirements. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Anthropogenic emissions in Ventura in 1990 totaled approximately 105 tons per day of VOC and 86 tons per day of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. The existing air pollution regulations controlling these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions are among the most stringent in the country. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, many of the CARB and VCACPD regulations prescribe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
future compliance requirements that are still more stringent, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the existing CARB standards on new engines and equipment will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deliver increasing reductions each year as a result of fleet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
turnover. Sections II.B and III.H of this NPRM discuss the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopted CARB and VCAPCD rules and EPA's assignment of credit to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the rules as part of the FIP/SIP attainment demonstration. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Unfortunately, projected emission reductions from many of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these State and local measures are offset by continued growth in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the area's population and activity levels. Consequently, in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
absence of further SIP or FIP measures, emissions in Ventura are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projected to be 98 tons per day VOC and 75 tons per day NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the year 2005. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA, CARB, and VCAPCD have performed extensive meteorologic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis and airshed modeling of ozone formation in the area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This modeling suggests that various combinations of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions can achieve attainment with the ozone standard. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in Section III.H.3, EPA is using reduction targets of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
40 percent VOC and 40 percent NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 from the 1990 base-year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions levels. Therefore, the SIP and FIP controls must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieve, by 2005, emission levels of 57 tons per day VOC and 46 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tons per day NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
b. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
SIP rules. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Irrespective of EPA's FIP obligation, California and Ventura 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
County are required to submit an ozone attainment SIP in November 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1994. As part of this and previous SIP efforts, CARB and VCAPCD 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are continually adopting and modifying regulations to reduce NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and VOC emissions. EPA is not proposing to act on any SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improvements as part of this NPRM, but will do so on a case-by-case basis in separate actions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For purposes of the attainment demonstration in this NPRM, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is generally not assigning emission reduction credit to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures until they are fully adopted in enforceable form. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
urges the responsible State and local agencies to complete rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development and submit rules for SIP approval as soon as they are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fully adopted. This will allow EPA to approve the regulations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and substitute the locally developed measures for FIP rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
c. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
FIP rules. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The set of FIP measures proposed for implementation in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura relies largely on those regulations discussed in Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.B as elements common to all three FIP areas. For mobile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources, this includes regulations for motor vehicles and I/M, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad vehicle and engine controls, forthcoming national 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations for locomotives, and strategies to reduce the ambient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impact of ship traffic along the coast. For stationary sources, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it includes regulations for solvent cleaning operations, wood 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products coatings, service stations, waste burning, and fugitive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions, as well as the statewide measures and the emission cap 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One FIP measure peculiar to Ventura affects emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ocean-going ships transitting up and down the Ventura coastline. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In Section III.D.5.e.(4) of this NPRM, EPA discusses rerouting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Santa Barbara shipping channel farther from the coast, and a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fee system designed to encourage ships to travel outside of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Channel Islands. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
d. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Attainment demonstration. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed in Section III.H., EPA believes that the set of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures proposed in this NPRM, along with adopted State and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local measures, will bring Ventura County into attainment with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ozone standard by 2005. This projection is based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extensive computer simulations of ozone formation performed using 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Urban Airshed Model. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
e. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Alternative attainment date discussion. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 181(a)(1) of the Act requires that ozone SIPs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate attainment as expeditiously as practicable but not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
later than, in the case of Ventura, 2005. As a result, EPA has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attempted to identify practicable measures that could achieve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment by an earlier date. For illustrative purposes, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
focused this investigation on a target attainment date of 2000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is not, however, proposing this attainment date or the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following strategies in this NPRM. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In today's NPRM, EPA is proposing two attainment options for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Sacramento area. 1999 is proposed because Sacramento is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently classified as a ``Serious'' ozone area, and 2005 is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed because EPA is recommending reclassifying Sacramento to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a ``Severe'' area. As discussed in paragraph III.B.3.e of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NPRM, EPA believes that attainment in Sacramento by 1999 would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
require unacceptably harsh control measures, and therefore 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prefers the 2005 option. Since Ventura's ozone problem is worse 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than Sacramento's (e.g., EPA is projecting that a 30% reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions is needed for attainment in Sacramento while a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
40% reduction is needed in Ventura), it is not surprising that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment in Ventura by 2000 would also prove to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impracticable. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Many of the mobile source FIP controls for the 2005 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment demonstration are proposed for near-term 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implementation in Ventura, and will achieve substantial emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions by 2000. These include an enhanced inspection and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance program, national standards for nonroad vehicles and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines, offroad recreational vehicle standards, motorcycle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards, and a parking cash-out program. The difficulty in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieving full attainment by that date, however, is that many of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed mobile source strategies rely on engine and vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
turn-over to reduce emissions. Tight emission standards for new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
onroad vehicles, for example, are not effective while people are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
still driving older cars. By 2000, new emission standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affecting heavy duty onroad and nonroad engines and locomotives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will just barely begin to be felt in new engine purchases. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In trying to identify practical measures for attainment in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura by 2000, EPA has considered control strategies similar to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those proposed to bring Sacramento into attainment by 1999. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These include the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Onroad heavy duty trucks. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA could accelerate replacement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of old high-emitting onroad heavy duty truck engines by requiring 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local truck fleet owners to register and sticker their equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to assure compliance with tight emission standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Nonroad growth cap. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA could propose that growth in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad heavy duty category be capped such that emissions not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceed 1995 projected levels. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Accelerate shipping fee system. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA could accelerate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fee system designed to encourage shipping companies to reroute 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their shipping activities beyond the Channel Islands. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
