<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
b. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
SIP rules.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 In section II.C.2., EPA proposes to conditionally approve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commitments by the SCAQMD and CARB to continue their development 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a number of new or strengthened regulations and to adopt them 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within the next two years to accomplish the first rate-of-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progress installments required under the Act. These committal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures have the potential to deliver approximately 83 tons per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
day of VOC. Scheduled SCAQMD rule development could also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contribute substantial additional NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In preparing the November 1994 ozone attainment plan, CARB, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SCAQMD, and SCAG are now reassessing strategies for adopting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures to reduce NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and VOC emissions. The SCAQMD is also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developing a VOC RECLAIM program for near-term adoption and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implementation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For purposes of this attainment demonstration, however, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is not assigning emission reduction credit to measures until they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are fully adopted in enforceable form. EPA urges the responsible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State and local agencies to complete rule development and submit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules for SIP approval as soon as they are fully adopted. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will allow EPA to approve the regulations and substitute the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
locally developed measures for FIP rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
c. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
FIP Rules.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The set of FIP measures proposed for implementation in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast relies largely on those regulations discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section III.B as elements common to all three FIP areas. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes the controls for onroad and nonroad mobile sources, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplemented by such elements as I/M and sticker programs. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, the proposed FIP for the South Coast includes the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile source controls discussed in the Ventura FIP summary, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
two measures peculiar to the South Coast FIP: A fee-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program to reduce emissions from ports and a locomotive cap rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For stationary and area sources, it includes regulations for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service stations, livestock waste, fugitive emission controls for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
petroleum and chemical facilities, and waste burning, as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the statewide measures and the emission cap program. Finally, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast FIP relies on the new technology commitments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed immediately below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
d. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Section 182(e)(5) provisions.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 (1) Statutory provision and General Preamble requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 182(e)(5) of the Clean Air Act applies exclusively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to ``Extreme'' ozone areas (i.e., only the South Coast area). This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provision authorizes the State (or EPA, standing in the shoes of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the State
<FOOTCITE>30</FOOTCITE>
) to rely for its ozone attainment demonstration on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conceptual, as yet unadopted measures that anticipate new or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improved technology. The South Coast's 1991 Air Quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Management Plan employs a similar approach in its ``Tier II'' and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``Tier III'' new technology measures, which consist of brief 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussions of control targets (percentage and emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions) for specific or general source categories, to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieved in future years through technological advances. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
30
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed above, the Ninth Circuit has held that ``Acting in place of the State of Arizona pursuant to a FIP under
42 U.S.C. &sect;7410(c), EPA `stands in the shoes of the defaulting State, and all of the rights and duties that would
otherwise fall to the State accrue instead to EPA.''' 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=3 -->
Central Arizona Water Conservation District et al. v. EPA,
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 990 F.2d 1531, 1541 (9th Cir. 1993).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For EPA to credit such measures toward the ozone attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstration (either by SIP approval or FIP promulgation), EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must find that the measures are not needed to meet the Act's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progress requirements for the first 10 years (see section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
182(e)(5)(A)), and EPA must approve State commitments or make its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
own commitments to submit contingency measures no later than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
three years before proposed implementation of the measures, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sufficient to ensure the required rate of progress and attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(section 182(e)(5)(B)). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's General Preamble for the Implementation of Title I 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provides the Agency's preliminary articulation of how it will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exercise its considerable discretion under section 182(e)(5). At 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this time, EPA, in approving or promulgating measures under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section, will look to see that the plan shows that the measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cannot be fully developed and adopted by the submittal date for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the attainment demonstration; that the plan contains a schedule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outlining the steps leading to final development and adoption of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the measures; and that the plan contains commitments from those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies that would be involved in developing and implementing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the schedule for each measure (57 FR 13524, April 16, 1992). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the Act's requirement that the technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
advancement measures not be needed to achieve progress required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the first 10 years, EPA believes that the Administrator 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should satisfy herself with respect to three separate progress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements applicable during the 10-year period following 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enactment of the 1990 Amendments to the Act: (1) 15 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction of VOC emissions for the 6-year period from 1990 to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1996, as required by section 182(b)(1); (2) 9 percent reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of VOC and/or NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions for the 3-year period from 1997 to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999, as required by section 182(c)(2)(B); and (3) a further 3 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent reduction of VOC and/or NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions during the year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2000, as the initial installment of the 9 percent reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required for the period 2000 to 2002.
<FOOTCITE>31</FOOTCITE>
 In section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.B.5.d.(4) of this NPRM, EPA demonstrates that each of these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
three progress requirements is satisfied (with regard to section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
182(e)(5)) by the combination of State and local measures and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP regulations proposed in this NPRM. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
31
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA policies on rate-of-progress plan requirements, including substitution of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 for VOC emissions reductions for the post-1996 period, appear in two recent guidance documents: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=3 -->
Guidance for Growth Factors, Projections, and Control Strategies for the 15 Percent Rate-of-Progress Plans
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 (EPA&hyph;452/R&hyph;93&hyph;002, March 1993); and 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=3 -->
Guidance on the Post-1996 Rate-of-Progress Plan and the Attainment Demonstration.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(2) Need for additional time to develop new technologies in enacting special provisions for ozone attainment in the

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast, Congress recognized that attainment in this area may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
require 20 years, even on an expeditious schedule of progress. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The difficulty of the ozone attainment project derives primarily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the topography and climate of the South Coast basin, which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combine to produce ideal conditions for the formation and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
persistence of tropospheric ozone. In this setting exists one of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the world's largest industrial and commercial centers, with a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
population projected to approach 18 million by the attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The technological challenge of attainment is evident in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extraordinary remaining ozone precursor reduction requirements_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approximately 90% for VOC and 70% for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
_which are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
superimposed on an already very strict level of existing control. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Complete elimination of all emissions from motor vehicles would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not accomplish even half of the needed reductions. In fact, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast could not reach attainment even if there were no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile source and industrial emissions in the basin. The area's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unique reduction responsibilities therefore necessitate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
breakthroughs in air pollution control technologies for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practically every pollution source, coupled with the most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
advanced techniques and initiatives for preventing pollution. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that the pace of air pollution control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technology development in the past provides a reasonable basis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for confidence that the South Coast can meet the Clean Air Act's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2010 ozone attainment goal using section 182(e)(5). Lacking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sufficiently detailed data to predict and mandate specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controls for each particular source category, however, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposes to employ section 182(e)(5) commitments to extend the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate of progress beyond the levels now foreseeable and reflected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the declining cap rules discussed above. EPA also proposes to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commit to achieving additional reductions from smaller stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source categories than are covered under the proposed cap rules, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as well as from area and mobile sources. The commitments would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allow EPA, the State, and the affected sources sufficient time to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cooperate in the further development and commercialization of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control approaches to deliver the remaining reductions needed for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment. EPA strongly encourages CARB and SCAQMD to devote 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their substantial resources to expediting development and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adoption of State and local controls in advance of the section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
182(e)(5) commitment dates for rule adoption and implementation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
(3) Federal Role in Support of Technological Development Activities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Scope of EPA's Responsibilities.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 From its inception, EPA has been charged with a leadership 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
role in coordinating, assisting, and independently advancing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national research efforts to develop methods and technologies for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air pollution control. This continuous program has taken many 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forms: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA laboratories to conduct research, testing, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA surveys, reports, networks, and clearinghouses to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disseminate the most current information on technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development and transfer; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA programs and EPA-lead task forces to coordinate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technology development and application studies and projects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with other Federal departments and agencies; with State, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local, and international air pollution control agencies; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with private agencies, institutions, and organizations; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with involved industries; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
