<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00069 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;EPA study and assessment (in accordance with section 405 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the Act and in conjunction with the Council of Economic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Advisors) of economic incentive measures and approaches for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stimulating additional air pollution control progress, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concentrating on air pollution problems for which no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing methods of control exist; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA research contracts with public and private agencies, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
institutions, and individuals to carry out specific projects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relating to new air pollution reduction technologies; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA grants under section 105 of the Act to State and local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air pollution control agencies and to other nonprofit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organizations to conduct research or demonstration projects; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA research fellowships both at EPA and at research 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
organizations or universities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the 1990 Amendments to the CAA, Congress assigned EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional responsibilities for several specific new air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pollution control technology projects, ranging from hydrogen fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cell vehicle test programs to studies of renewable energy and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
energy conservation incentives. Congress also broadened the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
scope of EPA's mission: ``the [EPA] Administrator shall conduct a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basic engineering research and technology program to develop, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluate, and demonstrate nonregulatory strategies and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technologies for air pollution prevention. Such strategies and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technologies shall be developed with priority on those pollutants 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which pose a significant risk to human health and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environment, and with opportunities for participation by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industry, public interest groups, scientists, and other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interested persons in the development of such strategies and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technologies.'' Section 103(g). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Recent National Initiatives. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Federal commitment to a leadership role in development 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of clean technologies and control approaches is reflected in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
several new comprehensive initiatives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
1
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Climate Change Action Plan. The Climate Change Action Plan was announced by President 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Clinton and Vice President Gore on October 19, 1993. The plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consists of 50 new or expanded programs and innovative public-private partnerships. The unifying goal of the plan
is reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in greenhouse gases, which include one of the two principal ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
precursors: oxides of nitrogen. In addition, the majority of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the plan's initiatives_whether in the area of energy demand 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actions, transportation actions, or energy supply actions_will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
directly advance progress toward ozone attainment in the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast, either through projects to reduce energy consumption or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs to spur increased reliance on clean energy sources. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Many provisions of the Action Plan are intended to produce 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
almost immediate progress, and some of these actions, like the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
parking cash-out program, are already incorporated as core 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures in this FIP. Successful implementation of this group of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
initiatives will reduce the growth in emissions in the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast associated with the area's projected population increases, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
economic development, rising energy use, and expanded 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transportation requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Other provisions of the Climate Change plan involve a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continuing effort to advance long term strategies and stimulate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs and technologies that will yield their greatest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in the first decade of the next century. As a means 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of ensuring overall coordination, the President has instructed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the White House National Economic Council, the Office on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Environmental Policy, and the Office of Science and Technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Policy to co-chair interagency working groups to examine all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
budget, technology, research and development, regulatory and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
economic policies that could impact greenhouse gas emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
levels beyond the year 2000. From this effort will come 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cooperative federal pilot projects and technical assistance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs designed to establish an empirical basis for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technological advances that will provide the foundation for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sustainable development in future decades. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An example of this coordinated long-term commitment is the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pesticide use reduction project of EPA, the Department of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agriculture, and the Food and Drug Administration. The specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies under this initiative include establishing incentives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the development, registration, and use of biological 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pesticides and the promotion of the high-technology bio-engineering industry; initiation of demonstration projects
for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in overall pesticide use; and support for integrated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pest management programs. The success of this program should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eventually, through regulatory and nonregulatory incentives, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accomplish further VOC reductions beyond the air pollution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achievements of the pesticide control measure proposed in this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP (see section III.C.4.d.). Besides impressive reductions in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
greenhouse gas emissions, this program is anticipated to result 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in energy savings worth $1.9 billion through 2000, and savings of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
$9 billion of the period of 2001&hyph;2010. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

