<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Regulations for Specific Source Categories in the FIP Areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
a. Introduction_(1) Rationale for Selection of Categories and Requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As discussed in section III.C.1 of this NPRM, EPA believes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP should include traditional category-specific stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source rules where possible. Such rules improve the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State/federal partnership, minimize federal intrusion into State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affairs, and contribute to other goals described in section I.B 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of this NPRM. While EPA is unable to develop rules for all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stationary source categories, the proposed FIP includes 15 rules 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for specific categories as described in sections III.C.3.b-p and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3 statewide area source regulations presented in section III.C.4 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Many of these rules have been adapted from regulations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
already existing or proposed in parts of California. In such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases, EPA has generally selected the most stringent requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available and relied on the technical work performed by CARB and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local Districts to estimate associated costs and emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions. While there may be additional rules that EPA could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have adapted for the FIP under this effort, EPA used the amount 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of potential emission reductions as a primary screening device 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for evaluating which rules were appropriate for the FIP proposal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, rules projected to reduce emissions by more than 0.1 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent of the uncontrolled attainment year inventory were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered good candidates. EPA also considered cost-effectiveness and District recommendations in selecting

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories and specific requirements under this effort. Examples 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of proposed FIP elements adapted from existing District rules are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those affecting solvent cleaning operations, wood products 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
coatings, and auto refinishing. Many of these category-specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules are based on regulations adopted by the SCAQMD or CARB. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's application of these rules to Sacramento and Ventura should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not discourage the SCAQMD and CARB from pursuing further emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions from these source categories in order to meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progress and attainment requirements of federal and State law. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also developed rules for source categories which have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not yet been regulated under the Clean Air Act but which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contribute substantial emissions in the FIP areas. For these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories, EPA worked with the State and Districts to evaluate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
various mechanisms for reducing emissions. Among the FIP rules 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developed under this effort are those affecting pesticides, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aerosol coatings, and livestock waste management. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Relationship to existing and pending SIP provisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Some stationary sources proposed for control through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
category-specific FIP rules are already regulated by existing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State or District SIP rules. Architectural coating, for example, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has been regulated in the South Coast by versions of South Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Rule 1113 since 1977. EPA's proposed FIP rule (section 52.2959), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
however, would reduce architectural coating emissions even 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
further. Where a State or local rule has been approved by EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into the SIP (as, for example, Rule 1113 was approved in 57 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
8104, August 11, 1992), affected sources would have to meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tighter of each element in the two rules.
<FOOTCITE>38</FOOTCITE>
 Sources will, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
however, need to continue to comply with applicable State and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
38
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 302(q) of the Act defines the applicable implementation plan as that ``portion .&blank;.&blank;. or most
recent revision thereof, which has been approved under section 110, or promulgated under section 110(c) .&blank;.&blank;.''
While the regulations discussed in sections III.C.3 and 4 of this notice may effectively supersede less stringent
provisions of federally approved SIP rules addressing the same source categories, the State or local rules would
remain in the SIP and be enforceable.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed in section III.J. and elsewhere in this NPRM, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA intends to delegate the FIP rules and/or remove them whenever 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the State submits an approvable, equivalent SIP provision.
<FOOTCITE>39</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If, for example, SCAQMD submits a proposed SIP amendment that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
makes Rule 1113 an approvable substitute for the FIP rule, upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA approval, EPA would amend section 52.2959 to rescind its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicability within the South Coast. In many cases, CARB and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected Districts are already developing rules similar to those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed in this NPRM. If these are submitted to EPA before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
February 1995, EPA may, upon SIP approval, simply amend or remove 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the analogous component from the final promulgated FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Alternatively, EPA may remove FIP elements under separate action. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
39
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Pursuant to &sect;110(1) of the Act, State rules are not approvable as revisions to an implementation plan if they
`interfere with any applicable requirement concerning attainment and reasonable further progress .&blank;.&blank;.
or any other applicable requirement of this Act.''
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If, however, EPA determines that a new or modified State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule does not achieve emission reductions equivalent to the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule, and no other State rule compensates for the differences, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will maintain implementation of the FIP measure.
<FOOTCITE>40</FOOTCITE>
 In such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cases, sources could again be subject to overlapping State and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal requirements. Therefore, EPA encourages the State to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopt approvable regulations as soon as possible to avoid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
duplicative effort for both regulators and affected sources. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
40
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA could also approve a substitute SIP attainment demonstration relying on a different mix of control measures to
achieve attainment and interim progress that at least equals the FIP's schedule. In this case, EPA may rescind individual
FIP rules that are more stringent than the substitute SIP provisions, if the particular FIP rules are no longer necessary
to provide for expeditious attainment because the SIP achieves greater reductions from controls on other source
categories.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(3) Relationship to other applicable FIP provisions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Many of the sources subject to the proposed category-specific rules described here will also be affected by the cap

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations described in section III.C.5 of this NPRM. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed, the cap regulations require almost all significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stationary sources to reduce emissions by a specified percentage. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The reduction rates are currently the same for sources which are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected by category-specific rules and for sources which are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not. As discussed in section III.C.5 of this NPRM, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specifically requests comments on whether fairness and other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concerns justify adjusting the reduction requirements to give 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources credit for emission reductions required in the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP rules described in sections III.C.2&hyph;4 of this NPRM. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Rule structure. These proposed rules generally follow the structure of New 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Source Performance Standards (NSPS) in 40 CFR part 60. They 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contain sections on applicability, definitions, specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements, exemptions, reporting, recordkeeping, test methods, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and additional components as necessary. Where immediate or near-term compliance is feasible, the effective dates
of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantive requirements are generally January 1, 1996, roughly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one year after final FIP promulgation is due. Issues specific to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual rules are discussed in sections III.C.3.b-p and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.4 below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Solvent Cleaning Operations (Sacramento, Ventura). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(a) controls VOC emissions resulting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from certain solvent cleaning operations in the Sacramento and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura nonattainment areas. Emission reductions are achieved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by: (1) Limiting the VOC content and vapor pressure of solvents 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in solvent cleaning operations; (2) allowing the use of add-on control equipment in lieu of meeting the VOC content
and vapor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pressure limits; and (3) prescribing procedures and requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for solvent cleaning operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule applies to facilities that use VOC-containing materials in the production, repair, maintenance,
or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
servicing of parts, products, tools, machinery, equipment, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general work areas, or that store and dispose of VOC-containing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
materials used in solvent cleaning operations. Affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities include manufacturing plants, printing presses, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shipyards, and repair and refinishing facilities such as auto 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
garages, auto body shops and workshops for buses, aircraft, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
trains and trucks. This proposed rule also applies to repair, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service, and production operations at institutional facilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such as government buildings, schools, hospitals, sanitariums, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prisons, restaurants, health clubs, and theaters.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00076 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
This proposed rule is based largely on SCAQMD Rule 1171 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Solvent Cleaning Operations) as adopted on August 2, 1991. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation of the environmental, economic, socioeconomic, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other impacts of the proposed FIP rule's requirements can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
found in SCAQMD's final staff report for Rule 1171, dated July 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1991. This proposed FIP rule also incorporates changes from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
draft amended Rule 1171 released by SCAQMD on October 6, 1993 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
components of the VCAPCD Rule 74.19 (Graphic Arts) as adopted on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
August 11, 1992. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is in the process of developing an Alternative Control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Technology (ACT) Document for solvent cleaning operations. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ACT will primarily address recordkeeping and will not establish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national guidelines for solvent content and work practices. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP rule requirements on VOC content and work practices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
do not conflict with the draft ACT. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected to total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
10.44 tpd in Sacramento in 1999, 11.82 tpd in Sacramento in 2005, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 3.55 tpd in Ventura in 2005. Implementation of this proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule is projected to reduce those emissions by 6.75, 7.66, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2.66 tpd respectively at an overall cost savings of $991 per ton 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC removed (1990 dollars). A cost savings is projected because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB and the Districts have identified compliant solvents which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are less expensive than high-VOC solvents currently in use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Wood Products Coatings (Sacramento, Ventura) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(b) controls VOC emissions resulting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from commercial and industrial application in the Sacramento and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura nonattainment areas of coatings while manufacturing wood 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products. Emission reductions are achieved by: (1) limiting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC content of various wood coating products; (2) allowing the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use of add-on control equipment that reduces VOC emissions by 85 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent in lieu of meeting the VOC limits on coatings; and (3) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restricting application of wood coatings to application methods 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and equipment with high transfer efficiencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule applies to all persons applying coating, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inks, stains, and/or strippers in wood product coating operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the purpose of manufacturing wood products, including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
furniture and other coated objects made of solid wood and/or wood 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
composition and/or simulated wood material. The proposed rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
does not apply to residential noncommercial operations. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
majority of the affected wood products coating operators are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected to comply with the proposed rule through the use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reformulated coatings rather than add-on emission control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
devices. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule is based largely on SCAQMD Rule 1136 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entitled ``Wood Products Coatings,'' as amended on August 2, 1991. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, EPA has incorporated some of SCAQMD's proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amendments to Rule 1136, dated July 23, 1993. Evaluation of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental, economic, socioeconomic, and other impacts of Rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1136 can be found in SCAQMD Staff Report ``Proposed Amended Rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1136_Wood Products Coatings,'' dated April 19, 1988, and SCAQMD 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Supplemental Staff Report ``Proposed Amended Rule 1136_Wood 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Products Coatings,'' dated July 5, 1988. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA has prepared a draft Control Techniques Guideline (CTG) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
document on wood products coatings that is entitled ``Control of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Volatile Organic Compound Emissions from Wood Furniture Coating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Operations.'' Upon publication of the CTG, revisions to this FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule may be appropriate for consistency. EPA expects, however, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the proposed FIP rule may be more stringent than the CTG in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some regards to reflect the unusually difficult air pollution 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
problems in the FIP areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected to total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.49 tpd in Sacramento in 1999, 0.55 tpd in Sacramento in 2005, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 0.65 tpd in Ventura in 2005. Implementation of this proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule is projected to reduce those emissions by 0.45, 0.51, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.61 tpd respectively at a cost of $18 per ton VOC removed (1988 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dollars). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Automotive Refinishing Operations (Sacramento). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(c) controls VOC emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
automobile refinishing operations in the Sacramento nonattainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area. Emission reductions are achieved by: (1) limiting the VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
content of various automobile refinishing coatings; (2) allowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the use of add-on control equipment that reduces VOC emissions by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
85 percent in lieu of meeting the VOC limits on coatings; and (3) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restricting application of automotive refinishing coatings to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application methods and equipment with high transfer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efficiencies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule applies to facilities that apply coatings 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to motor vehicles and mobile equipment or their existing parts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and components for the purpose of on-site refinishing and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modification. The proposed rule does not apply to application of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
original equipment manufacturing coatings applied at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturing plants. Affected facilities include autobody 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repair/paint shops, production autobody paint shops, new car 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dealer repair/paint shops, fleet operator repair/paint shops, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
custom-made car fabrication facilities, and truck body builders. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(c) is based largely on SCAQMD's Rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1151, Motor Vehicle and Mobile Equipment Non-Assembly Line 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coating Operations (September 6, 1991). Portions of the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP rule are based on CARB's Determination of Reasonably 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Available Control Technology and Best Available Retrofit Control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Technology (RACT/BARCT) for Automotive Refinishing Operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(January 8, 1991). An evaluation of the environmental, economic, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
socioeconomic, and other impacts of Rule 1151 requirements can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
found in SCAQMD's staff report for the proposed amended Rule 1151 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(August, 1991); this evaluation is also applicable to this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected to total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4.23 tpd in 1999 and 4.71 tpd in 2005. Implementation of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed rule is projected to reduce those emissions by 3.77 tpd 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 4.19 tpd respectively at a cost of $7,200 per ton of VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
removed (1990 dollars). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under section 183(e)(3) of the Act, EPA may in the future 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issue either national regulations or a CTG for this source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
category, as well as for other categories discussed in this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section. If EPA does so and the FIP rule is less stringent than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the national guidance or rule, EPA will amend the FIP rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriately. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Adhesives and Sealants (Sacramento) Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(d) controls VOC emissions resulting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from industrial and commercial use of adhesives and sealants in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Sacramento nonattainment area. Emission reductions are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieved by: (1) limiting the VOC content of various adhesives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and sealants; and (2) allowing the use of add-on control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment that reduces VOC emissions by 85 percent in lieu of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting the VOC limits on coatings. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This proposed rule applies to certain facilities that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supply, sell, or use adhesives, sealants, or adhesive primers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Affected facilities include (but are not limited to) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturing plants, architectural and construction companies, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and welding operations. Various activities are exempt from all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or part or the proposed rule, including tire repair, undersea-based weapon systems manufacturing, research and
development, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
small quantity users. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00077 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(d) is based largely on draft guidance and model rules developed by members of the CARB/California
Air Pollution Control Officers Association's (CAPCOA) Technical Review Group included in ``Reasonably Available
Control Technology and Best Available Retrofit Control Technology (RACT/BARCT) for Adhesives and Sealants,''
as presented in a workshop on September 30, 1993. In addition, portions of the proposed FIP rule are based on Bay Area
Air Quality Management District (BAAQMD) Regulation 8, Rule 51, Adhesive and Sealant Products; VCAPCD Rule 74.20,
Adhesives and Sealants; and SCAQMD Rule 1168, Control of Volatile Organic Compound Emissions from Adhesive Application.
Evaluation of the environmental, economic, socioeconomic, and other impacts of the proposed FIP rule's requirements
was partially based on the information provided in the BAAQMD and VCAPCD staff reports for their respective rules.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected
to total 2.64 tpd in 1999 and 2.99 tpd in 2005. Implementation of this proposed rule is projected to reduce those emissions
by 1.29 tpd and 1.47 tpd respectively at a cost of $890 per ton of VOC removed (1989 dollars).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Can and Coil Coating (Sacramento). Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(e) controls VOC emissions from certain metal container,
metal closure, and metal coil coating operations in the Sacramento nonattainment area. Emission reductions are
achieved by: (1) limiting the VOC content of various can and coil coatings; (2) allowing the use of add-on control equipment
that achieves a 90 percent capture efficiency and 95 percent destruction or removal efficiency in lieu of meeting
the VOC limits on coatings; and (3) restricting application of can and coil coatings to application methods and equipment
with high transfer efficiencies.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule applies to facilities that apply coatings to metal containers, metal closures, and/or metal coils.
Facilities that apply 1 gallon per day or less of spray coating, however, are exempt from the requirements of the proposed
rule. Sacramento and Placer counties have implemented can and coil rules (SMAQMD Rule 452 and PCAPCD Rule 223) similar
to this proposed FIP rule, although not as stringent. Currently, no sources exist in El Dorado and Sutter counties.
Therefore, while the rule is proposed for implementation in the entire Sacramento ozone nonattainment area (as described
in 40 CFR 81.305), most of the near-term emission reductions are projected to occur in Yolo and Solano counties.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(e) is based largely on SCAQMD's Rule 1125, Metal Container, Closure, and Coil Coating Operations.
Portions of the proposed FIP rule are based on the following: CARB's ``Determination of Reasonably Available Control
Technology and Best Available Retrofit Control Technology (RACT/BARCT) for Metal Container, Closure and Coil Coating
Operations'' (July 21, 1992); San Diego County Air Pollution Control District (SDAPCD) Rule 67.4, Metal Container,
Closure, and Coil Coating Operations (1990); and SMAQMD Rule 452, Can Coating (August 21, 1990).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00078 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
An evaluation of the environmental, economic, socioeconomic, and other impacts of Rule 1125 requirements can be
found in the SCAQMD staff reports for Rule 1125 (February 2, 1979 and October 13, 1989). Evaluations of these impacts
are also presented in CARB's RACT/BARCT Determination for Metal Containers, Metal Closures, and/or Metal Coils.
Both of these evaluations are applicable to this proposed FIP rule.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected
to total 2.09 tpd in 1999 and 2.34 tpd in 2005. Implementation of this proposed rule is projected to reduce those emissions
by 0.26 tpd and 0.30 tpd respectively at a cost savings of $540 per ton of VOC removed (1990 dollars). A cost savings is
projected in part because CARB and the Districts have identified compliant coatings which are less expensive than
high-VOC coatings currently in use.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Commercial Bakeries (Sacramento). Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(f) controls VOC emissions from commercial bakery
ovens in the Sacramento nonattainment area that emit VOCs while baking yeast-leavened products. The proposed rule
requires that certain bakeries install and operate a VOC pollution control system to reduce VOC emissions by at least
95 percent by weight. The following four yeast-leavened dough processes are affected: sponge dough, straight dough,
liquid ferments, and no-time dough.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Facilities with a combined rated heat input capacity of all ovens less than 2 million British Thermal Units (Btus)
per hour and facilities with ovens used exclusively for baking products leavened chemically (without yeast) are
exempt from all of the proposed rule requirements, including recordkeeping and test method provisions. Facilities
which emit less than 6.5 tons of VOC per year are exempt from the standards and compliance schedule requirements, but
still must observe the recordkeeping and test method requirements.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule is based largely on SDCAPCD proposed Rule 67.24 (Bakery Ovens), as presented in Workshop Draft form
on September 17, 1992. Portions of the proposed rule are based on SCAQMD Rule 1153 (Commercial Bakery Ovens) as adopted
on January 4, 1991 and BAAQMD Regulation 8, Rule 42 (Large Commercial Bread Bakeries) as adopted on September 20, 1989.
Evaluations of environmental, economic, socioeconomic, and other impacts of the proposed rule can be found in EPA's
ACT Document for Bakery Oven Emissions (EPA&hyph;453/R&hyph;92&hyph;017, December 1992).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected
to total 1.28 tpd in 1999 and 1.40 tpd in 2005. Implementation of this proposed rule is projected to reduce those emissions
by 1.22 tpd and 1.33 tpd respectively at a cost of $1,200 per ton VOC removed or 0.2 cents per pound of bread (1990 dollars).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(h) Municipal Waste Landfills (Sacramento). Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(g) controls emissions of non-methane organic
compounds (NMOC) from certain municipal solid waste (MSW) landfills in the Sacramento nonattainment area by collecting
and processing the landfill gases evolved from decomposition of organic material in MSW landfills. Emission reductions
are achieved by requiring collection of 90 percent of the landfill gases produced, and destruction of 98 percent of
the NMOCs collected. By controlling NMOCs, VOCs are also controlled because NMOCs include, but are not limited to,
VOCs.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The substantive requirements of the proposed rule apply to MSW landfills that have received more than 500,000 tons
of decomposable solid waste during their operational lifetime. The following types of landfills are exempt from
some or all requirements of this proposed rule: facilities that have accepted only hazardous waste; facilities issued
an Authority to Construct permit from the applicable air quality management district or air pollution control district
prior to April 1, 1995 and that have an NMOC control device in place with at least 90 percent efficiency; and facilities
that demonstrate a low concentration of landfill gas (i.e., less than 500 ppm by volume total organic compounds measured
as methane) at all points near the surface of the landfill.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(g) is based largely on VCAPCD Rule 74.17 (Solid Waste Disposal Sites), as adopted on September
17, 1991. Evaluation of the environmental, economic, socioeconomic, and other impacts of that rule can be found in
VCAPCD's ``Final Staff Report for Rule 74.17,'' dated September 17, 1991.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is in the process of developing ``Standards of Performance for New Stationary Sources and Guidelines for Control
of Existing Sources: Municipal Solid Waste Landfills'' (``MSW Guidelines''). This proposed FIP rule is also based
on work performed in development of the ``MSW Guidelines.'' Upon publication of the ``MSW Guidelines'' in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, it may be appropriate to revise the FIP rule for consistency.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the technical support document, uncontrolled VOC emissions from affected facilities are projected
to total 0.94 tpd in 1999 and 1.08 tpd in 2005. Implementation of this proposed rule is projected to reduce those emissions
by 0.60 tpd and 0.69 tpd respectively at a cost of $18,000 per ton of VOC removed.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) Livestock Waste (Sacramento, South Coast, and Ventura). Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(h) controls VOC emissions from
livestock waste operations. Livestock waste emission sources include cattle, hogs, sheep, and poultry operations.
VOC emissions are created during the anaerobic decomposition of livestock wastes. Although methane emissions are
estimated to make up 70 to 80 percent of the total organic gases (TOG) created, VOC emissions account for approximately
10 percent of the TOG. Livestock waste operations account for approximately 7.7 tpd of VOC emissions in the Sacramento
area, 34.4 tpd in the South Coast,
<FOOTCITE>41</FOOTCITE>
 and 3.8 tpd in Ventura. Although livestock waste operations have traditionally not been the subject of VOC regulation,
they are now under consideration because of the magnitude of livestock waste emissions and the need to achieve reductions
from all FIP area emission VOC sources, most of which have previously reduced or will soon be required to reduce their
VOC emissions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
41
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
These estimates do not include methane and ethane emissions. Because limited information was available regarding
the fraction of poultry operations within Riverside, San Bernardino, and Los Angeles counties which are located
in the South Coast Air Basin, the 34.4 tpd estimate includes the total poultry population found in the three aforementioned
counties. As a result, livestock waste emission estimates may be slightly overestimated for the South Coast Air Basin.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As proposed, the rule would only apply to dairy cattle operations of 400 head or greater. These operations are estimated
to represent approximately 1.8 tpd of VOC emissions in the Sacramento area and 10.8 tpd in the South Coast. Ventura
County currently does not have any dairy operations of greater than 200 head of cattle and, as a result, dairy operations
would not be affected in Ventura. Although other livestock waste operations (e.g., chicken, hogs, sheep facilities)
are not being proposed for regulation at this time, EPA will continue to investigate potential options for reducing
emissions from other livestock waste operations as appropriate.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Emissions from livestock waste are determined by the quantity of manure produced, how it is handled, and the temperature
at which it is handled. The manure management system employed is very important. Wet management systems, such as liquid
or slurry based systems (e.g., lagoons), convert large portions of the carbon to methane and other gases. Dry management
systems, which rely on placing the manure in storage piles, also result in methane and other volatile organic compound
emissions created by the anaerobic conditions within the storage piles. The primary emission reduction strategies
available to reduce VOC emissions include utilizing methane recovery systems and/or promoting enhanced aerobic
conditions in manure storage areas.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00079 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Methods recommended for promoting aerobic decomposition of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
livestock waste include: aerating manure storage piles every 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
three to seven days; scraping feedlots at least three times per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year; and spreading oxidizing agents on feedlots. For waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
management practices using a dry method, the proposed FIP measure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will require the periodic aeration and removal of manure storage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
piles. This measure is estimated to reduce VOC emissions by 25 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Methane recovery and utilization systems are considered a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potential control method for reducing VOC and methane emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Methane is considered an important greenhouse gas and also a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potential energy source. Depending on the size of the livestock 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waste operation, methane recovery systems can pay for themselves 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over time and result in cost savings for large-scale dairy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations. Methane recovery systems are currently operating at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
23 sites in the U.S., including at a swine facility near Tulare, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CA and a dairy near Modesto, CA. Near Chino, CA, a recovery 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system is under development which would process an estimated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2,500 tpd of cattle waste and 600 tpd of chicken waste. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Technologies for methane recovery include covered lagoons, plug 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flow digesters, and complete mix digesters. Although these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recovery systems represent a viable, demonstrated technology, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
some systems have not been as successful as expected because of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improper design or other factors. For these reasons, EPA requests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment and information on any examples, circumstances, and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasons why a previously installed recovery system may not have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieved its full expectations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP rule will require that at least 55 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the manure generated be collected and managed through a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methane recovery system. These systems are estimated to reduce 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions by 80 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The technical and economic feasibility of this technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depends on farm size, climate, and energy process. The off-gas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i.e., methane) collected can be utilized in the dairy operation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for processes such as: electricity production (e.g., to fuel an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine generator); heating (e.g., to fire boilers and/or space 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heaters); and cooling (e.g., to fire chillers or other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
refrigeration equipment). This in turn can create farm profits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from avoided energy costs or sales of excess electricity to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local utility. Although methane recovery systems would require a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
large initial investment, estimated annual costs range from $100 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to $1,300 per year (for a 400 head dairy) to a cost savings of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
$10,000 per year (for a 1,400 head dairy). The initial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investment could be recovered within six to fourteen years, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on dairy size. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The overall emission reductions from livestock waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations will depend on the percentage of waste which is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
managed via the dry method (e.g., placed in storage piles) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compared to the handled through a wet method (e.g., sent to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
covered lagoon or digester). For purposes of the FIP reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimates, it is assumed that a minimum of 55 percent of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
livestock waste would be sent to a lagoon or digester. Assuming 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that a hypothetical dairy farm would install a recovery system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for liquid management and use the dry method for the remaining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fraction of the wastes not sent to the recovery system, overall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected cost per ton of VOC reduced is estimated to range from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
about $2,100 per ton for a 400 head dairy to a $400 (cost) to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
$100 (savings) per ton for a 1,450 head dairy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Preliminary evaluation of the environmental, economic, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other impacts of the proposed rule are discussed in EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technical support document. VOC Emission reductions expected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from this proposed regulation are estimated at 1.0 tpd in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento area and 6.0 tpd in the South Coast. No reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are expected in Ventura County because all livestock waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations are believed to be less than 200 head. Additional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions could be achieved if the requirements were extended to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
smaller livestock waste operations, such as 250 head or greater.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is requesting comments and information on a number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issues related to this proposed measure. Comments are requested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the proposed FIP requirements and/or other potential emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction strategies not addressed in the proposal. Because each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dairy operation is unique and can vary in its waste management 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practices, comments are specifically requested on alternative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reduction methods and/or more cost effective strategies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which might achieve equivalent results to those proposed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also requests comments and information on the number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dairy farms in the FIP areas which contain at least 400 head and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the percentage of dairy farms which manage at least 55 percent of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the livestock waste through a wet method. Because the emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factors used to estimate VOC emissions from these sources should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be updated, EPA intends to explore options for additional testing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to verify that VOC emission factors accurately reflect emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected from both wet and dry waste management methods. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(j)(1) Fugitive Emissions from Oil and Gas Production Facilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and Conveying Stations (Sacramento, South Coast, Ventura).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(t) controls VOC fugitive leak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from valves, flanges, fittings, pumps, compressors, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
open ended lines and/or other components at oil and gas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
production facilities and conveying stations.
<FOOTCITE>42</FOOTCITE>
 1990 VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from these fugitive sources were estimated at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approximately 0.9 tons per day (tpd) in the Sacramento area, 4.4 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tpd in the South Coast, and 3.8 tpd in Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
42
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Conveying stations include any compressor station, metering station, or other transfer station located between
the extraction well and the refinery or gas processing plant.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed rule builds upon current regulations and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strengthens and improves existing rules through retrofits on gas-operated control valves, lower allowable leak
thresholds, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correction of rule deficiencies, and improved inspection and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repair programs. The proposed regulation is based on: CARB RACT 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
guidance for the control of fugitive emissions of VOCs from oil 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and gas production and processing facilities, refineries, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
chemical plants, and pipeline transfer stations; Santa Barbara 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
APCD Rule 331; SCAQMD Rule 1173; and new information regarding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gas-operated control valves. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Currently, gas-operated control valves are not regulated in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP areas. These valves are designed to bleed upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actuation, resulting in the release of uncontrolled VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions. The proposed FIP rule would require that these valves 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be retrofitted using a control valve which would reduce gas bleed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions by an estimated 95 percent. EPA has estimated a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California population of 10,000 gas-operated control valves and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assumed that these valves are evenly distributed throughout the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state. EPA has also assumed that the control valves are only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in natural gas conveyance and petroleum production. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Comments are specifically requested on the number of gas-operated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control valves in the FIP areas and whether these valves are used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in other operations besides those described above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP rule will establish a fugitives regulation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for oil and gas production and conveying stations within the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento FIP area. Previously, these fugitive sources were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unregulated. The majority of expected reductions will be from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Yolo-Solano portion of the FIP area. In the Ventura FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area, the rule builds upon requirements in VCAPCD Rule 74.10 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lower the number of allowable leaking components and leak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition to requirements consistent with existing rules and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB's RACT guidance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00080 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In the South Coast FIP area, the proposed FIP rule will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correct the gas leak definition in SCAQMD Rule 1173. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed correction defines a gas leak as total gaseous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hydrocarbon in excess of the applicable parts per million limit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measured as methane. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the Sacramento FIP area, the proposed FIP rule will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish an inspection and repair program. In the Ventura FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area, the proposed FIP rule will further strengthen the existing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program to make it consistent with those adopted in other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
districts and CARB's RACT guidance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation of the environmental, economic, and other impacts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the proposed rule are discussed in EPA's technical support 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
document. Emission reductions expected from this proposed rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are estimated at 0.8 tpd in the Sacramento area, 2.8 tpd in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast, and 2.1 tpd in Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(j)(2) Fugitive Emissions from Gas Processing Plants, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Refineries, Bulk Plants, Bulk Terminals, and Chemical Plants 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(Sacramento, South Coast, Ventura). Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(i) controls VOC emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fugitive leaks resulting from valves, flanges, fittings, pumps, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compressors, open ended lines and/or other components at gas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
processing plants, refineries, bulk plants, bulk terminals, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
chemical plants. 1990 VOC emissions from these fugitive sources 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were estimated to be less than 0.9 tons per day (tpd) in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento area, approximately 17.5 tpd in the South Coast, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.8 tpd in Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule builds upon current or proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations and strengthens and improves existing rules through: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation of additional fugitive sources; lower allowable leak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thresholds; correction of rule deficiencies; and improved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection and repair programs. The proposed FIP regulation is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on: CARB RACT guidance for the control of fugitive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions of VOCs from oil and gas production and processing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities, refineries, chemical plants, and pipeline transfer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stations; Bay Area AQMD (BAAQMD) Regulation 9_Rules 18, 22, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
25; SCAQMD Rule 1173; and EPA's proposed National Emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Standards for Organic Hazardous Air Pollutants (HON) from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
synthetic organic chemical manufacturing industry and equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
leaks from seven other processes (FR 52 62685).
<FOOTCITE>43</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
43
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Although EPA's proposed HON is expected to be finalized soon, the proposed HON covers only emissions designated as
hazardous air pollutants, which may make up only a fraction of VOC emissions from affected FIP sources. The proposed
FIP fugitives rule is intended to reduce VOC emissions. In some instances, the proposed HON is less stringent than
adopted SIP regulations in California and the proposed FIP measure. Although the proposed HON was considered, EPA's
proposed fugitives rule relies primarily on current California regulations and/or guidance. After the HON is finalized,
EPA will clarify the overlap between existing SIP and/or proposed FIP rules and the proposed HON.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed rule will establish and/or lower allowable leak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
levels for affected components. Allowable leak levels for valves 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and connectors are proposed at 500 parts per million based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
BAAQMD regulations
<FOOTCITE>44</FOOTCITE>
 and EPA's proposed HON. Allowable leak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
levels for other components are proposed at levels based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB's RACT guidance and SCAQMD Rule 1173. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
44
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
On January 1, 1997, BAAQMD Regulation 8, Rules 18 and 25 reduce allowable leak levels to 100 ppm for valves and connectors
and 500 ppm for pumps and compressors. Although these lower limits are not being proposed as FIP limits at this time,
EPA will continue to investigate the need for and potential reductions from leak thresholds consistent with the BAAQMD
regulations.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In all three FIP areas, the proposed measure will regulate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fugitives from bulk plants and bulk terminals. Because only a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portion of these sources have traditionally been regulated for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their fugitive emissions (i.e., loading and storage of petroleum 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liquids), fugitive emissions may have been underestimated in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
previous inventory projections. Because emission factors were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not readily available for these sources, emission estimates were 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
made using fugitive emission factors for refineries. Preliminary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analyses have determined that bulk plant and bulk terminal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions may account for as much as an additional 2.9 tpd in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento area, 9.3 tpd in the South Coast, and 1.7 tpd in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura. Potential reductions from these sources alone would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2.2 tpd in the Sacramento area, 4.1 tpd in the South Coast, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.4 tpd in Ventura. EPA requests comments and information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regarding appropriate emission factors to use for fugitive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from bulk plants and bulk terminals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP rule will correct the gas leak definition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in South Coast AQMD Rule 1173. The proposed correction defines a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gas leak as total gaseous hydrocarbon in excess of the applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
parts per million limit measured as methane. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Evaluation of the environmental, economic, and other impacts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the proposed rule are discussed in EPA's technical support 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
document. Emission reductions expected from this proposed rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are estimated to be 0.7 tpd in the Sacramento area, 3.1 tpd in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast, and 0.7 tpd in Ventura. Fugitive emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions from bulk plants and bulk terminals are not included 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the above estimates. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(k) Service Stations (Sacramento, Ventura, South Coast). Proposed rule 40 CFR 52.2961(j) reduces VOC emissions
from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gasoline dispensing facilities. Gasoline service stations are a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source of VOC emissions created during vehicle refueling and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
storage tank working/breathing losses. Service station VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions are estimated at approximately 3.2 tpd in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento area, 25.3 tpd in the South Coast, and 1.2 tpd in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura. Although service stations in the FIP areas currently 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have vapor recovery systems, the proposed FIP rule builds upon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current Phase I and Phase II regulations and strengthens and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improves existing rules by requiring pressure/vacuum relief 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
valves on open vent pipes and the phasing out of inefficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vapor recovery system components. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Pressure-vacuum relief valves are expected to virtually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eliminate breathing and working losses from the storage tank vent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pipe. Pressure-vacuum relief valves cost less than fifty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dollars, are easily installed without underground construction, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and improve efficiency of existing vapor recovery systems by one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to three percent. The pressure/vacuum relief valves typically 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pay for themselves within less than one year and result in a cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
savings. Additional emission reductions will be achieved through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
elimination of exemptions and the replacement of remote check 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
valves in Phase II control systems. The Phase I efficiency is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected to be increased through the combined impact of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements for poppetted drybreaks in the Phase I vapor control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
systems, the installation of the pressure-vacuum valve on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vent pipes, and the installation of CARB certified spill boxes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Phase II efficiency is expected to be increased through the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combined impact of using proper tubing between the riser and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dispenser cabinet, requiring a certified insertion interlock 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mechanism on all bellows-equipped nozzles, and replacing non- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
coaxial hose with coaxial hose. Many of the proposed revisions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are based on recent amendments to BAAQMD Regulation 8, Rule 7_
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Gasoline Dispensing Facilities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Emission reductions expected from this proposed rule are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated at 1.3 tpd in the Sacramento area, 8.9 tpd in the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast, and 0.3 tpd in Ventura. Because fuel savings result from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
installation of the pressure/vacuum relief valves and because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inefficient vapor recovery components wear out and can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replaced by more efficient components during regularly scheduled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance, the cost impacts of the proposed measure will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimized. The overall cost effectiveness is estimated at $1,600 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per ton of VOC reduced. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(l) Waste Burning (Sacramento, South Coast, and Ventura). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(k) controls VOC emissions from waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
burning. Waste burning activities are defined as agricultural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
burning, range management burning, forest management burning, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
open burning. 1990 waste burning emissions
<FOOTCITE>45</FOOTCITE>
 were estimated at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3.8 tpd in the Sacramento FIP area, 0.7 tpd in the South Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP area, and less than 0.1 in the Ventura FIP area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
45
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
These estimates do not include emissions from incineration and unplanned fires (e.g., structural fires and wildfires).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Most waste burning activities are currently restricted by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permissive burn/no-burn day program as specified in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California Health and Safety Code Regulations. This program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allows the local air district office to declare a permissive burn 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
day or a no-burn day, requires that subject sources obtain a burn 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permit from the local designated county or state agency, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limits burning to permissive burn days. Waste burning is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restricted to days with acceptable air quality based largely on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current and forecasted visibility and particulate levels. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP rule would complement and expand the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current permissive burn/no-burn day program to incorporate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ambient ozone air quality considerations. The purpose of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposal is not to ban waste burning but to restrict waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
burning to days when ambient ozone concentrations are within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acceptable levels. On days predicted to exceed the California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ambient air quality standard for ozone (0.09 ppm), all forms of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waste burning would be prohibited. EPA is currently 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
investigating the methodologies used to predict ozone exceedances 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and no-burn days. These methodologies could be used to predict 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air quality conditions using current and forecasted weather, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meteorology, ozone levels, and other relevant criteria. A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
similar forecasting strategy would be used as part of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP waste burning rule. SMAQMD has a voluntary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permissive burn/no-burn program which takes into account 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
predicted high ambient ozone levels. VCAPCD restricts burning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
when the California ozone standard of 0.09 ppm is expected to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeded. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although not contained within the proposed FIP rule, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expects to establish a notification system compatible with and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
similar to those already in place for current permissive burn 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
day/no-burn day programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because poor visibility or high particulate levels can also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
result during conditions conducive to high ambient ozone levels, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
there is expected to be some overlap between current no-burn days 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and any additional no-burn days resulting from the proposed FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. During 1990 no-burn days were declared on 16 days in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Sacramento FIP area, 68 days in the South Coast FIP area, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
311 days in the Ventura FIP area. Using 1990 air quality data, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed rule is expected to restrict burning on an estimated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional 45 days per year (dpy) in the Sacramento FIP area, 121 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dpy in the South Coast FIP area, 3 dpy in the Ventura FIP area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In the Sacramento area, the largest impact would occur on days 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during July through September when ozone levels typically reach 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their highest levels. In the South Coast, the largest impact 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would occur on high ozone days during March through October. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because of Ventura's current policy on waste burning, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposal would have minimal impact. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
