<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Open burning operations at single and two-family dwellings 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are currently exempt from most permissive burn/no-burn day 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs. Although EPA is not proposing that these sources be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to obtain a burn permit, the proposed FIP regulation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would also require that this type of burning activity be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restricted to permissive burn days. Comments are requested on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the impacts and issues resulting from including these sources in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed FIP regulation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The cost resulting from the lost opportunity to burn waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the additional no-burn days would be minimized by allowing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources to burn on days when exceedances are not predicted. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the case of Sacramento, the proposed rule is expected to allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waste burning during over 80 percent of the calendar days each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. However, in the South Coast, where the state ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard is exceeded roughly 50 percent of the calendar days each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year, the proposed rule would restrict burning to approximately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
50 percent of the calendar days per year. The impact of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP measure in Ventura is expected to be minimal since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the district has already instituted a policy of restricting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
burning when the state standard is predicted to be exceeded. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Comments are requested on the feasibility of this strategy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the FIP areas, especially for the South Coast FIP area. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also requests information on potential costs resulting from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP measure. Comments are also requested on alternative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies (e.g., soil incorporation, residue removal, after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
market disposal alternatives) which will reduce waste burning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impacts on ambient ozone air quality. EPA seeks comments on ways 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to avoid any increased costs and increased pesticide use that may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be associated with the rule. Comments are requested on potential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC reductions resulting from implementation of the Connelly-Areias-Chandler Rice Straw Burning Reduction Act
of 1991 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(California Health &amp; Safety Code, Part 4, Chapter 3, Article 3, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 41865). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Comments are requested on the necessity of exemptions in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP measure. For example, California district offices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may exempt a source from no burn day prohibitions during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
circumstances of imminent and substantial economic loss. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, these terms are undefined. Comments are requested on an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate definition for the aforementioned terms. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is also requesting comment on an alternative to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aforementioned strategy. As an alternative, a waste burning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measure could be designed to only allow waste burning during the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period of the year when ozone levels are within the state ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard, typically November through February depending on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area. This alternative would supplement the current no-burn day 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program and prohibit burning during the ozone season. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approach would provide greater certainty to affected parties so 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
they could adequately plan their burn schedules; however, it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would also provide less flexibility by prohibiting burning on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
days expected to be within ozone standard. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While EPA is continuing to evaluate the emissions inventory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and potential reductions from the proposed FIP measure, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preliminary emission reduction estimates from the 1990 baseline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are 1.8 to 3.6 tpd in the Sacramento area, 0.3 to 0.5 tpd in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast FIP area, and less than 0.1 tpd in the Ventura FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area. Actual reductions will depend on the overall effectiveness 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the FIP measure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(m) Residential Water Heaters (Sacramento). Proposed 40 CFR 52.2961(l) controls NO
