<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
c. Consumer Products.
<FOOTCITE>51</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) FIP Promulgation of Existing CARB Rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2957(a) and (b) control VOC emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from consumer products and antiperspirants and deodorants. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP measures reflect previously adopted CARB limits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because CARB has not submitted the measures as SIP revisions, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is promulgating these measures, which are needed for progress and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment in the three FIP areas. These measures would also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide the framework for additional future FIP reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies as needed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
51
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
As specified under section 183(e)(3) of the CAAA of 1990, EPA is required to study emissions of VOCs from consumer and
commercial products and to investigate the development of a national regulation or CTG for selected consumer products.
While in all likelihood EPA will be developing a national regulation or CTG for many of the consumer products covered
by the proposed FIP regulations, the proposed FIP regulation is not an attempt on EPA's part to meet its 183(e)(3) responsibilities.
When and if EPA promulgates a national regulation or CTG for consumer products, EPA will reevaluate its FIP strategy
for consumer products.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(a) Consumer Products. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2957(a) controls VOC emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer products, with the exception of antiperspirants, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deodorants, aerosol paints, and a few other consumer product 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories.
<FOOTCITE>52</FOOTCITE>
 Consumer products are chemical formulations used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in households and institutions and includes products such as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
detergents, cleaning compounds, polishes, floor finishes, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personal care products, disinfectants, and automotive specialty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products. The proposed regulation limits the VOC content in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products sold, supplied, offered for sale, or manufactured for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use within California. The rule establishes VOC limits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent with limits previously adopted by CARB. 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Statewide emissions from consumer products are estimated at 250 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tpd, 14.2 tpd in Sacramento, 108 tpd in South Coast, and 6 tpd in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
52
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
While CARB has attempted to regulate most consumer products, some products have not been regulated at this time. These
include: lubricants, hand washing detergents, toilet bowl cleaners, disinfectants, paint strippers, laundry
detergents, and herbicides. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The CARB and proposed FIP regulations for consumer products, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including antiperspirants and deodorants, cover just over half of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the consumer products in the inventory or approximately 130 tpd 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statewide. The CARB and proposed FIP consumer product rules are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated to achieve a VOC emission reduction of over 25 percent. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, these reductions will be partially offset by growth in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer product sales. The FIP area reductions after growth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were estimated at 1.3 tpd (1999) and -1.6 tpd (increase in 2005) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Sacramento, 1.6 tpd in South Coast, 0.2 tpd in Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because the FIP consumer product requirements are almost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identical to the CARB rules, the cost of the consumer products 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP measure is expected to be zero. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although the proposed FIP measure is very similar to that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
previously adopted by CARB, minor differences exist between the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP regulation and CARB's regulation. Differences are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intended to simplify or reduce the administrative burden on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected manufacturers or to correct rule deficiencies. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP consumer products rule differs from the CARB rule in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that it deletes: requirements for certification of charcoal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lighter materials; a variance section; and a federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforceability section. Otherwise the rules are virtually 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identical. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Antiperspirants and Deodorants. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2957(b) controls VOC emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
antiperspirants and deodorants. The regulation limits the VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
content of products sold, supplied, offered for sale, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufactured for use within California. The rule establishes VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limits based on those previously adopted by CARB. The rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishes a percent VOC by weight limit for both aerosol and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
non-aerosol products (e.g., sticks and roll-ons). Although 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aerosols make up approximately 25 percent of the market, they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
account for approximately 90 percent of the emissions from this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
category. Statewide emissions and expected reductions are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporated in the consumer product totals described under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.4.c.(1).(a). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although the proposed FIP measure is very similar to that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
previously adopted by CARB, minor differences exist between the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP regulation and CARB's regulation. Differences 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
between the proposed FIP antiperspirant and deodorant rule and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the CARB rule include: the compliance date
<FOOTCITE>53</FOOTCITE>
 for aerosol 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products has been extended to January 1, 1999; deletion of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administrative requirements that aerosol product manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apply to CARB by January, 1994 for a compliance date extension to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999; deletion of the section restricting toxic air contaminants; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deletion of certain reporting requirements; deletion of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variance section; and deletion of the federal enforceability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section. Otherwise the rules are virtually identical. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
53
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
CARB's regulation allows manufacturers to apply for the January 1, 1999 compliance date if certain requirements
are met. Based on conversations with CARB, EPA believes that most if not all aerosol product manufacturers have or
will apply for the 1999 compliance date.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(2) Aerosol Paints. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2958 reduces VOC emissions from aerosol 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
coatings. Aerosol coatings are self dispensing, pressurized 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products containing coating solids (i.e., pigments, resins, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
binders) and solvent designed to dispense product ingredients by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
means of a propellant. The solvent and propellant are typically 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOCs which make up from 70&hyph;95 percent of the product by weight. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The VOCs are emitted into the atmosphere during the coating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delivery and curing process. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Aerosol spray paints account for approximately 25 tpd of VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions in California. FIP area 1990 emissions were estimated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at 1.3 tpd in the Sacramento area, 11.6 tpd in the South Coast, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 0.6 tpd in Ventura. The use of aerosol paints has grown 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
steadily since the 1950s. Growth peaked in 1977, but in 1978, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the federal government restricted the use of chlorofluorocarbons 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as aerosol coating propellants and hydrocarbons were introduced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as a replacement for the CFC propellants. Since the mid 1980s, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use of aerosol paints has continued to steadily grow, with annual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sales reaching historical highs during the early 1990s. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Aerosol coatings have previously been regulated by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
BAAQMD (Regulation 8, Rule 49) and the SCAQMD (Rule 1129). CARB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recently assumed authority to regulate the VOC content of aerosol 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paints. The FIP aerosol paint rule is based on a draft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation workshopped by CARB on November 10, 1993. The draft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation is not scheduled for adoption until after EPA's court 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ordered deadline for the FIP proposal. In addition, current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State law giving CARB the authority to regulate aerosol coatings 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also limits CARB's ability to submit some future-effective limits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and reductions to EPA as SIP submittals. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because of the need to demonstrate attainment in each of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP areas, EPA is proposing an aerosol coating rule which is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generally consistent with the regulation expected to be adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by CARB in 1994. Minor differences exist between the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposal and the CARB draft, however. The proposed FIP aerosol 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paint rule does not include sections for a special recognition 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
label, variances, and federal enforceability. Also, the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP measure does not include a provision for a five year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance date extension as found in CARB's draft rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Instead, EPA intends to monitor industries progress and CARB's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999 determination regarding the coating limits. If necessary, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will consider amendments to the FIP aerosol coating rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
where appropriate. By adopting the FIP aerosol coating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation, EPA hopes to avoid having to impose more stringent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements on other VOC sources which would need to compensate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the reduction shortfall pending SIP submittal of the adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB regulation. While this proposed FIP aerosol coating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation will be published prior to CARB's adoption of its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation, EPA expects to modify the FIP regulation, if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate, to be consistent with the final CARB regulation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP regulation will build upon the previous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
work by the BAAQMD and the SCAQMD and closely parallel CARB's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation. The proposed FIP regulation would apply to any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
person who sells, supplies, offers for sale, applies, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufactures for use in California any aerosol coating products 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as described in the proposed regulation. Because of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diversity of uses and coating types, the proposed regulation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishes VOC limits for a variety of product coating types. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The initial 1996 limits are based on limits in the BAAQMD 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation. A second phase of lower limits goes into effect in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00088 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Aerosol coating manufacturers are expected to develop 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliant products through propellant replacement, product 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reformulation, and improvements in packaging and delivery 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
systems. Two promising propellent alternatives, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hydrofluorocarbon (HCFC) 152a and dimethyl ether, are potential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
replacements for current hydrocarbon propellants. Reformulation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to a lower VOC content could be achieved through increased 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solids, lower VOC solvents and propellants, or a combination of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
both. A further opportunity to reduce emissions lies in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
redesign of the packaging and delivery system such that a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonhydrocarbon gas or mechanical device acts to displace the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
container's contents. Improvements in the transfer efficiency of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the delivery systems would result in reduced overspray, higher 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surface coating percentage per container, and cost savings to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP aerosol coating regulation is to reduce VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission by approximately 60 percent from 1990 levels. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions from 1990 levels equate to approximately 15 tpd in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California. FIP area attainment year reductions are estimated at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.9 tpd in 1999 and 1.1 tpd in 2005 in the Sacramento area, 8.9 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tpd in 2010 for the South Coast, and 0.4 tpd in 2005 for Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Only limited data is available to estimate the cost of developing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliant products. The first phase of the regulation is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected to have a minimal cost because compliant products are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
already available to meet the BAAQMD rule. Costs of meeting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1999 limits will depend in part on the availability of HCFC 152a. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although a promising propellant substitute, HCFC 152a is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated to cost five to ten times more than current hydrocarbon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
propellants. However, with increased demand for HCFC 152a, its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost is expected to decrease. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Alternative Reduction Approaches. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB is currently developing an alternative compliance plan 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ACP) approach which would allow additional flexibility for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected consumer product manufacturers. The ACP would allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers to average a limited number of products when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining compliance. The ACP concept is under development and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is working with CARB to assure its consistency with EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Economic Incentive Program guidance. After the ACP program is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopted by CARB, EPA will consider it for adoption as part of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP. Additional alternative reduction approaches as described in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.4. for architectural coatings could also be applied to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer products. Comments are requested on the use of these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approaches to achieve additional emission reductions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer products. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Need for Additional Reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because additional reductions from consumer products will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
needed to attain the ozone standard in the South Coast, EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to investigate strategies such as setting VOC limits, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fees, a manufacturers bubble, or a corporate average to further 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
encourage reductions from these products. Because a fraction of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the consumer products inventory (e.g., laundry detergents, hand 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
washing detergents, lubricants) are not regulated by current CARB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations or the FIP proposal, EPA will investigate strategies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for reducing emission resulting from these unregulated products. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA intends to use section 182(e)(5) commitments for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional reductions and to develop appropriate control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies based on continuing evaluation of new technologies and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance options. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
d. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Pesticides
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Summary and Applicability. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Proposed 40 CFR 52.2960 is designed to reduce VOC emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from agricultural and structural pesticide application. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00089 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Producers of such pesticides must determine the VOC content
<FOOTCITE>54</FOOTCITE>
 of their products as defined by an analytical method. All agricultural and structural pesticides registered in California
must be tested, regardless of whether the producer is located inside or outside of the State. Distribution, application,
and storage of pesticides with high VOC contents will be restricted in California.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
54
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The actual volatility of the organic compounds in a particular pesticide depends on the temperature, moisture, substrate,
application technique, and other conditions under which it is applied. Therefore, ``VOC emission potential'' might
more accurately reflect the result of the analytical test methods. EPA, however, is using the term ``VOC content''
to be consistent with analogous coating regulations.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
9,500 pesticide formulations may be subject to the analysis requirement, and sale and use of several thousand may
be subsequently restricted. EPA cannot determine how industry will respond to restrictions on each specific formulation.
Generally, however, EPA expects that low-VOC reformulations will provide feasible alternatives for many restricted
pesticides.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed rule is not intended to apply to consumer-oriented pesticides that are subject to consumer products
regulations in this NPRM (40 CFR 52.2957) or in the California Code of Regulations (Title 17, Division 3, Subchapter
8.5_Consumer Products, &sect;&sect;94507&hyph;94517). The proposed rule is also not intended to apply to production
or distribution of pesticides intended for use outside of California.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Specific provisions. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Required submission of data.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 By June 1, 1996 or 120 days after the effective date of the rule, whichever is later, producers must submit analyses
identifying the VOC content of all affected pesticides. EPA believes that 120 days is ample time to perform this analysis,
and notes that the requirement is being proposed over two years before the analysis will come due. Concern has been
raised that independent laboratory capacity may be insufficient to analyze all regulated pesticides within 120
days. EPA believes, however, that most large pesticide manufacturers will perform the required analysis in-house,
and that independent laboratory capacity will be sufficient to handle the remaining analytic demand.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Analysis is required of all California-registered pesticides including those not currently in use. This information
is needed to determine an accurate base-year VOC emission inventory to compare against future emission reductions.
It is also important to identify all available low-VOC pesticides regardless of whether they are currently in use
in order to maximize the available pest-control options.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Establish VOC limit.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The proposed rule describes a six-step procedure for establishing a VOC limit for pesticides.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) EPA will establish a base-year pesticide use inventory for each pesticide registered in California. This inventory
may consist simply of the amount of each pesticide applied in the State according to the 1990 pesticide use report (PUR)
combined with the inerts data-base, both of which are maintained by the California Department of Pesticide Regulation
(DPR). The proposed rule is drafted, however, to provide the Agency discretion to establish the base-year inventory
using other information. EPA could, for example, use an average of the 1990 to 1993 PUR data for just the three FIP areas
if that was determined to be more appropriate. In any case, EPA intends to discuss the base-year inventory actually
used when promulgating the VOC limit pursuant to &sect;52.288(c)(2)(vi).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ii) EPA will rank the pesticides according to their VOC content. The pesticide with the largest VOC content (i.e.,
a gas or highly volatile liquid) will be ranked 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=6 f=1 -->
#
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
1. If EPA determines that adequate data for a particular pesticide is not submitted by the deadline, EPA may assign
the pesticide a VOC content equal to the largest VOC content reported for any pesticide (i.e., also ranking it 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=6 f=1 -->
#
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
1). This would effectively prohibit distribution and application of this pesticide in the affected area pursuant
to &sect;52.2960(c)(5). EPA's intention is to provide an incentive for manufacturers to submit the required data
while simultaneously enabling the Agency to implement the regulation in the absence of some data. Nevertheless,
the regulation is drafted to provide the Agency some discretion in assigning VOC contents for pesticides in the absence
of adequate data. EPA might, for example, determine it more appropriate to assign VOC contents for such a pesticide
based on the largest VOC content reported for its formulation type (e.g., oil, emulsifiable concentrate, pressurized
liquid, etc.) Or, if analysis of a pesticide failed to demonstrate a specific VOC content but did demonstrate that
the VOC content fell within a discrete range, EPA might assign the pesticide the largest VOC content within that range.
In any case, EPA intends to discuss the VOC contents actually assigned when promulgating the VOC limit pursuant to
&sect;52.288(c)(2)(vi).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(iii) EPA will multiply the VOC contents (weight percent basis) by the base-year inventory (kilogram-per-year basis)
to establish the individual base-year VOC emissions (kilogram-per-year basis). A pesticide not used in the affected
area in the base-year(s) would have a base-year inventory and a VOC emission of zero.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(iv) EPA will add the base-year VOC emissions of all affected pesticides to determine the total base-year VOC emissions
in the affected area.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(v) Starting with the highest-ranking pesticides (i.e., those with the largest VOC content and those without adequate
data), EPA will add the individual base-year VOC emissions until the sum equals 40 to 65 percent of the total base-year
VOC emissions. The VOC content of the last pesticide needed to achieve 40 to 65 percent is the VOC limit. EPA has assumed
that restricted pesticides will be replaced on a one to one mass basis with pesticides at the VOC limit.
<FOOTCITE>55</FOOTCITE>
 Based on an analysis described in the technical support document (TSD) for the proposed rule, EPA has calculated that
a 40 to 65 percent threshold value will achieve emission reductions of 20 to 45 percent off the base-year inventory.
The target reduction of 20 to 45 percent was chosen for consistency with the range of target reductions of the stationary
source cap rules described in section III.C.5 of this NPRM. Based on planned modeling and inventory analysis and comments
received during the comment period, EPA hopes to select a specific reduction target from within this range for use
in the final rule promulgation. The relationship between the 20&hyph;45 percent reduction target and the 40&hyph;65
percent threshold value is discussed further in the TSD.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
55
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Imbedded in the one-to-one replacement assumption is another assumption that, in the aggregate, pesticide efficacy
is not reduced by reformulation to lower VOC products. For some specific pesticides, efficacy may decrease and applicators
may respond by using greater quantities of the reformulated product. Emissions associated with this phenomenon
will be somewhat offset by replacement of specific restricted products by pesticides with VOC contents below the
VOC content limit.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(vi) EPA will publish the VOC limit. EPA intends to publish this limit in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 with a discussion of the data and assumptions used to generate the limit. If the limit is generated by a straightforward
execution of the procedure described in this NPRM, EPA may publish the limit directly in final form. If, however, EPA
exercises significant discretion (as provided for in the proposed rule) in generating the limit, EPA intends to take
public comment on a proposed limit before publishing the final limit.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Restrictions on pesticides.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 One year after EPA publishes the VOC limit, all persons are prohibited from distributing in the affected area pesticides
with VOC contents greater than the VOC limit. After that time, distributors with remaining inventory of high-VOC
pesticides could still sell them to persons outside the affected area.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00090 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Two years after EPA publishes the VOC limit, all persons are prohibited from applying or storing in the affected area
pesticides with VOC contents greater than the VOC limit. EPA believes this is sufficient time for affected applicators
to apply existing inventory of high-VOC pesticides or to sell them to persons outside the affected area.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Revisions to the VOC limit.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 EPA may require a producer to reanalyze its pesticides at any time. Based on this or other information, EPA may recalculate
the VOC limit at any time. EPA does not anticipate recalculating the VOC limit frequently, as recalculation has significant
resource implications for both the Agency and the regulated community. Recalculation may be appropriate, however,
if EPA determines that the existing VOC limit is not equitably achieving the targeted emission reductions of 20 to
45 percent. Developments that might justify a recalculation include changes in pesticide use patterns and improved
understanding of VOC emissions from pesticides.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Alternative ranking scheme.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Rather than ranking pesticides simply by VOC content, representatives of DPR and CARB have suggested ranking by VOC
emissions. Under the former scheme, as incorporated in this NPRM, application of a high-VOC low-use pesticide (e.g.,
a pheromone) would be regulated even if it contributed much fewer VOC emissions than unregulated application of a
medium-VOC high-use product. Under the latter scheme, pesticides would be ranked and regulated in order of their
overall emissions, as determined by VOC content times quantity applied in the base-year(s).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While the two ranking systems could provide equivalent emission reductions, EPA believes the simplicity of the former
makes it easier to implement and enforce. Once the VOC limit is established, Agency inspectors can simply determine
whether pesticides sold and used in the affected area comply with the limit. Under the latter scheme, however, rather
than publishing a single limit, EPA would need to publish and regulate a list of all the specific restricted pesticides,
which would need to be revised regularly to account for new and reformulated products and for changing use patterns.
If, for example, a high-VOC pesticide had low use and low emissions in the base-year but high use and high emissions
thereafter, it would need to be added to the list of restricted pesticides. Conversely, pesticides with unusually
high use in the base-year might need to be removed from the list of restricted pesticides, adding to the likelihood
of inequities and compliance problems.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Nevertheless, EPA understands that alternatives to the regulatory structure contained in the proposed FIP rule
offer different trade-offs between the various FIP goals (described in section I.B of this NPRM) of reducing emissions,
encouraging State action, minimizing federal interference, etc. EPA has attempted to address some of these alternatives
in paragraph III.C.4.d(4) of this NPRM.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Reporting, recordkeeping and test methods. Proposed &sect;52.2960(f) describes the test methods required
for determining the VOC content of pesticides. Pesticide producers may use one of two test methods:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA).
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This is one of the two test methods described in EPA's alternative control technology document (ACT) for pesticides
(``Alternative Control Technology Document; Control of VOC Emissions from the Application of Agricultural Pesticides,''
EPA&hyph;453/R&hyph;92&hyph;011, March 1993, Appendix C). The method is based on the ``American Standard Test
Method (ASTM) for Compositional Analysis by Thermogravimetry'' (ASTM E&hyph;1131&hyph;86).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ii) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Volatile organics in pesticides (VOP) method.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 This is the second test method described in EPA's ACT for pesticides. It includes a purge and trap procedure conducted
at constant temperature that allows for direct measurement of water content.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DPR has investigated various modifications to ASTM E&hyph;1131&hyph;86 for analyzing a wide variety of pesticide
formulations. Some of DPR's work is reflected in modifications to the method included in the ACT and in &sect;52.2960(f)(1)(i)
by reference. DPR's work is ongoing, however, and EPA expects that additional modifications to the method may be determined
appropriate. Depending on the timing and scope of such modifications, EPA may address them through the discretion
allowed in the proposed rule, by incorporating them into the final FIP rule, or by formally proposing and promulgating
subsequent rule modifications. Modifications may include the following:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00091 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(i) Modifications to the method for estimating water 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
content. DPR is currently investigating several analytical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methods for estimating water content including both Karl Fischer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
titration and spectroscopic analysis of the vapor-phase effluent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the TGA analysis. Until adequate validation of these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methods is available, DPR may calculate water content based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ``percentage water added'' component of the statement of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formulation required pursuant to FIFRA (7 U.S.C. 136a(c)(1)(E)). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because this generally does not account for water present as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impurity, water produced in reaction, or water present as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
component of other raw materials, it should provide a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conservative (i.e., low) estimate of true water content. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reliance on formulation data is consistent with EPA's historic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
policy regarding analogous coating regulations before reliable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test methods (e.g., ASTM D&hyph;4017 and D&hyph;3792) were available.
<FOOTCITE>56</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
56
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
See, for example, ``Procedure for Certifying Quantity of Volatile Organic Compounds Emitted by Paint, Ink, and Other
Coatings, ``U.S. EPA, EPA&hyph;450/3&hyph;84&hyph;019, December 1984.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(ii) Modifications to the method for estimating exempt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compounds. Again, until adequate validation of a specific test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
method(s) is available, DPR may rely on the statement of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formulation for estimating the amount of exempt compounds present 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in specific pesticides. This is also analogous to coating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations which generally relied on formulation data until ASTM 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
D&hyph;4457 was available.
<FOOTCITE>57</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
57
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
ibid.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(iii) Modifications to address solid pesticide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
formulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in &sect;52.2960 (d) and (e), producers of affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pesticides must submit copies of all raw data and a summary of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the results of the VOC content analyses. Distributors must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submit a distributor notification and an annual report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
summarizing the pesticides distributed in the affected area 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the preceding year. Applicators must submit a copy of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
PUR for all pesticide applications within the affected area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Pesticide producers, distributors, and applicators are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to maintain all information necessary to demonstrate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance with the regulation for a period of five years. At a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimum, this information must include copies of all reports 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specifically required in paragraph 52.2960(d), and any technical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
support to these reports. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Relationship to State rules. As discussed in sections I.D., III.J., and elsewhere in this NPRM, EPA believes that
State and local agencies can adopt and implement regulations that are better tailored to the specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
characteristics and needs of the affected areas. Therefore, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strongly encourages the State to continue ongoing efforts to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopt approvable rules to replace all or part of the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Pursuant to &sect;110(l) of the Act, State rules are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approvable if they, ``*&blank;*&blank;* interfere with any applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement concerning attainment and reasonable further 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progress *&blank;*&blank;* or any other applicable requirement.'' For purposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the pesticide rule, EPA intends to determine approvability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based largely on emissions. Modifications to the pesticide rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which EPA may consider approvable, if shown to result in no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant emission increases, include the following: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i) Provisions for exempting de minimis use of high-VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pesticides. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ii) Provisions for allowing use of high-VOC pesticides to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevent overwhelming economic dislocation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(iii) Provisions for allowing use of high VOC pesticides to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevent harm to human health or the environment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(iv) Provisions for allowing use of high-VOC pesticides 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during times of the year when low ambient ozone concentrations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are projected. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(v) Modifications of the test method discussed in section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
III.C.3.d(3) of this NPRM. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(vi) Modifications to 40 CFR 52.2960(c)(2) of the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule, such as ranking pesticides based on emissions instead of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC content as discussed in section III.C.3.d(2) of this NPRM. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(vii) Consideration of the relative reactivity of different 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOCs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Or, of course, the State could adopt rules based on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
completely different regulatory strategies that still achieve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equivalent emission reductions.
<FOOTCITE>58</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
58
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA, for example, considered several other regulatory strategies for inclusion in the FIP, and analyzed two in detail
through a contract to Midwest Research Institute (``Development of Pesticide Rules for the Sacramento, South Coast,
and Ventura Nonattainment Area FIPs,'' prepared by Midwest Research Institute for EPA, April 30, 1993 draft final
report).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The no-use day strategy was fashioned after existing no-burn day programs. EPA would establish meteorologic criteria
for predicting unfavorable ozone conditions and pesticide use would be restricted on a day-to-day basis. A major
drawback to this strategy is that postponing pesticide application for several consecutive days could result in
significant crop damage and an increase in overall annual pesticide use.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The users bubble strategy would be somewhat analogous to the stationary source emission cap programs proposed in
this notice. Pesticide users (e.g., growers) would calculate a base-year VOC emission rate as: amount of pesticide
applied times VOC content of pesticides divided by total acreage. Users would then be required to reduce their VOC
emission rate a certain percentage by the attainment year. A major drawback to this strategy is the difficulty in establishing
equitable baseline inventories.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA selected the VOC content strategy incorporated in this notice for a variety of reasons including better enforceability,
consistency with State regulatory plans, and minimization of disruption to the affected industries. EPA invites
comment on mechanisms to improve the proposed pesticides rule, as well as on other strategies to reduce VOC emissions
from pesticides. EPA is interested, for example, in mechanisms to encourage pesticide application technologies
(e.g., electrostatic application) that decrease VOC emissions. In addition, EPA requests comment on how to optimize
the interaction between the proposed FIP rule and ongoing pesticide programs and initiatives including those targeting
pesticide use reduction, pollution prevention, and integrated pest management. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
