 emissions similar to those proposed for the Ventura FIP area.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The FIP cap measures proposed today differ in several ways 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from those proposed in the 1990 South Coast FIP (55 FR 36458). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Most importantly, the current FIP proposal adds complete 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations for most categories of industrial sources of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions, whereas the 1990 proposal dealt only with VOC sources. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 cap regulations proposed today for Ventura (and discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as an alternative for the South Coast) contain comprehensive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control requirements, including specific detailed provisions for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source monitoring. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's cap regulations proposed today also make significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
changes from the 1990 rules in terms of the cap compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period, penalties for noncompliance with emission limits, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishment of emissions baselines. In addition, this NPRM 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly revises the discussion of two potential amendments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the cap program: the development of an emissions trading 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
element and the replacement of the industrial solvents/coatings 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules with a manufacturers bubble. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Many of these changes from the 1990 proposal derive from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extensive EPA involvement in the evolution of SCAQMD's RECLAIM 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. However, EPA's proposed cap rules deviate from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RECLAIM program in several areas. For example, in RECLAIM, which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is currently a NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and SO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 market, the term of each emission cap is one year. In the proposed FIP measures, the term of each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission cap is one month. EPA selected the shorter compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
term as providing more certainty that the one-hour ozone NAAQS 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would not be exceeded. EPA will continue to evaluate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance period as more information becomes available. For 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each FIP area, data may allow EPA to determine that different 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance periods would provide sufficient security against 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
short-term emission increases that jeopardize air quality 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
progress. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The 1990 proposal did not include regulations specifying the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
noncompliance penalty structure. This NPRM proposes penalty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provisions for emissions cap exceedances based on EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conclusion (expressed repeatedly in the context of the RECLAIM 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
development process) that exceedance-based systems of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishing penalties are the most appropriate for the use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mass emission caps. Exceedance-based penalty systems establish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
violations based on the amount of excess emissions above the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facility cap. This penalty structure is most protective of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NAAQS and comports best with the Act and with EPA's established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforcement policies. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA has included in this FIP a much more detailed method for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
setting baselines for the facilities subject to the cap program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The method requires sources to examine the following data to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculate their year 2001 cap emissions: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;1990 annual emissions, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Reductions to be made as a result of compliance with SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and/or FIP control measures between the years 1990 and 2001. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This methodology will inherently rely on emission inventory data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and planning projections to set year 2001 emissions caps. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, the methodology has the advantage that it will not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unfairly penalize facilities which have made reductions ahead of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
schedule. This baseline proposal provides flexibility beyond the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1990 proposal for facilities which have made early reductions of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and VOC emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is not now proposing to incorporate a trading system 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into the program in regulatory form. EPA will observe the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
functioning of the RECLAIM market system, the Acid Rain (CAA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Title IV) market system, and other markets to determine the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
design which is most successful for reducing emissions in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas while achieving other program goals, including the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
avoidance of air toxics hotspots. In the 1990 FIP proposal, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indicated that a trading scheme would be proposed in the future; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in this round, given that there will be more experience with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
trading markets by the time this FIP is finalized, EPA chose to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
observe the developing markets in parallel with its own research 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effort prior to selecting a market structure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, the 1990 proposal discussed the use of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers bubble as an alternative to emissions caps for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
users of industrial and commercial solvents and coatings. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current FIP proposal describes this concept in greater detail and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discusses EPA's various reasons for preferring this approach, if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is able to successfully resolve certain issues involved in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulating manufacturers at the national level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Rationale for Approach. All of the California FIP areas suffer from emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction shortfalls when the FIP attainment analysis is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performed. The shortfalls, or reductions beyond the other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed FIP and SIP measures, which must be accounted for in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to bring the FIP areas into attainment, range from at least 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
20 percent additional reductions for VOC to at least 30 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional reductions for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
.
<FOOTCITE>61</FOOTCITE>
 In consideration of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limitations of available technology-based regulations, and to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide the regulated community with maximum flexibility when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
trying to achieve the shortfall reductions, EPA is proposing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facility-wide declining emission cap program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
61
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The rates of reduction shown here represent the results of EPA's preliminary analysis of the FIP areas' emission inventories.
Further analysis of these inventories is currently being performed and may ultimately impact the final rates of reduction
for the FIP cap program. The reates of reduction may increase or stay the same as appropriate. The current rates of reduction
analysis shows that a 4 percent to 9 percent annual rate of reduction for VOC and 6 percent to 9 percent for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

