EPA will review and approve or disapprove these plans depending on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether they achieve the required emission reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. Given that these plans will encompass multiple 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission sources with different emission measurement methods, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that the compliance plan review effort will be resource 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
intensive. For those sources subject to Title V operating permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations, the provisions of the compliance plans would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federally enforceable permit conditions. For such sources, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures used in reviewing and approving the compliance plans 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would not supersede public participation and other requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
found in the Part 70 Title V regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Compliance with the monthly caps established in the FIP cap 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program will be determined by two means. Routine inspections to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine whether or not monthly caps are met will be conducted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, facilities subject to the FIP cap program will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to certify that they met their monthly caps on an annual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis by submitting annual reports within 60 days of the close of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each calendar year. Prior to the implementation of the FIP cap 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program, EPA will prepare a statistical showing that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aggregate effect of the specified averaging time is consistent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with attaining the ozone NAAQS and satisfying applicable progress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements on a typical summer day basis in accordance with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed economic incentive program rules and guidance (see 58 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
11110, February 23, 1993). EPA seeks comment on the term of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission caps (i.e., monthly as opposed to a shorter or longer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
timeframe) and the means by which to determine compliance at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilities subject to the FIP cap program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) EPA Resources. EPA believes that the compliance mechanisms outlined above require a significant resource investment
by the Agency. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resources include staff to review the compliance plan submissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and evaluate the subject facilities' compliance status. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seeks comment on alternative designs to those outlined in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cap regulations that may reduce this resource burden (including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the delegation of these responsibilities to the State or local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies discussed elsewhere in this NPRM). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Emission Quantification Mechanisms. EPA recognizes that in order to quantify mass emissions, the suggested
emissions quantification mechanisms may need further development. EPA will be evaluating the methods outlined
and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternatives for adequacy prior to implementation of the FIP cap 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program.
<FOOTCITE>64</FOOTCITE>
 EPA may develop and propose additional methods to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obtain the appropriate mass emission results required of the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cap program prior to its implementation. EPA seeks comment on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the methods outlined in the proposed regulations and any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative means for determining emissions from the affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source categories. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
64
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
This issue may impact the structure of any emission reduction trading market established as a result of subsequent
analysis. See discussion later in this section.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Control Approach_
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 (1) Rule Structure. This overview explains the format used in the proposed FIP cap program rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
