<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
c. Enhanced Inspection and Maintenance_(1) Introduction. In its studies of in-use vehicle emission performance,
EPA has found that vehicles typically emit at 2&hyph;3 times their certification standards in actual use. Factors
such as lack of durability, tampering with emission control equipment, design defects, and poor maintenance all
contribute to making cars and other mobile sources much dirtier in use than they should be. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA has found that an efficiently designed and managed vehicle inspection and maintenance (I/M) program is one of
the most effective and cost effective ways of cleaning the air. I/M programs are able to produce significant emission
reductions after only a single inspection cycle, and the effort the consumer must expend to comply with I/M can be offset
with improved fuel economy and performance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Enhanced I/M. I/M programs achieve their objectives by identifying vehicles that have high emissions as a result
of one or more malfunctions and requiring them to be repaired. An ``enhanced'' I/M program covers more of the vehicles
in operation, employs inspection methods which are better at finding high emitting vehicles, and has additional
features to better assure that all vehicles are properly tested and adequately repaired. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given the severity of the air quality problem in the FIP areas covered by this proposed action, EPA is proposing to implement
an enhanced I/M program in all FIP areas as one of the most significant and productive of its FIP measures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Background. The first mandatory 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M programs were required by the 1977 Amendments to the Clean Air Act, which called for I/M programs in areas with long
term air quality problems. The 1990 Amendments to the Act expanded the role of I/M, directing EPA to develop guidelines
and performance standards for two levels of I/M program_basic 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M for areas with moderately dirty air, and enhanced I/M for areas (like those addressed by this proposed FIP) with
more serious and chronic nonattainment problems. On November 5, 1992, EPA published a final regulation (40 CFR part
51 subpart S) establishing certain minimum requirements and performance standards for both basic and enhanced I/M
programs. It is this regulation and its supporting documentation that will form the basis for much of what will be proposed
in this action. Readers are encouraged to consult the preamble to the final I/M rule for a more thorough explanation
of the costs and benefits of I/M programs and the specific regulations.
<FOOTCITE>71</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
71
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Although the proposed I/M program will meet the requirements of the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990, as well as the
performance standard for enhanced I/M programs promulgated on November 5, 1992, EPA is not obligated to meet these
requirements as part of its California FIP proposal. EPA would only be required to do so within 2 years of a state failure
related to such requirements. EPA has chosen the proposed program design because it is one of the most productive and
cost effective means by which to meet the requirement that the FIP demonstrate attainment with the ozone NAAQS.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A state-administered I/M program known as Smog Check has been in existence in California since March 1984, and is currently
operating in all three of the FIP areas. The Smog Check program includes a biennial tailpipe and antitampering inspection
of 1966 and newer model passenger cars, as well as light- and heavy-duty trucks. The inspections are conducted in a
decentralized network of private garages and service stations which are licensed by the State and which may perform
both the emissions inspection and emissions-related repairs. The tailpipe test consists of a simple idle test for
VOCs and carbon monoxide (CO) for vehicles up to the 1979 model year and a two-speed idle test for model year 1980 and
newer vehicles. The program also includes antitampering inspections for all significant emission control components.
Owners of subject vehicles must present proof that the vehicle has either been tested and passed or been waived from
the program requirement prior to registering their vehicle(s) in even numbered years for even numbered license plates
and odd numbered years for odd numbered license plates. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the beginning of the program in California, a review committee of State and local air quality professionals
has been conducting a continuing evaluation of the Smog Check network in order to recommend improvements to the Legislature
and to address problems in program operation as they are identified. The ``California I/M Review Committee's Fourth
Report to the Legislature'' details the results of comprehensive station audits and vehicle emission tests and concludes
that the current program is yielding less than one-half the potential emission reduction benefits of the program
if properly implemented due to improper testing and inadequate repair in the garages. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Based upon its own audits, as well as those conducted by California and other states, EPA has concluded that I/M programs
throughout the country which combine the functions of testing and repair in a single facility are only roughly half
as effective as programs which separate the two functions. The I/M rule which applies to enhanced areas requires the
separation of test and repair unless a State can demonstrate that its current test-and- repair program is equal in
effectiveness to a test-only network. In making this demonstration states will receive only one half the emission
credit for the tailpipe test than that allowed for test-only facilities unless the state can demonstrate higher reductions
from its program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00112 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
During the 1993 legislative session, the California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
legislature considered but did not pass legislation which would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have authorized the upgrade of the Smog Check network. Citing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concerns over jobs and consumer convenience, state legislators 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
advanced a bill designed to improve the State's failing Smog 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Check system without fundamentally changing its test-and-repair 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
structure. Although the bill contained some important measures, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA had to inform the legislature that the bill fell short of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting the Clean Air Act's standards for an enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. EPA officials are continuing to work with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California legislative leadership in the hopes of achieving 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consensus on a program design which would meet both the Clean Air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act requirements and California's unique needs. EPA is proposing an enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M program design which incorporates the most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sophisticated testing techniques with features to maximize 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer convenience and to mimimize the cost of inspection. If 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the State should develop and adopt an alternative program design 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which meets the Act's performance standard for enhanced I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs prior to finalization of the FIP, then the alternative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program will be proposed for approval as a State plan revision 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and its reductions will be substituted for those which would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected from a federally imposed I/M program. EPA would propose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional measures if necessary to provide equivalent emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions as part of the FIP attainment demonstration. Once the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP I/M program is finalized, however, the State will need to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meet not only the requirements of the I/M rule, but will have to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
produce a plan that fills any shortfall between the State I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program and the FIP I/M program, so as not to jeopardize the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP's required attainment demonstration. This could be done 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through the State I/M program or other emission reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Summary of Proposed Federal Enhanced I/M Program.  The proposed program would cover all model year 1966 and newer
onroad, gasoline-fueled vehicles_including heavy-duty trucks but excluding motorcycles_and would be conducted
on a biennial basis to optimize the measure's emission reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potential and cost effectiveness, as well as to minimize the disruption created by any transition from the current,
state-run Smog Check program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed program would be conducted in a network of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test-only stations (i.e., stations in which both owners and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operators are legally barred from profiting either directly or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indirectly from the sale and servicing of motor vehicles and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motor vehicle parts). The tests performed would include a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transient, mass-emissions tailpipe test known as the IM240 which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would measure hydrocarbons, carbon monoxide, and oxides of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nitrogen; purge and pressure tests to assure that the evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control system is working properly; and a visual antitampering 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection for the presence and proper connection of the positive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
crankcase ventilation (PCV) and air systems. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA intends to enter into a legal agreement with one or more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entities for the performance of all possible program activities, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other than enforcement of the motorist registration requirement, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and oversight requirements which would constitute a conflict of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
interest. This entity shall hereafter be referred to as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``test provider.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The biennnial test is estimated to cost between $20 and $25, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the average wait time at the high volume inspection stations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should average less than ten minutes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A more detailed discussion of the FIP's proposed I/M program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and its various program elements follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Applicability.  The enhanced I/M requirement will apply to all vehicles registered, or required to be registered,
in the counties listed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Table IM&hyph;1 and to vehicles in commercial fleets which are primarily operated within these same counties.
Vehicles which are operated on Federal installations located within the I/M program area must also comply with the
I/M requirements regardless of their registration status. 
