<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA expects to propose to regulate the emissions of hydrocarbons (HC), oxides of nitrogen (NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
), and carbon monoxide (CO) from certain new nonroad SI engines that have a gross power output at or below 19 kilowatts
(kW) (small SI engines). An SI engine is an internal combustion engine in which the air/fuel mixture is ignited in the
combustion chamber by an electric spark and are generally fueled by gasoline. The scope of these rules are expected
to encompass a broad range of small SI engine applications, including farm and construction equipment, which states
are preempted from regulating under section 209(e)(1) of the CAA. Anticipated exclusions from the rules are detailed
below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1. Engines used to propel marine vessels as defined in the General Provisions of the United States Code, 1 U.S.C. 3 (1992).
A ``vessel'' includes every description of watercraft or other artificial contrivance used, or capable of being
used, as a means of transportation on water. 1 U.S.C. 3 (1992). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2. Engines used in underground mining or engines used in underground mining equipment and regulated by the Mining
Safety and Health Administration (MHSA). See 30 CFR parts 7, 31, 32, 36, 56, 57, 70, and 75. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3. Engines used in motorcycles and regulated in 40 CFR part 86 subpart E. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4. Engines used in aircraft as that term is defined in 40 CFR 87.1(a). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5. Engines used in recreational vehicles. Recreational engines are those engines which have no installed speed governor
and which have a rated speed of greater than or equal to 5000 revolutions per minute (RPM). Recreational engines are
not used to propel marine vessels and do not meet the criteria to be categorized as a Class III, IV, or V engine under this
rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) Standards.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
Phase 1
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
