<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00128 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
(d) Regulatory Scheme. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
Phase 1 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
This rule is expected to propose the following regulatory scheme: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Designation of product lines into groups of engines with similar emissions, and manufacturer emission testing
of selected engines with a specified test; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Labeling of engines, and alternatively, equipment labeling; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Submission of an application for certification for each engine family; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Inclusion of various certification requirements such as the prohibition of defeat devices; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Issuance of an emission certificate of conformity for each engine family; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Prohibition against sale of engines in the U.S. not certified by EPA; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Requirement that equipment manufacturers use the appropriate handheld or nonhandheld certified engine
in their equipment; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Recordkeeping and reporting requirements; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Design warranty provisions and prohibition on tampering; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Inclusion of all farm and construction engines preempted under the CAA from state regulation; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Development of an engine manufacturer's program to evaluate in-use emission deterioration; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Requirement that, if catalysts are used in an engine family, catalyst durability must be confirmed by means
of the evaluation procedure that is specified in this NPRM; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Defect reporting; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Importation provisions; and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Production line Selective Enforcement Auditing (SEA). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
Phase 2 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
The regulatory scheme for Phase 2 will be negotiated through the regulatory negotiation process. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=4 -->
3. Federal Requirements for SI Marine Engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
The EPA is currently in the process of developing a Federal rulemaking to control emissions from SI marine engines.
The proposed standards will be expected to result in a 70&hyph;80 percent reduction in HC emissions from two-stroke
outboard engines which comprise over half of the engines in this category. On average there will be a 50&hyph;60 percent
reduction in emissions from the fleet of SI marine engines. However, as a result of controlling HC emissions, NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions are expected to increase by a factor of 2&hyph;4 (baseline NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 for these engines, on average, is about 2 
<!-- PJG 0320 forcenewline -->
g/kWh). This Federal program will apply to FIP areas. EPA is under court ordered deadline for this regulation; the
schedule is for the NPRM to be signed by the Administrator by September 30, 1994 and final rule signed by November 24,
1995. The general provisions that the Agency anticipates will be included in the proposal for regulation of SI marine
engines are briefly described in the following section. Today, EPA is also proposing a system of emission based fees
for use of SI marine pleasure craft in the FIP areas. If the Agency finds that the Federal program for controlling emissions
from SI marine engines does not achieve the anticipated reduction, adjustments will be made to the stringency of the
fees or the standards to compensate for any loss in benefit from the Federal program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Overview of Requirements for SI Marine Engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will propose to regulate the emission of hydrocarbons from SI marine engines. Included under this regulation
will be emission standards for SI marine propulsion engines which include the following engine types: outboard,
sterndrive (inboard/outboard), inboard, and personal watercraft (e.g., Jet Ski). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A compliance program involving pre-sale certification and in-use enforcement is also expected to be proposed for
marine engines. The proposed program will be similar to the current on-highway engine program. EPA expects to include
the following provisions: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Designation of product line into groups of engines with similar emission characteristics (such groups are
called engine families); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Manufacturer emission testing of selected engines with the specified test procedure to demonstrate compliance
with emission standards; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Labeling of engines from each engine family; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Submission of application for certification for each engine family by model year; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00129 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;Issuance of an emission compliance certificate for each engine family;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Prohibition against sale of engines not certified by EPA;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Recordkeeping and reporting;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA confirmatory certification testing;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Production line Selective Enforcement Auditing (SEA);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;In-use testing and enforcement;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Warranty and prohibition on tampering; and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Importation provisions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Certain modifications to the on-highway program are expected to be proposed for this program. These modifications
include: (1) only one emission test engine required, and (2) a national averaging, banking, and trading program modified
to suit this industry.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For the FIP areas, EPA proposes that phased emission standards and certification of new engines commence with the
1998 model year for outboard, sterndrive and inboard engines, and 1999 model year for personal watercraft.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Definition of SI Marine Engine. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A spark-ignition engine is an internal combustion engine in which the air/fuel mixture is ignited in the combustion
chamber by an electric spark. EPA is expecting to propose defining marine engines as any nonroad engine which is used
on a ``vessel'' as defined in 1 U.S.C. Section 3 (1992).
<FOOTCITE>72</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
72
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The word vessel includes every description of watercraft or other artificial contrivance used, or capable of being
used, as a means of transportation on water.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(c) Applicability. Under the expected proposal all SI marine engines must comply with the proposed emission standards,
with some exceptions as discussed below.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
Exclusions from the Proposal
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Certain engines that are included within the proposed definition of marine engine just discussed are expected to
be excluded from the Federal marine engine emission standards. SI engines expected to be excluded are the following:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Engines used in marine ``vessels'' as defined in 1 U.S.C. Sections 3 (1992) which are used exclusively as marine
auxiliary power generation units;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Exported engines, either loose marine engines or marine engines in boats or vessels that are to be exported.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
Exemptions From the Proposal
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Pursuant to section 203(b)(1) of the CAA, the Agency is expecting to propose categories of exemptions from new SI marine
engine regulations similar to the existing exemptions for new large nonroad CI engines (see 40 CFR part 89 subpart
I). These include exemptions for purposes of research, investigations, studies, demonstrations, training, or
for reasons of national security. Exemptions are justified in these cases because the sources are limited in number
or scope so no environmental harm results, the particular use of the source is determined to further air quality research,
and/or the exemption is vital to the security of the nation. (See 39 FR 10601, March 21, 1974 for history of on-highway
exemptions policy.)
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Emission Standards. The numerical emission standards are expected to be proposed for the category of SI marine
vessels which includes inboard engines, sterndrives, and outboard engines. EPA realizes that these standards will
cover engines that use different technology, but EPA believes that creating separate standards based on categories
such as 2- and 4-stroke engines sets up artificial barriers to competition in the market and will not achieve as efficiently
reductions from this category as a single set of standards. EPA will in its proposal for the Federal program take comment
on the scheme of classification and level of standards. Separate standards are expected to be set for HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions on a brake-specific basis (i.e., grams of pollutant per brake kilowatt-hour). EPA realizes that there
is a tradeoff between the level of HC emissions and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions and intends to take comment on setting a combined HC+NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standard for this category of nonroad engines.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that for current outboard engines these standards will be technology forcing. For other types of engines
these standards may require better calibration and more advanced fueling systems. The emission standards will be
proposed to take effect at the earliest possible date, as required by paragraph 213(b) of the Act.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SI marine engines are very rarely designed to operate on alternative fuels. EPA does not believe that new emission
standards for marine engines will require increased use of alternative fuels. EPA therefore does not expect to propose
procedures or emission standards for SI alternative-fueled marine engines. EPA does expect to request comment on
the need for regulations and the potential for increased market share for SI marine engines that operate on alternative
fuels such as natural gas, methanol, or ethanol.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00130 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In addition to the numerical emission standards described above, EPA expects to propose to prohibit direct emissions
of crankcase vapors into the atmosphere. Motor vehicles have been subject to an analogous requirement since the first
stages of emission-control regulation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) General Enforcement Provisions. The Agency is expected to propose regulations that require marine sources to
obtain certification and subject them to selective enforcement auditing and in-use enforcement. The Agency is also
expected to propose regulations similar to those for on-highway vehicles under sections 203, 204, 205, and 208 of
the Act.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(f) Compliance Dates for Certification. The Federal marine program as it applies to the FIP areas will phase standards
in over time. EPA proposes that emission standards become effective in the FIP areas beginning in the 1998 model year
for outboard, sterndrive, and inboard SI marine engines, and in the 1999 model year for personal watercraft SI engines.
Companies considered small marine businesses will be defined in the Federal rule and would first be subject to emission
standards no earlier than the 2000 model year.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(g) Averaging, Banking, and Trading. Provisions of the national enforcement program are expected to include an averaging,
banking, and trading Program. EPA will not propose an averaging, banking, and trading program for marine engines
specifically for the FIP areas. However, EPA does plan on proposing such a program for the Federal regulation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Special FIP Measures. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Agency believes that the above Federal programs will have sound technical basis and will achieve projected emissions
reductions. The Agency also acknowledges that the marine pleasure craft standards are likely to be technology forcing.
However, the Agency also recognizes that many other sources, which have been previously regulated, will be required
to meet new and more stringent standards than they are required to meet federally. For the purposes of equity and to
achieve added emissions reductions from nonroad sources, the Agency proposes to and will take comment on a fees program
for certain classes of these nonroad engines. The Agency is also proposing today a program to control emissions from
engines used in recreational vehicles and nonroad and on-highway motorcycles as a special FIP measure.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) On-Highway Motorcycles and Nonroad Engines Used in Nonroad Motorcycles. The Agency intends to exclude from its
regulation of SI engines at or under 19 kW, engines used in recreational vehicles because they have fundamentally
different operating characteristics than other small SI nonroad engines. In general, engines used in recreational
equipment are similar to engines used in on-highway motorcycles in that they use a continuously variable throttle
(not a governor), have rated engines speeds in excess of 5000 RPM, and wide variations in both engine load and speed.
These engines, like motorcycle engines, are primarily used to transport people. CARB has estimated that engines
used in nonroad vehicles such as motorcycles, all terrain vehicles, go-carts and other nonroad recreational vehicles,
contribute 21 tons of HC and 100 tons of CO per day to the statewide inventory. Further, EPA estimates that on-highway
motorcycles and engines used in nonroad motorcycles, including dirt bikes, ATVs, and similar equipment contribute
3, l, 30.3 tons of HC per day to the emissions inventories in the Sacramento, Ventura, and South Coast, respectively.
To achieve reductions from this category of nonroad sources as well as to achieve reductions from on highway motorcycles
and mopeds, EPA proposes to promulgate emissions restrictions for motorcycles and nonroad recreational vehicles
similar to EPA's existing on-highway motorcycle program found in 40 CFR part 86 subpart E.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(a) Overview. EPA today proposes to promulgate a definition of on-highway and nonroad motorcycles applicable in
the State of California for the purposes of the FIP, as well as standards for those classes of motorcycles and engines
used in them. Today's proposal encompasses on-highway motorcycles, and engines used in such vehicles as mopeds,
dirt bikes, all terrain vehicles, and go-carts. EPA is also proposing a more stringent set of exhaust emission standards
applicable to all new on-highway and nonroad motorcycles sold in the State of California after January 1, 1996. EPA
proposes that all other requirements of the current Federal motorcycle regulations contained in 40 CFR part 86 subparts
E and F, apply to motorcycles sold in California, including provisions for certification, test procedures, and enforcement.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(b) Definition of On-highway and Nonroad Motorcycles. For the purpose of the FIPs, EPA proposes to use the definition
of motorcycle in 40 CFR part 86 to refer to on-highway motorcycles and add the following definition for nonroad motorcycles.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A nonroad motorcycle means any motorized land vehicle designed for transporting persons or property and manufactured
for sale, sold, offered for sale, introduced into commerce, or imported into the State of California and which has
two wheels, or three or more wheels and a curb mass less than or equal to 680 kilograms; it does not include devices regulated
by the State of California as other classes of motor vehicles or devices regulated by EPA as other classes of nonroad
engines.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00131 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(c) Applicability. The Agency believes that it is necessary to control emissions from all on-highway motorcycles
and engines used in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad motorcycles, as defined in the previous section, on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statewide basis to achieve needed emission reductions in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. It is the Agency's intent that the standards proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
today, as well as the requirements of 40 CFR part 86 subpart E, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apply to all on-highway motorcycles, and engines used in nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycles regardless of displacement. The following are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
examples of vehicles covered by the definition of nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycles: dirt bikes, all terrain vehicles, go-cart, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mopeds. It is the Agency's intent that engines used in these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles and similar nonroad vehicles meet the standards. It is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also the Agency's intent that on-highway motorcycles with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
displacements less than 50 cc (3.1 cu. in.) meet the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) Standards. EPA proposes that all motorcycles sold in California meet a 0.8 g/km standards for HC, a 0.8 g/km standard
for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, and a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
12 g/km standard for carbon monoxide. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Certification data from current technology on highway 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycles indicates that over 50 percent of these motorcycles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are certifying to exhaust HC emission levels at or below 0.8 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
g/km HC. EPA believes that by using existing on-highway 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycle technology it is reasonable to expect that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycles should be able to achieve comparable emission levels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for HC. The Agency understands that an 0.8 g/km standard may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
require substitution of 4-stroke nonroad engines for 2-stroke 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad engines currently used in dirt bikes, ATVs, and similar 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles. However, EPA is also aware that catalyst technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exists and is currently being applied to 2-stroke motorcycles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and mopeds sold in other countries to meet stringent legal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions levels in those countries. EPA believes that this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
simple oxidation technology is viable for reducing emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from engines used in both on-highway and nonroad motorcycles to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 0.8 g/km HC and 0.8 g/km NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 level. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Agency has considered a 0.5 g/km HC standard for on-highway motorcycles and takes comment on restricting emissions

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from on-highway motorcycles to this more stringent level and on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the applicability of this standard to the smaller displacement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-highway motorcycles under 50 cc (3.1 cu. in.). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Although the test data on NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions from motorcycles is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more limited, experience with light duty cars suggests that with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a catalyst, NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emission levels are approximately equal to HC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission levels. In addition, legal emission levels for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycles in Taiwan, beginning in 1996, will be 0.8 g/km for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
both HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. Testing data submitted by one catalyst 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer shows that HC+NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 levels range between 0.30 g/km 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 1.88 g/km. The Agency realizes that there is a trade-off 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
between HC emission and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emission and solicits comment on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefit of setting a combined standard of 1.6 g/km for HC + NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also realizes that less emission data exists for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad engines used in nonroad motorcycles and solicits data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from manufacturers as a basis for evaluating the level of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards proposed today. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(e) Certification and Test Procedure. EPA proposes that the test procedure for on-highway and nonroad motorcycles
will be the same as that procedure described in 40 CFR part 86 subpart F. EPA acknowledges that the test procedure described
in 40 CFR part 86 subpart F, in general is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performed on a dynamometer designed for two-wheeled motorcycles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and that many motorcycles as defined above have more than two 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wheels. The test procedure described in 40 CFR part 86 subpart 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
F, involves testing motorcycles as whole vehicles on chassis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dynamometers designed for two-wheeled motorcycles, driving the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
same driving cycle (vehicle speed versus time) that is used for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cars and light trucks. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Current Federal regulations contain a general provision for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA to authorize special test procedures for vehicles which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cannot be properly tested under the standard procedures. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes it is appropriate to test all of the new motorcycles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and engines included in today's proposal as whole vehicles under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this same procedure used for current motorcycles. The special 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test procedure provision would be applied as necessary to test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any vehicles which cannot be physically tested using current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycle dynamometers. For example, vehicles having more than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
one drive wheel on an axle exceeding the width of current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycle dynamometer rolls might be tested using normal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
passenger car chassis dynamometers, or motorcycle dynamometers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with specially widened rolls. Vehicles with multiple drive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
axles would be handled, as much as possible, like four-wheel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
drive passenger cars and light trucks are currently tested, with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all the power supplied through only one primary drive axle. If 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the foregoing adaptations of the currently available motorcycle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and passenger car chassis dynamometers cannot be applied, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would expect to apply special procedures involving directly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
connecting the drive axle to a dynamometer, driven at an axle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
speed that corresponds to the required vehicle speed. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solicits comments on the test procedures outlined in 40 CFR, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Subpart F, and its applicability to four-wheeled nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motorcycles. EPA specifically requests comments on J1088 as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative test procedure. For J1088 and other alternative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures suggested, EPA specifically requests data to allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correlation between the stringency of the proposed standards as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measured on the test procedure defined in 40 CFR, Subpart F, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any test suggested by commentors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
