<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00132 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(f) Authority. As explained above, in promulgating a FIP under section 110 (c) of the Act, EPA may take any actions that
the state could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
take. As section 209 of the CAA provides that California is not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preempted from adopting and implementing a motor vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions control program provided its program satisfies the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria of section 209(b), EPA believes that it, acting on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
behalf of California in the context of a FIP, may adopt a motor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle program or supplement California's own motor vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program provided that EPA's actions would satisfy the criteria 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of section 209(b) for a waiver of Federal preemption. Moreover, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that it, acting on behalf of California in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
context of a FIP, may adopt a nonroad engine program or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supplement California's nonroad engine program provided that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's actions satisfy the criteria of section 209(e)(2) for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waiver of Federal preemption and provided that the program does 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not establish emission standards or accompanying enforcement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures for any new nonroad engines that California, under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 209(e)(1) is precluded from regulating (i.e., new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
locomotive and new engines used in locomotives and new engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in construction equipment or vehicles or used in farm 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment or vehicles and which are smaller than 175 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
horsepower). Section 209 provides that EPA is to grant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California a waiver of preemption if the State determines that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its standards ``will be, in the aggregate, at least as protective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of public health and welfare as applicable Federal standards.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 209 further provides that no waiver or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authorization is to be granted if EPA finds that: (A) the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination of the State that the standards are, in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aggregate, at least as protective of public health and welfare 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as otherwise applicable Federal standards is arbitrary and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
capricious; (B) State standards are not needed to meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compelling and extraordinary conditions; or (C) the State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards and accompanying enforcement procedures are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistent with section 202(a) (or are not consistent with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 209, as is the case of nonroad engines under section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
209(e)). EPA has interpreted the consistency criterion as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meaning that California standards and accompanying enforcement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures must be technologically feasible within the leadtime 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provided, taking into account the cost of compliance, and that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
they not impose inconsistent certification test procedure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements such that the same vehicle or engine could not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used to comply with both State and Federal certification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that the on-highway and nonroad motorcycle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program that would be applicable to vehicles and engines in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California under the proposed FIP would satisfy the section 209 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waiver criteria. Thus, California could undertake the on-highway and nonroad motorcycle program described above,
and, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore, EPA has authority under section 110(c) to undertake 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the protectiveness of the standards, all of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed standards are at least as protective as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise applicable Federal standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the need to meet compelling and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extraordinary conditions, EPA has repeatedly found that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California's air quality problems satisfy the need criterion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 209. Moreover, the magnitude of the reductions in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions needed to reach attainment in the FIP areas, including 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile source emissions, provides ample evidence of the need for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more stringent motor vehicle emission standards in California. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, with respect to the criterion of consistency with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 202(a) or section 209, EPA believes that its proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced nonroad program is consistent with both the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technological feasibility and certification elements of that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criterion. EPA believes that the various elements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program are technologically feasible within the leadtime 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provided, taking into account the cost of compliance. Moreover, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
no aspect of the program establishes any new certification test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures that are inconsistent with either existing Federal or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California test procedure requirements. Consequently, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that its proposed on-highway and nonroad motorcycle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program satisfies this prong of section 209 as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, as none of the classes of nonroad equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is regulating under this program are new farm or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construction equipment or new locomotives, this program meets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the requirements of section 209(e)(1). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Fee on Marine Pleasure Craft. Although the new national emission standards for spark ignition engines in marine
pleasure craft described above will result in substantial emissions reductions per engine, overall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in emissions from this category will depend primarily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the amount of fleet turnover after the new standards are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
introduced. Under conditions of normal turnover, EPA expects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that approximately one half of the total fleet of marine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pleasure craft will meet the new standard by 2005. However, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
even normal turnover may not result in enough emission reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to accomplish the goals of this FIP. EPA believes that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional measures are needed to strongly encourage the use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only cleaner engines in the FIP areas. Therefore, EPA proposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to require that all owners of marine pleasure craft with spark 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ignition engines obtain an emission permit before operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their boats in the FIP areas during each area's ozone season 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beginning in 2004. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
