<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Under this proposed permit system, all operators of marine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pleasure craft will be required to carry an emission permit on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
board their boats while operating in the FIP area. Since boats 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are often operated in areas other than where the owner resides, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permits will be required only for operation of a boat within the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP area, not for ownership of a boat within the FIP area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Boat-owners who reside in the FIP area but only use their boats 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outside the FIP area would not be required to purchase permits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Two types of permits will be sold, annual permits and daily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use permits. Owners of boats meeting the new emission standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described above would be eligible for permanent emission permits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
free of charge. Owners of all other boats could choose to buy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
either an annual permit which would allow them to operate their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
boats in the FIP area for the entire year, or a daily use permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which would allow them to operate their boat on a single 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified day in the FIP area (owners could also purchase 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
multiple daily use permits if they plan to operate their boats 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the FIP area for only a few days per year). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The fees for emission permits will be based on the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excess emissions of pre-standard engines compared to engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting the new emissions standards described above. For the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purpose of permit fee calculation, the engines of marine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pleasure craft will be divided into the following classes: 4- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cycle gasoline inboard, 4-cycle gasoline outboard, 2-cycle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gasoline outboard, 4-cycle gasoline sterndrive, sailboat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
auxiliary engines, and personal watercraft (e.g., Jet Ski). EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
invites comment on whether these general categories are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate for permit fee calculation, or whether they should 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be further subdivided to reflect differences in emissions or use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within a class (e.g., some of these classes, such as 2-cycle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outboards, could be further subdivided based on horsepower to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reflect the very wide range of horsepower, usage, and total 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions that might be seen within the class). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Average excess emissions per engine would be calculated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
separately for each engine class based on the difference between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the sum of average HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions for pre-standard engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the sum of average HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions for engines meeting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the appropriate new standard. Total average excess emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would then be calculated as the product of average excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions per engine for the engine class and the time of use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the engine class. For an annual permit, time of use would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be based on the average annual use for the particular engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
class. For a daily permit, eight hours of continuous use would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be assumed for all engine classes. EPA requests comment on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other options for time of use when assessing fees for daily 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA currently estimates that a boat with a typical pre-standard 2-cycle gasoline outboard motor would be assessed
an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual fee of approximately $400, based on typical emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rates and hours of use and using a rate of $10,000 per ton of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excess emissions (this example assumes 35 hours of annual use, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with HC emissions reduced from 118 g/hp-hr to 35 g/hp-hr as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
result of the new standard while NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions increase from 2.4 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
g/hp-hr to 4.7 g/hp-hr; this example is based on EPA's best 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current estimates which may change as more information is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
received and as the engine standards are finalized). At this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time, EPA believes that this fee level will effectively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discourage the operation of pre-standard engines in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. However, EPA will monitor the effectiveness of this fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level in discouraging operation of pre-standard engines, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase the fee per ton if needed to discourage virtually all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such operation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To obtain a permit a boat owner would have to present to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the permit office documentation regarding the engine class and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether or not the engine meets the new emissions standard. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
boat's state registration documentation may be sufficient to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ascertain the engine class. If not, one time physical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspections may be needed, in which case EPA would seek to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delegate such inspections to local or state agencies. Engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting the new emissions standard will be required to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
labeled as such by the manufacturers. Boat owners will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required to show that their boat engines have such a label in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to obtain a free permit. In the absence of such proof, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the permit fee will be based on the assumption that the boat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine does not meet the new standard. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Some boat engine manufacturers may already be producing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines that meet the new standards, before the labeling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements have been finalized. These manufacturers have the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
option of presenting to EPA evidence (in the form of emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test data which meets EPA's test requirements for these engines) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of which engines met the requirement before it went into effect. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If EPA accepts this evidence, owners of such engines could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualify for a free permit if they can prove that their boat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine qualifies based on the serial number of the engine. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
