<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00137 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Commercial airline emission sources include aircraft, aircraft auxiliary power units (APUs), GSE, and any other
mobile sources under the control of airlines. For the purposes of this regulation, emission sources defined as ``under
the control'' of a commercial airline include all sources owned or leased by the airline, and all sources whose operations
are controlled under a contract agreement by the airline (for example, GSE servicing an airline that are owned and
operated by a fixed-base operator under contract to the airline).
<FOOTCITE>74</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
74
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
General aviation aircraft operated at commercial airports will be included in the general aviation control strategy
as described in section III.D.4.e(2)(d). Today's proposal for public aircraft operated at commercial airports
is described in section III.D.4.e.(2)(e).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Some GSE that service aircraft are owned by fixed based operators (FBOs) rather than airlines. Emissions from these
vehicles must be included in the airport bubble since they are a direct result of aircraft activity. EPA proposes today
to allocate these GSE to specific airlines based on servicing provided under contract agreements, and requests comment
on possible alternatives to such an allocation strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes a target-based emission reduction program for these commercial aircraft emission sources. Each commercial
airline will be required to comply with a declining emission limit, expressed in allowable emissions per passenger
and freight handled. The proposed target-based program will include emission fees assessed on any airline that fails
to meet this emission rate target. The proposed target has been selected so that, even with expected passenger and
freight growth, compliance with the target by all airlines will result in total emissions declining at a rate and to
a level equivalent to that required of many stationary sources in the same basin. EPA will through experience raise
the level of the emissions fees as needed to achieve such aggregate emission reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described above, EPA currently intends to establish emission reduction requirements for commercial aircraft
that are generally consistent with the emission cap requirements for stationary sources in the FIP areas. Although
not firmly decided, the current stationary source control strategy for the South Coast requires that VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions be reduced by 20&hyph;45 and 30&hyph;45 percent, respectively, from the 1990 baseline inventory by 2005,
through a series of uniform annual reductions of baseline emissions of each pollutant: 4&hyph;9 percent per year
for VOC and 6&hyph;9 percent per year for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, both starting in 2001. Stationary source requirements for the 2006&hyph;2010 period have not been established
at this time. The requirements for Ventura from 2001&hyph;2005 are identical to that for the South Coast. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For Sacramento, the strategy requires a VOC reduction of 20&hyph;45 percent, with a uniform annual reduction of 4&hyph;9
percent. The timeframe for achieving this reduction is the same as for the South Coast and Ventura areas (i.e., 2001&hyph;2005).
The stationary source control strategy for Sacramento does not require a NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reduction since other actions will be adequate to achieve the area-wide NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions level needed for attainment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For this proposal, the overall reduction targets are expressed as a range of percentages; the final rule will contain
a specific percentage reduction target. EPA requests comment on whether the stationary source emission reduction
targets are appropriate for use with the commercial aircraft control strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Geographic Scope. As already described, today's proposal applies to all three affected California control areas:
South Coast, Ventura, and Sacramento air basins. However, EPA requests comment on the desirability of removing the
Ventura and Sacramento control areas from this commercial aircraft control strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The Sacramento air basin includes a single commercial airport. Aircraft emissions from this airport contributed
significantly less than one percent of the air basin
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
s total VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 baseline emissions inventory for 1990. Current projections show that if uncontrolled, this airport
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
s emissions would consume less than one percent of the basin
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
s allowable 2005 inventory for VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. Additionally, the SIP and FIP for Sacramento are expected to achieve VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reductions adequate for attainment, not counting additional reductions from airports. This expectation is based
on the best available, but still preliminary, analysis of current and future emissions. Further analysis may change
the picture. Similarly, the Ventura control area has only minimal commercial aviation activity; an extensive emissions
control program for the limited emissions resulting from such activity may not be desirable given the resource expenditure
necessary for implementation. The Agency, therefore, requests comment on whether it is necessary to implement a
commercial aviation control program in Sacramento and Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00138 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Control Period. EPA proposes to establish a fee-based program focused on controlling commercial aviation emissions
only during the ozone season in each FIP area. Further, EPA proposes to assess fees on the basis of the season-long average
of emissions per passenger and freight carried. There would be no enforceable expectation for emissions in a single
day or month. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This extended averaging period differs from the stationary source cap program in which year-round control and monthly
control periods are proposed. Because of the complex nature of operational scheduling, commercial aircraft operations
appear to have much less potential to vary significantly over the course of the ozone season in comparison to stationary
sources. This reduces the need to be concerned about particular days with significantly more emissions than average
that could adversely affect attainment. In addition, if the program is fee-based rather than based on a specific emissions
limitation, enforcement does not require a short control period to be practicable as it does for the stationary source
cap program. The proposed program for commercial aviation is both novel in concept and stringent, and EPA does not
wish to burden the industry with controls outside the ozone season if they are not essential to the air quality goals
of the FIP. Furthermore, operational changes outside the ozone season may provide some opportunity to ease the compliance
burden during the season. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The annual ozone seasons for each of the three FIP areas are:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
South Coast_March through October 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura_April through October 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento_May through October 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Alternatively, EPA could establish a year-round fee program and apply fees separately in each month in order to even
better ensure that emissions are controlled each day. Beyond its contribution to ozone attainment, year-round control
of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions from commercial aviation may aid efforts to comply with the particulate air quality standard, particularly
in the South Coast control area. (Ensuring particulate air quality is not a requirement for this FIP.) Since state
and local regulation over commercial aviation emissions is preempted by the federal government, such control would
have to come at the federal level. The Agency, therefore, requests comment on potential alternative compliance strategies.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Key Regulatory Elements. (
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Summary. A detailed control strategy is described here for the South Coast FIP area, but will apply for all three control
areas. Any differences in detail or emission reduction requirements for Ventura and Sacramento will be identified
separately. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The control strategy for commercial aircraft proposed today relies on a fee system to achieve a series of annual emissions
reductions from a baseline commercial aircraft emissions inventory. Airlines subject to the requirements of this
program will report activity and emissions during the ozone season on an annual basis beginning in 1999. An airline's
compliance with the program requirements will be determined for a given year by comparing an environmental performance
factor, expressed as pounds of pollutant emitted per passenger-equivalent unit (PEU) as calculated in the annual
report, against EPA's published pounds-per-PEU target for that year's ozone season. Airlines that exceed a particular
year's pounds-per-PEU target will be required to pay a fee in proportion to the excess emissions. EPA is proposing
pounds-per-PEU targets that will achieve the requisite annual emission reductions based on projected growth in
passenger and freight activity. (A more complete description of the proposed PEU factor and emission targets is presented
in section III.D.4.e.(2)(b)(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
)(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
).) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to obtain airline compliance with these pounds-per-PEU targets, the Agency will observe the airlines' responses
to the initial fee level in 2001, and will adjust the fee level to a higher or lower level if needed. The final FIP regulations
will contain commitments to this effect, and will specify the specific level to which the fees will be adjusted or a
formula for determining such level. Comments on this adjustment process are requested. EPA expects that the final
fee program for this source will be consistent with the Agency's Economic Incentive Program rules as discussed in
section III.D.5 of this NPRM. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This commercial aircraft emission reduction program will apply to all commercial aircraft operators in the FIP areas,
including domestic airlines, foreign airlines, and new entrant airlines. EPA recognizes the economic and social
importance of accommodating new entrants and the expansion of existing airlines in any commercial aircraft emission
reduction program. This control strategy attempts to accommodate these important concerns by not directly requiring
EPA to allocate emissions or operational rights to individual airlines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00139 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The methodology for determining the environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance targets reflects projected growth in air travel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within the respective FIP control area. As a result, new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entrants simply would be required to achieve the appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental performance targets upon initiation of service. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Both new entrants and expanding airlines are automatically 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accommodated by the control strategy as long as growth beyond 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
predicted levels does not occur. In event that activity grows 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
faster than originally forecast, EPA will revise the performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
targets, as discussed in greater detail later in this section. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, the Agency believes that the proposed program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incorporates features which provide flexibility for new entrants 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or existing airlines to begin and expand environmentally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible aircraft operations in the basin. Nonetheless, all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
airlines will face significant economic and technical challenges 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as a result of this program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Pounds per PEU Scheme. The FIP strategy for commercial aircraft operations will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
center on a schedule of declining emission performance targets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(i.e., pounds per PEU) for the combined emissions from all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial airports in each control area. Separate targets will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be established for HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions. These emission targets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will yield benefits consistent with the percentage reduction from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1990 inventory for stationary sources. Ultimately, the emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from commercial aviation in the South Coast and Ventura areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be reducted by 20&hyph;45 percent and 30&hyph;45 percent from 1990 VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emission levels, respectively, by 2005. The goal for VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions in the Sacramento area is 20&hyph;45 percent by 2005. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
already discussed, there is currently no stationary source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions cap for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 in Sacramento. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This control strategy fundamentally relies on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculation of an emissions baseline as a reference point for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining future year emission limits during the control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period. The Agency proposes to use currently available emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inventory data for aircraft and aircraft-related activities to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establish the requisite emissions inventory for the baseline year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of 1990. Once established, the seasonal emission limits for each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year can be calculated from the baseline inventory established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
according to the annual uniform schedule of percentage reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The choice of 1990 as the baseline year is consistent with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Act's requirement for modeling reasonable further progress 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(RFP) and attainment of the ambient standards. However, aviation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
activity in 1990 may not be representative of typical operations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because the aviation industry experienced significant change 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during this period. Several air carriers, notably Eastern 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Airlines, ceased operations or stopped serving airports in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast and Sacramento areas. Others such as Southwest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Airlines had only a minimal presence in these areas during that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period, but greatly expanded operations in subsequent years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, the Desert Shield/Desert Storm military action 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
caused an increase in jet fuel prices and a rapid decline in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial passenger traffic. For these reasons it may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
desirable to use a two-year average, perhaps 1989&hyph;1990, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishing a baseline inventory in preference to using 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data alone; this could lessen the effect of unusual circumstances 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on a single year's inventory. EPA requests comment on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
desirability of using a two-year average for establishing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
baseline inventory. Any such multi-year average, however, may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
require an adjustment to the emission performance targets in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to assure consistency with the stationary source cap 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements and EPA's demonstration of attainment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Alternatively, EPA could utilize baseline reports from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial airlines to establish this inventory. Given the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
importance of this baseline inventory in setting realistic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission limits and environmental performance targets, such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industry baseline reports could provide information more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
representative of airline operations in the control area than 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generic default data currently being used EPA for some portions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the inventory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00140 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For example, EPA currently has only estimated information on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
GSE and limited primary data concerning commercial aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations in Ventura and Sacramento. These reports could also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide data on certain ground access vehicles that are directly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the control of airlines. These sources are presently 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unaccounted for in today's proposal due to insufficient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information.
<FOOTCITE>75</FOOTCITE>
 Therefore, baseline reports could provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional detail and precision, albeit at the expense of an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional administrative burden for commercial airlines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Information that could be requested for such baseline reports is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in ``Reporting and Recordkeeping'' below. EPA requests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment on the desirability of requiring airlines to submit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
baseline reports, and on other possible alternative methods for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishing a reasonable baseline emissions inventory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
75
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
See the Technical Support Document, available in the rulemaking docket, for further information.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Agency further proposes to modify the stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source-related approach just described in order to ``lock in'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional emission reductions that are expected to result from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other non-FIP requirements such as the commercial aircraft fleet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modernization that will occur in response to FAA noise reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. According to the Air Transport Association,
<FOOTCITE>76</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these requirements could generate a 40 percent reduction in VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions by 2000 from 1990 levels, when the requirements are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fully implemented. This represents the maximum period of effect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for these requirements, however, since the affected aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be retired eventually even in the absence of specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations. EPA estimates that the emissions reduction benefit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resulting from noise reduction requirements declines to zero by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2010 because of such expected retirement schedules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
76
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
See letter from R. Kettler, ATA to R. Wilson, EPA, dated December 27, 1993, available in the rulemaking docket.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
To ensure these potential emission benefits occur, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agency proposes to modify the stationary source approach for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
developing the commercial aircraft baseline inventory and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seasonal emission limits as follows. The target for basin-wide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions will be the lower of either the emissions level 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated from the percentage-reduction-from-1990 schedule being 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed for the stationary source cap program, or the emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level that is expected to result from non-FIP regulatory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements such as the fleet modernization under the noise 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction requirements (i.e., emission improvements accompanying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the retirement of older, dirtier aircraft). The Agency requests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment on this ``lock-in'' approach and potential alternatives 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that would ensure progress and attainment under the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The two tables below present examples of the proposed ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
season emission inventory limits for VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 in the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast area. The emission limits in these tables are presented 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for illustrative purposes and for the purpose of facilitating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
public comment only. They do not represent the final values from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which the enforceable emission performance targets will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
derived, since EPA will continue to refine the baseline emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inventory, aviation activity projections, and the effect of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
noise-driven fleet modernization based on information received 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the comment period.
<FOOTCITE>77</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
77
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
A detailed derivation of the emission limits for all areas is contained in the Technical Support Document, which is
available in the public docket.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



