<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For 2006 and beyond, EPA proposes to retain for now the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pounds-per-PEU limit used for 2005 in all three areas, pending 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgation of the additional rules envisioned under today's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed strategy for 2010 attainment in the South Coast and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preparation of maintenance SIPs for the Ventura and Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. Comment is invited on this aspect of the proposal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Fee System. EPA will establish, and as necessary adjust, the level of a fee-based compliance system in consultation
with the FAA to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieve the seasonal commercial aircraft emissions targets for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each pollutant. Each commercial airline will be expected to meet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the seasonal pounds-per-PEU environmental performance target as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described above. Any airline that exceeds the target in any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ozone season will pay an emissions fee proportional to the excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions. Expressed differently, the fee will be applied only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the incremental emissions which are in excess of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance target, not to the airline operator's total seasonal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions. Through an iterative process, EPA will set the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions fee at a level designed to discourage such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
noncompliance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comment on a triggering mechanism that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevent implementation of the fee system as long as the area-wide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial aircraft emission allowance target is achieved. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Beginning with the 2001 annual report, EPA could use airline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
annual emissions/activity reports to determine total actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area-wide commercial aircraft emissions. If this total emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quantity did not exceed the allowance for that year, airlines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
could operate undisturbed by the FIP. If the total actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions quantity did exceed a given year's allowance, however, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the emissions fee system would be implemented for that year and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would remain in effect for all subsequent years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
It is important that the fee paid by the airlines for excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions be high enough to encourage adherence to the pounds-per-PEU limit but not so high as to needlessly reduce
industry 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
profits or to drain away funds that should be spent for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mitigating emissions. For the other fee systems discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this FIP, EPA proposes a base fee of $10,000/ton of pollutant. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The following example describes the impact of a $10,000/ton fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on a commercial airline under the proposed control strategy. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1990 emissions per PEU are 0.087 pounds of VOC per PEU and 0.162 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pounds of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 per PEU using current EPA data (see discussion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above). The 2005 example environmental performance targets as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
presented in Tables D and E are 0.039 pounds of VOC per PEU and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.073 pounds of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 per PEU . Assuming that there is no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improvement in environmental performance from the current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
uncontrolled rate, a maximum fee for 2005 can be calculated as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
follows: 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=16 g=1 f=1 -->
VOC Fee: (0.087 lbs/PEU&hyph;0.039 lbs/PEU) X $10,000/ton 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
divided by 2000 lbs/ton = $0.24/PEU for VOC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=16 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=16 g=1 f=1 -->
 Fee: (0.162 lbs/PEU&hyph;0.073 lbs/PEU) X $10,000/ton 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
divided by 2000 lbs/ton = $0.45/PEU for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=16 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=16 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
On this basis the maximum fee would be $0.69 per PEU. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is equivalent to approximately $120 per landing plus takeoff, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an average airplane. To put this excess emissions fee in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
context of other fees, collected from commercial aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operators for other purposes, current landing fees at LAX are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
$1.56/1,000 pounds of gross landing weight; for a typical Boeing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
737 this is about $178 and for a Boeing 747 about $913. From 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
another perspective, the FAA allows airports to apply passenger 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facility charges of $3 per passenger to pay for qualified airport 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improvements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The $10,000/ton emission fee, therefore, may be an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inappropriate starting point for commercial aircraft, since the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
likely impact is so small, that between 2000 and 2005 upwards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment is very likely to be needed. EPA believes that a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantially higher fee may be necessary for commercial aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in order to ensure compliance with the seasonal environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance targets. On the other hand, the expectation of larger 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fees after 2001 may have an effect on its own. Also, there are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enough fee cycles between 2001 and 2005 that a fee level which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will produce the desired reductions by 2005 should be reachable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by 2005 even if the fees initially are based on $10,000 per ton. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comment on what fee levels will achieve the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
objective of discouraging noncompliance without being excessive, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and on what the starting level of the fees should be. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
