<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00146 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
An airline's compliance with a seasonal environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance target will be determined by comparing actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pounds-per-PEU performance against the target. An airline whose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actual performance exceeds the target will be required to submit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a fee proportional to the resultant excess emissions, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in ``Fee System'' above. This fee will be remitted along 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the annual compliance determination report. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An airline's annual emissions/activity report will include a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
statement signed by a corporate officer affirming the report's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accuracy. Failure to provide accurate information in the annual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions/activity report will be considered a FIP violation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to EPA enforcement. Reporting airlines will be required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to maintain copies of their annual reports for three years, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including all relevant calculation worksheets and supporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
f
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Exemptions. EPA requests comment on the desirability of providing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exemptions from the requirements of this control program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain types of commercial aircraft operations or unusual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
situations. Atypical aircraft operations beyond the control of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an airline and not anticipated in the design of the operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data collection system, such as in-flight emergency situations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requiring emergency landings, could be exempted from reporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements and would not count toward an airline's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental performance. Also, certain small commercial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft operators might be unnecessarily burdened under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program relative to the emissions reductions that might be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obtained. EPA requests comment on the desirability and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
feasibility of establishing a de minimis exemption for this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control program. Additionally, the Agency requests comment on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the desirability of waiving part or all of any fee incurred by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual airlines that can demonstrate unreasonable economic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hardship or burden on interstate commerce. Finally, comments are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
solicited on appropriate procedures and criteria for granting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exemptions to foreign flag air carriers to ensure compliance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
international treaties, bilaterial agreements, or when the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Secretary or Deputy Secretary of the Department of State find 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that granting such an exemption is in the national interest of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the United States. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
3
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Legal Authority and Regulatory Interactions. As noted in this preamble, EPA has broad authority under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 110(c) to promulgate regulations in a FIP when states 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have failed to submit a state plan or have submitted defective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state plans. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
See Southland Terminal Corp. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
v. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
504 F.2d 646, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
669 (1st Cir. 1979). The courts have not required EPA to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate explicit authority for promulgating specific control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures in FIPs. Instead, EPA may exercise its broad authority 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under section 110(c) unless such authority is expressly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prohibited under the Act, other statutes, or the United States 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Constitution. EPA's general regulatory authority and its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority under section 110(c) include the ability to impose and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforce pollution fees. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
See 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
42 U.S.C. Sections 110(a)(2)(A); 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
172(c)(6). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Congress has also granted express authority to EPA to set 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions standards for certain sources of pollution, such as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft and new locomotive engines. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
See 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
42 U.S.C. Sections 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
7547, 7571 (a)(2). In vesting EPA with authority to promulgate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft emissions standards, Congress recognized the authority 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to regulate aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
design and manage the navigable airspace to assure safety and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
efficiency. Sections 231 and 232 of the Act establish a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cooperative scheme in which EPA is required to consult with FAA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in setting emissions standards, and FAA is charged with enforcing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those standards. 42 U.S.C. Sections 7571&hyph;7472. The president 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may disapprove any standard after notice and an opportunity for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hearing, based upon a finding by the FAA that the standard would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
create a hazard to safety. Additionally, in section 233, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Congress preempted the states from enacting or enforcing any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission standards for aircraft that did not conform to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal standards. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Id. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 7573. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's broad powers under section 110(c) provide the legal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority for promulgating the control strategy proposed in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP_an emissions fee system based on a declining performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
target expressed in per passenger emissions that can be achieved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through either operational or technological means. EPA is not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exercising its authority under section 231 to promulgate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technology forcing emission standards for aircraft engines as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control strategy in this FIP. Nevertheless, the proposed control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy maintains cooperative interaction between EPA and FAA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and recognizes FAA's expertise in aircraft safety and navigation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA has consulted with FAA in drafting the proposed control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy and will continue to consult and cooperate with FAA in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
finalizing and implementing the control strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA has determined that the proposed control strategy is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
well within the broad scope of powers under section 110(c) and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the strategy is not prohibited under any provisions in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act. As explained elsewhere in this preamble, EPA has solid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
legal authority for fee systems to encourage emission reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Further, EPA believes that the proposed control strategy is not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prohibited by or in direct conflict with other federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations and statutes, such as the Airline Deregulation Act of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1978 (``ADA''), the Airport Noise and Capacity Act of 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(``ANCA''), or the Federal Aviation Act, which establishes FAA for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
overseeing aircraft safety, the efficient use of the navigable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
airspace, and the national air transportation system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00147 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed program is designed to ensure that substantial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reductions are achieved by the aviation industry. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, the strategy neither establishes a hard and fast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions budget or cap for individual commercial airline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operators, nor specifies compliance measures. Instead, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program relies on environmental performance targets or emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate goals with fees for excess emissions. The approach leaves 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each operator with the discretion to choose the best compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy for achieving environmental goals and satisfying market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demands. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A commercial airline may choose to satisfy the emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction requirements with compliance measures ranging from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relatively straightforward options to very challenging, more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difficult approaches. EPA's preliminary analysis suggests that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the lower end of this range includes such measures as converting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ground service equipment to cleaner power sources; increased use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of single engine taxiing; implementing procedural improvements to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduce congestion and taxi/idle times; purchasing cleaner, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current production aircraft; or possibly purchasing emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
credits. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
At the upper end of this range, airlines may choose to tow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft rather than taxi with onboard power; substantially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improve load factors; pursue the production of new engine designs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with low-NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 staged-combustion systems which are presently 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nearing production for certain applications; or develop 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional clean engine technology. An airline which continues 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to have excess emissions after choosing from among these possible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures will pay emission fees on its activities. It might then 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
choose to limit activity to reduce its total fee liability. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Concerns have been raised that this strategy may require airlines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to choose operational measures that, for safety reasons, should be left to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the discretion of the pilot in command. For example, the FAA has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
advised that the decisions to reduce use of reverse thrust and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increase use of single-engine taxiing should ultimately be those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the pilot. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Beyond these choices, concerns have been raised that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance with the no-fee emission reduction targets may depend 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on technology-forcing aircraft engine development, and that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability for noncompliance fees may directly or indirectly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
result in a limitation on activity. EPA requests comments on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
likelihood that, and the point within the range of proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reductions (e.g., 30&hyph;45 percent for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) at which, such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures as advanced engine development may be required. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agency also seeks comment on the legal and policy implications of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a strategy that has the potential to create an economic incentive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for airlines to limit operations at airports in the FIP areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also requests comments on the legal and policy implications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the proposed strategy if, after public comment and further 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
study the Agency determines that at the upper end of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions reduction range under consideration, the requirement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may have the potential to induce airlines to choose between such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
options as encouraging implemention of aircraft operational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures that currently are left to pilot discretion for safety 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasons on the one hand, and limiting the number of operations at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the airport on the other. Finally, comment is requested on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relationship between EPA's legal authority under section 110(c) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and section 231, should the strategy result in the regulated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
industry having as its only options, outside of activity limits, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reliance on either technology-forcing aircraft engine advances to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meet the emission reduction targets, or paying the proposed fees.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Regardless of the means by which compliance is achieved, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed control strategy does not appear to mandate or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
indirectly require an accelerated fleet turnover that would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potentially conflict with the schedule established under ANCA for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reducing noise by the end of 1999 (See 49 U.S.C. App. Section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2157 (a)), because the first FIP controls would not apply until 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2001 and the most stringent controls would not be required until 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2005. The proposed control strategy also appears consistent with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the statutory requirements and mandate for the FAA under the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal Aviation Act, 49 U.S.C. App. 1348. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
During the drafting of the proposed control strategy, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered but rejected requiring the airport proprietor to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement and enforce regulation of the commercial airlines. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rejected this concept and chose for EPA and FAA to implement and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforce the proposed strategy because the proprietor has more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited authority over the commercial airlines and because the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
courts have held that there are limits on EPA's ability to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mandate municipal entities to control the behavior of others by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requiring specific measures. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
See Brown v. EPA, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
521 F.2d 827 (9th 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Cir. 1475), 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
vacated on other grounds
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, 431 U.S. 99 (1977). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
