<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00149 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Persons submitting comments on these issues or other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific aspects of today's proposal should consider and discuss 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the possible impact of their comments on other elements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control program, particularly when such comments propose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative strategies that would achieve smaller or larger 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reductions from commercial aviation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
6
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Aviation Working Group Issues/Concerns To gather information used in preparing today's action, the Agency formed
a working group composed principally of representatives from EPA, FAA, various State and local regulatory authorities,
airport operators, and airlines. The mission of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
working group was to acquire emission inventory data, assess inventory methodologies, and identify operational
and technological issues regarding potential control strategies.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed environmental performance target/fee control program represents a new and innovative strategy for
aviation. EPA has attempted to address many of the issues and concerns 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
raised by the working group members, but recognizes that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant issues and differences of opinion remain. In order 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to facilitate public comment on these issues, EPA has requested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment on specific topics throughout the description of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposal. Nonetheless, EPA is taking an additional step to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure that concerns raised by the airline industry are fully 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aired as part of this rulemaking. The industry's concerns, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submitted by the Airline Transport Association,
<FOOTCITE>78</FOOTCITE>
 are summarized 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as follows:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
78
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
For more detail see the letter from R. Kettler, ATA to R. Wilson, EPA, dated December 27, 1993, available in the rulemaking
docket.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
&bull;Further regulation of aircraft emissions under the FIP is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 unnecessary because VOC emissions from aircraft have already 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 been reduced and will continue to decrease substantially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 through the next decade. Fleet modernization due to market 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 forces and phase out of Stage II aircraft will substantially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 decrease VOC emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Any FIP requirements for aircraft emission reductions would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 be illegal per section 110(a)(5)(C) of the Clean Air Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 It would also run counter to the jurisdiction of DOT to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 ensure a safe and efficient air transportation system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Emission reductions required by the FIP would ultimately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 require limiting the number of flights, which would have a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 devastating effect on the region's economy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Airlines will be subject to NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reductions under a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 regulatory scheme being developed by the International Civil 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Aviation Organization (ICAO). This international approach 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 is necessary to keep from placing U.S. airlines at a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 competitive disadvantage in the global marketplace. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Any FIP proposal that directly or indirectly affects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 aircraft operations would unwisely and unlawfully impinge on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 the control of the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 over the safety and efficiency of airport operations. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 pilot in command is the final authority as to the operation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 of the aircraft. Measures such as single engine taxiing, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 derated takeoffs, and limiting use of reverse thrust on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 landing may pose safety hazards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;EPA has no authority to prescribe a bubble to control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 airport or airline emissions. Proposal of an airline or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 airport-wide emissions cap would require either operational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 changes that raise serious safety and efficiency concerns or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 a reduction in the number of flights. These would violate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 either the Federal Aviation Act, FAA regulations or the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Airline Deregulation Act of 1978. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Airport proprietors are preempted from exerting operational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 controls with regard to aircraft and airlines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Use of a bubble would be unworkable, since neither EPA nor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 airport proprietors have the expertise or the resources to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 supervise and enforce restrictions on the hundreds of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 businesses and operations that are potential emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 sources at an airport. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Control of domestic aircraft emissions under the FIP would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 discriminate unfairly against U.S. air carriers. Foreign 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 carriers, military and general aviation must be subject to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 the same controls as commercial domestic carriers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Any emissions decreases from the closure or realignment of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 military airfields must be credited to the aviation sector 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 as a whole. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;Although the Clean Air Act requires EPA to use a 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 baseline for determining overall emission reduction goals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 for the FIP, EPA should allow the use of other years for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 particular sources. A 1987 baseline year for aircraft would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 be more equitable, because individual airlines have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 voluntarily reducing their VOC emissions since that date by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 phasing in quieter and cleaner engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;As an alternative to allowing an earlier baseline year, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 rule might provide that the benefit of percentage reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 by those industries that have made the least pre-1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 emission reductions be shared with industries that made the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 most pre-1990 progress. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;ATA questions the authority of EPA to regulate airline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 ground service equipment (GSE) emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The airline industry maintains that EPA has unfairly focused 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 on airline GSE regulation, while ignoring similar off-road 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 vehicles used by other industries. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;The airline industry has provided evidence that GSE 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 emissions amount to nearly 40 percent of airline-controlled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 emissions, in contrast to the substantially lower percentage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 estimated by EPA. Thus, there may be much greater 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 opportunity for emission reductions from GSE than EPA has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 previously contemplated. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
&bull;To the extent that GSE emission regulation is permissible, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA should develop a reasonable phase-in schedule for such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 regulations to allow for the development of appropriate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 controls and to work through the associated technical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 problems. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Agency requests comments on the concerns and issues 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
raised by the airline industry. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00150 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(c) Military Aviation. (
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
1
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Overview. (
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Military Air Base Operations. Military air bases are defined for the purpose of this proposal as facilities owned
and operated by the Department of Defense with the primary mission of aircraft operation. Aircraft operation emissions,
defined here as emissions from aircraft themselves, dominate total emissions at a military air base. Like commercial
airports, ground support equipment and ground access vehicles also contribute to a military air base's total emissions.
Mobile source-related emissions from these ``other base operations'' sources have never been fully inventoried.
EPA has established a working group in conjunction with the Department of Defense (DOD) to compile and assess complete
emission profiles for all air bases. The information that is currently available is contained in the Technical Support
Document, which is available in the rulemaking docket for today's proposal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Table F lists the military installations in the control areas where aircraft activity has been identified, ranging
from active air bases with intensive activity, to bases with only minimal transient operations. The South Coast Air
Basin contains most of the installations. As an example of their significance in FIP areas, emissions from military
aircraft operations in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
South Coast air basin currently are about 20 percent of those generated by commercial aircraft operations, or about
0.3 percent of the total South Coast Air basin's 1990 baseline emissions inventory based on current inventory data.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 A military air base usually has specific aircraft based on-site; the operational characteristics for these aircraft
can vary widely depending on the specific mission requirements for the based unit or units. Transient operations
resulting from military aircraft based elsewhere can comprise a significant portion of a military air base's total
aircraft operations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
depending on specific military requirements, but most of these transient operations are unscheduled and difficult
to characterize. The military aircraft fleet experiences relatively slow turnover; aircraft remain in service
for many years, and new airframe designs enter the fleet at a relatively slow pace. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The ongoing base closure and realignment process introduces an important element into the process of developing
a control strategy for military aircraft operations. The Secretary of Defense and the Defense Base Closure and Realignment
Commission have recommended a series of defense base closures across the nation, including the FIP areas, as well
as a concurrent sequence 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of ``realignment'' actions with shifts in the location and intensity of military activity. The closure of several
air bases within the control areas represents the more significant impact on the control strategies described here.
Of the seven military air installations located in the South Coast area, three are scheduled for closure and one will
be realigned. Aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations at the realigned installation are expected to decrease. (See the table below for the air bases recommended
for closure.) As a result of the Base Realignment and Closure (BRAC) process, emissions from military aircraft will
be significantly reduced by the year 2000. For example, emissions of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 will decline by at least 50 percent in the South Coast control area as a result of air base closures, according to current
information. (See the Technical Support Document for further discussion.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Certain bases are slated for re-use by civilian entities following closure; this re-use may include aircraft activity.
Some of any such activity may represent relocation of current or future growth in civilian aviation from other airports
in the respective air basins; some may be activity which would not have occurred in that basin but for the availability
of the closed base.  
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
Military Air Bases in California FIP Areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
Military Agency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
South Coast Air Basin 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
Production Flight Test-Air Force Plant 42_Air Force 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
El Toro MCAS [closing]_Marine Corps 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Los Alamitos AAF_Army National Guard 
