<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00151 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Control Strategy. As with commercial operations, the EPA examined various options for reducing total emissions
from military aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations and other base operations. These options included: (1) exempting military aircraft operations from
FIP requirements while maintaining a cap on all other mobile source emissions from other base operations at military
air stations; (2) an absolute cap on total emissions (including aircraft) under the direct control of the Department
of Defense; and (3) a target based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program using an allowable emission rate and a fee for excess emissions above this rate, like that proposed for commercial
airports. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes the first of these three options. EPA is proposing to exclude military aircraft operations from FIP requirements
for national security reasons, as explained in more detail below. Applying restrictions on military aircraft operations
will inevitably degrade training and combat readiness. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Restrictions would impair the ability of the Department of Defense to perform assigned missions (including humanitarian
and natural disaster relief, surveillance, and logistic support). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The options available for reducing emissions from military aircraft without requiring a significant decrease in
the number of aircraft operations are minimal. Military aircraft are designed to specific mission requirements
including power, maneuver, lift, and load. Application of control technologies to existing aircraft would degrade
characteristics inherent in military aircraft design. Arbitrary reductions in aircraft operations that may be
required to meet the second or third alternative control strategies listed above may be inconsistent with training
and operational requirements necessary for national security. In selecting this proposal to exempt military aircraft

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations from the FIP requirements, EPA has considered the approved base closings within California which will
provide significant reductions in current emissions from stationary, aircraft and other mobile sources under the
direct control of the Department of Defense. One consequence of these closures is that the reductions in operations
least harmful to military readiness have already been made. The incremental risk to readiness of further reductions
in operations is therefore that much more. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The DOD established an internal FIP working group in December 1992 to examine the problems associated with potential
FIP strategies, identify impacts to military operations and readiness, and collect data from military installations
in the FIP areas. Several concerns have been identified. The principal concern is that although military aircraft
operations contribute 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a very small portion of the total area-wide emissions, they would be severely restricted within an emissions cap.
On military air installations, the predominant source of mobile emissions is aircraft and aircraft support operations.
Severe restrictions will inevitably degrade training and combat readiness necessary to minimize the risks associated
with military aviation. An emission cap or reduction in training operations may impair the ability to perform assigned
national missions (including humanitarian relief, surveillance and logistics support). The options available
for reducing mobile source emissions from military air stations without requiring a decrease in aircraft operations
are minimal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Another issue is the limited ability for DOD to transfer aircraft to military air stations outside the FIP areas. To
move a specific aircraft type requires that the receiving installation have the necessary infrastructure to accommodate
that particular aircraft type. Additionally, permanent moves of military assets and personnel are also not fully
at the discretion of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military. Any significant base closure action or realignment that meets certain thresholds requires that the action
be presented to the BRAC Commission and subsequently be presented to Congress. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00152 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Based on the impact the second and third possible FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control strategies (listed above) would have on military 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
readiness, and the already scheduled decrease in military 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft operations, EPA proposes that military aircraft be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exempt from the FIP. EPA requests comments on excluding military 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft from the FIP control strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP program will control mobile source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from other base operations under the control of DOD. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These sources include APUs, GSE, captive vehicle fleets, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
privately-owned vehicles, and any other mobile sources operated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within an air base's boundaries. The military bubble also will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include DOD-controlled non-aircraft emissions sources that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operate at locations other than military bases, such as ground 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment based at civilian airports. The bubble will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basin-wide rather than air base-specific, given the variability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
introduced by base closure and realignment activities, as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the need for operational flexibility. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes to impose a declining cap on total emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for controlling these other base operations emissions from all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air bases within each FIP area. This avoids the difficulty of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
having to allocate baseline emissions among multiple entities (e. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
g., perhaps down to individual air bases). This approach also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
takes advantage of the military's centralized command structure; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
DOD (or each branch of the armed forces) can mandate air base 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance at a certain emissions level (excluding aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions) commensurate with environmental objectives while 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintaining the flexibility to stage specific missions and other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
functions among various bases inside or outside of the basin. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This control strategy provides individual base commanders with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the flexibility to implement specific operational and procedural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures necessary to achieve the specified emissions goal, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
avoids having EPA attempt to implement specific control measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for military operations. The centralized command structure means 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that a single entity is the focus of the control strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An absolute emissions cap approach does not require a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projection of activity in future years, one of the required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assumptions for a program based on allowable emissions per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
activity unit. Finally, a control strategy that fundamentally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relies upon the environmental efficiency of operations, such as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the emission rate-based program proposed for commercial aircraft, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appears less desirable for military air bases given the inherent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unpredictability of military operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also seeks comments on the second alternative approach, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to establish a declining cap for an emissions bubble which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes military aircraft emissions and therefore requires 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in aircraft emissions also. Further, comments are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requested on the desirability of the third possible control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approach, an approach that includes aircraft emissions but is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on a target average emission rate per aircraft operation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with a fee for emissions above the target. This third approach 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be similar to that being proposed today for commercial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aviation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00153 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
With respect to the second and third possible control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approaches, several additional issues have been identified if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military aircraft operations are included within the scope of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
bubble program. EPA requests comment on the following issues. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If emissions from aircraft are included, a fee system based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on an emission rate target (i.e., the third approach), consistent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the proposed strategy for commercial airlines, or a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variation of the second approach but with a fee for emissions in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excess of the cap, may be more equitable and desirable than an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
absolute emissions cap that applies uniquely to military 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aviation. Emissions that exceed the emissions target would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to emission fees instead of an enforcement-related fine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or penalty. The emission target would apply to air emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only during the ozone season in each FIP area. The method of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining the excess emissions and the resulting fee would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addressed in subsequent rulemaking. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Another issue identified involves the method of allotting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the emission caps or emission rate targets by the EPA. To be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
most effective, the DOD Working Group recommends that any caps on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total emissions, targets for total emissions, or emission rate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
targets which include aircraft emissions be allocated to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual Military Departments. Each Military Department will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be responsible for attaining the target for installations under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its control. This system is consistent with existing DOD command 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
structure and can be readily implemented. Trading between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Military Departments and between installations is proposed. Trading is applicable only to installations within
the same FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To reduce the potential impact on aircraft operations of an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions cap or emission rate target which includes aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions, the DOD working group identified as essential the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ability to trade excess emission reductions between stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and mobile sources. Maximum flexibility and minimal operational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impacts can be achieved by allowing trading between stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and mobile sources, in addition to trading between all types of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military installations within the same FIP area. In other words, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major reductions or elimination of a stationary source may allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continued essential flight operations. EPA acknowledges this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concern and supports such trading programs as created by State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and local governments as discussed elsewhere in today's proposal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Of particular concern to the Navy with respect to the second 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and third options identified above is the inclusion of Naval 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Auxiliary Landing Field San Clemente Island (SCI) in the list of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military air installations regulated by the FIP. SCI is an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
isolated and remote area, with few emissions sources, either 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile or stationary, other than aircraft. The impact on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft operations will be substantial, as almost the entire 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
amount of required reductions will have to be a result of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
decreased aircraft operations. SCI is the primary training 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
location for Navy pilots prior to carrier deployment. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
training is vital and represents a significant military readiness 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issue. SCI is located 55 miles southwest of Long Beach. Meteorological data indicates that SCI emissions do not impact

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast Air Basin, and at best the contribution of air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from SCI to the South Coast air quality is not well 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
defined. Consistent with EPA's request for comment on exempting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento and Ventura areas from the FIP commercial airport 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements, EPA also requests comments on exempting emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from on or near San Clemente Island from the FIP control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy, if aircraft are ultimately included in the final rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed emission reduction requirements under a bubble 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concept for military air bases_regardless of whether it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes aircraft emissions_would be generally consistent with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the emission cap requirements for stationary sources in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas. Although not firmly decided, the current stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source control strategy for the South Coast requires that VOC and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions be reduced by 20&hyph;45 and 30&hyph;45 percent, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
respectively, from the 1990 baseline inventory by 2005, through a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
series of uniform annual reductions of baseline emissions of each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pollutant: 4&hyph;9 percent per year for VOC and 6&hyph;9 percent per year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, both starting in 2001. Stationary source requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the 2006&hyph;2010 period have not been established at this time. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Until modified in a future rulemaking, the 2005 cap will apply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
through 2010. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The requirements for Ventura from 2001&hyph;2005 are identical to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that for the South Coast. For Sacramento, the strategy requires 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a VOC reduction of 20&hyph;45 percent, with uniform annual reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of 4&hyph;9 percent. The timeframe for achieving this reduction is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the same as for the South Coast and Ventura areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00154 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For this proposal the overall reduction targets are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expressed as a range of percentages; EPA anticipates establishing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a specific percentage reduction target in the final rule. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requests comment on whether these stationary source emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction targets are appropriate for use with the military air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base control strategy. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A military air base can utilize any of a variety of emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction options, at the discretion of the Department of Defense 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the base commander, to meet the annual emissions target. For 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example, reductions in ground equipment could be obtained through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
GSE electrification and restrictions on ground traffic within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base boundaries. If the final rule does include emissions from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military aircraft, a base commander may choose to restrict 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft taxi procedures, perhaps by mandating specific direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
routes to and from runways, or by towing aircraft into takeoff 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
position; certain training procedures could be altered to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimize the frequency of high-emission operating modes within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the basin mixing zone. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Geographic Scope. The control program for military air bases will cover the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
three FIP areas: South Coast, Ventura, and Sacramento Air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Basins. The Agency requests comments on the need to control air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base emissions in each area based on air quality effects or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equity considerations. In particular, EPA requests comments on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exempting emissions from on or near San Clemente Island from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP control strategy, if aircraft are ultimately included in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
d
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Control Period. EPA proposes to control military air base emissions only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during the ozone season in each FIP area. Further, EPA proposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to require compliance with the program based on the season-long 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average of emissions. As already described, such operations may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be highly variable. Although this variability tends to argue for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a shorter compliance (e.g., a monthly period as in the stationary 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cap program), the longer period seems preferable to provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military commanders with the flexibility to conduct operations as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary. EPA requests comment on the desirability of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seasonal compliance demonstration versus shorter periods. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Key Regulatory Elements. (
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Summary. EPA proposes to specify in the final FIP a linear declining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cap on total mobile source emissions from military air bases in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each FIP area, excluding emissions from aircraft themselves. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This declining cap will be designed to achieve the emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction targets for each area as described under ``Control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Strategy.'' Military air bases will submit a baseline emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
report and annual compliance determination reports similar to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
those required for commercial airlines as discussed previously. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Military air bases are defined for the purpose of this proposal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as facilities owned and operated by the Department of Defense 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with a primary mission of aircraft operation. The Department of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Defense also will be responsible for submitting baseline 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions reports and annual compliance reports for military 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations that occur at civilian facilities in the control area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Baseline Inventory and Emission Caps. EPA currently does not have sufficient data for all subject 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission sources included in the proposed military air base 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control program to establish a reasonable baseline inventory. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA, therefore, will require each military air base in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control area to calculate and report total subject emissions for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the baseline year of 1990 within 180 days following finalization 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of this rule. Information to be included in the baseline report 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is described in ``Reporting and Recordkeeping'' below. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military bubble will be the sum of emission inventories from all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air bases in the basin. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comment on the desirability of using an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative baseline scheme, given the variability introduced by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations related to Desert Shield/Desert Storm during 1990. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
One alternative could be to require air bases to submit data for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1989 and 1990 so that a two-year average inventory can be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated. The DOD working group recommended that installations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have the option of identifying the baseline year, between the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years 1989 and 1992, which resulted in the most representative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total emissions. The DOD working group also requested a one time 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adjustment of the baseline inventory be allowed, based on actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inventory data, once the FIP requirements are in effect. The one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time adjustment could help eliminate errors based on assumptions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and estimates used in developing the baseline. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00155 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA will establish in a subsequent rulemaking action a series of declining seasonal emissions allowances for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and VOC as described above using the modified baseline emissions inventory. Both the baseline inventory and the seasonal
targets will be expressed as allowable tons of pollutant emitted on average per day during the control period.  
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An additional concern regards the requirement for baseline inventories for installations that are closing. The
DOD working group further recommended that inventories not be required for bases closing before 2000. Emission sources
are already being phased out and there would be no benefit derived from funding such inventories in terms of helping
to design emission control strategies for those bases. On the other hand, the estimates of emissions eliminated by
base closures will affect the effective stringency of a declining cap program if the baseline for the cap was prior
to closure since there will be credit for base closings. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Averaging and Trading. EPA proposes to determine compliance for military air base operations by averaging emissions
across the control area, rather than establishing specific seasonal emission limits for individual air bases. Such
a control strategy would provide the military with the flexibility to transfer operations between air bases as needed
to meet realignment requirements or other specific mission needs, as long as the overall emissions reduction target
is met. Alternatively, the Agency could establish seasonal emission allowances for each military air base in the
control area. EPA requests comment on the desirability of using area-wide averaging to determine military compliance,
and on possible alternative compliance determination schemes.  
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Agency also requests comment on the inclusion of military air base operations in any civilian emission credit
trading scheme if such a program is established by a local government. Such an expansion could provide the Department
of Defense with additional incentive to implement emission reduction measures that generate tradable credits,
or provide a mechanism for obtaining credits from other sources to ensure compliance should unforeseen difficulties
arise. (See the discussion of averaging and trading for commercial aviation for more detail of this topic.)  
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Several of the military air installations in the FIP areas have already been identified for closure (Table F), and
military operations will cease at the identified bases prior to the year 2000. These reductions do not even consider
BRAC 95 which could result in further reductions. The DOD Working Group recommends that any emission reductions resulting
from closure or realignment should be credited to the Military Department. These credits would then be available
for transfer by the Military Department as part of the President's five point plan for community redevelopment at
closing bases and/or to meet service Department needs within the specific FIP areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
d
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Annual Compliance Determination. Beginning in 1999, military air bases will be required to submit annual emissions
reports as described below. The total military emissions inventory from subject mobile sources at all air bases in
the area will be compared to the given year's seasonal emissions target to determine compliance with the FIP requirements.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
e
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Reporting and recordkeeping. (i) Compliance Plans. The stationary source cap program includes a requirement for
affected sources to submit compliance plans that describe planned emission reduction measures. EPA could establish
a similar requirement for military air bases as part of today's proposed control strategy. Such compliance plans
would be submitted by Department of Defense in advance of the control period and would describe in some detail the various
emission reduction measures planned for air base operations in the control areas.  
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Compliance plans would impose a significant administrative burden on EPA and the Department of Defense, however.
EPA therefore requests comment on the desirability of requiring compliance plans, and on the information that should
be provided should they be required. EPA does not contemplate that these plans, if required, would legally bind the
military, since that would impede flexibility and is not essential to the effectiveness of the program. However,
any deliberate misrepresentation in the compliance plan may be made a violation of the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
