<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA requests comment on potential alternatives to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seasonal control period proposed today. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA proposes to use 1990 as the baseline year for each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military air base to establish a baseline emissions level, but 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also requests comment on the desirability of using an alternative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
baseline scheme, given the variability introduced by operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related to Desert Shield/Desert Storm. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA requests comment on the desirability of using area-wide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
averaging to determine military compliance, and on possible 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative compliance determination schemes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA requests comment on the desirability of issuing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional specific technical guidance concerning emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculations for military air base operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA requests comment on alternative methods of tracking and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reporting emissions resulting from military operations at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
civilian airports. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA requests comment on providing exemptions from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of any control program for certain types of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emergency or atypical military operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA requests comment on the desirability and feasibility of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishing a de minimis exemption for any control program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 EPA requests comment on national preparedness and response 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flexibility versus the degree and scope of the proposed emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Persons submitting comments on these issues or other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific aspects of today's proposal should consider and discuss 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the possible impact of their comments on other elements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control program, particularly when such comments propose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative strategies that would achieve smaller or larger 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reductions from military air bases. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(d) General Aviation. (
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
1
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Source Description. The general aviation category consists of privately-owned 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft operated under FAA Parts 91, 133, or 137. As such, it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
encompasses aircraft types with a variety of different engines, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
including piston, turboprop, and turbine. It also includes both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fixed-wing and rotary-wing aircraft. Most general aviation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft are owned and operated by private individuals. Other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft are owned and operated by businesses. Much of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general aviation fleet is old, with few significant technological 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
improvements in emissions performance expected in the foreseeable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
future. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 General aviation aircraft operate at a variety of facilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the control areas, ranging from large commercial airports to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
small privately-owned airstrips and helipads. The table below 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(``Civil Airports in FIP Areas'') lists the civil airfields in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control areas, based on information provided by the FAA and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in the Technical Support Document. In addition to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these civil airfields, FAA data for 1991 lists 198 helipads in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast control area, 3 helipads in the Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control area, and 12 helipads in the Ventura control area; this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes private helipads, medical facilities, and various local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
government facilities. 
