<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00158 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Much less specific information is available concerning the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions resulting from general aviation activity than is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available for commercial and military aircraft operations. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FAA collects some information on general aviation operations at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
airports with FAA-operated (or contractor-operated) control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
towers, but these data are not comprehensive, and do not capture 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations occurring at non-tower-controlled facilities. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particular, the lack of data specifically addressing general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aviation operations by type of aircraft makes an accurate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment of the emissions from this source more difficult. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Overall Control Strategy. Although general aviation operations represent a small 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fraction of total emissions in most FIP areas, EPA is proposing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to include this source in the FIP control strategy along with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
commercial and military aircraft operations as part of the broad, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comprehensive control scheme for these areas. As described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below, developing an effective comprehensive control strategy for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this source, and effectively administering it, is an especially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
challenging task due to the diverse aircraft types and usage, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
great number of individual owners/operators, and the large number 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of airfields in each most FIP areas. Nonetheless, if this source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were excluded from the FIP, the potential reductions from this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
category would have to be achieved from another source. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Today's proposal would establish an operations fee to reduce 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the emissions from general aviation activity during the ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
season in each FIP area. The Agency intends that the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approach provide the same level of benefits as the emission cap 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements for stationary sources in each of the FIP areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(See section III.D.4.e.(2)(a)(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
4
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
), above, for the specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percentage reductions.) Comments are also requested on three 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative control schemes. EPA has chosen not to apply a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comprehensive emission cap to this source because the significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data requirements and level of EPA involvement in such a program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
make that strategy impractical. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In developing the proposed strategy, EPA notes that there 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appear to be few, if any, regulatory options for innovatively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reducing emissions from general aviation aircraft, given the age 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and emissions performance of the general aviation fleet, and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
economic status of the general aviation industry. Unlike many 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other sources, improving the emissions performance of these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aircraft through new, industry-wide emission standards is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
problematic. Even if cleaner engines could be made available, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the general aviation fleet is unlikely to experience significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
introduction of cleaner alternatives, since fleet turnover is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relatively low (sales of general aviation aircraft in fact have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
declined in the past several years). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This is not to suggest that emission benefits are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unavailable through the careful selection of aircraft/engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combinations or the possible limited introduction of new models 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the future. Rather, there are no substantially cleaner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternatives to current technology engines for the vast majority 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the fleet. Therefore, the strategies that rely on turnover 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to cleaner technologies are unavailable in this instance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Operational and procedural measures such as reduced-power 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
takeoffs and direct routing from hardstand to runway also would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be difficult to implement for general aviation on an area-wide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
basis, given the widely varying aircraft and facility 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
characteristics found amongst general aviation operations. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures also offer little opportunity for emission reduction, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
due to minimal congestion at most general aviation airports, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
short taxi distances, and relatively low power-range engines. As 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a result, the only available measures that would provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significant emission benefits appear to involve activity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The diverse and diffuse nature of general aviation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operations creates significant obstacles for any emission control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. General aviation aircraft and the facilities at which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
they operate are controlled by a large and varied group of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individuals and organizations. General aviation operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
typically occur on a highly variable schedule and many operations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
can occur at completely uncontrolled airports. Despite these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
difficulties, EPA believes that an emission reduction program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general aviation could be implemented. The specific proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control measures and alternatives discussed below reflect varying 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
degrees of direct EPA involvement and participation by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general aviation community, with resultant variation in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certainty of obtaining emissions reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The control strategy applies to general aviation aircraft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operated at commercial airports as well as those operated at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
public and private general aviation facilities. It does not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apply to civilian general aviation aircraft operations at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
military bases; these are included in the military control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategy described above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Comments are specifically requested on the need to control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from general aviation in each area based on local air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quality concerns, given the potential difficulty and likely 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resource-intensive nature of administering an effective program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
3
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Key Regulatory Elements. The EPA proposes an operations fee program to reduce 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions from general aviation activity. By implementing a fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system, EPA can avoid establishing direct allocation of landing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
slots for general aviation as discussed later in this section, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thereby maintaining the flexibility that is desirable for general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aviation operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
