<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00159 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA specifically proposes two options for an operations fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. The first option implements a takeoff fee for all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
general aviation operations in the affected areas. Conceptually, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fee would be high enough to limit general aviation activity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
without making such activity prohibitively expensive. The second 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program option also implements a takeoff fee for all general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aviation activity in these areas, but would incorporate a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lifetime fee exemption for engines/aircraft meeting ``clean'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission levels. For example, aircraft engines with emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comparable to the levels originally required by EPA in 1973 (38 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FR 19088), but subsequently withdrawn, would be deemed ``clean'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the purpose of this program and would receive a lifetime 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exemption from any FIP general aviation takeoff fees. Certified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines/aircraft would be identified by a certificate or decal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As with commercial aircraft, EPA proposes an initial fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based on $10,000 per ton of pollutant, and will commit in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
final FIP to review the effect of this fee and adjust it upwards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if necessary to achieve the target emission reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Emissions per LTO based on a representative fleet of general 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aviation aircraft are:
<FOOTCITE>79</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
