<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Retarded Injection Timing. A feasible and simple means of reducing NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 from diesel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines is by retarding injection timing. This method lowers the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
peak combustion temperature and pressure in the cylinder, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
resulting in lower levels of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. This strategy has been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated to reduce NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions by 10&hyph;30 percent. However, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
disadvantages include higher specific fuel consumption, lower 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
power, harder startability, and higher levels of HC, CO, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
particulate matter and smoke emissions. To recover the lost fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
economy and performance or to reduce the amount of injection 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
timing retard, additional technologies that improve fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
atomization have been employed. Fuel atomization can be improved 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by increasing fuel pump pressure, improving fuel pump advance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
strategies, and incorporating smaller injector nozzle tip holes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Engine Fine Tuning.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Engine fine tuning includes modification of essential engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
components and could result in 20&hyph;40 percent reduction in NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions. More specifically, engine fine tuning could include 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modifications in the injection system, charge air system, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combustion chamber design. Such changes on new onhighway engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have already achieved more than 50 percent NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reductions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, retrofitting such changes on existing engines makes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equally large reductions for marine vessels unlikely. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Exhaust Gas Recirculation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Exhaust gas recirculation involves recirculating some of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust gas back into the intake manifold. This lowers the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combustion temperature and therefore can lower NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as much as 20&hyph;50 percent. For marine engines, the applicability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of EGR is complicated by the quality of the fuel. Sulfur and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
soot from combustion gases can cause increased wear of piston 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rings, valves, and other components. Therefore, EGR is more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
likely to be used with engines running on cleaner distillate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fuels. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
d
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Water Emulsified Fuel.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Water emulsification of the fuel is a technique which also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lowers maximum combustion temperature, reducing NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 by 20&hyph;50 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
percent without an increase in fuel consumption. There are at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
least two ways to accomplish the emulsification during 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combustion: in the combustion chamber or in the fuel tank. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Combining water and fuel for the first time in the chamber 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requires significant changes to the cylinder head to add an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
injector. Combining water with fuel in the tank may introduce 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combustion problems due to unstable emulsion. Also, this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technique requires a significantly redesigned fuel handling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system to overcome the potential risk of corrosion and to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintain power output. In any event, extra liquid storage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
availability is necessary to retain similar range. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
e
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Selective Catalytic Reduction (SCR).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 One of the most effective, but also most complex and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expensive, means of reducing NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 from large diesel engines is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the use of selective catalytic reduction (SCR). Emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in excess of 90 percent can be achieved using SCR. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SCR systems, a reducing agent, such as ammonia, is injected into 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the exhaust and both are channelled through a catalyst where NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions are reduced. These systems are being successfully used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for large stationary source applications which operate under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
constant, high load conditions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
A number of disadvantages are apparent for the use of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current technology SCR systems on ships. The SCR system is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effective only over a narrow range of exhaust temperatures. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effectiveness of the system is decreased at reduced temperatures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhibited during engine operation at partial loads. Also, excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ammonia in the exhaust (referred to as ``ammonia slip'') can occur 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
during transient operation, where control of optimum ammonia 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
injection is difficult. However, non-toxic urea can be used in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
place of toxic ammonia. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SCR is currently being used in the South Coast region on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
three new ships built with the technology incorporated (not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
retrofitted). Retrofitting ships with this technology is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
problematic and raises significant space and cost concerns. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SCR systems are very bulky and require high investment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operational, and maintenance costs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
f
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Speed Reduction.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 A potential operational method for reducing NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 is speed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction. By reducing speed, the load requirements on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine are reduced. Speed reduction cannot be applied to all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different ship types and sizes in the same manner, and may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
beneficial only for certain types and sizes. Also, speed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduction does not address emissions from auxiliary engines. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
addition, many coastal areas already have factors that limit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vessel speed; for example, amount of traffic, distance to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shore and port, and water depth. Since speed reduction will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affect the amount of cargo transported within a specified time, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional transport capacity or travel time must be accounted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for when calculating emission benefits from such a strategy. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requests comments on how speed reduction could be figured into 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fee scheme. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The technologies listed above are not intended to be an all- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inclusive list of potential emission reduction technologies. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requests comments on other technologies that may be employed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reduce emission of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 from diesel marine engines. Comments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should address the emission reduction potential, technological 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
feasibility, cost, and safety issues of additional NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methods. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
