<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Refineries may choose whether to conform to the flat limits which apply to all batches of gasoline when it is initially
sold from the product facility, or to the average limits which apply to the average of all fuel produced by a given refinery.
In the case of a refiner choosing averaging, the absolute cap or absolute limit also must be met. California is also
allowing the sale of alternative fuel formulations which meet the emission reduction potential of a fuel meeting
the average limits. However, even these alternative gasolines must still meet the cap limits described above. Gasoline
meeting the average limits will reduce VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions by 17&hyph;18 and 13&hyph;14 percent (relative to pre-1995), respectively, according to EPA's complex
model as revised in November 1993. Thus, California Phase II gasoline should provide at least as much VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emission reductions as the federal Phase II standards.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In its recent rule, EPA determined that fuel meeting California's Phase II standards will produce greater emission
benefits than federal Phase I reformulated gasoline and it was therefore appropriate to exempt California gasoline
from the enforcement procedures for the federal Phase I standards. Thus, there would be no attainment benefit to requiring
federal Phase I reformulated gasoline to be sold in Sacramento, although emissions reductions would be achieved
during the summer of 1995. At this time it appears from the available data that California's fuel achieves as much reduction
as federal Phase II reformulated gasoline and therefore EPA is not proposing any changes to California's gasoline
program today. If later EPA determines that further reductions can be achieved through use of either federal Phase
II reformulated gasoline or some new formulation, EPA will propose appropriate changes to the fuel program in California
as a FIP revision if such reductions are needed.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. California Reformulated Diesel Fuel.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 On August 21, 1990, EPA finalized its low-sulfur diesel rulemaking. CARB adopted their clean diesel fuel regulations
in November 1988 and on December 26, 1991, enacted their clean diesel fuel program. Both the federal and CARB programs
became effective on October 1, 1993. The two programs are similar, and are designed to substantially reduce sulfate
particulate and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions, and to allow manufacturers to comply with 1994 and newer emission standards for diesel vehicles. Important
differences exist between California and the federal program, as outlined below.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The EPA program only applies to diesel fuel for use in on-highway vehicles. Trucks, automobiles, and buses would be
affected, for example, but construction and farm equipment would not, unless they chose to use the low-sulfur diesel
fuel. The regulation sets a 0.05 percent by weight sulfur limit for all on-road diesels, and requires a minimum cetane
index of 40 or maximum 35 percent by volume aromatics percentage. The current maximum sulfur content in on-highway
diesel fuel is approximately 500 ppm. Since direct measurement of aromatics is a somewhat complicated procedure,
EPA chose to use a minimum cetane index as a surrogate for capping aromatics. Few refiners have had trouble meeting
this requirement and those who cannot can sell fuel into the nonroad market. The price differential between high-sulfur
and low-sulfur fuels is expected to be approximately 2&cent;/gallon once initial distribution blips have resolved
themselves.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00181 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The California program applies to vehicular diesel fuel. Essentially any vehicle with wheels, including construction
and farm equipment, is covered. The regulation establishes a 500 ppm sulfur limit as well as a 10 percent cap on aromatics
(20 percent for small refiners). Diesel normally has about 30 percent aromatics. California and EPA believe aromatics
contribute to the formation of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and particulate emissions. California includes an equivalency provision which allows refiners to make diesel with
more than 10 percent aromatics if engine testing demonstrates equivalent emissions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Most if not all of the large California refiners are pursuing higher-aromatic equivalent fuels. While a few oil companies
have received CARB certification for their equivalent fuels, only Chevron has gone public with its formulation at
this time. One of Chevron's alternatives has 19 percent aromatics with 200 ppm sulfur and a cetane number of 59. The
company estimates that it can manufacture this fuel at an incremental cost of 6&hyph;7 cents per gallon. This is about
half the incremental cost to make a strict 10 percent aromatics fuel. California has granted temporary waivers from
the requirements provided the refiner pays 6&cent;/gallon into a trust fund. However, prices in California initially
jumped far more than 20 cents per gallon and California is in the process of investigating its rule.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that California's rule is likely to achieve NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions reductions which will not be achieved by the federal program. CARB estimates these reductions will be approximately
4&hyph;7 percent of diesel engine NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. EPA therefore is not proposing any changes to California's diesel fuel regulations. EPA is working with California
to help understand the reasons behind the huge price rise, supply, and injector/pump seal problems and will take note
of any change to California's rule. The South Coast and Ventura have been using the low-sulfur fuel since 1985 without
any problems.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Even if California were to relax its rule, EPA's diesel fuel standards still apply. However, since the FIP takes credit
for the California rule which delivers more NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 credits than the federal rule, EPA may have to propose additional FIP reductions if the California rule were to be relaxed.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
F. Severe Area Requirements for Sacramento
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Introduction
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed in section III.B.3., EPA is proposing attainment demonstrations for 1999 and 2005 for the Sacramento
FIP. If EPA promulgates a 2005 attainment demonstration, the Sacramento area will be reclassified as a severe ozone
nonattainment area and become responsible for meeting all applicable deadlines and requirements for SIP purposes,
which include the requirements outlined in section III.B.3.e. As part of the alternative proposal for 2005, EPA will
address the additional severe area requirements with the following.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Employee Commute Options Program
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Introduction and Statutory Requirements.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 In the alternative that EPA bumps up Sacramento to ``Severe'' and establishes a 2005 attainment date, EPA proposes
that an Employee Commute Options (ECO) program be included in the FIP package. Because more people are driving than
ever before and they are driving longer distances, the ECO provision was included in the Clean Air Act Amendments of
1990 at section 182(d)(1)(B) (42 U.S.C. 7511a(d)(1)(B)). Currently, increasing numbers of drivers and vehicle
miles traveled (VMT) offset a large portion of the emissions reductions achieved through producing and selling vehicles
that operate more cleanly. Without limits on increasing vehicle travel, the emissions caused by more vehicles being
driven more miles under more congested conditions will outweigh the emissions reduced per vehicle from cleaner fuels
and improved emission control technologies. Ultimately, this will result in an overall increase in motor vehicle
emissions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00182 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Section 182(d)(1)(B) of the CAA requires State or nonattainment areas with ozone pollution levels defined as ``Severe''
or ``Extreme'' under the CAA to develop and implement an ECO program designed to reduce work-related vehicle trips
and vehicle miles travelled during peak commuting periods. Specifically, section 182(d)(1)(B) requires employers
with 100 or more employees to participate in a trip reduction program. These employers must submit compliance plans
to the state or governing agency two years after an ECO regulation is submitted to EPA for inclusion in the SIP. These
employer compliance plans must convincingly demonstrate an increase in the average passenger occupancy (APO) of
their employees who commute to work during the peak travel period by no less than 25 percent above the average vehicle
occupancy (AVO) of the nonattainment area during the peak travel period. These compliance plans must also convincingly
demonstrate that employers will meet the 25 percent increase APO target no later than four years after the ECO regulation
is submitted to EPA.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. ECO program requirements and Sacramento ECO regulation implementation approach. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed ECO regulation meets the basic ECO program requirements:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) calculate an AVO for the nonattainment area or for each zone if the area is divided into zones;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) specify target APO(s) which are no less than 25 percent above the AVO(s);
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) include enforcement procedures to ensure that employers develop and implement compliance plans; and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) provide an ECO program that has a process for ensuring that compliance plans are a ``convincing'' compliance demonstration.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed ECO regulation uses data collected from the 1991 Caltrans (California Department of Transportation)
State Travel Survey to produce the baseline AVO for the nonattainment area and the target APO for the regulation. This
survey estimated Sacramento regional AVO to be 1.11 in 1991. It is EPA's opinion that no substantial changes in travel
patterns have taken place since that time such that there is reason to believe this number has changed since 1991. Consequently,
a 25 percent increase in AVO produces a regional APO target of 1.38; the performance target included within the ECO
regulation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Caltrans Statewide Travel Study attempted to estimate the number of persons telecommuting by asking the question,
``do you work at home?''. This survey question does not distinguish between those who work at home because they are
self-employed and their business is located in their home and those who work away from home but participate in an employer-sponsored
telecommuting program. EPA believes that a large percentage of those who work at home would be self-employed. Consequently,
any APO target based on the Caltrans study and including all of these home workers as telecommuters would be inflated
by some degree and result in a regional target APO of 1.46. EPA has chosen the 1.38 APO target because it is comparatively
a more reliable baseline AVO estimate.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The third ECO program requirement concerns sufficient enforcement procedures. Through the provisions of the ECO
regulation, EPA must be able to invoke penalties that are severe enough to provide an adequate incentive for employers
to comply and no less than the expected cost of compliance. Section 113 of the Act provides the statutory authority
for federal enforcement and the ability to assess penalties for violations under this ECO regulation. EPA may assess
civil or criminal penalties up to $25,000 per day per violation; however, Section 113 requires EPA to consider the
size of the business, the economic impact of the penalty on the business, the violator's compliance history and good
faith efforts to comply, the duration of the violation, payment of penalties for previous similar violations, the
economic benefit of noncompliance, and the seriousness of the violation in assessing a penalty fee.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, Section 182(d)(1)(B) requires that employer compliance plans ``convincingly demonstrate'' prospective
compliance with the target APO. EPA's 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
ECO Guidance 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
defines four ECO regulation design options for eliciting compliance plans that make this convincing demonstration.
An ECO regulation may:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) provide for effective plan-by-plan review of employer-selected measures to ensure the high quality of compliance
plans and that unconvincing compliance plans will be rejected;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00183 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
(2) contain a convincing minimum set of measures that all employers must implement to reach the target APO;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) provide that failure by the employer to meet the target APO will result in implementation of a regulation-specified,
multi-measure contingency plan designed to meet the target APO; or
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) include financial penalties and/or compliance incentives for employers who fail to meet the target APO that are
large enough to provide a significant prospective incentive for the employer to design and implement an effective
compliance plan.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
An option can be used by itself or in combination with others. The proposed ECO regulation uses a combination of the
first and the fourth program design options.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In sum, the proposed ECO regulation meets the basic ECO program requirements. Consequently, the proposed ECO regulation
may be adopted by a state or locality to meet the requirements of Section 182(d)(1)(B). A general discussion of its
requirements is included below in section (c). The detailed proposed ECO regulation is included in this Federal Implementation
Plan NPRM at 40 CFR 52.2999.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Employer and compliance plan requirements. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The proposed ECO regulation requires the following seven actions of employers:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) register with EPA as an employer subject to the regulation;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) designate an Employee Transportation Coordinator (ETC) at each work location;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) conduct an annual APO survey of its employees at each work location to determine employees' current commute patterns
and report the results of the APO survey to the EPA;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) develop and submit to the EPA before March 1, 1997, 2 years from program inception, and in subsequent years as required
by the regulation, trip reduction plans that describe activities to be implemented at the location under an ECO program;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(5) implement the ECO program described in the approved trip reduction plan;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(6) no later than March 1, 1999, 4 years from program inception, increase the average passenger occupancy (APO) at
each location by not less than 25 percent over the average vehicle occupancy (AVO) for Sacramento ECO program area;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(7) maintain the target APO upon achieving it.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=10 g=1 f=1 -->
An employer failing to accomplish any or all of these actions would be in violation of the proposed ECO regulation and
would be subject to penalties under section 113 of the Clean Air Act.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The required components of an employer's compliance plan are as follows:
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=15 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) the name of the employer, address of the work location, and name, title, and signature of the Employee Transportation
Coordinator (ETC) at the work location;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) the results of the most recent APO survey and a description of the procedure used to conduct the APO survey;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) a description of the physical and transportation service characteristics of the work location, and demographic,
work, and travel-related characteristics of the employee population;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) a description of ECO strategies currently implemented that provide commute alternative incentives to employees
at the work location and additional ECO strategies the employer will implement at the work location;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(5) activities planned by the employer to implement the ECO program and a time schedule for implementation of the program;
and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(6) a description of the process by which the employer will periodically monitor and review progress toward the APO
target.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG QTAG 02 -->
<!-- PJG /QTAG -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Employer plans will be certified by a third party for completeness in order to reduce EPA's implementation burden.
EPA will approve or disapprove plans. The Air Pollution Control Districts for the Sacramento nonattainment area
will have the right of first refusal to certify plans of employers in their district. Should one or more APCDs decline
the opportunity to certify employers, another organization or other organizations will be designated by EPA as plan
certifiers.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
By March 1, 1999, all employers must submit either a maintenance plan describing either how the employer will maintain
the target APO, or an update compliance plan describing what additional measures the employer will take to reach the
APO target within one year. An employer's update compliance plan must discuss good faith efforts to achieve the target
APO, provide an explanation of why the ECO strategies included in the last plan did not produce the target APO, and discuss
how the update compliance plans will cause the employer to achieve the target within one year if the target has not been
met by then as required.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00184 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As part of the FIP, EPA is including a parking cash out regulation. Under this regulation, employers would offer employees
the option of taking the cash value of employer paid parking as a financial incentive to reduce solo commuting. Where
applicable, employers would be able to include a parking cash out incentive as part of their ECO program trip reduction
plans. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
d. ECO rule development issues. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Because the following components are common to other trip reduction regulations in California or are discussed extensively
in EPA's 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
ECO Guidance
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, EPA wishes to discuss these issues and the agency's rationale for not incorporating them into the proposed ECO regulation.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
First, this proposed ECO regulation does not allow for APO averaging, banking or trading. Including such a component
within the ECO regulation would add an administrative and enforcement burden beyond EPA's present resources and
is perhaps better administered at the local level. Should the state or local agencies develop an ECO regulation with
APO averaging, banking, or trading consistent with EPA's 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
ECO Guidance, 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA would accept such a regulation for review and possible approval and incorporation into the SIP as a replacement
for the proposed federal ECO program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Related to the issue of APO averaging and trading is the issue of consolidated compliance plans. The proposed ECO regulation
requires employers to achieve the APO target at each work location. Again, this is due to EPA's administrative and
enforcement resource constraints. Therefore, to provide this type of individual employer accountability for achieving
the APO target, the proposed ECO regulation does not allow consolidated compliance plans. A single employer with
multiple work locations or a confederation of employers with geographically related work locations, such as a business
park, are thus not allowed to average their APO survey results together to achieve the target APO. However, the proposed
ECO regulation does not preclude employers from forming or participating in transportation management associations
or using complementary ridesharing and commuter matching services to reduce trips to their respective work locations
and promote ridesharing, so long as they demonstrate compliance at each individual worksite. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Second, this ECO proposed regulation does not divide the Sacramento ECO program area into subregional AVO zones or
target areas for the purpose of achieving the target APO. Subregional AVO zones or target areas would allow different
geographic subregions to start from different existing AVO baselines for the purpose of calculating the APO target.
Thus, different subregions could have different APO targets, but still meet the performance requirement of Section
182(d)(1)(B), a 25 percent increase in APO from the AVO baseline. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
AVO zones may be applicable within the Sacramento ECO program area if there are significant subregional differences
in commute patterns, land use, transit availability, and baseline AVO. However, EPA has no information to suggest
that these differences do exist. To conclusively assess whether or not these differences exist and develop subregional
AVO zones would require detailed surveys of home to work commute patterns in the Sacramento ECO program area to supplement
1990 census data and the 1991 Caltrans State Travel Survey. Should California conduct such surveys and determine
it appropriate, the State could include AVO zones in its ECO SIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As a third and final issue, this regulation does not allow for APO crediting from the use of satellite work stations.
Cold start emissions from motor vehicles are a significant proportion of motor vehicle emissions and will become
an even larger proportion as future motor vehicles run cleaner. Consequently, EPA believes that satellite work stations
are a relatively ineffective strategy for reducing vehicle emissions and vehicle trips unless they can reduce a significantly
large vehicle miles traveled component of a work trip or allow an employee to substitute a commute mode of travel other
than a motor vehicle. Based on its understanding of Sacramento commuting patterns, EPA concluded that this kind of
vehicles miles traveled reduction through use of satellite work stations would not occur in Sacramento and has therefore
not included credit for them in the FIP. However, this proposed ECO regulation does provide for telecommuting from
home as an acceptable trip reduction strategy in employer compliance plans. EPA believes that telecommuting from
home is a very effective trip reduction measure because an entire commute trip is eliminated when an employee works
out of his or her home. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00185 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
In summary, although EPA's 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
ECO Guidance
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 addresses the use of averaging, banking, and trading of APO credits as well as using AVO zones, EPA does not believe
these elements are appropriate for the proposed ECO program. APO averaging, banking, and trading would add an administrative
and enforcement burden beyond EPA's present resources and is perhaps better administered at the local level. In the
case of AVO zones, EPA has no information suggesting that significant subregional differences in commute patterns,
land use, transit availability, and baseline AVO exist to justify this approach. Finally, while EPA may approve a
local or state ECO regulation incorporating crediting of satellite work stations under very restrictive conditions,
EPA has chosen not to credit satellite work stations in the proposed ECO regulation for the reasons stated above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3. RACT for 25 Ton Per Year Sources
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Applicable requirements. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed in the General Preamble for Implementation of Title I of the Clean Air Act Amendments of 1990 (57 FR 13521),
for ``Severe'' areas, reasonable available control technology (RACT) must be applied to any source that emits or
has the potential to emit 25 tons per year of VOC or NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. If the Sacramento area is bumped-up from ``Serious'' (major source = 50 tons per year) to ``Severe'', in order to address
this requirement, RACT regulations must be developed for sources which are caught by the more stringent major source
definition. EPA believes that between current State and local regulatory requirements and today's proposed FIP
regulations (Section III.C.), there should be few, if any, uncontrolled stationary sources which emit more than
25 tons per year. However, EPA plans to continue working with the State and local agencies to identify all stationary
sources which have the potential to emit greater than 25 tons per year or more of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and apply RACT regulations as needed. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. New Source Review 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Background. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Today's proposed rule is based on the New Source Review rules in the affected air pollution control districts (APCDs).
It includes necessary revisions to those rules that will satisfy severe area requirements, including a major source
definition of 25 tons/year and a 1.3:1 offset ratio requirement. The rule also incorporates EPA's permitting requirements
(primarily those at 40 CFR 51.165), and changes necessitated by the 1990 CAAA. EPA is expecting to shortly publish
proposed changes to the regulations at 40 CFR 51.165 in order to codify and interpret changes to nonattainment permitting
requirements pursuant to the CAAA. As appropriate, EPA will rely on that proposal as guidance in taking final action
on today's rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If EPA's final action includes a severe-area classification for the Sacramento FIP areas, this rule will apply in
each of the affected APCDs. EPA's main goal in following the structure and, where possible, the actual language of
the existing rules is to provide a rule that is easy for the local APCDs to adopt and implement and familiar to industrial
sources subject to NSR, and thus provide the least disruption when incorporated in the local programs. However, should
the local APCDs fail to adopt the necessary requirements, EPA will implement the severe area NSR requirements through
today's rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is specifically soliciting comment on the issue of the source size affected by the rule. As the rule is structured
in this proposal, it requires permits of all sources currently required to obtain permits at the APCDs. EPA has several
reasons for including this requirement. First, EPA wants to make this rule as similar to the current District rules
as possible so that the Districts can be reasonably certain that adopting this rule will lead to rapid SIP approval
such that EPA will move into an oversight rather than permit issuance mode. The current District rules use an accumulation
structure that sums all emissions increases, and triggers substantive requirements based on that accumulation.
EPA considered making the rule only apply to sources with a potential to emit greater than 25 tpy. However, because
the Districts' emissions inventory is based on actual emissions rather than potential emissions, EPA would be unable
to know clearly to which sources the rule applies. Second, EPA is concerned that excising those portions of the rules
related to minor sources will lead to increased confusion by sources as to jurisdiction and applicable requirements.
Third, EPA believes that not including minor sources will result in a rule capturing fewer emissions than the current
District rules, a result contrary to the intent of the FIP. On the other hand, EPA is concerned about the effective use
of its resources, and about the burden placed on companies constructing very small sources having to obtain air pollution
permits from two agencies. Even so, because EPA has the ability to delegate today's rule to the Districts, the effects
of dual permitting should be short lived. EPA is therefore soliciting comment on whether to include minor sources
or not, and, if a commenter suggests excluding minor sources, EPA solicits comment on the most effective way to exclude
minor sources while maintaining the structure of the rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00186 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Overview of Rule Requirements.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 There are three sections of the rule that together establish 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the NSR requirements for the FIP areas. The first, 40 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
52.3000, describes which sources are required to obtain a pre-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construction permit, and sets out most of the procedural 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements involved in permitting. The second section, 40 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
52.3001, contains the substantive requirements related to New 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Source Review, including control technology determinations and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offset requirements. The third section, 40 CFR 52.3002, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
describes the procedure EPA will use to determine the amount of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
creditable reductions available and authorize them for use as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offsets. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 In general, the rule is designed to meet each of the severe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
area NSR requirements mandated by the Act, meet each applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement for State and local NSR rules as set forth in 40 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
51.165, as well as be consistent with EPA's policies for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permitting. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The rule requires that any source
<FOOTCITE>98</FOOTCITE>
 owner proposing to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construct an emitting piece of equipment obtain a permit prior to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
construction of that equipment. A permit applicant must provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA all information necessary to process the permit. Once all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such information is received by EPA, the application is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administratively complete and EPA will begin the process of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis and permit preparation, and will then issue or deny the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permit. Except for certain small sources, EPA will present a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
draft permit for public notice and provide an opportunity for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
98
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Source is generally defined as all emitting equipment at one location. This document uses the same definition as 40
CFR 51.165.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 </FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The substantive requirements that may apply to construction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or modification of certain larger emission units include: Best 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Available Control Technology (BACT), offsets, statewide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance, and alternative siting analysis, and are discussed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(1) Lowest Achievable Emission Rate (LAER). LAER is required for any emission unit construction or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modification where the potential emissions of that unit exceed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ten pounds per day. This requirement is consistent with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Clean Air Act and is based on the requirements of the Districts' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules. The applicable Clean Air Act control technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement is Lowest Achievable Emissions Rate (LAER) (CAA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 173(a)(2)). The District rules use the term Best 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Available Control Technology, but define it to meet the LAER 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement. Therefore, as used in today's rule, the term ``BACT'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
satisfies CAA section 173(a)(2). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Offsets. Today's rule implements the offset requirement mandated by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Act for severe areas by adding a 1.3:1 offset ratio to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
base District rule. The rule triggers the offset requirement once 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the potential to emit of the source exceeds 7,500 pounds per 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quarter (approximately 15 tons per year). Once the source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeds the offset trigger, all subsequent emissions increases 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are required to be offset at this ratio. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Statewide Compliance. The owner or operator of all major new sources and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modifications is required to demonstrate that all major sources 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under his or her ownership or control within the state are in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance or on a schedule for compliance with all Clean Air Act 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. This requirement has been in effect since the 1977 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Clean Air Act amendments, and is already part of all of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
District rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Alternative Siting Analysis. The requirement for an alternative siting analysis is found 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in section 173(a)(5) of the Clean Air Act. An applicant for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major new source or modification is required to perform an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
analysis of alternative sites, sizes, production processes and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
environmental control techniques and demonstrate that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefits of the source significantly outweigh the environmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and social costs of construction of the source at that location. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Today's rule implements the alternative siting analysis 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement at 40 CFR 52.3001 (c)(7). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Existing NSR Rules.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The five Districts affected by today's Rule are all within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the same air basin and Metropolitan Statistical Area. While 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each District adopts NSR rules independently, all but one of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Districts' rules are similar in structure and substance. Feather 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
River Air Quality Management District's rules have similar 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definitions and substantially similar requirements as the other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Disticts, but differ in minor structural and other respects. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Today's rule is similar to the existing rules in Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Metropolitan Air Quality Management District, Placer Air 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Pollution Control District, Yolo-Solano Air Pollution Control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
District and El Dorado Air Pollution Control District.
<FOOTCITE>99</FOOTCITE>
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, today's rule is numbered differently and is written to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address only ozone precursors. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
99
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The rules related to New Source Review in each of the Districts are as follows: Sacramento Metroplitan AQMD Rules 201,
202, and 204; Placer APCD Rules 501, 502, and 504; El Dorado APCD Rules 501, 523, and 524 (board  adoption expected April
1994); Yolo-Solano APCD Rules 501, 502, and 504; Feather River APCD Rules 4.0, 10.1 and 10.2.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 </FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
None of the District NSR rules in the State Implementation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Plan currently meet all the requirements of the 1990 CAAA. On 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
January 15, 1993, EPA found that Feather River AQMD, El Dorado 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
APCD, Placer APCD, and Yolo-Solano APCD failed to submit NSR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules meeting certain requirements mandated by the CAAA. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, Feather River AQMD, Placer APCD, and Yolo-Solano APCD 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have since submitted rules for inclusion into the State 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Implementation Plan. EPA expects submittals from El Dorado APCD 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and Sacramento Metropolitan AQMD in the next year. Because all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of these rules were written to only meet the requirements for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
serious nonattainment areas, they do not meet the requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for severe nonattainment areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=3 -->
d. Changes From Original APCD Rules. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) Changes to Address Severe Area Requirements. In order to satisfy the provisions required by the Clean Air Act for
severe areas, EPA has included several requirements in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
today's rule which differ from those of the current Districts' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules. The changes include the addition of a more stringent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major source threshold and a more stringent offset ratio. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements are in accordance with Section 182(e) of the CAA. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The minimum size threshold for a major source is 25 tons per year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the lowest acceptable offset ratio is 1.3 to 1. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00187 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(2) Changes to Address Federal Approvability. Significant changes in today's rule include deletion or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revision of certain sections which exist in the Districts' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current rules, as well as the inclusion of new sections to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address federal requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Federal new source review at 40 CFR 51.165 applies to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certain source categories which were exempt from District new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source review requirements. Therefore, such exemptions are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
excluded. Some of the District rules allow trading between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different precursors of a secondary pollutant. As EPA does not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently allow trading between either of the ozone precursor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria pollutants addressed in this rule, this rule does not, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as proposed, allow such trading. In addition, 40 CFR 52.3000 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contains wording which emphasizes the federal enforceability of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an Authority to Construct and its validity for the life of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source. In order to match EPA's enforcement authority over past 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
violations, and to be consistent with requirements in EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Operating Permits Program (40 CFR 70.6(a)(3)), today's rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requires retention of records for five years. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 This rule contains certain other provisions which are not in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the current District rules. A subsection is included in this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule to address the minimum offset ratio for emission reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieved from mobile sources. Furthermore, the ERC Authorization 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
includes provisions to certify that ERCs are surplus at time of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use, that prior shutdown credits are federally approvable, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that ERCs from non-permitted sources are surplus, permanent, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforceable, and quantifiable. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Changes to Address Federal Implementation. Some significant changes in the APCD rule are necessary for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
federal implementation. Today's rule excludes all references to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements contained in state law. All district fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements are removed and are replaced with a provision 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowing EPA to collect fees. Upon delegation of authority to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement and enforce any portion of this NSR rule, EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cease to collect the fees associated with that portion of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule, and the delegated agency will calculate and collect fees in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
accordance with the fee rules of that agency. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Language was added to section 52.3001 to provide the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
opportunity to request a public hearing as provided by 40 CFR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
124.12. Language was also added to section 52.3000 to allow for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
petition to the Environmental Appeals Board for review and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reconsideration within 30 days of any final decision issued 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pursuant to this NSR rule, including decisions made by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delegated agency pursuant to this NSR rule. A petition for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review can also be filed with the U.S. Court of Appeals as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provided in section 307(b) of the Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Because this rule is explicitly intended for preconstruction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review for ozone precursors, it does not reference other criteria 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pollutants nor District operating permits programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The ERC Authorization section of this Rule allows sources to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meet the offset requirements of this Rule through use of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing District banking system. Functions of the District 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Banks and District Priority Reserve Banks are not affected by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
today's Rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
e. Dual Permit Application and Review. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Today's proposed NSR Rule does not replace the Districts' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing permitting programs. New or modified sources of ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
precursors must continue to meet the applicable District NSR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. This may result in sources of such pollutants 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
having to apply for permits from both the EPA and the District in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which the source is located.
<FOOTCITE>100</FOOTCITE>
 As a practical matter, however, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because EPA has maintained the structure and requirements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing District Rules, by meeting the requirements of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``bumped up'' rule, the source will have also met the District NSR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. Additional preparation in the form of permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applications, modeling, or research on the part of the applicant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be minimal, if required at all. EPA has no statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority to repeal or supplant the existing State or local laws, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nor would this be desirable since the existing District 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permitting programs regulate the emissions of pollutants not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
covered by this rule. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
100
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Sources must follow this ``dual'' permitting scheme in any area that does not have authority to implement federally
mandated permitting programs.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 </FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
One difficulty EPA has encountered in proposing this ``dual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
system'' is how to operate federal emission reduction credit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``banks'' that meet federal requirements and still preserve the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
integrity of existing District banks. As required by the Act and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
set forth in section 40 CFR 52.3001 of this rule, new sources or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modifications that emit ozone precursors above an established 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
threshold must obtain offsets. Under current District programs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reduction credits can be certified and obtained from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
District banks to meet offset requirements. However, to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
creditable for use as offsets under this rule, EPA must ensure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the credits in the District banks meet federal approvability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria as set forth in the Emission Trading Policy Statement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(ETPS, 51 FR 43815) and in the General Preamble to Title I at (57 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FR 13553). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The ERC authorization process described in today's rule 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would allow emissions reductions to be certified directly by EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and would also allow sources to use ERCs which have previously 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been certified by a District rule and authorized to meet federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NSR requirements. EPA requests comment on today's proposal and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative methods that would preserve the integrity of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
District banks under the proposed ``dual'' permitting system. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
f. Operating Permits.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The permits issued by EPA under today's NSR rule will not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expire upon issuance of a District operating permit. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Districts are encouraged to include the requirements contained in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permits issued by EPA, pursuant to this rule, in their operating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permits since sources must continue to comply with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of these EPA issued construction permits. Districts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must include conditions contained in permits issued pursuant to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this rule in any operating permit issued under a program adopted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pursuant to 40 CFR part 70 or Title V of the Act, as discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section III.J of this preamble. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
