<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00188 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
g. Federal Implementation and Delegation to Local Agencies. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As discussed below in section III.J of this preamble, if EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reclassifies the ozone nonattainment areas of the Sacramento 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Valley Air Basin as ``severe'', the applicable portions of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California SIP must be revised to meet the CAA requirements for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
severe ozone nonattainment area and submitted to EPA on or before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the date of final promulgation of this FIP. Until such time as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA approves the revisions for the NSR portions of the applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SIP, the NSR requirements of this FIP shall apply. When the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revisions are approved into the SIP, EPA will withdraw this NSR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portion of the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will encourage delegation of the final version of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NSR rule to the local Districts prior to SIP approvability, thus 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
eliminating the need for sources to apply for permits from both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the EPA and the local permitting agency. To do so, Districts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
must revise their existing NSR rule to meet the requirements of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this federal rule, or adopt EPA's final NSR rule, through their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
existing notice and comment rulemaking procedures. The revised 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule must then be submitted to EPA, which will evaluate the NSR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule for consistency with the requirements of the NSR portion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Federal Implementation Plan. EPA will then delegate to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
District, by letter, the authority to implement and enforce those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
portions of the FIP that are consistent with District 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. When the revisions are ultimately approved in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
SIP, EPA will withdraw the NSR portion of the FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Revision of the District regulations is necessary to allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the local agencies to use their own requirements, such as fee 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provisions and enforcement procedures, to implement the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of this NSR rule. While both SIP revisions and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delegation will require revision of the District regulations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delegation is a much more expedient process than EPA approval of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revisions as part of the SIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5. TCMs to offset VMT increases. If bumped up to a severe nonattainment classification, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento would also need to consider the requirements of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 182(d)(1)(A). This section requires States containing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
severe ozone nonattainment areas to submit a SIP that identifies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and adopts specific, enforceable transportation control measures, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as necessary, to offset growth in vehicle emissions due to growth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in vehicle miles traveled. Beyond demonstrating that these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures offset growth in emissions, they must be sufficient to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allow total regional emission levels to comply with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonable further progress and attainment requirements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Act. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the General Preamble to Title I of the CAAA, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
57 FR 13498 (April 16, 1992), the TCM offset SIPs required from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas already designated severe or extreme must: (1) Identify and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adopt specific and enforceable transportation control strategies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and TCMs to offset any growth in emissions from growth in VMT or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of vehicle trips; (2) identify and adopt specific and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforceable transportation control strategies and TCMs that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attain reductions in motor vehicle emissions (in combination with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other emission reduction requirements) as necessary to comply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with RFP milestones; and (3) identify and adopt specific and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforceable transportation control strategies and TCMs that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attain reductions in motor vehicle emissions (in combination with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other emission reduction requirements) as necessary to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrate attainment of the NAAQS. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As discussed in the General Preamble, EPA has interpreted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the provision to offset any growth in VOC from VMT increases to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``require that sufficient measures be adopted so that projected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle VOC emissions will never be higher during the ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
season in one year than during the ozone season in the year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
before.'' Further, the attainment year is the last year for which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this test applies. The calculation of VOC emissions from on road 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mobile sources may ``include the effects of Federal measures such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as new motor vehicle standards, Phase II RVP controls, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reformulated gasoline, as well as Clean Air Act-mandated SIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements such as enhanced I/M, the fleet clean-fuel vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program, and the employer trip reduction program.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For Sacramento, as can be seen in the Technical Support 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Document, on-road mobile source emissions through 2005 are never 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projected to be greater in one year than the year before once the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FIP control strategies are implemented. Therefore, there is no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
need for TCMs in Sacramento to offset emissions increases from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
growth in VMT. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As for requirements (2) and (3) pertaining to TCMs for RFP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and attainment demonstration, the FIP has addressed TCMs for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento in the discussion of Employee Commute Options 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(III.F.2) and the discussion pertaining to fees on light duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles (III.D.2.f). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
6. Reformulated fuel. a. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Provision satisfied in FIP/SIP actions above. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Reclassification of the Sacramento area to ``Severe'' would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mandate sale of reformulated gasoline in the area, effective one 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year after reclassification. There may be no emission reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefits from applying the federal reformulated fuel requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to Sacramento, however, since the California reformulated fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program already applies throughout the State. If the federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reformulated fuel program achieves greater reductions than the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California program, EPA will impose the federal requirement in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Sacramento. If the California program is more stringent, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would consider modifications to its enforcement program to avoid 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
duplication with California's program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
G. Section 182(e)(5) New Technology Measures for the South 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Coast 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Introduction.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
After application of the FIP's stationary, area, and mobile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
source rule requirements, the ozone attainment requirements for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast demand further reductions from the same source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories in order to achieve cumulative reductions from 1990 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
baseyear emissions of approximately 90 percent VOC and 70 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
