<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4. State and local control measures. For all three FIP areas, most existing State and local control measures are included
in the emission projections used to compare to the target emission levels. However, since EPA uses the CALI5a motor
vehicle emissions model and its own non-road mobile source inventory, the associated emission reductions may not
match those assumed by California in its own projected inventories.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5. Attainment demonstrations. Emission reductions projected from proposed FIP measures are discussed in sections
3.C through 3.G of this NPRM. To show attainment, the projected ton/day benefits of the FIP control measures are subtracted
from the baseline inventories for the appropriate attainment year in each FIP area. The results are then compared
to the attainment target in each area as discussed in section 3.H.3 of this NPRM. This calculation is provided in detail
in the technical support document.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=4 -->
I. Transportation Conformity
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Section 176(c) of the Clean Air Act requires transportation plans, transportation programs, and transportation
projects which are funded with title 23 U.S.C. or Federal Transit Act funds or approved by the Federal Highway Administration
(FHWA) or Federal Transit Administration (FTA) of the U.S. Department of Transportation (U.S. DOT) to conform with
the applicable implementation plan for a nonattainment area. After promulgation, the FIPs will be part of the applicable
ozone and CO implementation plans for the Sacramento, Ventura, and South Coast nonattainment areas, and will therefore
add certain criteria which must be satisfied before the MPOs for these areas or FHWA/FTA can find that a transportation
plan, program, or project conforms. However, these additional criteria are limited, as explained below.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed FIP will not alter the Criteria and Procedures for Determining Transportation Conformity which EPA
promulgated on November 24, 1993 (58 FR 62188). The FIP itself will not impose any restriction on the selection and
implementation of transportation projects which can be found to conform under the terms of the promulgated transportation
conformity criteria and procedures. Furthermore, the FIP does not direct the expenditure of funds designated under
title 23 U.S.C. or the Federal Transit Act to any specific projects or type of project.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The State of California is responsible for submitting to EPA a conformity SIP revision by November 24, 1994, as required
by 40 CFR 51.396. The conformity SIP revision is a requirement added in the 1990 Clean Air Act Amendments, is not mentioned
in any of the settlement agreements, and therefore is not an obligation for EPA under the court orders.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because it establishes strategies for Sacramento and Ventura to attain by 2005, the FIP will establish motor vehicle
emissions budgets for VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 for those FIP areas for the year 2005. In addition, the CO attainment demonstration will establish a CO budget for the
year 2000 for the South Coast. These budgets play no role for the purposes of conformity until the FIP is promulgated.
(This is in contrast to state-submitted budgets, which do play a role upon submission to EPA.) Conformity determinations
made after the FIP is promulgated must use the FIP's budgets for the years indicated and any subsequent analysis years,
until the SIP process establishes different budgets for subsequent years (e.g., state-submitted plan with budgets
is approved). At a minimum, the conformity of the transportation plan must be redetermined using the FIP's budget
within 18 months of the FIP's promulgation (see 40 CFR 51.400(b)(3)(iii)). The conformity of transportation improvement
programs must be redetermined within six months following the adoption of a transportation plan (see 40 CFR 51.400(c)(3)).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
There is some ambiguity in Clean Air Act section 176(c) and EPA's conformity rule regarding whether the proposed FIP,
when final, will create VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 budgets for the South Coast, and if so, for which years. While the FIP analysis contains emissions projections for
2000, 2005, and 2010, EPA believes that its application of Clean Air Act section 182(e)(5) to the FIP means that the
South Coast FIP will not specifically allocate 2010 emission reductions between transportation and other sources.
Furthermore, there are no specific emission reduction requirements that the FIP must satisfy in 2000 and 2005. Therefore,
the South Coast FIP will establish no emissions budget binding on the MPOs or U.S. DOT. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00201 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The FIP will not alter the fact that the motor vehicle emissions budgets for 1996, which are contained in the 15 percent
SIPs submitted by California for these three nonattainment areas (required by Clean Air Act section 182(b)(1)(A)),
must be used for the purposes of conformity according to the requirements of 40 CFR 51.448. If EPA disapproves such
a SIP or finds it incomplete, the 1996 emissions budget shall be used for the purposes of conformity for 12 months following
the final disapproval or incompleteness finding, if EPA explicitly states that the SIP submittal would have been
considered approvable or complete with respect to requirements for emission reductions if all committed measures
had been submitted in enforceable form (see 40 CFR 51.448(a)(3) and (c)(1)(iii)). However, if there are other reasons
for the disapproval or incompleteness finding, time clocks will be started after which new plans and TIPs may not be
found to conform and existing plans and TIPs will lapse.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Budgets contained in any future state-submitted attainment and post-1996 RFP plans must also be observed. Such state-submitted
budgets may not replace budgets established by the final FIP until the state-submitted budgets have been approved
by EPA.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Demonstrations of conformity to the FIP may use the same motor vehicle emissions model which EPA uses to demonstrate
attainment in the final FIP, unless EPA announces a revision to the model. (In that case, a grace period for use of the
latest motor vehicle emissions model applies, as described in 40 CFR 51.414.) The final FIP will be based on a VMT growth
estimate which is the MPO's own growth projection at the time of FIP promulgation. Difficulty demonstrating consistency
with the FIP's budget would therefore most likely be due to VMT growth which is higher than the MPO expected.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The FIP does not propose any TCMs that are eligible for funding under title 23 U.S.C. or the Federal Transit Act. The
Employee Commute Options program proposed for Sacramento might be eligible for such funding if it were adopted by
local authorities, but EPA itself is not an eligible recipient. Therefore, determinations of conformity to the FIP
will not have to demonstrate timely implementation of TCMs in the FIP.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
J. FIP Implementation by State and Local Agencies
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Congress has determined that the primary responsibility for air pollution control rests with state and local governments.
CAA section 101(a)(3). Moreover, as discussed elsewhere in this NPRM, EPA lacks many of the authorities, instruments,
and established local and regional organizations and trained staff to administer, on a daily basis, comprehensive
air pollution control initiatives such as these FIPs, which affects hundreds of thousands of individual sources
and activities. Massive federal intrusion would inevitably result if EPA were to shoulder the chief burden of enforcing
the California FIP.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Accordingly, EPA has attempted to design the FIP rules to ensure that, wherever possible, State and local implementation
is encouraged and facilitated by the FIP's regulatory approach. Thus, for example, the FIP generally employs local
California rule organization and terminology in the proposed stationary and area source rules, including the new
source review permitting rules (40 CFR 52.284 through 52.289, and 52.304).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1. State and local obligation to incorporate FIP requirements in operating permits. In Title V of the 1990 CAA Amendments,
Congress established an important new state responsibility. Title V and EPA's implementing regulations require
state and local air pollution control agencies to adopt and submit for EPA approval a regulatory program for issuing
operating permits to major stationary sources, sources covered by emissions standards for hazardous air pollutants
pursuant to section 112 of the Act, sources covered by any new source performance standards set under section 111 of
the Act, and affected sources under the acid rain program. The operating permits must contain necessary limits and
conditions to assure compliance with all applicable requirements of the Act, including all provisions of the applicable
SIP and the FIP. Title V includes specific requirements for EPA oversight and for sanctions against states for failure
to adopt, administer, or enforce the operating permit program.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Title V establishes the following timeframes for state and EPA action:
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1. Program submittal by November 15, 1993;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
2. EPA approval or disapproval within one year of program submittal, with the possibility of interim approval for
up to two years;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3. EPA application of sanctions for failure to submit approvable programs, and establishment of a federal program
by November 15, 1995, if a state or local program has not been approved;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4. Source operating permit application within one year after EPA approval (or interim approval) of a state or local
program, or after EPA establishment of a federal program;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
5. Issuance of all permits within three years after EPA approval of a state or local program or promulgation of a federal
program; and
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
6. Operating permit application within one year of commencement of operation for new sources subject to New Source
Review.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
On July 21, 1992, EPA promulgated 40 CFR part 70, specifying the minimum elements of state operating permit programs
(57 FR 32250). EPA's promulgation makes clear the Agency's interpretation that approvable operating permit programs
must provide that the applicable permit include FIP emission limitations and other FIP requirements (57 FR 32258).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As noted, operating permits are required for all ``major'' stationary sources, as well as air toxics sources subject
to requirements under section 112 of the Act (unless exempted), and sources subject to any new source performance
standard under section 111.
<FOOTCITE>105</FOOTCITE>
 In ozone nonattainment areas, section 182 defines major source according to the area's classification and the source's
potential to emit VOC or NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
: 50 tons per year in ``Serious'' areas, 25 tons per year in ``Severe'' areas, and 10 tons per year in ``Extreme'' areas.
<FOOTCITE>106</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
105
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Part 70 provides that certain nonmajor sources, including those subject to standards under section 111 and 112, may
be exempted from the obligation to obtain a part 70 permit until EPA completes a rulemaking to determine how the program
should be structured for nonmajor sources. Section 70.3(b)(1).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
106
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
For this purpose, Title I treats VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 sources somewhat differently. In areas qualifying for an exemption under section 182(f), NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 sources with the potential to emit less than 100 tons per year would not be considered major sources. None of the three
FIP areas covered in this Notice, however, qualify for the exemption, and thus the major sources cutoff is the same
in these areas for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
 as for VOC.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00202 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Consequently, each air pollution control agency in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas must, by virtue of Title V, incorporate and enforce all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable FIP requirements in operating permits for every source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with a potential to emit at or above the level prescribed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 182 for the area's classification. Title V and EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating permit regulations do not, however, mandate adoption of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an operating permit program for nonmajor source categories (for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example, in ``Severe'' areas, sources with a potential to emit less 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than 25 tons per year of VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
). Nevertheless, some 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonmajor sources are subject to operating permit requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because they are major sources of air toxics,
<FOOTCITE>107</FOOTCITE>
 and for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources all applicable FIP requirements must be included in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permit and enforced by the air pollution control agency. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
107
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Certain nonmajor sources subject to section 111 or 112 standards may also be subject in the future to air toxics and
operating permit requirements. New section 112 standards may apply to area sources (nonmajor) as well as major sources;
EPA will determine whether operating permits are required for these sources when the standards are promulgated.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Full transfer of authority through delegation.  EPA hopes that the State and local air pollution control agencies
will quickly adopt SIP rules that are identical or comparable to the FIP rules, and which could be approved before the
FIP regulation is scheduled for initial implementation. If a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
State or local agency adopts an essentially indistinguishable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rule, EPA intends to delegate full implementation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibilities to the agency immediately following State or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local adoption, without opportunity for public comment, unless 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the delegation is under the authority of section 112, and not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
covered by a good cause exception. See EPA's enabling guidance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for section 112(1). EPA will publish a notice of this delegation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and will otherwise try to ensure that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected sources are aware of that a State or local agency has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assumed program implementation responsibilities. Subsequently, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will take normal SIP approval action on the State or local 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substitute rule, simultaneously rescinding the FIP rule in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
jurisdiction's control area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 As noted in the discussion above regarding the California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consumer product rules, EPA is issuing federal rules for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
categories because the State has so far chosen not to submit the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rules for SIP action. Since EPA's FIP rules are essentially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identical, EPA intends to delegate to CARB the authority to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement the program if CARB has not yet submitted its own rules 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for SIP approval, and if California adopts adequate authority to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement the FIP. In the event that CARB fails to implement its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
own rules or amends the State's rules so that they become less 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stringent than the otherwise applicable federal rule, EPA will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to directly enforce the provisions of the FIP consumer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
product rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 In the future, EPA intends to promulgate in 40 CFR part 71 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provisions for delegation of certain federal operating permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program responsibilities to State and local agencies with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adequate authority to administer the program but lacking EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approval of the State or local program. EPA could use this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure to grant State and local agencies the authority to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
issue operating permits prior to full EPA approval of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies' permit program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
3. Full transfer of authority through SIP approval.  A State or local agency may adopt a rule that is comparable to the
FIP rule in emissions capture, but is significantly different in approach. In that event, immediate transfer of implementation
and enforcement authority through delegation is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inappropriate without opportunity for public review and comment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Instead, EPA will try to ``parallel process'' SIP approval and FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rescission as the means to expedite State or local assumption of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority.
<FOOTCITE>108</FOOTCITE>
 This will minimize the time during which affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources are subject to overlapping and conflicting obligations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and during which EPA and the State or local agencies must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
undertake redundant permitting, compliance plan review, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforcement. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
108
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
In parallel process SIP approval, EPA, with the consent of the State, proposes approval of draft rules which may not
yet have been fully adopted and submitted by the State. Final SIP approval could not occur until after all procedural
SIP requirements (including State adoption and SIP submittal) have been completed.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Partial transfer of authority through delegation.  To the extent that State law allows agencies to perform certain
implementation responsibilities for FIP rules even without State and local rule adoption, EPA will encourage these
agencies to agree to undertake, on EPA's behalf, administrative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and substantive roles of FIP implementation consistent with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
agencies' authorities and capabilities. These roles could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include: procedural and engineering review of permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applications and compliance plans, preparation of draft permit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and plan approval or disapproval actions, administration and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
oversight of compliance reporting and recordkeeping requirements, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conduct of source inspections, and preparation of draft notices 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of violation. Under these circumstances, EPA would retain the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for actual federal permit issuance, where the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requires permits, and for enforcement actions against sources 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
violating FIP provisions. EPA would also maintain direct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
authority to terminate, modify, or revoke permits, as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for disapproving compliance plans. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Partial transfer of FIP implementation authorities would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effected through delegation by MOU between the EPA Region IX and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the air pollution control officer or executive officer of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible agency. EPA will announce the terms of the partial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
delegation in a 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Federal Register
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 notice, and will inform affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sources, with or without additional notice and opportunity for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
IV. Administrative Requirements
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Executive Order 12866
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Under Executive Order 12866, (58 FR 51735 (October 4, 1993), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Agency must determine whether the regulatory actions are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``significant'' and therefore subject to OMB review and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of the Executive Order. The Order defines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``significant regulatory actions'' as ones that are likely to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
result in rules that may: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00203 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(1) Have an annual effect on the economy of $100 million or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more or adversely affect in a material way the economy, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
productivity, competition, jobs, the environment, public health 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or safety, or State, local, or tribal governments or communities; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(2) Create a serious inconsistency or otherwise interfere 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with an action taken or planned by another agency; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(3) Materially alter the budgetary impact of entitlements, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
grants, user fees, or loan programs or the rights and obligations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of recipients thereof; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(4) Raise novel legal or policy issues arising out of legal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
mandates, the President's priorities, or the principles set forth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the Executive Order. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Pursuant to the terms of the Executive Order 12866, it has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been determined that the proposed Federal Implementation Plans 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are ``significant regulatory actions''. As such, these actions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were submitted to OMB along with regulatory impact assessments of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed actions and salient alternatives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The estimated annualized control cost to meet the Ozone 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NAAQS is $150 million in the Sacramento Metro Area as well as in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ventura County with 2005 as the attainment date for both areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The estimated annualized control cost to meet the Ozone NAAQS in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast Air Basin is $3 to $4 billion with 2010 as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment date. The incremental cost to achieve the Carbon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Monoxide NAAQS in the South Coast Air Basin is zero. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced inspection and maintenance provisions to achieve the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Ozone NAAQS will together with other acceptable state measures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also accomplish the Carbon Monoxide NAAQS objective. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
estimated cost for the enhanced inspection and maintenance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provision was reflected in the cost to achieve the Ozone NAAQS 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the South Coast. The attainment date for the Carbon Monoxide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NAAQS is 2000. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, the environmental and economic gains from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal Implementation Plans are real and potentially large in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
terms of the human population affected as well as the amount of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
crop and forest acreage affected. Furthermore, because of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
jointness of certain control measures, benefits will not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limited to reductions in ozone and carbon monoxide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concentrations. Benefits will also accrue in the form of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in nitrogen dioxide, particulate matter and air toxics 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concentrations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Scientific and data limitations preclude a quantitative and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comprehensive assessment of benefits. Without such an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assessment, the Agency cannot determine whether these FIPs are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allocatively efficient. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Due to the short deadlines in the court ordered schedule for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this rulemaking, OMB did not have time to carry out its normal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
review under Executive Order 12866. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Regulatory Flexibility Act
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Regulatory Flexibility Act of 1980 and 1992 EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
guidelines require the identification of potentially adverse 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impacts of Federal regulations on small entities. Small entities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
include small businesses, organizations, and governmental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
jurisdictions. These proposed rules may adversely affect such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entities. Consequently, a Regulatory Flexibility Analysis is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required and has been prepared. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The potential impact of the proposed rules will vary among 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected entities both large and small. In some cases, cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
savings may accrue to affected entities. In other instances, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost increases may be relatively modest. In other instances, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control costs may exceed 10 percent of anticipated sales. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, due to uncertainties regarding source identification, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Agency cannot distinguish between small emission sources 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
owned by small entities and those owned by larger firms. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Furthermore, uncertainties in the cost and process economics data 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preclude specific findings on where averting and mitigating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measures are warranted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Paperwork Reduction Act
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The Paperwork Reduction Act requires that a Federal agency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prepare an Information Collection Request to obtain OMB clearance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for any activity that will involve collecting information from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ten or more non-Federal respondents. The proposed Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Implementation Plans necessitate preparation of an Information 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Collection Request. The information requirements associated with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed rules include reporting, monitoring, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recordkeeping and will affect the private sector as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
local, state, and Federal authorities. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The information collection requirements have been estimated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and summarized in the Information Collection Request and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
forwarded to OMB for review and approval under the Paperwork 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Reduction Act, 44 U.S.C. 3501 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
et seq. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Until OMB approves the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Information Collection Request, the reporting, monitoring, and/or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recordkeeping aspects of these rules are not effective. OMB is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
currently reviewing the Information Collection Request and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
supporting documentation. Given the potential for changes in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
elements of the package prior to promulgation, OMB will not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
approve the Information Collection Request until that time. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
D. Federalism Implications
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
A federalism assessment has not been conducted under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Executive Order 12612. However, federalism effects have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered through the development of the proposed rules and will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to be addressed in the period between proposal and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgation. In particular, the Agency has worked closely with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the local and state authorities to reflect their concerns in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fashioning of these rules. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



