<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=84 g=1 f=1 -->
Appendix I. Enhanced In-Use Compliance Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
A. Light-Duty Vehicles and Trucks 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Introduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As described in Section III.D.2.a and b of this preamble, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions from light-duty vehicles (LDVs) and trucks 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(LDTs) represented significant fractions of the total VOC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission inventories in the three FIP areas in 1990. Light-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles have remained a major source of emissions even though 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission standards for light-duty vehicle exhaust hydrocarbon 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions have dropped more than 90 percent since 1970. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continuing high emissions derive from increases in VMT, the long 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
life of vehicles in the temperate California climate and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure of many current in-use vehicles to meet the standards to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which they are certified when new. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California has adopted very stringent light-duty vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission certification standards known as the LEV program. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program will assure that new vehicles sold in California will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have very low emissions. This will be accomplished through use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of advanced emission controls and a new onboard diagnostic (OBD) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement which is intended by CARB to identify for the driver 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and the repair technician whenever an emission control system has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
degraded sufficiently to increase emissions 50 percent above the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA expects the California Low Emission Vehicle (LEV) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program will substantially reduce emissions of cars and light-duty trucks, compared to the federal standards. However,
despite 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the use of advanced technologies and the OBD requirements, CARB 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projects LEVs will exceed their certification standards in-use, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
although, for the reasons noted in Section III.D.1.a(2), by a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
much smaller margin than has occurred in the past. Emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above the certification standards, referred to as excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions, will occur because some models may experience more in-use deterioration than expected, or because individual
vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
experience component failure or improper maintenance which is not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
immediately corrected by the various existing in-use compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs, including enhanced I/M. Because the magnitude of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission reductions needed for attainment is so great, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adoption of further measures to reduce these remaining excess in-use emissions will help reach attainment of the
ozone standard. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is therefore seeking comments on the need for and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefits of adopting an enhanced in-use compliance program which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would provide extra emission reductions above and beyond the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantial reduction provided by the California LEV program, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cause in-use emissions to approach the certification standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that much of the responsibility to ensure low 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use emissions must rest with vehicle manufacturers, since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
owners, although they have a significant part to play cannot 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure performance that is not available from the hardware. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Traditionally, the incentive for manufacturers to build emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
durability into their vehicles has been limited. Owners do not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have the ability, nor often the interest, to ensure their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions control equipment is performing properly. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This section presents EPA's proposed enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance program for LDVs, LDTs and certain medium-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles (MDVs). MDVs, as defined by CARB, have a gross vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
weight rating (GVWR) between 6000 and 14,000 pounds. For those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MDVs above 8500 pounds GVWR, CARB gives manufacturers the option 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of certifying as a specific and complete vehicle, or as a heavy-duty engine which can be placed in any heavy-duty vehicle.
Since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MDVs certified as complete vehicles are much more analogous to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
LDVs and LDTs than heavy-duty engines, chassis-certified MDVs are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
being included here in the enhanced in-use compliance program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
light-duty vehicles and trucks. The enhanced in-use compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
programs for heavy-duty engines and nonroad engines are similar 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in purpose and are presented in Sections B. and C. of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appendix below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The light-duty enhanced in-use compliance program attempts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to encourage manufacturers to build additional durability into 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their emission control equipment by increasing the manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for repairing problem vehicles identified by the inspection and maintenance (I/M) and recall programs.
The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M program is revised by shifting responsibility for repair to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers if a significant percentage of vehicles are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performing close to certification levels in use. The potential 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility shift should encourage manufacturers to build 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional durability and improved repairability into their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles. The lower allowable I/M emission levels will ensure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that vehicles are repaired if not performing satisfactorily. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Also, the recall program is extended for the full useful life and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
all vehicles are tested and included provided they have not been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
obviously tampered or abused. Additionally, MDV emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards are reduced as significantly as light-duty standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have already been reduced. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. New Vehicle Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
New vehicle certification, and particularly the associated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission standards, play an important role in defining the types 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of emission control hardware placed on vehicles. These standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also essentially define the best level of emission control that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
can be expected from in-use vehicles. The majority of current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use emissions come not from vehicles meeting their emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards, but from vehicles which exceed their standards. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
believes that the CARB LEV program with enhanced I/M will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantially reduce the occurrence of vehicles which exceed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their standards by a substantial margin. However, emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
predictions indicate that on average vehicles will not meet their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission standards in use. Therefore, the focus of EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed enhanced in-use compliance program is on two programs, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall and I/M, which can provide additional incentives to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle manufacturers to build the lowest emitting vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possible. However, no changes to CARB's motor vehicle control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program are necessary to implement these two approaches to an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use compliance program for new LDVs, LDTs and MDVs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing that the enhanced in-use compliance program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be applied to all new vehicles that are defined under California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulations as passenger cars, light-duty trucks, or medium-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles, beginning in model year 1999. This would include all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-highway vehicles up to 14,000 pounds GVWR, with the exception 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of those MDVs which are certified as engines under both CARB's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program and EPA's enhanced in-use compliance program described in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section B. Any of these vehicles offered for sale or first 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
registered in California would need to comply with the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use standards described below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
