<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The application of this program on a statewide basis is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered necessary because vehicle traffic flow into and out of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the areas covered by the FIPs is large. Any program constrained 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to only the FIP areas would be extremely difficult to implement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and enforce. EPA is concerned that the benefits of the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use compliance program may be diminished if a large number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles first registered in other states are brought into 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California. These vehicles may be less expensive because they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would not have to meet enhanced in-use or California low emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle standards. EPA proposes, therefore, very strict 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
restrictions on importation of 49-state vehicles (Section d.2.h. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the Preamble). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because new vehicle programs such as this one are relatively 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
slow in achieving their full emission reduction benefit due to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the time required for fleet turnover, EPA solicits comments on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the feasibility of implementing this program earlier, such as via 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a partial phase-in in model year 1998. The emissions reduction 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieved by earlier implementation could reduce the need for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increased stringency in other source categories. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described below, the proposed enhanced in-use compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program concerns only non-methane hydrocarbon (NMHC) and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

