<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For NMHC, EPA is proposing a composite (exhaust plus 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative) NMHC standard, as opposed to separate standards for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust NMHC emissions and the many types of evaporative-related 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NMHC emissions. This composite standard will allow greater 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
flexibility, as well as reduced costs, while ensuring that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
necessary in-use performance is attained. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to determine compliance with a composite NMHC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard, it was necessary to develop a method for combining 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust emissions and the three different components of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emissions (i.e., diurnal and hot soak emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(measured on a gram/test basis) and running loss emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(measured on a gram/mile basis)). Resting loss emissions are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
another form of evaporative emissions. However, there is no 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
current standard or separate test for resting losses because they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are measured during the various parts of the evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions test procedure, particularly during the diurnal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission test, and are accounted for in those results. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In support of its efforts to model in-use emissions (e.g., 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MOBILE5a), EPA has developed a methodology for combining exhaust 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and evaporative emissions into a composite emission factor. It 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
essentially consists of converting those components not measured 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on a gram per mile basis to grams per mile and then summing the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual components. The key conversion factors are the number 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of trips per day (for hot soaks) and the number of miles driven 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per day (for hot soaks and diurnals). Estimates of these factors 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over the useful life of LDVs and LDTs were taken from MOBILE5a. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Their use in converting individual NMHC emission levels into a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
single composite level or standard is shown in the equation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below.
<FOOTCITE>109</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
109
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
``Average Trips/Day and Miles/Day in MOBILE5 for Light Duty Autos,'' EPA memorandum from Lois Platte to the Record,
January 4, 1994. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4725 -->

<IMPORT>

<!-- PJG importformat s,w279,d57 -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=1 -->
E:GRAPHICSEP05my94.002
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</IMPORT>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->

<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA has not included refueling emissions in this equation given 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the uncertainty with respect to the use of onboard vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controls in California. EPA requests comments on their exclusion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and on the option of including refueling emissions in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
composite standard should California vehicles be subject to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
onboard refueling requirement. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Refueling emissions would be relatively simple to include, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as refueling emissions in terms of grams per gallon could simply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be divided by fuel economy to obtain NMHC emissions per mile. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The fuel economy used could either be measured over the FTP when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust NMHC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 were measured (an urban only fuel economy) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or taken from the certified urban/rural composite fuel economy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
value for that engine family. Including refueling emissions in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this manner would give some credit for high efficiency vehicles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
but would still represent additional testing and resultant cost. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given the high level of refueling emissions control expected with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
onboard controls, EPA believes that including refueling emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the composite standard would not justify the cost of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
additional testing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that the composite NMHC standards contained in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Table AI.A&hyph;1 are feasible, particularly given the flexibility 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
afforded in trading off exhaust and evaporative emissions. For 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example, a vehicle meeting the current CARB LEV standards for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust NMOG emissions and CARB's evaporative emission standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable to all LDVs and LDTs would comply with the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use standards. The CARB LDV/LDT standards are 0.05 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gram/mile for running losses and a total of 2.0 grams/test for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diurnal and hot soak emissions combined. A vehicle meeting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
running loss standard would contribute 0.05 g/mi or less to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equation presented above. The contribution of a vehicle meeting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the 2.0 grams/test emission standard would depend on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
breakdown between diurnal and hot soak emissions. The newly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
revised CARB and EPA evaporative emissions test procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
contain a one hour hot soak test and a multi-day diurnal test. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This requires very stringent control of resting loss emissions, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which occur during both tests. As the 2 gram standard requires 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the almost total elimination of NMHC emissions through the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emission control canister, EPA expects that the bulk 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of in-use emissions will be resting losses. Given that hot soak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions only include a single hour of resting losses, albeit at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
somewhat elevated temperatures, EPA expects that hot soak 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions will be very low relative to diurnal emissions and that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the bulk of the 2 grams will be diurnal emissions. Specifically, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
assuming that vehicles meeting the 2.0 grams/test evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission in-use emitted 1.9 grams/test diurnal and 0.1 gram/test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hot soak, the overall contribution in the above equation would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
0.082 g/mi. With running losses of 0.05 g/mi, this represents a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
total non-exhaust NMHC emission of 0.132 g/mi. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
