<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00210 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
For flexibility, EPA is also proposing optional engine-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards for diesel-powered and incomplete vehicle engines used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in MDVs with GVWR between 8,501 and 14,000 pounds. (Under CARB's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motor vehicle control program, such MDV engines have the option 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to certify to engine-based standards.) Manufacturers of such MDV 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines may elect to comply with the chassis-based program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in this section or they may elect to certify to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine-based standards described in Section B. of this appendix. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The reader is directed to that section for more information on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed engine-based standards and other MDV/engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements under this program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Authority.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 As explained above in Section III.A.2.a of the preamble, in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgating a FIP under section 110(c), EPA may take any actions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that the state could take. As section 209 of the CAA provides 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that California is not preempted from adopting and implementing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
motor vehicle emissions control program provided its program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
satisfies the criteria of section 209(b), EPA believes that it, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acting on behalf of California in the context of a FIP, may adopt 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a motor vehicle program or supplement California's own motor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle program provided that EPA's actions would satisfy the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria of section 209(b) for a waiver of federal preemption. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
That section provides that EPA is to grant California a waiver of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preemption if the State determines that its standards ``will be, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the aggregate, at least as protective of public health and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
welfare as applicable federal standards.'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section 209(b) further provides that no waiver is to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
granted if EPA finds that (A) the determination of the State that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the standards are, in the aggregate, at least as protective of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
public health and welfare as the otherwise applicable federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards is arbitrary and capricious, (B) State standards are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
not needed to meet compelling and extraordinary conditions, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(C) the State standards and accompanying enforcement procedures 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are not consistent with section 202(a). EPA has interpreted the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consistency criterion as meaning that California motor vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards and accompanying enforcement procedures must satisfy 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the leadtime requirements of section 202(a)_that they be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
technologically feasible within the leadtime provided, taking 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into account the cost of compliance_and not impose inconsistent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification test procedure requirements such that the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle could not be used to comply with both state and federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification requirements (see e.g., 43 FR 32182 (July 25, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1978), 40 FR 30131 (July 18, 1975), 43 FR 1839 (January 12, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1978)). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that the enhanced in-use compliance program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
light and medium-duty vehicles that would be applicable to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles and engines in California under the proposed FIP would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
satisfy the section 209 waiver criteria. Thus, California could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
undertake the enhanced in-use compliance program described in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this appendix, and, therefore, EPA has authority under section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
110(c) to undertake the proposed programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the protectiveness of the standards, all of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed standards are at least as protective as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise applicable federal standards (e.g., the Tier 1 NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NMHC standards in the case of light-duty vehicles and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generally applicable NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and NMHC standards in the case of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles and engines covered by the medium-duty and heavy-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
aspects of the enhanced in-use compliance program). Indeed, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these standards are at least as stringent as the comparable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards that California has already adopted and for which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waivers have already been granted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the need to meet compelling and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extraordinary conditions, EPA has repeatedly found that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California's air quality problems satisfy the need criterion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 209(b) (see, e.g., 49 FR 18887 and 18890 (May 3, 1984), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
58 FR 4144 (January 13, 1993)). Moreover, the magnitude of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in emissions needed to reach attainment in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas, including mobile source emissions (discussed above in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section III.H.2 of the preamble), provides ample evidence of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
need for more stringent motor vehicle emission standards in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, with respect to the criterion of consistency with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 202(a), EPA believes that its proposed enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance program is consistent with both the technological 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
feasibility and certification elements of that criterion. For 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the reasons explained below, EPA believes that the enhanced in-use compliance program is technologically feasible
within the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
leadtime provided, taking into account the cost of compliance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Moreover, no aspect of the enhanced in-use compliance program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
establishes any new certification test procedures that are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inconsistent with either existing federal or California test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure requirements. Consequently, EPA believes that its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed enhanced in-use compliance programs satisfy this prong 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of section 209 as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Enhanced In-use Compliance Recall Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Historically, EPA has enforced emissions standards through 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an in-use testing program commonly referred to as the ``recall'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program (39 FR 44375, Dec. 23, 1974), as provided by section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
207(c) of the CAA. This section requires manufacturers to take 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
remedial actions if EPA finds that a ``substantial'' number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles, although properly maintained and used, fail to conform 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to an applicable emissions standard during their useful life. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing a recall program under the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance program that is different in several aspects compared 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the program established pursuant to CAA section 207(c) for 49 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state vehicles and engines. The differences and the rationale 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for them are described fully in the paragraphs below. The recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provisions described in this section apply to 1999 and later 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
model year light-duty vehicles, light-duty trucks, and medium-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
duty vehicles certified to the chassis-based standards described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the previous section. These recall provisions also apply to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-highway HDEs and nonroad engines, except as noted in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall provisions contained in section B for HDEs and section C 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for nonroad engines below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA has based the enhanced in-use compliance recall program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on its current recall program, where possible. Thus, aspects of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the program not specifically discussed here will be the same as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in the current program (see 40 CFR part 85 subpart S). For 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
example, all test procedures utilized will be current Federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures, with the exception of ambient temperatures used in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the evaporative emission and running loss testing and the fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in all testing. EPA will utilize CARB's ambient 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
temperatures and CARB Phase II reformulated gasoline in recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If CARB decides to enforce the enhanced in-use compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall program and assume responsibility for the recall program, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CARB will likely use its own detailed recall methodologies, for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which it has received waivers under section 209 of the CAA, with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate changes to ensure compliance with the changes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described below. EPA requests comments on the use of current 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall practices where not modified below, as well as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possible performance of recall testing by CARB using their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The proposed enhanced in-use compliance recall program is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in detail in the following sections: (a) Recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards (b) consideration of maintenance, tampering, and abuse, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(c) test vehicle selection, (d) testing throughout the vehicles' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
useful life, (e) vehicle family selection, (f) criteria for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requiring recall, (g) mandatory owner participation in recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repairs, (h) geographic extent of recall, (i) technical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
feasibility, and (j) alternatives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
