<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00211 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Recall standards.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The key premise of the enhanced in-use compliance recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program is that vehicles meet their certified emission levels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
throughout their entire useful life. The standards which will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
apply under the enhanced in-use compliance recall program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
LDVs, LDTs and MDVs will be those in the tables presented above 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in Section A.(2). Calculation of composite NMHC emissions will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be according to the equation also presented in Appendix I.A.2.a. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Consideration of maintenance, tampering, and abuse.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 For 49 state vehicles, CAA section 207(c) limits 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers' responsibility to those vehicles that have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``properly maintained and used.'' Manufacturers are able to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exclude vehicles that do not receive such care or cannot be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proven to have received such care from the recall testing pool 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or, if they believe that such vehicles have been included, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
challenge the validity of any findings made. Due to this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limitation, EPA generally only tests vehicles that have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintained and used in strict accordance to the owner's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance manual. It is particularly difficult to find older 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles with sufficient documentation or to prove ``proper 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
care.'' The current program, therefore, provides little incentive 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the manufacturer to design and build vehicles with durable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions performance under normal or typical maintenance and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As mentioned in the introduction in Section 2.d of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preamble, the primary goal of the enhanced in-use compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program is to ensure that vehicles remain in compliance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions standards in use over their full life under normal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating conditions. If motor vehicles are to cease to be a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
major source of ozone-related emissions and noncompliance with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the NAAQS for ozone, manufacturers must make greater efforts to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure continued in-use emissions performance. Therefore, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposes not to exclude vehicles from recall testing unless they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been subject to obvious abuse or tampering. Obvious abuse 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or tampering includes: (1) Less than one half the recommended 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level of oil in the engine, (2) the deliberate removal of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
catalyst or evaporative canister, (3) the turbocharging of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
naturally aspirated engine, and (4) damage to the engine or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission controls resulting from a vehicular accident. EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing to exclude such vehicles from recall testing because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such actions can severely affect vehicle emissions and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer has no reasonable way of controlling or limiting the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impact of these items on emissions. EPA requests comments on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other specific items that would meet these criteria. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, EPA will not perform a vehicle ``tune-up'' or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
perform other emission related repairs or maintenance before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing. EPA believes that pre-recall maintenance would defeat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the purpose of the enhanced in-use compliance recall program, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which is to further encourage durable designs. Even hoses whose 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proper connection is critical to acceptable emission performance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
can be designed to be unlikely to disconnect during normal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintenance and use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As with current recall testing, EPA will inspect vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prior to testing to determine if obvious abuse or tampering has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occurred. As is current EPA practice, the vehicle manufacturer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
can be present at time of vehicle procurement and testing to help 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure that vehicles are appropriately screened for obvious abuse 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and tampering problems. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
It is also important to point out, as will be discussed in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
more detail in the next section, that only vehicles which are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to the new enhanced I/M program are candidates for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use recall testing. Participation in this 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program should encourage owners to perform reasonable maintenance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and deter emission related tampering. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Again, EPA requests comments on the types of abuse or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tampering which would allow for the exclusion of vehicles from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the recall sample. Commenters should provide supporting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rationale and data to the extent practicable so that EPA may 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ascertain the severity, likelihood, and possible mitigating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
actions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Test vehicle selection.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 EPA proposes to select vehicles for enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance recall testing from the portion of the vehicle family 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
population that is subject to the enhanced I/M testing program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described above. This would essentially limit testing to only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles that are registered in one of the three FIP areas, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unless the State of California opts to adopt enhanced I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures in other areas of the state. EPA is proposing to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
limit the sampling in this way because recall testing resources 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are limited and the vehicles registered in the three FIP areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are the most relevant with regard to air quality in these areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Furthermore, problems that cause noncompliance within this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subpopulation of vehicles are very likely to exist in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
population as a whole. The reverse may not be true because I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be insuring reasonable maintenance and catching and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
correcting emissions problems. EPA believes it would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inappropriate to require a recall campaign due to a problem found 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
outside of a FIP area that enhanced I/M would have possibly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
already corrected. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on the above sampling methods. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also requests comments on other alternatives, including limiting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the sampling to vehicles subject to basic I/M as well as enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M or sampling from the entire state's vehicle population. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
d. Testing throughout the vehicle's useful life.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 EPA proposes to conduct recall testing throughout the useful 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
life of the vehicles specified in the applicable regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA would also require any necessary remedial actions to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performed on all vehicles in the vehicle family still within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their useful life. EPA believes that conducting recall in this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manner is consistent with the intent of the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance program, which is to hold the manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible for in-use emissions compliance over the full useful 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
life period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00212 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA recognizes that vehicles are often used for periods longer than the useful life period specified in the applicable
regulations. In California, over 40 percent of onroad light-duty vehicles have over 100,000 miles on their odometers.
EPA is concerned about the emissions of vehicles after the end of the vehicle's useful life because, although such
vehicles will still be subject to I/M testing, manufacturers will be no longer responsible for ensuring that the vehicles
remain in compliance with the in-use recall standards. Comments are requested on whether other in-use compliance
programs such an enhanced OBD, which remain effective beyond the vehicle's statutory useful life, are feasible and
may result in equal or greater emission reductions than the proposed enhanced in-use recall program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For heavy-duty engines (discussed in section B. below), which are rebuilt several times and are used significantly
longer than the useful life specified by the manufacturer on a regular basis, EPA is proposing rebuild regulations
(see section B.) for engines beyond their originally certified useful life. EPA requests comments on adopting comparable
rebuild provisions for light-duty and medium-duty vehicles that are certified to chassis-based standards in order
to ensure that these vehicles continue to meet in-use recall standards throughout their operating lives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
e. Vehicle family selection. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As with the 49 state recall program, EPA may select vehicle families for enhanced in-use compliance recall testing
at its discretion. For the 49 state recall program, EPA examines a variety of items when choosing a vehicle family for
recall testing including testing data from certification and production line audits, production volumes, and defect
reports. EPA is likely to also examine these items for the enhanced in-use compliance recall program. Additionally,
EPA is planning to examine enhanced I/M testing results for patterns of failure within exhaust engine families. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also plans to track the various technologies being used to meet enhanced in-use standards. If EPA believes that
a technology may be more susceptible to in-use emissions performance deterioration, EPA may choose to proceed with
recall testing of vehicle families using that particular technology. Special concerns may arise related to the use
of a particular fuel or, more likely, related to the complexity of a technology and therefore its additional areas
for failure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
f. Criteria for requiring recall.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
  EPA proposes to, at its discretion, require a manufacturer to recall vehicles and take remedial action if the average
emissions of the test vehicles for any regulated pollutant exceed the applicable emissions standard. EPA proposes
to retain the discretion not to order a recall if it determines that a recall action is not warranted. Manufacturers
would be able to present data and reasoning to EPA for the Agency to consider in making a final decision on whether or
not to proceed with a recall. However, EPA is concerned that the recall program may not be viewed as a strong enforcement
tool if its authority is too discretionary. EPA wishes to make clear its intent to hold manufacturers responsible
for in-use failures of essentially all vehicles except those that have been subject to obvious abuse or tampering.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also recognizes that outlier vehicles in the recall sample could cause an inappropriate recall. The impact of
an outlier on the results of a recall test could be substantial because the recall test sample could be relatively small
(about 10 vehicles). EPA would allow manufacturers to provide additional test data showing that a vehicle is unrepresentative
(i.e., an outlier), and this data may then be considered by EPA in making a final determination. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is also concerned that vehicles representing a small percentage of the engine family population (e.g., 5 percent
or less) could exhibit such relatively high emissions as to cause the average emissions of the entire engine family
to greatly exceed the enhanced in-use standards. For example, if five vehicles per one hundred failed to their pre-control
levels of 7 or 8 g/mi NMHC and the remaining vehicles had zero emissions, the engine family would still exceed its standard
by 100 percent. EPA is concerned about the emissions impact of this engine family, but is also concerned about the cost,
inefficiency and vehicle owner inconvenience of a traditional recall where 95 out of 100 vehicles would be returned
to the dealer but require no repair. While the enhanced I/M program will reduce the likelihood of occurrence, such
situations may still be common. Comments are requested on alternatives to conventional recall in such cases. Comments
on innovative onboard diagnostic capabilities or other screening techniques are also requested. One such approach
is discussed in section (j). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on the recall criteria and authority described above for the enhanced in-use compliance recall
program. EPA encourages any recommendations for adopting different criteria be supported by data and detailed rationale.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
g. Mandatory owner participation in recall repairs. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Because of the potential large negative impact that vehicles exceeding the standards have on air quality, EPA believes
that owners should be required to bring their recalled vehicles in for repairs. While EPA's 49 state recall program
does not require owners of recalled vehicles to go to a dealership for needed repairs, California does require recall
repairs to be performed (see section 2129 of title 13 of the California Code of Regulations) and the requirement is
enforced by denying vehicle reregistration until the repairs have been made. (Driving unregistered vehicles is,
of course, unlawful and prohibited.) California currently begins to deny reregistrations six months after the manufacturer
begins its remedial action plan. EPA proposes that vehicles subject to the enhanced in-use compliance program also
be subject to the same mandatory repair requirements as other California vehicles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00213 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
h. Geographic extent of recall.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
  EPA is proposing that the enhanced in-use compliance program apply to all vehicles sold in California for both enforceability
and environmental reasons. EPA therefore proposes that any required recalls also apply to all vehicles of the affected
engine family which have been sold throughout the state. This will also include vehicles from all model years that
are part of the same engine family. Engine families consist of vehicles with the same basic emissions design and performance
and problems with a single model year's vehicles should also exist on other model years' vehicles. EPA requests comments
on the extension of recalls to all vehicles statewide versus only those vehicles registered in the three FIP areas.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
i. Technical feasibility.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 As discussed in Section A.(2) above, EPA does not expect manufacturers to have to reduce the low-mileage emissions
of properly operating vehicles in order to comply with the proposed recall provisions. The degree that vehicle design
changes are necessary to better ensure improved in-use emission performance under this program will depend on the
technologies and cost tradeoffs manufacturers will make in response to CARB's existing LEV and OBD programs and the
enhanced I/M program. If a manufacturer has sufficient confidence that its vehicles will comply with the enhanced
in-use standards, there will obviously be no technological or cost implications. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, if a manufacturer lacks such confidence, then vehicle designs could be improved. In general, the durability
of every aspect of a vehicles' current and future emission control system can always be improved. The primary issues
are cost and the level of reliability. EPA expects each manufacturer to review the durability of each of its emission
control system components and assess whether it is adequate given the additional financial disincentives of the
emissions in-use compliance program which would occur should it fail. The decision would then be made to further improve
design durability or accept the current durability as adequate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If a manufacturer decided that it needed to increase the probability that its vehicles would comply with the enhanced
in-use standards under typical conditions of maintenance and use, EPA believes that numerous methods exist with
which this can be done. For example, vehicular emission control can be divided into two basic components, efficient
combustion and catalytic conversion. Efficient combustion relies primarily on fuel management and sufficient
ignition spark, including its timing. Efficient catalytic conversion depends most heavily on fuel management and
sufficient active catalytic material. Fuel management in turn depends heavily on the onboard computer and the feedback
control mechanism (i.e., the oxygen sensor) and the fuel injector. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA believes that CARB's existing fuel detergent program will help address manufacturers' concerns related to fuel
injector, intake valve and combustion chamber deposits and the possible associated degradation of emission control
performance. EPA also believes that fuel injection technology has reached a sufficient stage of development such
that maintenance free operation is possible (i.e., EPA is not aware of any required maintenance for fuel injectors
on LDVs, LDTs, or MDVs). Catastrophic failure of an individual injector can always occur, but this should be immediately
identified by the OBD system. Likewise, recent electronic ignition systems are also maintenance free, with the exception
of the spark plugs and possibly the ignition wires. If improvements were desired in these areas, gold and platinum
tipped spark plugs could be considered. These plugs are already in limited use and offer the advantage of improved
durability. Manufacturers have already utilized, to a varying degree, high voltage ignition to lessen the importance
of wire separation, as the higher ignition voltage will cross a sizable gap in the wire as well as across the spark plug
tip. Onboard computers have clearly generated a track record of proper operation over the life of the vehicle. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emission control as well as efficient combustion also depends on the proper operation of exhaust gas recirculation
(EGR) valves. The primary in-use problem with EGR valves is plugging or sticking due to combustion products, particularly
unburned fuel which can solidify with prolonged heating. A manufacturer could decide to redesign its valves by smoothing
its channels, eliminating sharp bends, better lubricating valve axles, etc., as well as changing the valve location
to control temperature and deposit formation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00214 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The two remaining emission controls, the oxygen sensor and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
catalysts (both under body and quick light-off) are widely 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recognized as key components in maintaining proper in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions performance. A manufacturer could decide to improve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the durability of the active surface of the oxygen sensor or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
change its placement in the exhaust to limit deposits and thermal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
degradation. Likewise, more durable catalyst washcoats exist 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
relative to those commonly used and the overall loading of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
catalytic material can also be increased. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Regarding evaporative emissions, its more basic operation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should make a manufacturer's durability assessment much easier. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The key components besides the onboard computer are the purge 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
valve, the vapor lines and the charcoal canister. The design of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a durable purge valve should be much easier to assess and improve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if necessary, relative to an EGR valve, given the clean nature of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the vapor flowing through the valve. Non-metal vapor lines can 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be replaced with metal lines if necessary to provide increased 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
confidence of leak-free operation. With adequate purge, charcoal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
canister should not fall below their in-use design capacity with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time or usage. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While uncertainty exists as to exactly how manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will choose to comply with this program, the main issue appears 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be one of further improvements in durability versus cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tradeoffs. The program appears to be technologically feasible. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As for the costs, estimates of which are contained in the draft 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
RIA supporting this FIP, EPA believes them to be reasonable. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
j. Alternatives.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
  The objective of the proposed enhanced in-use compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall program is to identify and correct systemic design defects 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or lack of durability within an engine family. However, as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed in section A.(f)., the proposed enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards could be exceeded if just a few percent of the vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within an engine family had high emissions, even though the rest 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emitted at well below the standard. In this situation, recalling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the entire engine family to correct random defects which result 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in a few high emitters is inefficient, inconvenient and costly. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Hopefully, the enhanced I/M program will identify and correct 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these high emitters. However, in some situations such as a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
defect in the cold start emission control system, the I/M program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will not identify the problem. On-board diagnostics could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
identify the problem, but it does not guarantee that the owner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will seek repair of the defect in a timely manner. This may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
especially a concern for older vehicles where the relative cost 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of repairs compared to the value of the car is high. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
An alternative to the proposed enhanced in-use recall 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program involves the use of remote sensing to quickly identify 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual vehicles with excess emissions. Remote sensing of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions is now required by federal law in all non-attainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas subject to enhanced I/M, including the three FIP areas. A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potentially more effective approach could utilize recent advances 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in electronics which allow communication between the vehicle's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-board diagnostic system and a roadside transmitter. This type 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of communication with moving vehicles has been developed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substitute for toll booths on highways and bridges, and for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
collecting use-fees for heavy trucks. CARB will soon be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
demonstrating the application of this technology to on-board 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diagnostics. Under this concept, when prompted, a small 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
transmitter on the vehicle would communicate the VIN and if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on-board computer has sensed any emission control system defects. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The owners of vehicles with defects would be notified by mail to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repair the vehicle and report to a smog inspection station for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
verification. This is the same remedy that will soon be required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of owners whose vehicles fail a remote or roadside emission test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in an area with enhanced I/M. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The advantages of such a system include assuring that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles with high emissions are repaired quickly, rather than up 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to two years later at the next 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M test. This would help lower 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average in-use emissions closer to the certification standard. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, this approach would remain effective throughout the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle's life, an important advantage over the proposed enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use compliance program, since over 40 percent of California's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
light-duty on-road vehicles have exceeded their 100,000 mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
useful life. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Like the I/M 240 test, the objective of CARB's OBD program is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to identify nearly all problems causing excess emissions. Thus, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this same remote detection system could also lower the cost of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
periodic inspections since the inspection could be performed by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
driving by an unmanned sensor at any time. The cost of such an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inspection would be minimal, and could quickly offset the cost of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipping new vehicles with an on-board transmitter. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The major disadvantage of this approach is that the remote 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sensing technology has not been fully demonstrated. Thus, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cannot yet propose the details of a regulation which would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement such a requirement as part of the FIP. However, EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seeking comments on whether this approach has sufficient merit to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be considered as an alternative or complement to the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use compliance program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00215 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. I/M-Based Recall Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The I/M aspect of the enhanced in-use compliance program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(also referred to as the I/M-based recall program) proposed today 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applies only if a significant portion of vehicles do not meet the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M standards. The enhanced I/M test equipment, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test procedures, and other physical elements of the program will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
remain unchanged from that described in Section III.D.2.c. of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this preamble or from any equivalent program adopted by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California. All 1999 and later model year California LDVs, LDTs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and MDVs covered by the enhanced in-use standards described in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Section A.(2) above will be included in this program, regardless 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of fuel type. (See section III.D.2.c. of this preamble for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
description of I/M for non-California vehicles.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Overall, the enhanced in-use I/M program does three things. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
First, it implements a more stringent set of I/M emission levels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(hereafter referred to as the enhanced in-use I/M standards). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Second, it creates a maximum fraction of any particular exhaust 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission engine family which can fail the more stringent enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use I/M standards without consequence for the manufacturer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(hereafter referred to as the liability threshold). Third, once 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the failure rate of the enhanced in-use I/M standards exceeds the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability threshold, it initiates a selective recall and makes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the vehicle manufacturer liable for the repairs resulting from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subsequent I/M failures. As described above, the purpose of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program is to encourage manufacturers to place a high design 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
priority on in-use vehicle performance and ease of repair in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
order to ensure that vehicles comply with their certification 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards in-use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The sequence of events at a typical enhanced I/M test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facility will be as follows. Vehicles brought for testing will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be measured against two sets of standards: the conventional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M standards described in Section III.D.2.c. of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preamble and the lower enhanced in-use I/M standards described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below. Unless a significant portion of vehicles in any engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
family have failed the enhanced in-use I/M standards, vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
owners will only be required to repair their vehicles if they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fail the conventional enhanced I/M standards. However, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of vehicles failing the more stringent enhanced in-use I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards will be tallied. If the percentage of a specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine family failing the enhanced in-use I/M standards exceeds 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the liability thresholds described below, two events occur. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
First, future vehicles of this particular engine family failing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the more stringent enhanced in-use I/M standards now must be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repaired and retested. Second, the manufacturer, and not the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle owner, will be responsible for the repairs for vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
still within their statutory useful life which have not obviously 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been abused or tampered with in a manner likely to affect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions (i.e., an I/M-based selective recall). As described 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below, the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M standards are numerically higher 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
than the enhanced in-use FTP emission levels. Thus, as long as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles are performing close to the standards to which they are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certified, only the conventional enhanced I/M program will apply 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and there will be no post-manufacturing consequence for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer of the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The sections below describe the nature of the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M program including testing, enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M standards, threshold for manufacturer liability, tracking of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M test results, repair responsibility, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic coverage, and other administrative aspects of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Testing.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
  As discussed in more detail in Section III.D.2.c. of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preamble, three types of I/M tests will be performed on light- 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and medium-duty vehicles. First, exhaust emissions will be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
measured using the I/M 240 test. Second, purge and pressure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tests will be performed to establish the evaporative system's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
functionality and integrity. Finally, a visual inspection will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be performed to check for missing canisters, catalysts, etc. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which would indicate that obvious tampering or abuse has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
occurred. Vehicles with obvious tampering or abuse which fail 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the enhanced I/M 240, pressure, or purge tests will require 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repair at the owner's expense. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes to include post-1998 model year diesel- and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
alternative-fueled (CNG and LPG) vehicles in enhanced I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing, as well as gasoline vehicles. Evaporative system checks 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be revised where appropriate for alternative-fueled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles, since some fuel types (e.g., diesel fuel and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pressurized fuels) produce little or no evaporative emissions as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
long as their fuel tanks are not ruptured. Diesel-fueled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles are currently exempt from most I/M programs, because 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their HC emissions are inherently low and their historical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions has not depended on technologies that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
deteriorate significantly over time. Alternative-fueled vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have been exempted because of their low numbers. However, as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission standards applicable to post-1998 model year vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be very stringent regardless of fuel type, EPA is proposing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that every vehicle be tested under the enhanced I/M program, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regardless of fuel type. EPA requests comment on the testing of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diesel- and alternative-fueled vehicles in the proposed enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use I/M program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00216 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Proposed enhanced in-use I/M standards.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 The table below (``Proposed Enhanced In-Use I/M Standards for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
LDVs and LDTs'') summarizes the enhanced in-use I/M standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed in this NPRM for 1999 and later model year LDVs and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
LDTs. Passage or failure of these standards will be used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determine compliance with the liability thresholds. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards will also be used to determine the need for repairs and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
retesting after the liability threshold has been exceeded. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<TABLE>

<!-- PJG tableformat 5,L2,i1,s100,r100,10,10,10 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=4 -->
Proposed Enhanced In-Use I/M Standards for LDVs and LDTs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Vehicle class
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Category
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
