<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As mentioned above, under enhanced I/M, exhaust emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be measured over the I/M 240 test procedure, a shortened 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
version of the certification test procedure. Emissions measured 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over the I/M 240 test procedure and the certification test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure are somewhat different for a number of reasons. First, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different vehicles with the same overall FTP emissions will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
perform differently over each segment of the FTP. Because only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
part of the certification driving cycle is used in the I/M 240 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
test, individual vehicles with the same FTP emissions may produce 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
somewhat different I/M 240 emissions. Also, and most importantly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for NMHC emissions, the absence of a cold start in the I/M test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will tend to reduce variability and result in a lower g/mi value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the I/M test. Other factors contribute to greater 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variability between the full FTP &blank;&blank;&blank;&blank; and I/M 240 testing. These 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
factors include the type of driving a vehicle encountered 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
immediately previous to the test, the specific fuel in its tank, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and numerous other environmental factors which can be controlled 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in certification and recall testing, but which cannot be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controlled in I/M testing, such as temperature and humidity. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, an important objective of the enhanced in-use I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards is to provide an incentive for manufacturers to design 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles which do not have significant emissions increases when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operated under such typical in-use conditions as compared to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
highly standardized FTP. If vehicles continue to be produced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with a high degree of emissions sensitivity, their increased 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions under typical use (versus the FTP) would substantially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
add to the emissions burden caused by light- and medium-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Considering the factors just described and non-vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
related test variability, the enhanced in-use I/M standards in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the table above (``Proposed Enhanced In-Use I/M Standards for LDVs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and LDTs'') are being proposed. EPA believes these levels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
represent a reasonable balance between granting a cushion between 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification and I/M emissions levels, and in establishing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer liability and the need to provide a greater 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentive for the design of emission controls systems which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maintain low emissions under variations in typical in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions. (For example, the proposed enhanced in-use THC I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard is 0.20 g/mi, 2.2 times higher than CARB's 100,000 mile 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification NMHC standard for LDV LEVs of 0.9 g/mi.) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Due to the fact that the I/M 240 test only measures warmed-up 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust emissions, compliance with these standards should not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any more technologically challenging or costly than compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the recall standards already discussed in Section A.(3) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above. The only issue could be a vehicle's emissions variability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over the IM240 test relative to the full FTP, as mentioned above. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
There is no inherent technical reason for exhibiting this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variabiity, except a lack of concern that vehicles' emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
controls operate fully over all types of normal driving and not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
only over the exact driving sequence embodied in the FTP. In 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fact, many if not most vehicles already exhibit similar control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
over both tests. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The enhanced in-use I/M standards for LDTs were derived 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
using the same factors and methodology described above for LDVs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As with LDVs, EPA is also proposing a single THC I/M standard for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each subclass of LDTs that will apply throughout the useful life 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of a LDT. EPA believes that the technology and emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance of LDTs will be similar to that of LDVs and therefore 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the same factors can be used for establishing the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M standards for LDTs. EPA requests comments on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reasonableness of this assumption. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Similar factors were used to derive the transitional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M standards. As described in Section A.(2) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above, these standards will apply to up to 25 percent of each 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer's sales of 1999 model year vehicles. EPA requests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments on these levels, as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The table below (``Proposed Enhanced In-Use I/M Standards for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MDVs'') contains the proposed enhanced in-use I/M standards for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
MDVs. These standards will apply to all California MDVs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certified to chassis-based standards. (Those MDV engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certified to the engine-based standards are not subject to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M provisions, as described in Section B. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
below.) The enhanced in-use I/M standards for MDVs provide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
essentially the same cushion between themselves and the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use standards for MDVs as described above for the enhanced in-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use I/M standards for LDVs and LDTs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
