<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Similar to the light-duty enhanced in-use I/M standards, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use compliance I/M standards for MDVs should be more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
easily met relative to the enhanced in-use MDV FTP emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards for composite NMHC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 described in Section A.(3) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above. As was the case for light-duty vehicles, the lack of a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cold start in the IM240 test, generally higher numerical standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
levels and the fact that evaporative emissions are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
quantitatively included together should more than compensate for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any variability resulting from the use of the shorter IM240 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cycle. This is particularly true given manufacturers will have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ability to design their vehicles for good emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
performance over the IM240 cycle as well as over the full FTP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on the level of the proposed enhanced in-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use I/M standards for LDVs, LDTs and MDVs, as well as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed transitional enhanced in-use I/M standards. EPA also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requests comments on the use of a single enhanced in-use I/M NMHC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard and on the option of applying a more stringent standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the first five years or 50,000 miles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Threshold for manufacturers responsibility.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Under enhanced I/M programs for conventional vehicles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
owners are responsible for repairs of those vehicles which fail 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the enhanced I/M tests. EPA is proposing that manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would become liable for repairs of a specific vehicle engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
family (i.e., an I/M-based selective recall) if 5.0 percent of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the tested vehicles of that engine family failed the enhanced in-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
use I/M standards during the first half of the family's useful 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
life or if 10.0 percent failed before the end of the family's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
full useful life. That is, if more than 5.0 percent of tested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles of any engine family/model year combination fail the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M standards before 50,001 miles or fifth year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
after the production year (whichever comes first), then the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for repair of any future failures of vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
belonging to that engine family/model year combination will shift 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the manufacturer. This percentage rises to 10.0 percent for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles up to 100,000 miles or in their sixth through the tenth 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
years of use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In staying below these thresholds, the manufacturer must 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
consider the typical level of maintenance provided its vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in enhanced I/M areas. It is extremely difficult to identify the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lowest level of maintenance short of abuse. It is also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
impossible to build vehicles such that none of the vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
develop problems in-use. However, participation in the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M programs will encourage individual vehicle owners to perform 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
increased maintenance to reduce the likelihood of I/M failure and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the subsequent burden of repair and re-test. Thus, a lower 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure rate may be reasonable to expect from vehicles in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced I/M areas. EPA believes that the 5 and 10 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure rates proposed above balance the responsibilities of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer to build a vehicle which performs well under typical 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use conditions and those of the vehicle owner to properly care 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for the vehicle. EPA requests comment on the appropriateness of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the two liability thresholds presented here. EPA also asks for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comments on alternative liability thresholds, as well as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate justification for those alternatives. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes that the liability threshold for the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use compliance I/M program apply separately to each affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust emission family in each model year after 1998. Since 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
several exhaust families can fall into a single evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
family, but generally not vice versa, EPA believes that isolating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M repair liability within an exhaust family is the most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate approach. In addition, EPA is proposing that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability thresholds apply separately for HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Thus, if the number of HC emission failures exceeded a threshold, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
but the number of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emission failures did not, manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would only be responsible for repairing those vehicles which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failed the enhanced in-use I/M HC standards. A difficult 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
question arises when one of the two pollutant thresholds have 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been exceeded and a vehicle fails both of the enhanced in-use I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards. EPA desires to limit manufacturer liability as much 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as possible to the repair of the problems related to that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
pollutant exceeding its threshold. However, it may not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practical to separate what repairs affect each pollutant and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determining such a split could lead to frequent disagreement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
between manufacturer and owner. Thus, EPA proposes that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers be responsible for performing all repairs to those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles which failed both the enhanced in-use I/M HC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards, but have exceeded only one (i.e, HC or NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) threshold. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, EPA requests comments on requiring manufacturers to only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be liable for repairing the vehicle in order to meet the I/M 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard for the pollutant exceeding its threshold. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

