<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00218 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
d. Tracking of vehicle failures.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 Compliance with the liability thresholds will be determined on a model year/engine family basis (i.e., I/M test results
will be segregated by engine family and model year). EPA proposes to segregate tracking by model year to prevent the

<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M test results of more recent model year vehicles within a given engine family from diluting the test results of earlier
model year vehicles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on this approach. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While EPA will receive actual I/M 240 emission results from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the performers of enhanced I/M testing, only pass or fail results 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
will be tallied against the 5 and 10 percent thresholds. A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle will enter the tracking system when it receives its first 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced I/M test. As long as it passes that and any subsequent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced I/M test, it will be considered a ``pass'' vehicle. Once 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
it fails any of the three HC related tests (i.e., the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use I/M standard for exhaust HC or the pressure or purge 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tests), it will be considered an HC failure. Once it fails the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standard, it will be considered a NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure. If a failure occurred while the vehicle is within its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
first half life (e.g., for a LDV, 5.0 years old and 50,001 miles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
or less), the failure will be included in the failure tally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
against both the 5 percent half-life and the 10 percent full-life 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
thresholds. If the failure occurred while the vehicle is within 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
its second half life (e.g., for a LDV, more than 5.0 years old 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 50,000 miles but no more than 10.0 years old and 100,000 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
miles), the failure will only be included in the failure tally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
against the 10 percent full-life threshold. The failure rate for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
each model year/engine family combination will consist of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of vehicles failing the enhanced in-use I/M HC or NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards divided by the number of vehicles which have been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tested in an enhanced I/M program. The I/M test results would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cease to be counted after individual vehicles exceeded an age of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
10 years or 100,000 miles (120,000 miles for MDVs), respectively. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For example, if 2.0 percent of an engine family's vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(from a single model year) fail the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
I/M standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for a given pollutant over its first two years (i.e., a biennial 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program) and an additional 2.0 percent (i.e., different vehicles) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fail over the next two years, the failure rate after 4 years is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
4.0 percent. Comments are requested on the use of only pass/fail 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information and the calculation procedure. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Once a specific model year/engine family combination 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeded a liability threshold, EPA proposes that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer become liable for the selective recall and repair of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles not only from that model year, but for the repair of all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles which are part of that engine family. While this could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly increase the potential liability faced by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers, it will more equitably protect the consumer by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
treating all vehicles with essentially the same emission control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
design (and thus, performance) the same. EPA requests comments 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on this approach, as well as the option whereby manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
were only held liable for the repairs of vehicles from the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specific model year which caused the exceedance of the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use I/M liability threshold. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If exceedance of the liability threshold appears likely, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
typical failure modes could be determined and efficient repair 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
methods developed. A manufacturer could even go so far as to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
take preventative action (e.g., a voluntary recall) to reduce the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of I/M failures (and the triggering of the liability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
threshold) and avoid the negative publicity or market response 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that may otherwise result. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also proposes to exclude the I/M test results of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles if they have been obviously abused or tampered. (More 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussion on what might constitute obvious abuse or tampering is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
included in Section A.(3) above addressing enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance recall requirements.) EPA proposes to limit the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
definition of such abuse or tampering for the purpose of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced in-use I/M program to a missing catalyst or evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
canister. Tampering can be discouraged by the vehicle's design 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and performance. While EPA is aware that manufacturers cannot 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
prevent all forms of less obvious tampering problems, the Agency 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
wants to create incentives for manufacturers to design vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as tamper-resistant as is reasonable. EPA requests comments on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the appropriate inclusion of specific determinations of obvious 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
abuse and tampering which would be practical by I/M test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
personnel in an enhanced I/M test, while maintaining the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
integrity and purpose of the enhanced in-use I/M program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
e. Repair responsibility.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
  Under conventional enhanced I/M, individual vehicle owners are responsible for repairs based on failures of either
the enhanced I/M 240 cutpoints and the pressure/purge functional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tests. Motorists are required to spend a maximum of $450 on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repairs within a biennial test cycle related to the emission test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure before being eligible for a waiver of the requirement to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comply. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00219 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If the enhanced in-use I/M liability threshold is reached, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes that manufacturers become subject to a selective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall and be responsible for all subsequent vehicle repairs that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are identified by the enhanced I/M emission test at the enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in-use I/M standards (described previously) and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purge/pressure tests. Manufacturers would thereafter be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible for all vehicle repairs within that engine family 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
model year until the end of the vehicle's useful life. A 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer's repair responsibility would begin after the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability threshold is reached, with no retroactive liability. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is not proposing reimbursement from the manufacturer to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
individual vehicle owners for repairs that occurred prior to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeding the enhanced in-use I/M liability threshold. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is also concerned that some repairs to high mileage 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles could cost more than the vehicle is worth. EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore proposing to allow a manufacturer to remedy an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions failure by purchasing (or offering to purchase) the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle. The manufacturer could then remove the vehicle from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
service instead of making the necessary repairs. The actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
value could be determined through a National Automobile Dealer's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Association (NADA) publication (e.g., ``Blue Book value'') or via 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
negotiation. The latter is always available, but, as the only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
option, could become quite involved and result in vastly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different prices being paid for similar vehicles. EPA therefore 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposes that the manufacturer be allowed to offer the average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
retail value for the vehicle in question as specified in the NADA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
``Official Used Car Guide,'' if the manufacturers elects this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
option. One option would utilize the national ``Blue Book'' to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
avoid the possible effects of this program on ``Blue Book'' levels 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in California. Another option would be to use the ``Blue Book'' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable in the specific FIP area. EPA is also concerned that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the ``Blue Book'' value may not fully compensate an owner for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
time and energy needed to find a new vehicle and other intangibles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comment on other methods of determining values that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proximate the market value of the vehicle, as well as the value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to its owner, but provide reasonable protection for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Under this proposal, the vehicle owner has the option of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rejecting the manufacturer's offer to purchase their vehicle. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Instead, the owner could repair the vehicle at his or her own 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expense or sell the vehicle in an area not subject to enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M and the enhanced in-use compliance program. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer's liability to repair the vehicle would end after 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the offer to purchase the vehicle was either consummated or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rejected in writing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is also concerned whether the manufacturer would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exercise its responsibility to repair the vehicle in a way which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
minimized inconvenience to the owner. EPA therefore proposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that all required repairs be performed in an expeditious manner 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and requests comments on specific requirements that could be used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to ensure this was in fact done. Specifically, EPA proposes, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requests comments on, requiring manufacturers to provide a rental 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle of equivalent value and function free of charge if the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
repairs required more than two days in total. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
f. Geographic coverage
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing that enhanced I/M be used in the designated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California FIP areas of Sacramento, Los Angeles, and Ventura. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing to limit the geographic scope of the I/M-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recall program to the FIP areas. EPA does not believe it would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be appropriate to apply the I/M-based recall program to areas not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
having full enhanced I/M based on the I/M 240 test. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
However, vehicles from outside the three FIP areas do impact 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air quality in the three FIP areas. Therefore, EPA requests 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comment on the option of including the vehicles tested in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifying enhanced I/M 240 test programs in other nonattainment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas in California in the I/M-based recall program. The test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results from such vehicles would then also be used to determine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance with the liability threshold. Under this option, only 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a state enhanced I/M program which either duplicates the federal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program or is approved by the Administrator as equivalent would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be allowed to participate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
g. Data collection from test-sites.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The Agency proposes that enhanced I/M testing facilities 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
submit the results of all I/M tests, as well as engine family 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
data from the underhood label, on all vehicles tested, to EPA on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a monthly basis. This information will be used to calculate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
failure rates. In addition, personnel at such facilities would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be required to retain and maintain results from I/M tests for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period of one year. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
h. Manufacturer notification of liability.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The Agency will provide manufacturers with cumulative test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
results on their engine families on a monthly basis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additionally EPA will notify vehicle manufacturers, by letter, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
within thirty days after the rate of failure meets or exceeds the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability threshold. Manufacturers' responsibility for repairs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
begins 30 days after EPA sends notification. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
I/M personnel will notify owners of failing vehicles, when 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles are brought in for emissions testing, with whom 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility for repair lies. Vehicle owners must be informed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
when repair responsibility for vehicles has been shifted to the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers. EPA requests comment on requiring manufacturers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to inform all vehicle owners, by letter, if the failure rate for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the enhanced in-use I/M standards has exceeded the liability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
threshold. Such a letter would state that vehicle owner repair 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsibility has ended and that the manufacturer is thereafter 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible for vehicle emission repairs until the statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
useful life ends as long as the vehicle is participating in a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
qualifying enhanced I/M program and has not been abused or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
tampered. One negative aspect of such a requirement is that it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
may lead vehicle owners to care less carefully for their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles, given the knowledge that much maintenance would now be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
covered by the manufacturer. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5. Fleets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00220 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As indicated by the Clean Air Act's fleet program, Congress believed it appropriate to place more stringent controls
on centrally fueled fleet vehicles due fleet operators' greater control over and flexibility in the fuel used by their
vehicles. Likewise, EPA believes it appropriate to propose similar actions here to obtain necessary additional
emission reductions.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Consequently, in addition to meeting the requirements of the enhanced in-use compliance program described above,
EPA proposes an enhanced ILEV fleet program. This program would require that a fraction of the post-1998 model year
vehicles purchased by fleets in the South Coast area having 10 of more vehicles which currently are, or are capable
of, being centrally fueled must also qualify as ILEVs. EPA established a special class of clean fuel vehicles called
Inherently Low Emission Vehicles (ILEVs) in regulations concerning exemptions from transportation control measures
for clean fuel fleet vehicles (40 CFR 88.311&hyph;93). ILEVs are required to have no significant evaporative emissions
even if their evaporative control systems malfunction. ILEVs therefore have an inherent emissions advantage over
other clean fuel vehicles since under any realistic enforcement program, some vehicles will be operating with dysfunctional
emission controls. Generally, ILEVs are alternative fueled vehicles since gasoline is a very volatile liquid; however,
high-technology diesel or low volatility gasoline vehicles might also be able to comply. Enhanced ILEVs would produce
all the emission benefits of other enhanced in-use compliance vehicles plus the benefits of no significant evaporative
emissions in-use.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes that 50 percent of all LDVs, LDTs, and MDVs purchased in 1999 by covered fleet operators for their covered
fleet meet this requirement and 70 percent of LDVs, LDTs, and MDVs purchased in each calendar year thereafter.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes to exempt from the enhanced ILEV fleet program the same types of vehicles which are exempt from the statutory
Clean Fuel Fleet program (section 241(5) of the Clean Air Act). The definitions promulgated by EPA for the Clean Fuel
Fleet program in 40 CFR 88.302&hyph;94 (i.e., for ``covered fleet operator'', ``centrally fueled'', and ``capable
of being centrally fueled'', as well as several others) are proposed to apply to the enhanced ILEV fleet program. In
addition, EPA proposes that the procedures laid out in the preamble for that final rule for determining how many vehicles
are to be considered capable of being centrally fueled (58 FR 64684) are to be applied by fleet operators in the enhanced
ILEV fleet program. EPA requests comment on whether this procedure should be converted to regulatory text and published
in the final rule on this FIP.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In addition, EPA is proposing an enhanced ILEV purchase credit program similar to the Clean Fuel Fleet credit program
(40 CFR 88.304&hyph;94). Each ILEV purchased early or in excess of a covered fleet operator's purchase requirement
could generate one purchase credit. EPA would issue a qualifying covered fleet operator a credit document which could
be held or sold. The original operator or any other covered fleet operator could redeem such credit documents in lieu
of enhanced ILEV purchases.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes that violations of the enhanced ILEV fleet program be subject to civil penalties of not more than $25,000.
This proposed penalty is identical to that applied by the Clean Air Act (section 205(a)) to violations of the Clean
Fuel Fleet program required by Part C of Title II.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the enhanced in-use vehicle standards apply to composite NMHC, it is possible that enhanced ILEVs could have
higher exhaust NMHC emissions to compensate for their inherently low evaporative emissions. Therefore, EPA requests
comments on the likelihood of this occurring and on the option of establishing a numerically lower composite NMHC
standard for ILEVs. These more stringent enhanced in-use standards for ILEVs are shown in the table below. EPA is not
proposing to change the enhanced in-use NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standards for ILEVs from those proposed for all enhanced in-use compliance vehicles in Section A.(2) above. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
