<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA is proposing that the enhanced ILEV fleet program be applicable to only vehicles purchased by covered fleet operators
in the South Coast due to the fact that even with all the additional emission controls being proposed today, the South
Coast is still not projected to in compliance with the ozone NAAQS by 2010. EPA requests comment on extending the enhanced
ILEV fleet program to the other two FIP areas. EPA also requests comment on whether the program should be revised to
ensure that vehicle turnover does not become artificially low in response to the enhanced ILEV fleet program but rather
remains at or near current levels.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
B. Enhanced In-Use Compliance Program for Heavy-duty Vehicles
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Applicability
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA is proposing that the enhanced heavy-duty vehicle program be applied to all new engines used in on-highway vehicles
over 14,000 pounds GVWR, beginning in model year 1999. All such heavy-duty vehicles offered for sale or registered
exclusively in California will need to comply with the enhanced vehicle standards described in the sections which
follow. (As described in the section on 49-state vehicles, importation of heavy-duty vehicles not complying with
the requirement of this program is prohibited after 1999.) Requirements that apply to multi-state registered vehicles
are discussed later, in section B(7). The enhanced vehicle program would, as an option, allow manufacturers to certify
engines for use in MDVs of GVWR between 8,500 and 14,000 pounds to engine-based standards, similar to the approach
taken in California's program. However, such vehicles would need to meet all of the requirements of the enhanced heavy-duty
vehicle program described in the sections below.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The application of the enhanced heavy-duty vehicle program on a statewide basis is considered necessary because
of the large volume of heavy-duty vehicle traffic flow into and out of the areas covered by the FIP. Any program requiring
usage of enhanced vehicles only in the FIP areas would be extremely difficult to implement and enforce. Also, EPA is
concerned about the emissions from HDVs from other states operating in the FIP areas, and about the potential for an
increase in this interstate traffic due to higher costs associated with enhanced HDVs. This issue is addressed in
section B(6).
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As with light-duty vehicles, EPA is interested in the possibility of achieving earlier inventory reductions by phasing
in new vehicle emission standards sooner. The inventory reduction achieved by earlier implementation could reduce
the rate at which heavy-duty vehicle turnover would have to occur. Therefore, in addition to requesting comment on
the program proposed below, EPA requests comment on an earlier phase-in, requiring a portion of the 1998 model year
heavy-duty vehicles sold to meet the enhanced vehicle requirements.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Authority
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As explained above, in promulgating a FIP under section 110(c) of the Act, EPA may take any actions that the state could
take and EPA believes that it, acting on behalf of California in the context of a FIP, may adopt a motor vehicle program
or supplement California's own motor vehicle program provided that EPA's actions would satisfy the criteria of section
209(b) for a waiver of federal preemption. These criteria are discussed above in the context of the enhanced light-duty
vehicle program. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00222 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA believes that the enhanced heavy-duty vehicle program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that would be applicable to vehicles and engines in California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the proposed FIP would satisfy the section 209 waiver 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
criteria. Thus, California could undertake the enhanced vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program described below, and, therefore, EPA has authority under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 110(c) to undertake the proposed programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the protectiveness of the standards, all of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the proposed standards are at least as protective as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise applicable federal standards (e.g., the generally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applicable NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and NMHC standards in the case of the vehicles and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines covered by the enhanced heavy-duty vehicle program). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Indeed, these standards are at least as stringent as the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
comparable standards that California has already adopted and for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which waivers have already been granted. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
With respect to the need to meet compelling and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
extraordinary conditions, EPA has repeatedly found that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California's air quality problems satisfy the need criterion of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 209(b) (see, e.g., 49 FR 18887 and 18890 (May 3, 1984), 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
58 FR 4144 (January 13, 1993)). Moreover, the magnitude of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reductions in emissions needed to reach attainment in the FIP 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas, including mobile source emissions (discussed in Preamble 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section III.H), provides ample evidence of the need for more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stringent motor vehicle emission standards in California. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Finally, with respect to the criterion of consistency with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section 202(a), EPA believes that its proposed enhanced vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program is consistent with both the technological feasibility and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification elements of that criterion. For the reasons 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
explained below, EPA believes that the various elements of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced vehicle program are technologically feasible within the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
leadtime provided, taking into account the cost of compliance. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Moreover, no aspect of the enhanced vehicle program establishes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any new certification test procedures that are inconsistent with 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
either existing federal or California test procedure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. Consequently, EPA believes that its proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced vehicle programs satisfy this prong of section 209 as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA also notes that its proposed rebuild requirements for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heavy-duty vehicles and its use restrictions on heavy-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles need not satisfy the section 209(b) criteria since those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements, being in-use regulation of vehicles directed at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle owners, and not the original manufacturers, are not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preempted by section 209(a). Thus, EPA has the authority to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
promulgate those requirements just as any other requirements not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to preemption under section 209. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Emissions Standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Oxides of Nitrogen Standard. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As mentioned earlier, heavy-duty engines are a major source 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions in the FIP areas. Because of this, EPA proposes 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to establish lower exhaust emission standards for heavy-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines in California vehicles beginning with the 1999 model 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. The emission standard for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 is proposed to be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.5 g/bhp-hr for all heavy-duty engines used in vehicles of GVWR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above 8,500 pounds, except those optionally certified to chassis-
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
based standards as discussed in previous sections. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Agency is proposing this standard because this level of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control is necessary in order to meet the FIP air quality goals 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and because this level represents a comparable reduction relative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to other vehicle classes. As discussed further below, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recognizes that this standard presents a significant feasibility 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
challenge for diesel engines. However, other fuel technologies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such as natural gas and alcohol fuels have demonstrated the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
potential to achieve these stringent levels, as well as other 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine designs. Given this, the Agency believes that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.5 g/bhp-hr standard is appropriate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
At the same time, EPA recognizes that implementing a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.5 g/bhp-hr standard may present limitations that may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unacceptable for some unique heavy-duty vehicle applications. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Therefore, the Agency is considering an additional, economic 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentive program that allows the sale of higher emitting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines. The Agency solicits comments on a plan that would allow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines to be sold at a relaxed level of 2.5 g/bhp-hr NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
, if a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
new vehicle emissions surcharge is paid. The program would begin 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the 1999 model year and would apply to all engines subject 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to the 1.5 g/bhp-hr standard and their associated vehicles. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
surcharge would be assessed also for an engine bought separately 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to be installed in an existing chassis. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
