<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Manufacturers of engines subject to the inherently low 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emissions requirement would submit test results or an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engineering evaluation to demonstrate that the certified vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have inherently low evaporative emissions. Test procedures are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in EPA Clean Fuel Fleet program regulations (58 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
11888, March 1, 1993). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
For vehicles certified under chassis-based requirements, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also proposes to require evaporative emission testing using the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Federal test procedure and standards. CARB adopted a new 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emission test in August 1990 and is now in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process of making improvements to its test procedure before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requesting a waiver from Federal requirements. It is unclear at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this point how closely aligned the two test procedures will be. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If CARB receives a waiver, EPA will consider the appropriateness 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of allowing manufacturers to certify vehicles using CARB's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emission test. EPA and CARB specify standards of 4 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 2 grams per test, respectively, for vehicles between 14,000 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and 26,000 pounds GVWR. EPA proposes to retain the 2 
<!-- PJG 0034 unknown -->
g/test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard for California vehicles. The numerical emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards proposed for heavy-duty vehicles apply equally to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification and recall testing. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Other California standards and procedures would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unaffected by the FIP, as described above for light-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles. Applicable Federal or California regulations would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continue to apply in all aspects of control programs for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission sources, including certification, assembly line testing, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and recall. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Heavy-Duty Engine Rebuild Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Introduction.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Heavy-duty engines are generally operated over mileages far 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exceeding the useful life specified when the engines are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certified. This is due to their high mileage accumulation rates, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
high replacement costs, and overall durable designs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additionally, the engine life is often extended by one or more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine rebuilds. Larger diesel engines, for example those used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in line-haul trucks and transit bus applications, are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufactured with replaceable cylinder liners to facilitate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine rebuilding. Engines with replaceable cylinder liners can 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
be readily rebuilt numerous times. It is not uncommon for such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines used in line-haul applications to continue operating for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
many times longer than their statutory useful life. Rebuilds are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
typically performed by a variety of parties including fleet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operators, small independent garages, the original engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer, and large independent assembly line operations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additionally, there are many independent rebuilders of engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
components.
<FOOTCITE>113</FOOTCITE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
113
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
``Heavy-duty Engine Rebuild Practices'', EPA Draft Report.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Testing conducted on rebuilt engines by EPA has indicated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that engine rebuilding is generally well done and emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
control does not usually suffer. However, EPA is concerned about 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
whether this will continue to be the case in the future. Given 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the very stringent enhanced in-use compliance program emissions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards for HDEs being proposed and the new control technology 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which will be applied, the in-use emissions performance of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rebuilt HDEs is unclear. There may be cost incentives to rebuild 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these engines to a non-OEM configuration which could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
substantially increase emissions. Given that most of the actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
life of some larger engines is beyond the traditional statutory 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
useful life, it is important to ensure that engines continue to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meet standards even after rebuilds. Therefore, EPA is proposing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to require rebuilt engines to be subject to the standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposed today for new engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
These requirements will apply to any 1999 and later model 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year heavy-duty vehicle subject to the enhanced standards that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is registered in California and is equipped with an engine that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
has been certified to engine-based standards as opposed to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
chassis-based standards. Vehicles certified to chassis-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards in these classes would be subject to full life I/M, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
similar to light-duty enhanced vehicles. Both requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure that enhanced vehicles continue to meet applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Program description
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA's primary concern is that HDEs meet applicable standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in use throughout their life cycle, including after engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rebuilding. To do this requires ensuring that, if vehicles are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
found not to comply at any time, someone is responsible for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
remedying the nonconformity. EPA proposes to require vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
owners to be able to demonstrate at all times that their engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is certified to meet the emissions standards it originally met. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
If the engine is within its original useful life, nothing more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be required. After the end of the engine's original useful 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
life, the owner would be required to obtain a useful life renewal 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certificate showing that the engine still met its original 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards. Such a certificate must be issued by a party that has 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
brought the owner's engine to a configuration that has been 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
previously certified by EPA (certification requirements are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
discussed below). The certifier would specify a useful life 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
renewal period on the certificate over which the certifier is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
responsible for emissions performance of the engine. Once the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified period expires, the vehicle owner would be required to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
acquire another useful life renewal certificate before 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
reregistering their vehicle in California. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
