<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=4 -->
Compliance with the fleet average standard would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determined by comparing the values in the second table with those 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculated in the first. If the actual values are below the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard, no emission surcharge is due. If either of the actual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
values exceed the standard for that year, a surcharge would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
due based on the difference. It would be determined by 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
multiplying the difference by the number of vehicles affected (in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the specific vehicle category) and then by the surcharge rate of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
$10,000 per ton. It is important to note that so long as a fleet 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
continues to replace vehicles at historical rates, no surcharge 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be required. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In order to assure the maximum flexibility, EPA is also 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
proposing to allow individual fleets to join together into larger 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fleets for determining compliance with the fleet standard. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provision should accommodate those fleets which may turn over 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
slower or more quickly than the average, notwithstanding any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
effects of the enhanced vehicle program. Additionally, it could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
benefit very small fleets, such as independently owned trucks. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The flexibility to join together should ease the impact of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
steady reduction in the fleet average emissions allowed on any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fleets with special compliance problems. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The annual emission rates developed in the above two tables 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
are based on diesel engine values, even though the program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
applies to all heavy-duty vehicles above 19,500 pounds GVWR. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This is consistent with the fact that diesels make up the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dominant fraction of the affected fleets. It also could serve to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
encourage turnover of gasoline fueled engines faster than they 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise would. This is because, even though they are generally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
less expensive than diesel engines, they would provide the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
credit in the fleet average as turning over a diesel engine. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Given the high evaporative emissions from gasoline engines, this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is a desirable result. Comments are invited on this approach. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
It should also be noted that the declining fleet average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards described above are proposed to apply only to heavy-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles above 19,500 pounds. Lighter HDVs are not included 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because EPA is proposing to include these vehicles in its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0320 forcenewline -->
I/M program. Because this group has a large number of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gasoline fueled vehicles (which would particularly benefit from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
an I/M program) and a large number of small fleets (which would 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
find the declining fleet average program particularly 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
burdensome), the Agency believes this to be the best approach for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
these vehicles. Exclusion of LHDVs from the averaging program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would not affect the role of 1999 and later LHDV engines in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other enhanced heavy-duty vehicle requirements already described. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Comments are invited on the alternative of including LHDVs in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
declining fleet average program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As discussed earlier, EPA is considering allowing enhanced 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
HDVs to be equipped with engines certified to a 2.5 g/bhp-hr 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard rather than a 1.5 g/bhp-hr standard as long as an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate new vehicle surcharge is paid by the purchaser. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because the surcharge is based on the excess NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emitted over the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
entire life of the vehicle, EPA believes that it would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate to count these vehicles in the fleet average as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emitting at 
<!-- PJG 0320 forcenewline -->
1.5 g/bhp-hr rather than 2.5 g/bhp-hr. Otherwise, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fleet owner would be required to pay twice for the same 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incremental emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
