<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As described more fully below in the nonroad fleet averaging 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program, EPA is concerned that engine and truck dealers may be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly hurt if fleet owners prebuy 4.0 g/bhp-hr NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines to reduce the need for purchases of 1.5 g/bhp-hr NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines in the initial years of the program. Dealers are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generally local, unlike all manufacturers and some fleet owners. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
They therefore may have trouble surviving more than a year of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
slow movement in the California market. Therefore, EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requesting comment on a manufacturer sales mandate for heavy-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
onhighway similar to that for nonroad engines. Interested 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
readers should review section C.(7)(b) of this appendix for more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
information. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on all aspects of the heavy-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
declining fleet average proposal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
10. Fleets 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As described in the enhanced light-duty vehicle section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(A(6)), EPA is proposing a revised Clean Fuel Fleet program in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the South Coast and requesting comment on expanding it into the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other two FIP areas. As described in the Clean Air Act Title II, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Part C and in EPA's Clean Fuel Fleet program regulations (see 58 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
FR 32474, June 10, 1993 and 58 FR 64679, December 9, 1993) the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national Clean Fuel Fleet program would require certain centrally 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fueled fleets to make a certain percentage of their new vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
purchases clean fuel vehicles. The CAA HD fleet program applies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to fleets up to 26,000 pounds GVWR and requires that 50 percent 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of all purchased be Clean Fuel Vehicles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As described in the light-duty enhanced vehicle program, EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is proposing to require that the affected fleets purchase ILEVs 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which meet a composite NMHC standard set to reflect their low 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emission potential, rather than merely clean fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles. This requirement ensures that the fleet program will 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
achieve significant hydrocarbon reductions beyond what is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
required by the general enhanced vehicle program. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
application of this program is straightforward for most vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
up to 14,000 lbs. GVWR. On the other hand, for vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certified to engine based standards (all vehicles above 14,000 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lbs GVWR and some under that threshold), an ILEV requirement is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
already in place and therefore no composite is necessary. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore is proposing to apply the fleet program only to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles certified to a chassis-based standard, currently to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicles up to 14,000 lbs GVWR. If EPA extends the chassis-based 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certification option to vehicles between 14,000 and 26,000 lbs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
GVWR, the fleet program will extend to these vehicles as well. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
11. General Enforcement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
It would be a violation of federal law for any engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers or owners to fail to comply with the specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements and prohibitions described above. In addition, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the enhanced heavy-duty vehicle program, it would be a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
violation of federal law for the California Department of Motor 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Vehicles (DMV) or any other department of the State of California 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(or any political subdivision thereof) to register any vehicle 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
subject to any requirement for which a valid certificate of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance or waiver (either paper-based or electronic) has not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
been presented. Any person providing fraudulent certification or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
otherwise aiding or abetting in the violation of this section, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would also be violating federal law. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
That is, it is a violation to permit registration or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facilitate reregistration of a model year 1999 or later engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for which there is not in force a valid certificate of conformity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from EPA (either for the engine or for the rebuild). Further it 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
is a violation to register any model year 1998 or previous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle or engine after 2001 without EPA confirmation that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
appropriate fleet average fee, if any, has been paid. Violations 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
could result in civil penalties under federal law of up to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
$25,000 per violation. Each instance of unlawful registration 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would be considered a separate offense. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=83 g=1 f=1 -->
C. Enhanced In-Use Compliance Program for Nonroad Engines Over 37 kw 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
1. Applicability 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA intends the enhanced in-use compliance program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad engines to apply to most nonroad engines at or above 37 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
kw, with the exceptions described above. This means that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agency is adding two categories of nonroad engines not covered 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
under the nationwide federal rulemaking. The first category is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
spark-ignition engines for all categories except recreational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
marine engines. The second is high speed Diesel marine engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
which are at or above 37 kw; these engines are not included in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
other FIP regulations. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 The enhanced nonroad engine program is proposed to include 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
both CI and SI engines. EPA is concerned that applying more 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stringent emission standards only to CI engines would create an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
incentive to replace diesel (CI) engines with gasoline (SI) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines, because if left unregulated these engines could have a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost advantage. This shift would increase emissions because the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unregulated gasoline engines would have higher exhaust levels and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly higher evaporative emissions than diesel engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is therefore proposing exhaust emission regulations for both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CI and SI engines (i.e., all engine types in the size range 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described) and adding a requirement to prevent evaporative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emission increases. This strategy should level the playing field 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from an emissions perspective and avoid introducing an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
inappropriate encouragement to shift to gasoline and higher HC 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00234 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The enhanced nonroad engine program is also proposed to include CI marine engines over 37 kw which have rated speeds
at maximum torque of over 1200 revolutions per minute. These engines are generally used in non-oceangoing commercial
vessels; oceangoing ships are dealt with in other portions of this FIP. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The Agency notes that the proposal would include generators at or above 37 kw used on oil platforms, if they fit the mobility
requirements defining nonroad engines in EPA's nationwide rule. If not, they are regulated as part of the platform
under EPA's Outer Continental Shelf (OCS) rules if they are permanently placed at the OCS site. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The provisions of this section apply only to engines sold for use in the FIP areas (although, as discussed later, EPA
is considering options extending beyond the FIP areas). Nonroad engines are significantly less mobile than onhighway
engines. Thus EPA has tailored its program to apply only to engines in the FIP areas. Any equipment with an applicable
engine sold for use in a FIP area will be required to have a prominent exterior sticker showing that it meets the standards
and other provisions of this program. A fleet averaging program, similar to that for on-highway, will also be applied
to engines used in the FIP area to ensure continued turnover to cleaner engines. The enforcement of these two requirements
is discussed further in the nonroad fleets section below. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA proposes that after the inception of the nonroad engine program described in this Appendix, only engines complying
with the provisions of this program may be imported for use into the FIP areas. Use of older engines, not previously
used in the areas, is prohibited. Additionally, use of new engines which do not comply with the program is also prohibited.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
2. Authority 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA is proposing this enhanced in-use compliance program for nonroad engines using its broad remedial authority
under section 110(c). Section 213 of the Clean Air Act provides EPA with specific authority to regulate nonroad engines.
Moreover, no provision of section 213, or any other section of the Clean Air Act, prohibits EPA from exercising its
section 110(c) authority in the manner proposed herein. EPA's data indicate that the engines regulated herein, nonroad
engines at or above 37 kw including SI engines and diesel marine engines with speeds greater than 1200 RPM, contribute
to ozone pollution in the FIP areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
3. Emissions Standards 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
a. Oxides of Nitrogen Standard.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Parallel to the onhighway requirements, EPA proposes that 1999 and later model year nonroad engines for use in the
Ventura and South Coast nonattainment areas must meet a NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standard of 1.5 g/bhp-hr. For Sacramento, EPA proposes a 2.5 g/bhp-hr standard, reflecting a somewhat lesser need
for control in that area, but is considering a 1.5 g/bhp-hr standard for reasons discussed below. EPA requests comment
on these proposed standards and their areas of application. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As with onhighway engines, EPA is also considering an option to allow use of engines emitting up to 2.5 g/bhp-hr NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 in the South Coast or Ventura areas, subject to an emissions surcharge which would be paid at the time of equipment purchase.
This option is intended to cover unique applications where the 1.5 g/bhp-hr standard might present unacceptable
limitations. The surcharge, shown in the table below for applicable nonroad categories, is based on a cost of $10,000
per ton of increased NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions over the life of the engine. The calculations are based on usage data supplied by manufacturers, as presented
in EPA's ``Draft Regulatory Support Document for Control of NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and Smoke Emissions from Nonroad Compression-Ignition Engines Greater than or Equal to 50 Horsepower'' (April 1993).
EPA assumed that each category of engines would be operated at its average annual rate (hours per year) for sixteen
years. Lifetime emissions are discounted at a seven percent annual rate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In computing the emissions surcharges, the Agency combined equipment types with similar surcharges into 7 different
groups and assigned a surcharge value to each based on information from manufacturers. EPA is proposing that manufacturers
of nonroad equipment containing an engine of over 37 kw must classify each type of equipment into one of these categories.
Any equipment fitting possibly into two categories will be presumed to fit into the higher surcharge category unless
further information is provided. Details of the individual values can be found in the docket.
<FOOTCITE>116</FOOTCITE>
 EPA requests comment on the categories listed in the table below, especially whether any engine applications may
be missing from the list, or whether the categories need further definition to distinguish between similar applications.
EPA also requests comment on the possible need to further divide categories, perhaps by engine size, to avoid inappropriate
fees for some engines. Any comments should reflect the need to keep the set of surcharges simple enough to maintain
a manageable program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
116
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
``New Vehicle/Equipment Surcharges for Heavy-Duty Onhighway and Nonroad Engines Under the California FIP,'' EPA
memo to the Docket from Alan Stout, February 1994.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
