<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00237 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
c. Test requirements.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 EPA is proposing to conduct certification and enforcement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
testing of nonroad heavy-duty engines regulated under this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program using the federal nonroad procedures for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
hydrocarbon emission measurement expected to be finalized by May 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
30, 1994, except that test fuels would be based on applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California specifications. The eight mode steady state duty-cycle and other test procedures that will be finalized
in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
national rule for CI engines are proposed here to apply to both 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compression-ignition and spark-ignition engines, regardless of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fuel type. Exhaust hydrocarbon measurements would be quantified 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as nonmethane hydrocarbons (NMHC), or other fuel specific 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equivalents. Assembly-line testing would be performed under EPA's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Selective Enforcement Audit testing program as described in 40 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
CFR part 89 subpart F. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Since the test procedure being proposed today is a steady-state procedure, it may not result in control designs for
nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines that produce the degree of in-use emissions reductions 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected. For example, spark-ignition engines can exhibit very 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
different emissions characteristics on different test cycles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such as cycles with transient operating conditions. The Agency is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
also concerned that the 8-mode test may be inadequate for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
verifying the compliance of CI engines with the NMHC and NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards proposed here, as discussed in the May 17, 1993 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nationwide nonroad proposal. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Because of these concerns, the Agency will monitor engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
produced and certified very carefully to be assured that engines 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
do, in fact, yield proportional in-use emissions reductions. The 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Agency will also remain alert to the possible use of defeat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
devices or design approaches that result in non-proportional 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
emissions reductions under operating conditions that are outside 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the test procedure operating conditions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Additionally, the Agency is engaged in a program to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
characterize the in-use operation of nonroad engines. This 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program will allow EPA to develop representative duty cycles, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaluate the emissions impact of those cycles and determine which 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cycle will best ensure engine designs that will yield the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
expected level of in-use control. The Agency may change the duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cycle used to test nonroad engines in a subsequent rulemaking if 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the Agency determines that such a change is needed to assure that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
projected emission reductions actually occur in use. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Manufacturers of engines subject to the inherently low 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
evaporative emissions requirement would submit test results or an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engineering evaluation to demonstrate that the certified vehicles 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
have inherently low evaporative emissions. Test procedures are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in EPA Clean Fuel Fleet program regulations (58 FR 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
11888, March 1, 1993). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The standards and procedures proposed for enhanced nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heavy-duty engines would apply equally to certification and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle recall testing. EPA requests comment on these test 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedures, especially on the use of the steady-state test for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
spark ignition engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
4. Inspection &amp; Maintenance (I/M) Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As mentioned earlier, EPA is not proposing I/M requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for nonroad engines. Developing a new I/M program for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
diverse set of applications involved would be a very difficult 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and complex undertaking and would present significant testing and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
administrative burdens. However, an in-use testing program (such 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as I/M) would allow EPA to reconsider the need to require nonroad 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines to be low evaporative and the need for the nonroad engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rebuild requirements. EPA therefore requests comment on the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
feasibility and desirability of adopting an I/M program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heavy-duty nonroad engines covered under the enhanced in-use 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance program. The I/M program could apply to any or all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad engines over 50 hp sold for use in California's 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designated FIP areas. The reader is referred to the light-duty 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
vehicle I/M discussion (section A.(4) of this appendix) for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
detailed discussion of such enhanced vehicle requirements as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
geographic scope, testing, enhanced I/M threshold, manufacturer 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability, tracking of enhanced I/M test results, threshold 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
calculation, data collection, and manufacturer notification of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
liability. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Commenters recommending a nonroad I/M program should comment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
on the proper I/M test for heavy-duty nonroad engines and whether 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the capabilities for such tests would be needed at every 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
facility. They should also comment on what the specifications 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
for such a facility would be and whether any such facility could 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
handle all types of nonroad equipment. An I/M program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad engines would apply for the full operating life of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engine. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00238 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
5. Nonroad Engine Rebuild Requirements
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
As with onhighway engines, nonroad engines are used for periods of time greatly exceeding the proposed useful life
for nonroad engines (10 years, or 8000 hours, whichever comes first). For this reason, EPA proposes to apply the full
rebuild program described for onhighway heavy-duty vehicles (section B.(4) of this appendix) to enhanced nonroad
engines. As with onhighway engines, nonroad engines will not be subject to an I/M program and EPA is concerned about
the possible adverse effect of engine rebuilding on the emissions from engines certified to the enhanced nonroad
engine standards. If these engines are rebuilt improperly, they may have very high emissions which could outweigh
many of the emissions benefits of the FIP programs. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on all aspects of this proposal, especially on any aspects of the onhighway heavy-duty engine
rebuild program which are inappropriate for nonroad engines. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
6. Recall Program
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA proposes to conduct all aspects of the enhanced nonroad engine recall program in the same manner as the enhanced
onhighway recall program, as highlighted below. Interested readers should review the light-duty vehicle recall
provisions (section A.(5) of this appendix) and the heavy-duty vehicle recall provisions (section B.(6) of this
appendix) for additional details regarding this program. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Nonroad engines will be recall tested using certification test procedures and fuels discussed above in section C.(3)(c).
As for the other enhanced vehicle programs, EPA proposes to test nonroad engines unless they have been subject to obvious
abuse or tampering. If there are particular usage characteristics that could contribute to in-use emissions failures,
manufacturers must consider them in the design of their products. For example, nonroad engines are likely to be used
in environmental conditions that may include rough terrain, high levels of dust, and exposure to water. EPA would
not consider exposure to such conditions grounds for excluding engines from the recall test sample. The Agency believes
that addressing these challenges is appropriate and necessary to provide assurance that applicable emissions standards
continue to be met in actual use throughout the life of the engine. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
In connection with the engine rebuilding requirements described above, EPA also proposes to conduct recall testing
throughout the operating life of engines. EPA believes that this is the most appropriate way to ensure that engines
are meeting the enhanced nonroad engine standards in use. For recall testing after the end of the original useful life,
EPA will group engines by the certified configuration. Any subsequent recall orders will hold the certifier of that
engine configuration responsible for failure to comply with applicable emissions standards. Remedial actions
would be solely the responsibility of the certifier. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA requests comments on all aspects of the enhanced in-use compliance recall program for nonroad engines, including
the recall provisions that have been proposed for onhighway heavy- duty vehicles and light-duty vehicles. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=81 g=1 f=1 -->
7. Fleet Averaging Program 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Like the heavy-duty onhighway program, the enhanced in-use compliance program for nonroad equipment will reduce
emissions as projected only if new equipment is purchased and old equipment is retired at historical rates. Additionally,
the use requirements which apply in the FIP areas must be obeyed to be effective. If old equipment is kept longer than
in the past, the emissions reductions could be substantially less. These reductions are critical for achieving attainment;
therefore, EPA is proposing fleet average and sticker programs to ensure traditional turnover rates and usage patterns
continue. Due to the inherent complexities of such a program, among other things, EPA is also considering and requesting
comment on a statewide nonroad program with sales requirements. Details of this alternative program are provided
at the end of this fleet averaging section. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Just as with heavy-duty onhighway engines, EPA is proposing to ensure that turnover is maintained by implementing
a declining fleet average NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions program. Under the proposed fleet average program, if a fleet's turnover rate falls behind the historic
average turnover rate for nonroad equipment, then an emissions surcharge will be assessed on the additional NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions caused by the higher emitting engines. The fleet average NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions level allowed is reduced each calendar year to reflect the appropriate turnover rates. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
