<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00242 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
a. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Administration of the fleet average nonroad program. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The fleet average requirement would be enforced through a program very similar to annual registration for onhighway
vehicles. Nonroad registration is proposed to occur every January beginning in 1999. At that time, owners would bring
documents representing their equipment to EPA along with a preliminary calculation of the surcharge due, if any.
EPA would issue stickers for the equipment in the fleet as well as a certification that the fleet complies for that year.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Each piece of equipment that falls under the provisions of this section would be required to have an EPA sticker prominently
displayed on it to be used or stored (except under certain conditions) in the FIP areas. EPA's regulations describe
the size and color of the sticker and its placement. Individual stickers would be issued for each engine document (as
described below) that has been reviewed by EPA. The engine documents would be designed to be marked each year in such
a way that a fleet owner would be unable to use the same document to obtain two stickers. Documents would also show the
name of the equipment owner, and only EPA could reissue documents when ownership is transferred. This system would
be set up to limit the potential market for stolen documentation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The documentation required must include the serial number of the engine, the equipment type, the horsepower rating,
and the certified emission standard. For equipment produced after the effective date of this FIP, EPA proposes to
require manufacturers to supply the documentation to purchasers just as do vehicle manufacturers. In the case of
existing equipment, documentation would be created by EPA based on sworn statements by the owner. The documentation
must also include the useful life renewal certificates as described in the rebuild program (for 1999 and later model
year nonroad engines that are beyond their original useful life period) and proof that any necessary repairs have
been performed by an authorized party for any nonroad engine subject to a recall action. Owners would be required to
have documented all engines in their fleet by or during the first year of registration cycle in January 1999. Only those
pre-1999 nonroad engines shown in a California fleet in that first year may be re-registered to be used in the FIP areas
at any time after 1999. EPA retains the right to conduct physical inspections of the engines or deputize local government
agencies to do so. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
As with onhighway registration programs, stickers will be applied by owners since the equipment could not be brought
into the surcharge office. To prevent cheating through the placement of stickers on improper equipment, stickers
would be serially numbered and the sticker number recorded on the engine document and on a separate card with all fleet
engine serial numbers which would be given to the owner. Stickers would also describe the engine type and serial number.
EPA proposes that it or its deputies be able to inspect any site where affected engines are used or stored in the FIP areas
and require surrender of such documentation. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is proposing a system based on paper documents. However, a computerized registration data system, similar to
the types of registration systems used for onhighway vehicles is possible. Such a system has the potential to minimize
transaction problems, assist in title transfers, and reduce problems associated with sole reliance on physical
documents. It would also require more resources. EPA is open to working with the California Department of Motor Vehicles
to develop such a system and invites comments on its benefits and feasibility. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This sticker system will apply only to engines operated in the FIP areas. However, the system is proposed to be accessible
statewide to accommodate leasing companies which own a significant portion of the nonroad equipment used in the state.
New nonroad equipment not meeting the enhanced in-use compliance program requirements will be allowed to be sold
even in the FIP areas to accommodate distributors and such leasing companies. EPA plans to monitor sales and will target
audits on any FIP area purchaser of significant numbers of non-FIP area equipment. EPA is proposing to require use
of only stickered equipment in FIP areas year round. Such a program eases enforcement and is necessary, at least in
Los Angeles, to prevent ozone exceedances. Nonstickered equipment will be allowed only if it is being transported
through, or out of, the FIP area (and remains in a loaded condition while in the FIP area) or is being stored on an equipment
leasing or distributor's lot for later use outside the FIP area. Such lots should not be on or adjacent to any job site
in the FIP area. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA will have the authority to enter premises to inspect equipment, stickers, and other documentation. The unauthorized
presence or operation of equipment without an appropriate sticker, the application of a sticker to any engine except
the one for which it was designated, the use of false documents to obtain an EPA sticker, or the falsification of any
documentation required under this section is strictly prohibited and will be punishable by fines of up to $25,000.

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00243 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
b. Optional state-wide program. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
EPA is concerned that the FIP areas may be too small to create sufficient market incentive for equipment manufacturers
to develop and offer for sale equipment meeting the proposed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards and that therefore, little equipment would be provided 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
meeting the requirements of this program. EPA is further 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
concerned that even if there are such engines, that the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
availability of more traditional equipment in areas adjacent to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the FIP areas will create incentives for people to attempt to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
avoid the fleet average requirements. Finally, EPA is concerned 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that FIP residents may choose to comply with the first years of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the fleet average requirements by purchasing extra nationally-regulated engines right before the start of the program.
While 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
such a strategy does not impact air quality, provided full 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance occurs, it could be extremely destabilizing for the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment dealers and could put the viability of dealers' 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
businesses at risk. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
To address these concerns, EPA is requesting comment on 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
various revisions to the enhanced in-use compliance program for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonroad engines. First, as described in the standards discussion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(section C.(3)), the Agency is requesting comment on a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1.5 g/bhp-hr NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standard for all three FIP areas. This revision 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would eliminate any problems that a Sacramento-only standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would create for manufacturers. Unfortunately, it would not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
address most of the other concerns. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Another option is to apply the enhanced nonroad emission 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standards statewide (including the 1.5 g/bhp-hr NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 standard, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exhaust NMHC standard and the low evaporative requirements 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
described in section C.(3)). Under this approach, only equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
certified to the enhanced nonroad engine standards would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
available in California beginning in 1999. The statewide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement expands the market available for engine and equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers and should provide them sufficient incentive to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
offer all types of nonroad equipment. Further, in a statewide 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program, the size of the state and the location of the FIP areas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
should counteract any incentive to cheat by using higher emitting 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment available in neighboring areas. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Unfortunately, statewide application will not eliminate the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possibility of significant pre-FIP purchasing and corresponding 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
lack of purchasing in the early years of the program and the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
destabilizing effect on the economy both could have. EPA is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
therefore asking for comment on a requirement that engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers sell a similar fraction of their engines in the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
state in the FIP years as they did on average for the three years 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
preceding the FIP. Fractional requirements would be converted 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
into numerical requirements to facilitate averaging and trading 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
among engine types and manufacturers. No banking would be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
allowed, since banking would create the destabilizing problems 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the program was designed to avoid. Trading and averaging 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
permitted would be modeled on the current averaging, and trading 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
program for heavy-duty on-highway engines (55 FR 30584, July 26, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
1990). EPA is proposing to apply this requirement to engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers, rather than equipment manufacturers, because the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
equipment manufacturers cannot control the emissions level of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
their equipment without the cooperation of the engine 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
EPA is also considering a more flexible sales requirement to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ensure that natural economic fluctuations do not affect 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance. Such a program would require that in the first year 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the program manufacturers sell at least eighty percent of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number of engines they sold in the previous year. In the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following several years the sales requirement could be reduced to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
seventy, sixty and fifty percent of the sales in the 1998 model 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
year. Finally, once the transition to FIP engines was complete 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and market fluctuations were unlikely, the sales requirement 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
could be dropped entirely. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Either form of the option would diminish somewhat the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
importance of the requirements for registration, fleet average 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
determination, stickers and other enforcement programs directed 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
towards the equipment user. Presumably, if manufacturers are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
selling the new equipment at historical rates, it is getting used 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and is replacing the old equipment at historical rates. EPA 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would consider, therefore, if it adopted a strict sales 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirement, dropping the fleet average program and sticker 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
enforcement. The Agency almost certainly would need to maintain 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
a nonroad registration program as a check on the efficacy of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sales requirement. If the requirements proved to be ineffective 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
in maintaining historical turnover, then EPA would have to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implement a fleet average requirement in order to ensure the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
attainment targets were met. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
While EPA acknowledges that sales requirements can force 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturers to offer discounts and otherwise subsidize their 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
engines, they are in a better position to accommodate the impacts 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of the program than fleets or dealers. Manufacturers sell 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
throughout the nation and can recover costs by spreading small 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
cost increases across a larger market than can fleets or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dealers. On the other hand, lack of business in one area for a 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
few years could put many dealers, which operate in much smaller 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
areas, out of business. It is important to note that so long as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the turnover rates stay at historical levels through the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
implementation of the FIP, both programs would be transparent to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the regulated parties. 
