standards specified in paragraph (u)(3(i) of this section, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
chooses to perform a compliance test to demonstrate compliance 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
with the requirements of paragraph (u)(3)(ii) of this section, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
shall comply with the requirements of &sect;60.8 (c), (d), and (e). 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
The test shall be performed and data reduced and reported as 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
follows: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(A) Each emission test shall be conducted while the unit is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operated at maximum capacity and operating under representative 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
operating conditions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(B) Each emission test shall be conducted in accordance with the appropriate test methods in 40 CFR part 60, appendix
A. Except for the modifications in this paragraph and unless otherwise approved by the Administrator: NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 shall be measured in accordance with 40 CFR part 60, appendix A, Method 7E; CO shall be measured in accordance with 40
CFR part 60, appendix A, Method 10; and the diluent shall be measured using 40 CFR part 60, appendix A, Method 3A. Testing
shall be conducted for twelve 15 minute testing periods. Compliance shall be evaluated for each 15 minute period.
Data recorded during testing to determine the 15 minute average shall either be continuously integrated by the testing
instrument and data recorder, or recorded manually at 30 second intervals during each 15 minute test period. Zero
and calibration checks shall be conducted after each 15 minute test period. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(C) The ppm CO emission limit required by paragraph (u)(3)(i)(B) of this section shall be established during the initial
compliance test for NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions to establish a ppm CO emission limit that represents good operating and combustion practices for fuel(s)
burned in the unit. The CO emission limit established for determining subsequent compliance shall be documented
in the applicable certification report required under paragraph (u)(6)(i)(C) of this section. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00433 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(ii) Any owner or operator that chooses to comply with the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of paragraph (u)(3)(ii) of this section by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
compliance method specified in paragraph (u)(3)(ii)(A) of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section shall tune the unit using the equipment tuning procedure 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified in this paragraph.
<FOOTCITE>38</FOOTCITE>
 Nothing in the equipment tuning 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure shall be construed to require any act or omission that 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
would result in unsafe conditions or would be in violation of any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
regulation or requirement established by Factory Mutual, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Industrial Risk Insurers, National Fire Prevention Association, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
California Department of Industrial Relations (Occupational 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Safety and Health Division), Federal Occupational Safety and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Health Administration, or other relevant regulations and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
38
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
This tuning procedure is based on a tune-up procedure developed by KVD, Inc. for the USEPA.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(A) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 1. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Operate the unit at the firing rate most 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
typical of normal operation. If the unit experiences significant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
load variations during normal operation, operate it at its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
average firing rate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(B) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 2. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
At this firing rate, record stack gas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
temperature, oxygen concentration, and CO concentration (for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gaseous fuels) or smoke-spot
<FOOTCITE>39</FOOTCITE>
 number (for liquid fuels), and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
observe flame conditions after unit operation stabilizes at the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rate selected. If the excess oxygen in the stack gas is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at the lower end of the range of typical minimum values,
<FOOTCITE>40</FOOTCITE>
 and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
if the CO emissions are low and there is no smoke, the unit is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
probably operating at near optimum efficiency at this particular 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rate. However, complete the remaining portion of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
procedure to determine whether still lower oxygen levels are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
practical. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
39
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
The smoke-spot number can be determined with ASTM Test Method D&hyph;2156 or with the Bacharach method.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<FOOTNOTE>
<FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
40
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</FOOTNAME>
<!-- PJG ITAG l=28 g=1 f=1 -->
Typical minimum oxygen levels for boilers at high firing rates are: 0.5 to 3 percent for natural gas; and 2 to 4 percent
for liquid fuels.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
</FOOTNOTE>

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(C) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 3.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 Increase combustion air flow to the furnace 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
until stack gas oxygen levels increase by 1 to 2 percent over the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
level measured in Step 2. As in Step 2, record the stack gas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
temperature, CO concentration (for gaseous fuels) or smoke-spot 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number (for liquid fuels), and observe flame conditions for these 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
higher oxygen levels after boiler operation stabilizes. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(D) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 4. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Decrease combustion air flow until the stack 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gas oxygen concentration is at the level measured in Step 2. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
From this level gradually reduce the combustion air flow, in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
small increments. After each increment, record the stack gas 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
temperature, oxygen concentration, CO concentration (for gaseous 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fuels) and smoke-spot number (for liquid fuels). Also, observe 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the flame and record any changes in its condition. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(E) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 5. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Continue to reduce combustion air flow 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stepwise, until one of these limits is reached: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
1
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Unacceptable flame conditions_such as flame impingement on furnace walls or burner parts, excessive flame 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
carryover, or flame instability. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Stack gas CO concentrations greater than 400 ppm. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
3
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Smoking at the stack. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
4
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
) Equipment-related limitations_such as low windbox/furnace pressure differential, built in air-flow limits,
etc. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(F) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 6. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Develop an O
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
/CO curve (for gaseous fuels) or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
O
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=2 -->
2
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
/smoke curve (for liquid fuels) similar to those shown in 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Figures 1 and 2 using the excess oxygen and CO or smoke-spot 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number data obtained at each combustion air flow setting. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 (G) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 7. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
From the curves prepared in Step 6, find the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
stack gas oxygen levels where the CO emissions or smoke-spot 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
number equal the following values: 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<TABLE>

<!-- PJG tableformat 3,L2,tp0,i1,s25,r25,r25  -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=95 g=1 f=1 -->
&blank;
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Fuel 
<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Measurement 
<!-- PJG 007 0150 intable -->
1Value
<!-- PJG 007 0152 intable -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=1 f=1 -->
Gaseous 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
CO Emissions 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
400 ppm. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=6 f=1 -->
#1
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=1 f=1 -->
 &amp; 2 oils 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Smoke-spot 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Number 1.
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=6 f=1 -->
#4
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=1 f=1 -->
 oil 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Smoke-spot number 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Number 2. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=6 f=1 -->
#5
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=01 g=1 f=1 -->
 oil 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Smoke-spot number 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Number 3. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
Other oils 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Smoke-spot number 
<!-- PJG 007 0104 intable -->
Number 4.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

</TABLE>

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The above conditions are referred to as CO or smoke threshold, or as the minimum excess oxygen level. Compare this minimum
value of excess oxygen to the expected value provided by the combustion unit manufacturer. If the minimum level found
is substantially higher than the value provided by the combustion unit manufacturer, burner adjustments can probably
be made to improve fuel and air mixing, thereby allowing operation with less air. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(H) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 8. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Add 0.5 to 2.0 percent to the minimum excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
oxygen level found in Step 7 and reset burner controls to operate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
automatically at this higher stack gas oxygen level. This margin 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above the minimum oxygen level accounts for fuel variations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variations in atmospheric conditions, load changes, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonrepeatability or play in automatic controls. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(I) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 9. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If the load of the combustion unit varies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly during normal operation, repeat Steps 1&hyph;8 for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rates that represent the upper and lower limits of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
range of the load. Because control adjustments at one firing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate may affect conditions at other firing rates, it may not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possible to establish the optimum excess oxygen level at all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rates. If this is the case, choose the burner control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
settings that give best performance over the range of firing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rates. If one firing rate predominates, settings should optimize 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions at that rate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(J) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 10. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Verify that the new settings can accommodate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the sudden load changes that may occur in daily operation without 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
adverse effects. Do this by increasing and decreasing load 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rapidly while observing the flame and stack. If any of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions in Step 5 result, reset the combustion controls to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
provide a slightly higher level of excess oxygen at the affected 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rates. Next, verify these new settings in a similar 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fashion. Then make sure that the final control settings are 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
recorded at steady-state operating conditions. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG /STAG -->
</TEXT>
</DOC>



<DOC>
<DOCNO> FR940505-1-00434 </DOCNO>
<PARENT> FR940505-1-00012 </PARENT>
<TEXT>
 
<!-- PJG STAG 4702 -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
(v) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Emissions of oxides of nitrogen from small 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
institutional, commercial, and industrial boilers, steam 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
generators, and process heaters. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 (1) Applicability.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 For the purposes of this paragraph, the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
affected area includes the Sacramento Metro Area as described for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
ozone in 40 CFR 81.305. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 (i) Any owner or operator of a boiler, steam generator, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process heater with a rated heat input capacity equal to or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
greater than 1 million British thermal units per hour (mmBtu/hr) 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and less than 5 mmBtu/hr shall comply with the applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements standards of paragraphs (v)(1) through (v)(7) of 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
this section. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 (ii) Any owner or operator of a boiler, steam generator, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process heater with a rated heat input capacity equal to or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
greater than 1 mmBtu/hr and less than 5 mmBtu/hr that claims an 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
exemption from the standards of paragraph (v)(3) of this section 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
because of the exemption criteria specified under 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
paragraph (v)(4) of this section shall comply with the applicable 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
requirements of paragraph (v)(6)(iii)(H) of this section. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 (2) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Definitions.
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 For the purposes of paragraph (v) of this 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
section, the following definitions shall apply. All terms not 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
defined herein shall have the meaning given them in &sect;52.2950. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Annual heat input
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means the actual amount of heat released 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
by fuels burned in a unit during a 12 calendar month rolling 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
period, based on the fuel's higher heating value. The annual 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heat input shall be calculated as the sum of the previous 12 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
monthly fuel use rates multiplied by the fuel's higher heating 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
value. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Boiler or steam generator
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means any combustion equipment 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
used in any institutional, commercial, or industrial operation 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
that is designed to burn gaseous and/or liquid fuel and used to 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
produce steam, heat water and/or other fluids, and/or generate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
electricity. A boiler or steam generator does not include any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
waste heat recovery unit that is used to recover sensible heat 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
from the exhaust of a combustion turbine or any unfired waste 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heat recovery boiler that is used to recover sensible heat from 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
the exhaust of any combustion equipment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
British thermal unit (Btu)
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means the amount of heat required 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
to raise the temperature of one pound of water from 59
<!-- PJG 0017 degreeslatlong -->
&cir;F to 60
<!-- PJG 0017 degreeslatlong -->
&cir;F 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
at one atmosphere. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Heat input
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means the chemical heat released due to fuel 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combustion in a unit, using the higher heating value of the fuel. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
This does not include the sensible heat of incoming combustion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
air. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Higher heating value (HHV)
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means the total heat liberated 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
per mass of fuel burned (Btu per pound), when fuel and dry air at 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
standard conditions undergo complete combustion and all resultant 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
products are brought to their standard states at standard 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions. If certification of the HHV is not provided by the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
third party fuel supplier, it shall be determined by one of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
following test methods: ASTM D2015&hyph;85 for solid fuels; ASTM 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
D240&hyph;87 or ASTM D2382&hyph;88 for liquid hydrocarbon fuels; or ASTM 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
D1826&hyph;88 or ASTM D1945&hyph;81 in conjunction with ASTM D3588&hyph;89 for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
gaseous fuels. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Non-gaseous
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 fuel means any fuel which is not a gas at 68 
<!-- PJG 0040 degreeslatlong -->
&cir;F 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
and one atmosphere. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
NO
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=5 f=4 -->
X
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 emissions means the sum of nitric oxides and nitrogen dioxide in the flue gas, collectively expressed as nitrogen

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
dioxide. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Process heater
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means any combustion equipment used in any 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
institutional, commercial, or industrial operation that is 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
designed to burn gaseous and/or liquid fuel and which transfers 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
heat from combustion gases to water, heat transfer medium, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
process streams. Process heater does not include any kiln or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
oven used for drying, baking, cooking, calcining or vitrifying; 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any unfired waste heat recovery heater that is used to recover 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
sensible heat from the exhaust of any combustion equipment; or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
any fuel-fired degreasing or metal finishing equipment. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Rated heat input capacity
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
 means the heat input capacity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified on the nameplate of the combustion unit. If the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
combustion unit has been altered or modified such that its 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
maximum heat input is different than the heat input capacity 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
specified on the nameplate, the new maximum heat input shall be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
considered as the rated heat input capacity. This alteration or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modification can be through either burner alteration or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
modification or installation of a fixed orifice. The new maximum 
