<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
The above conditions are referred to as CO or smoke threshold, or 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
as the minimum excess oxygen level. Compare this minimum value 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
of excess oxygen to the expected value provided by the combustion 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
unit manufacturer. If the minimum level found is substantially 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
higher than the value provided by the combustion unit 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
manufacturer, burner adjustments can probably be made to improve 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
fuel and air mixing, thereby allowing operation with less air. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(H) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 8. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
Add 0.5 to 2.0 percent to the minimum excess 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
oxygen level found in Step 7 and reset burner controls to operate 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
automatically at this higher stack gas oxygen level. This margin 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
above the minimum oxygen level accounts for fuel variations, 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
variations in atmospheric conditions, load changes, and 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
nonrepeatability or play in automatic controls. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(I) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 9. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
If the load of the combustion unit varies 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
significantly during normal operation, repeat Steps 1&hyph;8 for 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rates that represent the upper and lower limits of the 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
range of the load. Because control adjustments at one firing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rate may affect conditions at other firing rates, it may not be 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
possible to establish the optimum excess oxygen level at all 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
firing rates. If this is the case, choose the burner control 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
settings that give best performance over the range of firing 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
rates. If one firing rate predominates, settings should optimize 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
conditions at that rate. 
<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->

<!-- PJG 0012 frnewline -->
(J) 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=3 -->
Step 10. 
<!-- PJG /ITAG -->

<!-- PJG ITAG l=11 g=1 f=1 -->
